window.bookIndex=[{"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "url": "chapter01.html#real-numbers-algebraic-expressions-and-mathematical-reasoning", "text": "Chapter overview. A mathematical model is only as\ntrustworthy as its numbers, units, and algebra. In health sciences, a\nconversion error can change the meaning of a measurement by a factor of\none thousand. This chapter builds a precise language for numbers and\nexpressions, develops methods for simplifying and interpreting them, and\nintroduces R and Python as tools for numerical exploration and\nverification. Learning outcomes. By the end of the chapter, you\nshould be able to: classify real numbers; use order and interval\nnotation; interpret absolute value as distance; apply exponent and\nradical laws while respecting domains; simplify and factor expressions;\nmanipulate rational expressions with restrictions; use scientific\nnotation and significant figures responsibly; perform dimensional\nanalysis; distinguish exact from approximate results; and verify\ncalculations with R and Python."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Opening\ninvestigation: How much does a unit matter?", "url": "chapter01.html#opening-investigation-how-much-does-a-unit-matter", "text": "A laboratory report lists a mass of \\(0.075\\) grams. A second report lists \\(75\\) milligrams. Are these different\nmeasurements? Since \\(1\\,\\mathrm{g}=1000\\,\\mathrm{mg}\\) , \\(0.075\\,\\mathrm{g}\\times(1000\\,\\mathrm{mg}/1\\,\\mathrm{g})=75\\,\\mathrm{mg}\\) .\nThe numbers look different, but the quantities are equal. Throughout the\nchapter, always record the number and its unit . Concept check 1. A measurement is \\(0.0042\\) meters. Express it in millimeters,\nand explain whether the numerical value becomes larger or smaller."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Real numbers and\nmathematical language", "url": "chapter01.html#real-numbers-and-mathematical-language", "text": "The natural numbers are \\(1,2,3,\\ldots\\) ; the whole numbers also include \\(0\\) ; the integers include negative whole numbers. A rational\nnumber can be expressed as \\(a/b\\) with integers \\(a,b\\) and \\(b\\ne0\\) . Irrational numbers such as \\(\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\pi\\) cannot be written as such ratios.\nTogether the rational and irrational numbers form \\(\\mathbb R\\) . Example 1: Classify numbers. Classify \\(-7\\) , \\(0\\) , \\(3/8\\) , \\(\\sqrt{49}\\) , and \\(\\sqrt{5}\\) . Worked solution. \\(-7\\) is an integer and rational; \\(0\\) is whole, integer, and rational; \\(3/8\\) is rational; \\(\\sqrt{49}=7\\) is natural and rational; \\(\\sqrt5\\) is irrational. Every number\nlisted is real."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Order and intervals", "url": "chapter01.html#order-and-intervals", "text": "For real \\(a<b\\) , \\((a,b)\\) excludes endpoints while \\([a,b]\\) includes both. Mixed intervals \\([a,b)\\) and \\((a,b]\\) include exactly one endpoint.\nInfinite endpoints always use parentheses: \\((-\\infty,4]\\) . Union \\(A\\cup B\\) joins sets; intersection \\(A\\cap B\\) contains their common\nelements. Example 2: Translate an inequality. Write \\(-2\\le x<5\\) as an interval and describe\nthe endpoints. Worked solution. The interval is \\([-2,5)\\) . The left endpoint \\(-2\\) belongs to the set; \\(5\\) does not. The interval \\([-2,5)\\) has a closed endpoint at \\(-2\\) and an open endpoint at \\(5\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Absolute value as distance", "url": "chapter01.html#absolute-value-as-distance", "text": "The absolute value \\(|x|\\) is the\ndistance from \\(x\\) to \\(0\\) on the number line: \\[|x|=\\begin{cases}x,&x\\ge0,\\\\-x,&x<0.\\end{cases}\\] More generally \\(|x-a|\\) is the\ndistance between \\(x\\) and \\(a\\) . Thus \\(|x-3|\\le2\\) means \\(1\\le x\\le5\\) , whereas \\(|x-3|>2\\) means \\(x<1\\) or \\(x>5\\) . Example 3: Measurement tolerance. A target\ntemperature is \\(37.0^\\circ\\mathrm C\\) with tolerance \\(0.4^\\circ\\mathrm C\\) .\nExpress acceptable temperatures using absolute value and an\ninterval. Worked solution. \\(|T-37.0|\\le0.4\\) . Subtracting the tolerance\nfrom the target gives \\(36.6\\le\nT\\le37.4\\) , or \\([36.6,37.4]\\) degrees Celsius. Concept check 2. Why is \\(\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|\\) , not always \\(x\\) ? Test \\(x=-4\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Algebraic\nexpressions and the order of operations", "url": "chapter01.html#algebraic-expressions-and-the-order-of-operations", "text": "An expression represents a value, whereas an equation asserts equality. A variable stands for a\nnumber in an allowed domain. Grouping symbols, powers,\nmultiplication/division, and addition/subtraction govern the order of\noperations; multiplication and division at the same level are evaluated\nleft to right. Example 4: Grouping changes a result. Compare \\(6+12/3^2\\) and \\((6+12)/3^2\\) . Worked solution. Since \\(3^2=9\\) , the first is \\(6+12/9=22/3\\) . The second is \\(18/9=2\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Distributive\nproperty and combining like terms", "url": "chapter01.html#distributive-property-and-combining-like-terms", "text": "For real \\(a,b,c\\) , \\(a(b+c)=ab+ac\\) . Only terms with the same\nvariable part and exponent can be combined. Example 5: Simplify with care. Simplify \\(3(2x-5)-2(x+4)+7\\) . Worked solution. Distribute: \\(6x-15-2x-8+7\\) . Combine like terms: \\(4x-16\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Exponents and scientific\nnotation", "url": "chapter01.html#exponents-and-scientific-notation", "text": "For nonzero \\(a\\) and integers \\(m,n\\) , \\(a^ma^n=a^{m+n}\\) , \\(a^m/a^n=a^{m-n}\\) , \\((a^m)^n=a^{mn}\\) , \\(a^0=1\\) , and \\(a^{-n}=1/a^n\\) . Fractional exponents\nrequire domain care: for \\(a>0\\) , \\(a^{m/n}=(\\sqrt[n]{a})^m\\) . When \\(n\\) is even, real even roots require\nnonnegative radicands. Example 6: Exponent rules. Simplify \\(\\dfrac{(2x^3)^2}{4x^{-1}}\\) for \\(x\\ne0\\) . Worked solution. The numerator is \\(4x^6\\) . Dividing by \\(4x^{-1}\\) yields \\(x^{6-(-1)}=x^7\\) . The original expression\nexcludes \\(x=0\\) . Example 7: Negative base versus negative sign. Evaluate \\((-3)^2\\) and \\(-3^2\\) . Worked solution. \\((-3)^2=9\\) , because the base is \\(-3\\) . But \\(-3^2=-(3^2)=-9\\) , because exponentiation\nprecedes the leading minus sign."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Scientific notation", "url": "chapter01.html#scientific-notation", "text": "A nonzero number in scientific notation has the form \\(a\\times10^n\\) with \\(1\\le|a|<10\\) and integer \\(n\\) . For example, \\(0.000036=3.6\\times10^{-5}\\) and \\(7{,}200{,}000=7.2\\times10^6\\) . A number\nwritten with a decimal point does not automatically convey infinite\nmeasurement precision. Example 8: Scale of a sample. A sample contains \\(2.4\\times10^6\\) cells per milliliter.\nEstimate the number in \\(0.015\\) mL. Worked solution. \\((2.4\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm{cells/mL})(0.015\\,\\mathrm{mL})=3.6\\times10^4\\) cells. The mL units cancel."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Radicals and rational\nexponents", "url": "chapter01.html#radicals-and-rational-exponents", "text": "For \\(a\\ge0\\) , \\(\\sqrt a\\) denotes the nonnegative square root. Hence \\(\\sqrt{36}=6\\) ,\neven though the equation \\(x^2=36\\) has\ntwo solutions \\(x=\\pm6\\) . For\nnonnegative \\(a,b\\) , \\(\\sqrt{ab}=\\sqrt a\\sqrt b\\) ; it is generally\nfalse that \\(\\sqrt{a+b}=\\sqrt a+\\sqrt\nb\\) . Example 9: Simplifying radicals. Simplify \\(\\sqrt{72x^2}\\) for real \\(x\\) . Worked solution. \\(\\sqrt{72x^2}=\\sqrt{36\\cdot2}\\sqrt{x^2}=6|x|\\sqrt2\\) .\nIf \\(x\\ge0\\) is known, this becomes \\(6x\\sqrt2\\) . Example 10: Rationalize a denominator. Simplify \\(\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt5}\\) . Worked solution. Multiply numerator and denominator\nby \\(\\sqrt5\\) : \\(3\\sqrt5/5\\) . This changes the form, not the\nvalue. Concept check 3. Test the false identity \\(\\sqrt{9+16}=\\sqrt9+\\sqrt{16}\\) . What values\ndo its two sides produce?"}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Factoring and rational\nexpressions", "url": "chapter01.html#factoring-and-rational-expressions", "text": "Factoring reverses distribution. Useful identities include \\[a^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b),\\qquad\na^2+2ab+b^2=(a+b)^2.\\] A rational expression is a quotient of\npolynomials; its denominator cannot be zero. Cancel factors ,\nnot arbitrary terms. Example 11: Factor a trinomial. Factor \\(x^2-5x+6\\) . Worked solution. Find two numbers with product \\(6\\) and sum \\(-5\\) : \\(-2\\) and \\(-3\\) . Thus \\(x^2-5x+6=(x-2)(x-3)\\) . Expanding checks the\nresult. Example 12: Simplify and retain restrictions. Simplify \\(\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x^2-3x}\\) . Worked solution. Factor numerator and denominator: \\(\\dfrac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x(x-3)}\\) . For \\(x\\ne0,3\\) , cancel the common factor\nto obtain \\((x+3)/x\\) . The original\nexpression is undefined at both \\(0\\) and \\(3\\) , even though the simplified\nformula alone is defined at \\(3\\) . Example 13: Add rational expressions. Simplify \\(\\dfrac{2}{x}+\\dfrac{3}{x+1}\\) . Worked solution. The common denominator is \\(x(x+1)\\) . Therefore \\(\\dfrac{2(x+1)+3x}{x(x+1)}=\\dfrac{5x+2}{x(x+1)}\\) ,\nfor \\(x\\ne0,-1\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Dimensional\nanalysis and quantitative reasoning", "url": "chapter01.html#dimensional-analysis-and-quantitative-reasoning", "text": "Units behave like algebraic factors. To convert \\(2.5\\) liters to milliliters, multiply by \\(1000\\,\\mathrm{mL}/1\\,\\mathrm L\\) ,\nobtaining \\(2500\\) mL. To convert a\nrate, convert both its numerator and denominator as needed. A physically\nmeaningful formula must be dimensionally consistent: adding a time to a\ndistance has no meaning. Example 14: Infusion rate: arithmetic illustration\nonly. A hypothetical nonclinical liquid transfer moves \\(180\\) mL in \\(45\\) minutes. Find the rate in mL/hour. Worked solution. \\(\\dfrac{180\\,\\mathrm{mL}}{45\\,\\mathrm{min}}\\cdot\\dfrac{60\\,\\mathrm{min}}{1\\,\\mathrm{h}}=240\\,\\mathrm{mL/h}\\) .\nThe minutes cancel. This is a units exercise, not a medical dosing\nrecommendation. Example 15: Concentration conversion. A laboratory\nsolution has \\(0.08\\) g of solute in \\(200\\) mL. Express the concentration\nin mg/mL. Worked solution. \\(0.08\\,\\mathrm g=80\\,\\mathrm{mg}\\) . Dividing\nby \\(200\\,\\mathrm{mL}\\) gives \\(0.4\\,\\mathrm{mg/mL}\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Exact and approximate\nquantities", "url": "chapter01.html#exact-and-approximate-quantities", "text": "The identity \\(1\\,\\mathrm\nm=100\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) is exact by definition; a measured length of \\(1.23\\) m is not exact. Computed\nanswers should reflect measurement precision and the context. Avoid\nrounding intermediate results unnecessarily. Computers store many\ndecimal quantities approximately, so equality tests on floating-point\nresults need care."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Visual\nconcept investigation: distance and tolerance", "url": "chapter01.html#visual-concept-investigation-distance-and-tolerance", "text": "The plot below compares distance from a target with a horizontal\ntolerance level. The intersection points identify the boundary of an\nacceptable interval. The condition \\(|x-3|\\le2\\) holds between the two\nintersections, including endpoints."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Computational\nlaboratories: the same mathematics in R and Python", "url": "chapter01.html#computational-laboratories-the-same-mathematics-in-r-and-python", "text": "Both labs use the same numbers, definitions, and questions. No\nprevious programming experience is assumed. Code outputs are numerical\nevidence, not mathematical proofs."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Laboratory\nA: numbers, powers, and floating-point arithmetic", "url": "chapter01.html#laboratory-a-numbers-powers-and-floating-point-arithmetic", "text": "Run the accompanying chapter01_lab.R or chapter01_lab.py . Compare \\((-3)^2\\) with \\(-3^2\\) , verify a scientific-notation\ncalculation, and inspect \\(0.1+0.2\\) versus \\(0.3\\) . Explain why a tiny\nfloating-point difference does not imply an algebraic error. Record the\noriginal denominator restrictions when checking rational\nexpressions."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Laboratory\nB: intervals and absolute-value tolerance", "url": "chapter01.html#laboratory-b-intervals-and-absolute-value-tolerance", "text": "Plot \\(y=|x-3|\\) together with \\(y=2\\) over \\(-1\\le x\\le7\\) . Identify where \\(|x-3|\\le2\\) and check \\(x=1,3,5\\) . State why the graph supports but\ndoes not replace the exact solution \\([1,5]\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Laboratory C: unit\nconversion and a model", "url": "chapter01.html#laboratory-c-unit-conversion-and-a-model", "text": "For the hypothetical sample mass \\(0.075\\) g, calculate milligrams. Then\nconsider a simple linear mass model \\(m(v)=0.4v\\) mg, where \\(v\\) is volume in mL. Plot it for \\(0\\le v\\le10\\) , label units on both axes,\nand explain the meaning of its slope. Do not infer clinical suitability\nfrom this illustrative relationship."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Applied\ncase study: a laboratory concentration audit", "url": "chapter01.html#applied-case-study-a-laboratory-concentration-audit", "text": "A laboratory prepares a hypothetical solution containing \\(0.12\\) g of a substance in \\(300\\) mL. A technician records a\nconcentration of \\(0.4\\) mg/mL. Is the\nentry internally consistent? Convert \\(0.12\\) g to \\(120\\) mg and divide by \\(300\\) mL to obtain \\(0.4\\) mg/mL. Next, if \\(25\\) mL is sampled, the model predicts \\(10\\) mg of substance. Explain the\nassumptions: the solution is uniformly mixed, the stated amounts are\naccurate, and no material is lost. This is a mathematical consistency\ncheck, not a validation of any medical preparation."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Common\nmisconceptions and mathematical habits", "url": "chapter01.html#common-misconceptions-and-mathematical-habits", "text": "A negative exponent means a reciprocal, not a negative value: \\(2^{-3}=1/8\\) . \\(\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|\\) for real \\(x\\) . Cancellation applies to common multiplicative factors, not sums: \\((x+2)/x\\ne2\\) in general. An algebraically simplified expression may retain excluded inputs\nfrom its original form. A computer-generated graph or decimal approximation does not\nestablish an identity. Numerical answers without units can be ambiguous in applied\nsettings."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Deepening\nthe foundations: mathematical statements and justification", "url": "chapter01.html#deepening-the-foundations-mathematical-statements-and-justification", "text": "Mathematics involves more than obtaining a numerical answer. A\ncorrect solution must identify the quantities involved, apply a valid\nproperty, and explain why the result answers the question. In this text\nwe distinguish an example , which illustrates a rule, from a justification , which establishes that the rule applies to all\nallowed inputs. Testing a statement at several numbers may reveal a\nfalse claim, but it does not prove a universal claim."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Equality,\nidentities, and conditional statements", "url": "chapter01.html#equality-identities-and-conditional-statements", "text": "An equality such as \\(2x+3=11\\) is\ntrue only for certain values of \\(x\\) ;\nsolving gives \\(x=4\\) . By contrast, \\(2(x+3)=2x+6\\) is an identity : it is true for every real \\(x\\) . An expression such as \\((x^2-1)/(x-1)\\) agrees with \\(x+1\\) whenever \\(x\\ne1\\) , but the expressions do not have\nidentical domains. Thus the precise statement is \\[\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}=x+1\\qquad\\text{for\n}x\\ne1.\\] Domain restrictions are part of the mathematical claim,\nnot optional footnotes. Example 16: Is a proposed rule always true?. A\nstudent suggests that \\((a+b)^2=a^2+b^2\\) . Determine whether this\nis an identity and derive the correct expansion. Worked solution. Testing \\(a=b=1\\) gives \\(4\\) on the left and \\(2\\) on the right, so the proposed rule is\nfalse. Distribute carefully: \\[(a+b)^2=(a+b)(a+b)=a^2+ab+ba+b^2=a^2+2ab+b^2.\\] The cross term \\(2ab\\) cannot be\nomitted. Guided practice 1. Decide whether each statement is\nalways true, sometimes true, or never true over the real numbers: (a) \\(x^2\\ge0\\) ; (b) \\(x^2=x\\) ; (c) \\(\\sqrt{x^2}=x\\) . Give an example or\ncounterexample and explain your classification."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "A reliable\nfour-stage problem-solving routine", "url": "chapter01.html#a-reliable-four-stage-problem-solving-routine", "text": "For a quantitative question, first interpret the requested\nquantity and its units. Second, represent the relationships\nusing expressions, diagrams, or equations. Third, calculate using valid algebra and carefully tracked restrictions. Finally, check and interpret : substitute into the original condition,\nevaluate reasonableness, and state units. These stages will recur\nthroughout the book and become particularly important in calculus\nmodeling. Example 17: Checking a laboratory conversion. A\ncontainer holds \\(0.0065\\) liters.\nExpress its volume in milliliters and check by reversing the\nconversion. Worked solution. Because \\(1\\,\\mathrm L=1000\\,\\mathrm{mL}\\) , we obtain \\(0.0065\\,\\mathrm\nL\\times1000\\,\\mathrm{mL/L}=6.5\\,\\mathrm{mL}\\) . Reversing gives \\(6.5\\,\\mathrm{mL}\\times(1\\,\\mathrm\nL/1000\\,\\mathrm{mL})=0.0065\\,\\mathrm L\\) . The cancellation of\nunits confirms the direction of conversion."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation I: the number line as a model of constraints", "url": "chapter01.html#visual-investigation-i-the-number-line-as-a-model-of-constraints", "text": "A number line is a one-dimensional coordinate system. It makes the\ndifference between a single solution, a continuous interval, and two\ndisconnected intervals visible. In an applied setting, an interval might\nrepresent acceptable measurement values, a range of plausible inputs, or\na region in which a model is valid. Intersecting two inequalities: only the\nvalues satisfying both remain in the bottom row. Example 18: Intersect two requirements. A\nquality-control measurement must be at least \\(-1\\) units and strictly below \\(3\\) units. Represent the requirements\nindividually and jointly. Worked solution. The conditions are \\(x\\ge-1\\) and \\(x<3\\) . Their intersection is \\([-1,\\infty)\\cap(-\\infty,3)=[-1,3)\\) . The\nleft endpoint is included because equality is permitted; the right\nendpoint is excluded. Guided practice 2. Sketch \\(x>-2\\) and \\(x\\le4\\) on separate number lines. Shade\ntheir intersection. Then sketch their union. Explain why the union\ndiffers from the intersection."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Distance, midpoint, and\nradius", "url": "chapter01.html#distance-midpoint-and-radius", "text": "A useful way to describe an interval is through its center \\(c\\) and radius \\(r\\) . For \\(r>0\\) , the inequality \\(|x-c|\\le r\\) is equivalent to \\(c-r\\le x\\le c+r\\) . Conversely, an interval \\([a,b]\\) has midpoint \\(c=(a+b)/2\\) and half-width \\(r=(b-a)/2\\) . This correspondence is useful\nwhen communicating tolerances. Example 19: Recover a tolerance statement. Express\nthe interval \\([18.4,19.6]\\) as an\nabsolute-value inequality. Worked solution. The midpoint is \\((18.4+19.6)/2=19.0\\) and the radius is \\((19.6-18.4)/2=0.6\\) . Therefore the\ninterval is exactly \\(|x-19.0|\\le0.6\\) . Guided practice 3. A measured diameter is acceptable\nwhen \\(|d-12.5|<0.2\\) . Write the\ncorresponding open interval. What changes if the inequality is \\(\\le\\) instead of \\(<\\) ?"}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation II: why order of operations matters", "url": "chapter01.html#visual-investigation-ii-why-order-of-operations-matters", "text": "A mathematical expression has structure. Parentheses determine which\noperations form a single unit, and a negative sign is not automatically\npart of a base. The expression \\(-2^4\\) means \\(-(2^4)=-16\\) , whereas \\((-2)^4=16\\) . Likewise, \\((-2)^3=-8\\) . The parity of the exponent\nmatters when the base is negative. Even powers remove the sign of a negative\nbase; odd powers retain it. The plotted nonnegative curves illustrate\nthe contrast in magnitude."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "From repeated\nmultiplication to exponent laws", "url": "chapter01.html#from-repeated-multiplication-to-exponent-laws", "text": "For positive integers \\(m,n\\) , the\nproduct \\(a^m a^n\\) contains \\(m+n\\) factors of \\(a\\) , giving \\(a^{m+n}\\) . The quotient \\(a^m/a^n\\) , for \\(a\\ne0\\) , removes \\(n\\) factors and leads to \\(a^{m-n}\\) . To preserve this law when \\(m=n\\) , we must have \\(a^0=1\\) for \\(a\\ne0\\) . When \\(m<n\\) , negative exponents arise\nnaturally: \\(a^{-n}=1/a^n\\) . These are\nconsequences of consistent definitions, not arbitrary memorized\nrules. Example 20: Explain each simplification. Simplify \\(\\dfrac{(2x^{-2}y^3)^2}{4x^{-1}y}\\) for \\(x\\ne0\\) , \\(y\\ne0\\) . Worked solution. First square each factor in the\nnumerator: \\((2x^{-2}y^3)^2=4x^{-4}y^6\\) . Divide\ncoefficients and subtract exponents: \\(4x^{-4}y^6/(4x^{-1}y)=x^{-3}y^5=y^5/x^3\\) .\nThe exclusions \\(x\\ne0\\) and \\(y\\ne0\\) come from the original\nexpression. Guided practice 4. Simplify \\(\\dfrac{(3a^2b^{-1})^2}{9ab^{-3}}\\) . State\nthe original restrictions before simplifying, and verify the result by\nsubstituting \\(a=2\\) , \\(b=3\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Powers of ten and estimation", "url": "chapter01.html#powers-of-ten-and-estimation", "text": "Powers of ten provide a systematic way to compare scales. The ratio\nof \\(3\\times10^7\\) to \\(6\\times10^4\\) is \\((3/6)\\times10^3=500\\) . Thus the first\nquantity is five hundred times the second, not merely three orders of\nmagnitude larger. When working with scientific notation, separate the\ncoefficient calculation from the exponent calculation and then normalize\nthe coefficient to the interval \\([1,10)\\) . Example 21: Estimate before computing. A fictional\nsample contains \\(4.8\\times10^5\\) particles per mL. Estimate the count in \\(0.025\\) mL. Worked solution. Compute \\((4.8\\times10^5)(2.5\\times10^{-2})=12\\times10^3=1.2\\times10^4\\) particles. As a reasonableness check, \\(0.025\\) mL is one-fortieth of a mL, and \\(480{,}000/40=12{,}000\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation III: radicals, principal roots, and domains", "url": "chapter01.html#visual-investigation-iii-radicals-principal-roots-and-domains", "text": "The equation \\(x^2=9\\) has two\nsolutions because both \\(3\\) and \\(-3\\) square to \\(9\\) . However, the symbol \\(\\sqrt9\\) denotes the principal (nonnegative) square root, namely \\(3\\) . The distinction is important: the\nsquare-root function assigns one output to each allowed input, while\nsolving a quadratic equation can produce two solutions. The horizontal line \\(y=9\\) intersects \\(y=x^2\\) at \\(x=-3\\) and \\(x=3\\) , but \\(\\sqrt9=3\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Why \\(\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|\\)", "url": "chapter01.html#why-sqrtx2x", "text": "For \\(x\\ge0\\) , the nonnegative\nsquare root of \\(x^2\\) is \\(x\\) . For \\(x<0\\) , the nonnegative square root is \\(-x\\) . Combining these cases gives \\(\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|\\) . In particular,\nreplacing \\(\\sqrt{x^2}\\) with \\(x\\) without knowing that \\(x\\ge0\\) may change the value of an\nexpression. Example 22: Simplify with an unknown sign. Simplify \\(\\sqrt{50t^4}\\) for real \\(t\\) . Worked solution. Factor the radicand: \\(\\sqrt{50t^4}=\\sqrt{25\\cdot2}\\sqrt{(t^2)^2}=5|t^2|\\sqrt2=5t^2\\sqrt2\\) ,\nbecause \\(t^2\\ge0\\) for every real \\(t\\) . Contrast this with \\(\\sqrt{50t^2}=5|t|\\sqrt2\\) . Guided practice 5. Simplify \\(\\sqrt{108x^2y^4}\\) for arbitrary real \\(x,y\\) . Explain which absolute-value bars\ncan be removed and why."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Rational exponents and\nallowable inputs", "url": "chapter01.html#rational-exponents-and-allowable-inputs", "text": "For \\(a\\ge0\\) , \\(a^{1/n}\\) denotes the principal \\(n\\) th root when \\(n\\) is even. For odd \\(n\\) , real roots also exist for negative\ninputs. The expression \\(a^{m/n}\\) can\nbe interpreted as \\((\\sqrt[n]{a})^m\\) whenever this root is defined and the exponent notation is unambiguous.\nFor example, \\(16^{3/4}=(\\sqrt[4]{16})^3=2^3=8\\) . A\nreal-valued square root \\(\\sqrt{x-5}\\) requires \\(x\\ge5\\) . Domain restrictions\nshould be identified before manipulating radicals. Example 23: Determine a domain. Find the real domain\nof \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{2x+6}/(x-1)\\) . Worked solution. The square root requires \\(2x+6\\ge0\\) , hence \\(x\\ge-3\\) . The denominator requires \\(x\\ne1\\) . The domain is \\([-3,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)\\) . Both restrictions\nmust hold simultaneously."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation IV: factoring as geometric decomposition", "url": "chapter01.html#visual-investigation-iv-factoring-as-geometric-decomposition", "text": "Factoring is the reverse of expansion. The identity \\((x+a)(x+b)=x^2+(a+b)x+ab\\) can be\nvisualized as the area of a rectangle partitioned into four smaller\nrectangles when the lengths are positive. This picture explains why the\ncoefficient of \\(x\\) is the sum \\(a+b\\) and the constant term is the product \\(ab\\) . An area model for \\((x+a)(x+b)=x^2+(a+b)x+ab\\) when side\nlengths are positive. Example 24: Factoring by reasoning about sum and\nproduct. Factor \\(x^2+7x+12\\) and verify by multiplication. Worked solution. We seek \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b=7\\) and \\(ab=12\\) . The pair \\(3,4\\) works, so \\(x^2+7x+12=(x+3)(x+4)\\) . Expanding gives \\(x^2+4x+3x+12=x^2+7x+12\\) . Guided practice 6. Factor \\(x^2-x-12\\) . Explain why the signs of the\ntwo factors differ, and check by expanding."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Recognizing patterns\nwithout guessing", "url": "chapter01.html#recognizing-patterns-without-guessing", "text": "Common-factor extraction should be considered first. For instance, \\(6x^3-24x=6x(x^2-4)=6x(x-2)(x+2)\\) .\nDifference-of-squares factoring works because \\((a-b)(a+b)=a^2-b^2\\) ; it does not directly\napply to \\(a^2+b^2\\) over the real\nnumbers. Perfect-square trinomials follow from \\((a\\pm b)^2=a^2\\pm2ab+b^2\\) . Recognizing a\npattern is useful only when every term fits it. Example 25: A multistep factorization. Factor \\(2x^3+8x^2+8x\\) completely. Worked solution. First extract the greatest common\nfactor: \\(2x(x^2+4x+4)\\) . The trinomial\nis a perfect square, giving \\(2x(x+2)^2\\) . Expanding verifies the\nanswer."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Rational expressions\nand removable holes", "url": "chapter01.html#rational-expressions-and-removable-holes", "text": "Consider \\(r(x)=(x^2-4)/(x-2)\\) . For \\(x\\ne2\\) , the expression equals \\(x+2\\) . At \\(x=2\\) , however, the original formula is\nundefined. Its graph is the line \\(y=x+2\\) with a hole at \\((2,4)\\) . This distinction anticipates an\nessential calculus idea: a function can approach a value at a point even\nwhen it is not defined there. The graph of \\((x^2-4)/(x-2)\\) agrees with \\(y=x+2\\) except at the excluded input \\(x=2\\) . Guided practice 7. Simplify \\((x^2-25)/(x-5)\\) and state its domain.\nSketch the resulting line with the appropriate missing point. Explain\nwhy substituting \\(x=5\\) into the\nsimplified expression is not a valid evaluation of the original\nexpression."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation V: units as algebraic objects", "url": "chapter01.html#visual-investigation-v-units-as-algebraic-objects", "text": "A measurement consists of a numerical value and a unit. Units can be\nmultiplied, divided, and canceled, but only when the operation is\nmeaningful. For example, a speed has units of distance divided by time;\nmultiplying by a duration produces a distance. Adding \\(5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\) to \\(3\\,\\mathrm{s}\\) is undefined as a physical\nquantity because the units are incompatible."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Building a conversion chain", "url": "chapter01.html#building-a-conversion-chain", "text": "Suppose a nonclinical pump transfers \\(2.4\\) liters of water in \\(3\\) hours. To express the average rate in\nmL/min, write \\[\\frac{2.4\\,\\mathrm\nL}{3\\,\\mathrm h}\\cdot\\frac{1000\\,\\mathrm{mL}}{1\\,\\mathrm\nL}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\mathrm\nh}{60\\,\\mathrm{min}}=\\frac{2400}{180}\\,\\mathrm{mL/min}\\approx13.33\\,\\mathrm{mL/min}.\\] The unit cancellations show that the result has the requested\ndimensions. A numerical calculator alone cannot detect a reversed\nconversion factor, but writing units makes such mistakes visible. Example 26: Concentration and sample quantity. A\nfictional, uniformly mixed solution contains \\(0.18\\) g of a marker in \\(450\\) mL. Find the concentration in mg/mL\nand the marker mass in a \\(30\\) mL\nsample. Worked solution. Convert \\(0.18\\) g to \\(180\\) mg. Then \\(180/450=0.4\\) mg/mL. A \\(30\\) mL sample contains \\((0.4\\,\\mathrm{mg/mL})(30\\,\\mathrm{mL})=12\\) mg. The result assumes uniform mixing; it is not a clinical dosing\ncalculation. Guided practice 8. A fictional container has \\(0.075\\) g of dissolved marker in \\(250\\) mL. Compute mg/mL and the amount in a \\(40\\) mL aliquot. Write each\nconversion factor explicitly."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Dimensional\nconsistency and model checking", "url": "chapter01.html#dimensional-consistency-and-model-checking", "text": "Suppose a model predicts mass from volume as \\(m(v)=kv\\) . If \\(m\\) is in mg and \\(v\\) is in mL, then \\(k\\) must have units mg/mL. If a proposed\nformula instead adds \\(v\\) (mL) to \\(k\\) (mg/mL), the terms have\nincompatible dimensions. Dimensional consistency does not prove that a\nmodel is scientifically correct, but inconsistency reveals that\nsomething is wrong with its formulation."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation VI: exact values and numerical approximation", "url": "chapter01.html#visual-investigation-vi-exact-values-and-numerical-approximation", "text": "A fraction such as \\(1/3\\) is an\nexact number. A computer may display it as \\(0.3333333\\) , which is a finite\napproximation. Similarly, \\(\\sqrt2\\) is\nexact notation for an irrational number, while \\(1.4142\\) is an approximation. R and Python\ntypically use finite-precision floating-point numbers for decimal\ncalculations. A result that is extremely close to zero may therefore\nappear as a tiny nonzero value. Example 27: Approximation versus identity. Why might\na program report that \\(0.1+0.2\\) is\nnot exactly equal to \\(0.3\\) ? Worked solution. Many decimal fractions do not have\nfinite binary floating-point representations. The stored approximations\ncan differ slightly after arithmetic. This is a property of the\nrepresentation, not a failure of the real-number identity \\(0.1+0.2=0.3\\) . For numerical comparisons,\nuse a suitable tolerance, such as \\(|a-b|<10^{-10}\\) , when the scale and\nrequired precision justify it. Guided practice 9. Calculate \\(\\sqrt2\\) to four decimal places. Square the\nrounded value. Is the result exactly \\(2\\) ? Explain why the difference is\nexpected."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Percent change and relative\nerror", "url": "chapter01.html#percent-change-and-relative-error", "text": "Absolute error measures a difference in the original units, while\nrelative error compares the difference to a reference value. If an\nobserved mass is \\(9.8\\) g and a\nreference is \\(10.0\\) g, the absolute\nerror is \\(|9.8-10.0|=0.2\\) g and the\nrelative error is \\(0.2/10.0=0.02\\) , or \\(2\\%\\) . The reference value must be\nnonzero to define relative error. Percent change uses a signed\ndifference divided by the original value; percent error often uses an\nabsolute difference. State which quantity you are computing. Example 28: Interpreting measurement variation. A\nsensor reads \\(51.5\\) units when the\nreference is \\(50.0\\) units. Find the\nsigned difference and absolute relative error. Worked solution. The signed difference is \\(51.5-50.0=1.5\\) units. The absolute\nrelative error is \\(|1.5|/50.0=0.03=3\\%\\) . The reading is \\(3\\%\\) above the reference."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Extended\ncomputational investigation: predict, run, explain", "url": "chapter01.html#extended-computational-investigation-predict-run-explain", "text": "The accompanying R and Python laboratory files provide complete,\nrunnable versions of the investigations below. Each investigation\npresents the R code first, followed by the corresponding Python code, in\nfull-width listings as in the companion calculus volumes. Before running\na command, write down the expected mathematical result. Then run the\ncalculation and explain any discrepancy. This routine prevents software\nfrom becoming a substitute for mathematical reasoning."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Investigation A:\ntest an identity numerically", "url": "chapter01.html#investigation-a-test-an-identity-numerically", "text": "For several values of \\(x\\) , compare \\((x+2)^2\\) with \\(x^2+4x+4\\) . Then compare \\((x+2)^2\\) with \\(x^2+4\\) . One matching input is not a proof;\na single mismatch disproves a proposed identity. R R Copy code x <- c ( - 3 , - 1 , 0 , 2 , 5 ) lhs <- (x + 2 ) ^ 2 correct <- x ^ 2 + 4 * x + 4 incorrect <- x ^ 2 + 4 print ( data.frame (x, lhs, correct, incorrect)) Python Python Copy code x_values = [ - 3 , - 1 , 0 , 2 , 5 ] for x in x_values: lhs = (x + 2 ) ** 2 correct = x ** 2 + 4 * x + 4 incorrect = x ** 2 + 4 print (x, lhs, correct, incorrect) Interpretation questions. Which formula agrees with\nthe left side at every tested input? Find an input where the incorrect\nformula happens to agree. Why would that agreement not establish an\nidentity?"}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Investigation\nB: numerical tolerance as a visual region", "url": "chapter01.html#investigation-b-numerical-tolerance-as-a-visual-region", "text": "For a target value \\(c=3\\) and\ntolerance \\(r=2\\) , evaluate \\(|x-c|\\) on a grid and compare it with \\(r\\) . The accepted region is \\([1,5]\\) . Use a graph to support the result,\nthen justify the endpoints algebraically. Modify \\(c\\) and \\(r\\) to explore how the accepted interval\nshifts and changes width. R R Copy code x <- seq ( - 1 , 7 , length.out= 401 ) distance <- abs (x - 3 ) plot (x, distance, type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"Distance\" ) abline ( h= 2 , col= \"red\" , lty= 2 ) print ( range (x[distance <= 2 ])) Python Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt x = np.linspace( - 1 , 7 , 401 ) distance = np. abs (x - 3 ) plt.plot(x, distance) plt.axhline( 2 , color = \"red\" , ls = \"--\" ) plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Distance\" ) plt.show() print (x[distance <= 2 ]. min (), x[distance <= 2 ]. max ()) Interpretation questions. Why do the grid-based\nendpoints depend on sampling resolution? How can exact algebra avoid\nthis issue? What happens if the tolerance is zero?"}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Investigation C:\nverify a unit-based model", "url": "chapter01.html#investigation-c-verify-a-unit-based-model", "text": "Assume a fictional, uniformly mixed marker solution has concentration \\(0.4\\) mg/mL. Let \\(m(v)=0.4v\\) for \\(0\\le v\\le10\\) . Make a table of \\(v=0,2,4,6,8,10\\) and corresponding masses.\nPlot the points and connect them. Interpret the slope as \\(0.4\\) mg for each additional mL. Explain\nwhy a straight line is appropriate only under the assumed constant\nconcentration. R R Copy code v <- seq ( 0 , 10 , by= 2 ) m <- 0.4 * v print ( data.frame ( volume_mL= v, mass_mg= m)) plot (v, m, type= \"b\" , pch= 19 , xlab= \"Volume (mL)\" , ylab= \"Marker mass (mg)\" ) Python Python Copy code import matplotlib.pyplot as plt v = list ( range ( 0 , 11 , 2 )) m = [ 0.4 * t for t in v] print ( list ( zip (v, m))) plt.plot(v, m, marker = \"o\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Volume (mL)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Marker mass (mg)\" ) plt.show() Interpretation questions. What is \\(m(0)\\) and why? How much does mass change\nwhen volume increases from \\(4\\) to \\(7\\) mL? Why must the units of the\nslope be mg/mL?"}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Guided\npractice workshop: from explanation to independence", "url": "chapter01.html#guided-practice-workshop-from-explanation-to-independence", "text": "The following activities are designed for collaborative work or\nindividual study. For each problem, write the principle used before the\narithmetic. Compare methods with a partner, and explain whether a\ncomputational check would add useful evidence."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Workshop 1:\ninterval reasoning", "url": "chapter01.html#workshop-1-interval-reasoning", "text": "A manufacturing measurement is acceptable when it is within \\(0.15\\) mm of a target of \\(8.00\\) mm. (a) Write an absolute-value\ninequality. (b) Write the interval. (c) Sketch the interval and label\nboth endpoints. (d) Explain whether \\(7.85\\) mm and \\(8.16\\) mm qualify. (e) If the allowed\ntolerance is halved, determine the new interval."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Workshop 2:\nexponent reasoning", "url": "chapter01.html#workshop-2-exponent-reasoning", "text": "A fictional sample has \\(3.2\\times10^6\\) cells per mL. (a) Estimate\nthe count in \\(0.005\\) mL. (b) Express\nthe result in scientific notation. (c) Explain the exponent arithmetic.\n(d) Check using R or Python and compare with a mental estimate."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Workshop 3:\nrational-expression reasoning", "url": "chapter01.html#workshop-3-rational-expression-reasoning", "text": "Consider \\(q(x)=(x^2-6x+8)/(x-2)\\) .\n(a) Factor the numerator. (b) Simplify wherever allowed. (c) Identify\nthe excluded input. (d) Evaluate \\(q(3)\\) using both formulas. (e) Explain why \\(q(2)\\) is undefined even though the\nsimplified expression has a value at \\(2\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Workshop 4:\ndimensional reasoning", "url": "chapter01.html#workshop-4-dimensional-reasoning", "text": "A fictional laboratory mixture contains \\(0.24\\) g of a marker in \\(600\\) mL. (a) Convert grams to milligrams.\n(b) Find the concentration in mg/mL. (c) Find the marker mass in \\(75\\) mL. (d) State the assumption that\nmakes the proportional model appropriate. (e) Identify which quantities\nare measured and which conversions are exact."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Workshop 5:\ncalculus readiness", "url": "chapter01.html#workshop-5-calculus-readiness", "text": "For \\(h\\ne0\\) , simplify \\(\\big((3+h)^2-9\\big)/h\\) . (a) Expand the\nnumerator. (b) Factor out \\(h\\) . (c)\nCancel only after stating the restriction. (d) Evaluate the simplified\nexpression at \\(h=0.1\\) , \\(0.01\\) , and \\(0.001\\) . (e) Explain what value the results\napproach and why the original expression cannot be evaluated at \\(h=0\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Self-assessment:\nexplain the mathematics", "url": "chapter01.html#self-assessment-explain-the-mathematics", "text": "Before proceeding to the chapter summary, check whether you can do\nmore than reproduce a formula. Can you explain why negative exponents\nrepresent reciprocals? Can you derive a tolerance interval from a center\nand half-width? Can you distinguish a square-root symbol from the\nsolutions of a squared equation? Can you simplify a rational expression\nwhile preserving excluded inputs? Can you use units to decide whether a\nmodel is meaningful? Can you identify when a numerical check is evidence\nrather than proof? If any answer is uncertain, revisit the relevant\ninvestigation and complete its guided practice without consulting the\nworked example."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Additional\nworked applications and error analysis", "url": "chapter01.html#additional-worked-applications-and-error-analysis", "text": "Example 29: A complete tolerance analysis. A\nfictional instrument is calibrated to \\(25.0\\) units with a maximum allowed\ndeviation of \\(0.75\\) units. Determine\nthe acceptance region and assess readings of \\(24.20\\) , \\(25.70\\) , and \\(25.80\\) units. Worked solution. Let \\(x\\) be the reading. The deviation is \\(|x-25.0|\\) , so acceptance requires \\(|x-25.0|\\le0.75\\) . Subtracting and adding \\(0.75\\) gives \\(24.25\\le x\\le25.75\\) . The reading \\(24.20\\) is too low, \\(25.70\\) is acceptable, and \\(25.80\\) is too high. The endpoints are\naccepted because the condition is non-strict. Example 30: Error detection in a rational\nexpression. A student writes \\(\\dfrac{x^2+5x}{x}=x+5\\) for every real \\(x\\) . Evaluate the student’s\nreasoning. Worked solution. Factoring gives \\(x(x+5)/x\\) . Canceling \\(x\\) is valid only when \\(x\\ne0\\) . The simplified formula \\(x+5\\) agrees with the original for all\nnonzero inputs, but the original is undefined at \\(x=0\\) . The student’s algebra is correct on\nthe restricted domain; the phrase “for every real \\(x\\) ” is not. Example 31: An application involving a rectangular\nregion. A rectangular laboratory work surface has length \\(x+4\\) cm and width \\(x+2\\) cm, where \\(x>0\\) . Write and expand its area, then\nfind the area when \\(x=6\\) . Worked solution. The area is \\(A(x)=(x+4)(x+2)=x^2+6x+8\\) square\ncentimeters. At \\(x=6\\) , \\(A(6)=36+36+8=80\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2\\) . Checking\ngeometrically, the sides are \\(10\\) cm\nand \\(8\\) cm, and their product is \\(80\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2\\) . The two methods\nagree. Example 32: Comparing absolute and relative\ndifferences. Two fictional devices measure the same reference\nquantity of \\(200\\) units. Device A\nreads \\(196\\) and device B reads \\(207\\) . Which has the smaller absolute error\nand relative error? Worked solution. Device A has absolute error \\(|196-200|=4\\) units and relative error \\(4/200=2\\%\\) . Device B has absolute\nerror \\(|207-200|=7\\) units and\nrelative error \\(7/200=3.5\\%\\) . Device\nA has the smaller error by both measures. These comparisons do not by\nthemselves establish which device is more reliable across repeated\nmeasurements."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Try it without\nlooking back", "url": "chapter01.html#try-it-without-looking-back", "text": "Write the acceptance interval for a target of \\(42\\) with tolerance \\(1.5\\) , then test readings \\(40.4\\) and \\(43.5\\) . Simplify \\(\\dfrac{3x^2-12x}{3x}\\) and retain the\ndomain of the original expression. A rectangle has sides \\(x+1\\) and \\(x+7\\) . Expand its area and\nevaluate at \\(x=2\\) . A measurement of \\(81\\) is\ncompared with reference \\(80\\) . Find\nthe signed difference and absolute relative error. Reflection. For each solution, identify one\nmathematical check that would catch an error: substitution, reversing a\nconversion, expanding a factorization, checking an endpoint, or\nexamining units. Explain which check is most informative and why."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Chapter summary and\ncalculus connection", "url": "chapter01.html#chapter-summary-and-calculus-connection", "text": "The real number system, inequalities, exponents, radicals, factoring,\nrational expressions, and units form the working language of\nprecalculus. In calculus, difference quotients often require factoring\nand canceling a factor while carefully preserving domain restrictions.\nFor example, for \\(h\\ne0\\) , \\[\\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}=\\frac{2xh+h^2}{h}=2x+h.\\] The restriction \\(h\\ne0\\) is essential\nduring simplification; a later calculus course will study what happens\nas \\(h\\) approaches zero."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Chapter exercises", "url": "chapter01.html#chapter-exercises", "text": "Show intermediate steps, state restrictions, and include units\nwhen appropriate. For computational questions, explain the output in\nwords."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Number systems\nand intervals", "url": "chapter01.html#number-systems-and-intervals", "text": "Classify \\(-12\\) , \\(0\\) , \\(5/6\\) , \\(\\sqrt{81}\\) , and \\(\\pi\\) as natural, whole, integer, rational,\nor irrational where applicable. Write \\(-4<x\\le2\\) in\ninterval notation and sketch it. Write \\((-\\infty,-1)\\cup[3,\\infty)\\) as an\ninequality using “or. Find \\([-3,4)\\cap(1,7]\\) . Express all real numbers within \\(0.6\\) of \\(8\\) as an absolute-value inequality and\ninterval. Solve \\(|x+2|<5\\) and express\nthe answer in interval notation."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Expressions and\npowers", "url": "chapter01.html#expressions-and-powers", "text": "Evaluate \\(5+18/3^2\\) exactly. Simplify \\(4(3x-2)-5(x+1)\\) . Simplify \\((3a^2b^{-1})^2/(9a^{-1})\\) and state\nrestrictions. Explain the difference between \\((-4)^2\\) and \\(-4^2\\) . Write \\(0.00000082\\) in\nscientific notation. Compute \\((6\\times10^5)(2\\times10^{-3})\\) in\nscientific notation."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Radicals and\nfactoring", "url": "chapter01.html#radicals-and-factoring", "text": "Simplify \\(\\sqrt{98x^2}\\) for\nreal \\(x\\) . Simplify \\(\\sqrt{48}+2\\sqrt{3}\\) . Rationalize \\(5/\\sqrt{7}\\) . Factor \\(x^2-7x+12\\) . Factor \\(9y^2-25\\) . Explain why \\(\\sqrt{a+b}=\\sqrt a+\\sqrt\nb\\) is not a general identity."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Rational\nexpressions", "url": "chapter01.html#rational-expressions", "text": "Simplify \\((x^2-16)/(x^2+4x)\\) and state all exclusions. Add \\(1/x+2/(x-3)\\) and state\nrestrictions. Simplify \\((x^2-1)/(x-1)\\) ,\nnoting the original domain. Identify the error in canceling the \\(x\\) terms of \\((x+4)/x\\) to obtain \\(4\\) ."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Measurement and\nmodeling", "url": "chapter01.html#measurement-and-modeling", "text": "Convert \\(0.035\\) g to\nmg. Convert \\(1.8\\) L to\nmL. A nonclinical liquid transfer moves \\(150\\) mL in \\(30\\) minutes. Express its rate in\nmL/h. A sample has \\(0.09\\) g of\nsolute in \\(150\\) mL. Find\nmg/mL. A measurement is \\(12.0\\pm0.3\\) cm. Give its allowable interval and absolute-value inequality. Explain the distinction between exact conversion factors and\nmeasured quantities."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Computational\nand challenge", "url": "chapter01.html#computational-and-challenge", "text": "Run both scripts and compare \\(0.1+0.2\\) to \\(0.3\\) . Explain the observation. Modify both plotting scripts to display \\(|x-4|\\) and the threshold \\(1.5\\) . Find the interval\nalgebraically. Explain why \\((x^2-9)/(x-3)\\) and \\(x+3\\) are not the same function\non all real numbers. Simplify \\(((x+h)^2-x^2)/h\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) and explain why \\(h=0\\) is excluded."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Calculus readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter01.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "Factor \\(x^2-4\\) and simplify \\((x^2-4)/(x-2)\\) with the correct\nrestriction. For \\(h\\ne0\\) , simplify \\(((3+h)^2-9)/h\\) . Interpret \\(|x-5|<0.2\\) as a\ndistance statement and an interval. Explain why units matter when interpreting the slope of a\ngraph. Looking ahead. Chapter 2 will use these skills to\nsolve equations and inequalities, identify extraneous solutions, and\nconstruct algebraic models."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Extended\napplication: Laboratory dilution audit", "url": "chapter01.html#extended-application-laboratory-dilution-audit", "text": "A teaching laboratory mixes 120 mg of dye into a variable volume of\nwater. Assume complete mixing and negligible volume change from the dye.\nThe quantities in this investigation are illustrative rather than\nmeasured observations."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter01.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[C(v)=120/v.\\] Before calculating, identify\nthe input and output, their units, and the interval on which the model\nis being studied. Distinguish the mathematical domain from the practical\ndomain. The numerical study uses inputs from \\(100\\) to \\(600\\) . Example 33: Analytical investigation. Derive the\nvolume required for a concentration of \\(0.4\\) mg/mL. Convert the original mass to\ngrams and explain why volume must be positive. Worked solution. \\(120/0.4=300\\) mL; \\(120\\) mg is \\(0.12\\) g. Zero volume makes the quotient\nundefined."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter01.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Volume (mL) Concentration (mg/mL) 100 1.2 225 0.5333 350 0.3429 475 0.2526 600 0.2 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter01.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Laboratory dilution audit # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 100 , 600 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 120 / x # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Volume (mL)\" , ylab = \"Concentration (mg/mL)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter01.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Laboratory dilution audit import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 100 , 600 , 301 ) y = 120 / x # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Volume (mL)\" , ylabel = \"Concentration (mg/mL)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter01.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Laboratory dilution audit", "url": "chapter01.html#chapter-project-investigating-laboratory-dilution-audit", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter01.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "url": "chapter02.html#equations-inequalities-and-applications", "text": ""}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Opening\ninvestigation: When is a measurement acceptable?", "url": "chapter02.html#opening-investigation-when-is-a-measurement-acceptable", "text": "A clinical laboratory reports a target concentration of \\(50\\) units per milliliter and allows a\ntolerance of \\(4\\) units per\nmilliliter. The acceptable measurements satisfy \\(|c-50|\\leq4\\) . Before solving, sketch a\nnumber line and identify which values you expect to be included. Then\ntranslate the absolute-value statement into two ordinary inequalities.\nThis single example anticipates a central theme of the chapter: an\nequation describes exact agreement, whereas an inequality describes a\nrange of possibilities."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter02.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "By the end of this chapter, students should be able to (1) solve and\nverify linear equations; (2) solve quadratic equations by factoring,\nsquare roots, completing the square, and the quadratic formula; (3)\nidentify excluded values and check proposed solutions of rational and\nradical equations; (4) solve and graph linear, compound, quadratic,\nrational, and absolute-value inequalities; (5) build and interpret\nequations and constraints from applied situations; and (6) use R and\nPython to visualize and numerically verify results without mistaking\nnumerical evidence for proof."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Prerequisite\ndiagnostic", "url": "chapter02.html#prerequisite-diagnostic", "text": "Without technology, simplify \\(3(2x-1)-2(x+4)\\) ; factor \\(x^2-9\\) ; determine the sign of \\(-3(2-5)\\) ; and describe \\(|x-7|\\) as a distance. Answers: \\(4x-11\\) , \\((x-3)(x+3)\\) , positive, and the distance\nfrom \\(x\\) to \\(7\\) . Review Chapter 1 if any of these ideas\nremain uncertain."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Equations as statements of\nequality", "url": "chapter02.html#equations-as-statements-of-equality", "text": "An equation asserts that two expressions have equal values.\nSolving means finding all values in the stated domain for which the\nassertion is true. An equation may have one solution, several solutions,\nno solutions, or infinitely many solutions. The distinction between a\nsolution and an algebraic manipulation is important: every final\ncandidate should be substituted into the original equation."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Equivalent transformations", "url": "chapter02.html#equivalent-transformations", "text": "Adding the same real number to both sides, subtracting the same\nexpression, and multiplying or dividing both sides by the same nonzero number preserve equality. Multiplication by an\nexpression that could equal zero requires caution. Squaring both sides\ncan introduce extraneous candidates, while dividing by a variable\nexpression can discard legitimate solutions if that expression might\nvanish. Example 1: A linear equation with parentheses. Solve \\(3(2x-5)-2(x+1)=11\\) . Worked solution. Distribute: \\(6x-15-2x-2=11\\) . Combine like terms: \\(4x-17=11\\) . Add \\(17\\) : \\(4x=28\\) . Divide by \\(4\\) : \\(x=7\\) . Check: \\(3(14-5)-2(8)=27-16=11\\) . Thus \\(\\boxed{x=7}\\) . Example 2: An identity and a contradiction. Compare \\(2(x+3)=2x+6\\) and \\(2(x+3)=2x+7\\) . Worked solution. The first simplifies to \\(2x+6=2x+6\\) , true for every real \\(x\\) ; its solution set is \\(\\mathbb R\\) . The second becomes \\(6=7\\) , which is false for every \\(x\\) ; its solution set is \\(\\varnothing\\) . Neither should be reported\nas “ \\(x=0\\) .” Guided practice 1. Solve \\(5-2(3x-4)=19\\) , and verify\nby substitution. Then classify \\(4(x-2)=4x-8\\) as conditional, identity, or\ncontradiction. Check: \\(x=-1\\) ; identity."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Modeling with linear\nequations", "url": "chapter02.html#modeling-with-linear-equations", "text": "The most difficult step in many applications is not algebra but\nchoosing a variable and translating words into quantities. A robust\nmodeling routine is: define the unknown with units; write a\nrelationship; solve; check units and assumptions; and interpret the\nresult in context. Never interpret a negative mathematical answer as\nphysically meaningful without examining the model. Example 3: Mixture concentration. A solution\ncontains \\(12\\) mg of a substance in \\(80\\) mL. How much pure water must be\nadded to reduce the concentration to \\(0.10\\) mg/mL? Worked solution. Let \\(w\\) be milliliters of added water. The\namount of substance remains \\(12\\) mg\nand the final volume is \\(80+w\\) mL.\nHence \\(12/(80+w)=0.10\\) . Multiply: \\(12=8+0.10w\\) , so \\(w=40\\) mL. The final concentration is \\(12/120=0.10\\) mg/mL. Guided practice 2. A facility charges a fixed fee of 18 dollars plus 7 dollars per hour.\nIf the bill is 74 dollars, how many hours were used? Check: \\(8\\) hours."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Quadratic equations and\ntheir structure", "url": "chapter02.html#quadratic-equations-and-their-structure", "text": "A quadratic equation has the form \\(ax^2+bx+c=0\\) with \\(a\\ne0\\) . Its graph is a parabola. The zeros\nof the quadratic expression correspond to the \\(x\\) -intercepts of the graph, when real\nzeros exist. Different solving methods expose different features of the\nsame mathematical object."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "The zero-product\nprinciple and factoring", "url": "chapter02.html#the-zero-product-principle-and-factoring", "text": "If \\(AB=0\\) for real numbers \\(A,B\\) , then \\(A=0\\) or \\(B=0\\) . Therefore factoring is effective\nwhen the quadratic splits into manageable factors. Example 4: Factoring. Solve \\(2x^2-5x-3=0\\) . Worked solution. Factor \\((2x+1)(x-3)=0\\) . Hence \\(2x+1=0\\) or \\(x-3=0\\) , giving \\(\\boxed{x=-\\tfrac12,\\ 3}\\) . Verify: at \\(3\\) , \\(18-15-3=0\\) ; at \\(-1/2\\) , \\(1/2+5/2-3=0\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Square roots and\ncompleting the square", "url": "chapter02.html#square-roots-and-completing-the-square", "text": "For \\((x-h)^2=k\\) , real solutions\nexist only when \\(k\\ge0\\) , and are \\(x=h\\pm\\sqrt{k}\\) . Completing the\nsquare rewrites a quadratic to make this structure visible. The identity \\(x^2+px=(x+p/2)^2-(p/2)^2\\) is the key\nstep. Example 5: Completing the square. Solve \\(x^2+6x-7=0\\) . Worked solution. Move the constant: \\(x^2+6x=7\\) . Add \\(9\\) to both sides: \\((x+3)^2=16\\) . Therefore \\(x+3=\\pm4\\) , so \\(\\boxed{x=1,-7}\\) . The vertex form is also \\(y=(x+3)^2-16\\) , showing a vertex at \\((-3,-16)\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Deriving the quadratic\nformula", "url": "chapter02.html#deriving-the-quadratic-formula", "text": "Starting with \\(ax^2+bx+c=0\\) ,\ndivide by \\(a\\) and move the constant: \\[x^2+\\frac ba x=-\\frac ca.\\] Add \\((b/2a)^2\\) to both sides and factor: \\[\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)^2=\\frac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2}.\\] Taking square roots and isolating \\(x\\) gives \\[\\boxed{x=\\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}}.\\] The discriminant \\(D=b^2-4ac\\) determines the number of distinct real roots: two when \\(D>0\\) , one repeated root when \\(D=0\\) , and no real roots when \\(D<0\\) . In the last case the roots are\ncomplex, a topic that may be introduced later. Example 6: Discriminant and interpretation. Solve \\(3x^2-4x-2=0\\) exactly and\napproximately. Worked solution. \\(D=(-4)^2-4(3)(-2)=40\\) . Thus \\(x=(4\\pm\\sqrt{40})/6=(2\\pm\\sqrt{10})/3\\) .\nSince \\(\\sqrt{10}\\approx3.1623\\) , the\nroots are approximately \\(1.7208\\) and \\(-0.3874\\) . Both are real because \\(D>0\\) . Guided practice 3. Solve \\(x^2-8x+12=0\\) by factoring\nand completing the square. Then classify the real roots of \\(x^2+4x+5=0\\) . Check: \\(2,6\\) ; no real roots."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation I: quadratic roots and the discriminant", "url": "chapter02.html#visual-investigation-i-quadratic-roots-and-the-discriminant", "text": "Consider the family \\(y=x^2-4x+c\\) .\nComplete the square to obtain \\(y=(x-2)^2+(c-4)\\) . The vertex always has\nhorizontal coordinate \\(2\\) ; changing \\(c\\) moves the parabola vertically.\nWhen \\(c<4\\) the curve crosses the\nhorizontal axis twice; when \\(c=4\\) it\ntouches once; and when \\(c>4\\) it\ndoes not cross. Explain how this matches \\(D=16-4c\\) . image Investigate. (a) Predict the number of real roots before graphing \\(x^2-4x+3=0\\) . (b) Explain why the vertex\nalone determines the root count for this family. (c) Describe one\nlimitation of using a graph to determine exact roots."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Rational equations: domain\nfirst", "url": "chapter02.html#rational-equations-domain-first", "text": "A rational equation contains a variable in a denominator. Every\ndenominator must be nonzero. Determine excluded values before multiplying by a common denominator; otherwise the algebra may hide a\nforbidden candidate. Example 7: A rational equation. Solve \\(\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+1}=\\frac{3}{x^2-1}\\) . Worked solution. The domain excludes \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=-1\\) . Multiply both sides by \\((x-1)(x+1)\\) , valid on the stated domain: \\(2(x+1)+(x-1)=3\\) . Thus \\(3x+1=3\\) , giving \\(x=2/3\\) . It is not excluded. Substitution\nconfirms the equality, so the solution set is \\(\\{2/3\\}\\) . Example 8: A false candidate after clearing\ndenominators. Solve \\(\\frac{x}{x-2}=\\frac{2}{x-2}\\) . Worked solution. The domain excludes \\(x=2\\) . Multiplication by \\(x-2\\) gives \\(x=2\\) , but this is forbidden in the\noriginal equation. Therefore there are no solutions . Guided practice 4. Solve \\(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac34\\) ,\nstating restrictions first. Check: \\(x=2\\) or \\(x=-4/3\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Radical equations\nand extraneous solutions", "url": "chapter02.html#radical-equations-and-extraneous-solutions", "text": "An even root requires a nonnegative radicand in the real number\nsystem. Isolate the radical before squaring; then substitute every\ncandidate into the original equation. Squaring is not reversible without\nadditional sign information: \\(a=b\\) implies \\(a^2=b^2\\) , but the converse\nis false. Example 9: A radical equation. Solve \\(\\sqrt{x+5}=x-1\\) . Worked solution. Because the square root is\nnonnegative, we require \\(x-1\\ge0\\) .\nSquare: \\(x+5=(x-1)^2=x^2-2x+1\\) . Hence \\(x^2-3x-4=0\\) , so \\((x-4)(x+1)=0\\) . Candidates are \\(4\\) and \\(-1\\) . Check: at \\(4\\) , \\(\\sqrt9=3=4-1\\) ; at \\(-1\\) , \\(\\sqrt4=2\\ne-2\\) . Therefore \\(\\boxed{x=4}\\) . Guided practice 5. Solve \\(\\sqrt{2x+3}=x\\) and check\nall candidates. Check: \\(x=3\\) only."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Linear inequalities\nand interval notation", "url": "chapter02.html#linear-inequalities-and-interval-notation", "text": "Solving inequalities resembles solving equations, except multiplying\nor dividing by a negative number reverses the comparison. For example, \\(-2x<6\\) becomes \\(x>-3\\) . Open endpoints represent strict\ninequalities; filled endpoints represent inclusive inequalities. Example 10: A linear inequality. Solve \\(4-3(2x-1)\\le13\\) . Worked solution. \\(4-6x+3\\le13\\) , so \\(-6x\\le6\\) . Dividing by \\(-6\\) reverses the sign: \\(x\\ge-1\\) . Interval notation is \\([-1,\\infty)\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Compound\ninequalities and absolute-value constraints", "url": "chapter02.html#compound-inequalities-and-absolute-value-constraints", "text": "An “and” compound inequality represents an intersection; an “or”\nstatement represents a union. The statement \\(|x-a|\\le r\\) describes all points within\ndistance \\(r\\) of \\(a\\) , so \\(a-r\\le\nx\\le a+r\\) for \\(r\\ge0\\) . By\ncontrast, \\(|x-a|>r\\) describes\npoints outside that interval. Example 11: Measurement tolerance. A sample should\nhave mass \\(25.0\\pm0.6\\) g. Write and\nsolve a tolerance inequality. Worked solution. Let \\(m\\) be the measured mass in grams. The\ncondition is \\(|m-25.0|\\le0.6\\) ,\nequivalent to \\(-0.6\\le m-25.0\\le0.6\\) .\nAdding \\(25.0\\) gives \\(\\boxed{24.4\\le m\\le25.6}\\) . Both endpoints\nare accepted. image Guided practice 6. Solve \\(|2x-5|<7\\) and write the\nanswer in interval notation. Check: \\((-1,6)\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Quadratic\ninequalities: a sign-chart method", "url": "chapter02.html#quadratic-inequalities-a-sign-chart-method", "text": "A quadratic inequality asks where a parabola lies above or below the\nhorizontal axis. Factor when possible, identify critical values, divide\nthe real line into intervals, and test the sign in each interval.\nRemember that the zeros are included only for non-strict\ninequalities. Example 12: Quadratic inequality. Solve \\(x^2-x-6\\le0\\) . Worked solution. Factor \\((x-3)(x+2)\\le0\\) . Critical values are \\(-2\\) and \\(3\\) . On \\((-\\infty,-2)\\) both factors are negative,\nso their product is positive. On \\((-2,3)\\) the factors have opposite signs,\nso the product is negative. On \\((3,\\infty)\\) both are positive. Include the\nzeros: \\(\\boxed{[-2,3]}\\) . image Guided practice 7. Solve \\(x^2+2x-8>0\\) . Explain why\nthe interval between the roots is not part of the solution. Check: \\((-\\infty,-4)\\cup(2,\\infty)\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Rational inequalities and\nsign charts", "url": "chapter02.html#rational-inequalities-and-sign-charts", "text": "A rational inequality requires attention to both numerator zeros and\ndenominator zeros. Numerator zeros may be included when equality is\nallowed; denominator zeros are always excluded . A sign chart\ntracks changes across all critical values. Example 13: A rational inequality. Solve \\(\\frac{x-2}{x+1}\\ge0\\) . Worked solution. Critical values are \\(x=2\\) (zero) and \\(x=-1\\) (undefined). Test \\(x=-2\\) : \\((-4)/(-1)>0\\) . Test \\(x=0\\) : \\((-2)/(1)<0\\) . Test \\(x=3\\) : \\((1)/(4)>0\\) . Include \\(2\\) because the inequality permits zero;\nexclude \\(-1\\) because division by zero\nis undefined. Hence \\(\\boxed{(-\\infty,-1)\\cup[2,\\infty)}\\) . Guided practice 8. Solve \\((x+3)/(x-4)<0\\) . Check: \\((-3,4)\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation II: intersection, not just algebra", "url": "chapter02.html#visual-investigation-ii-intersection-not-just-algebra", "text": "An equation \\(f(x)=g(x)\\) asks where\ntwo graphs intersect. An inequality \\(f(x)<g(x)\\) asks where the first graph\nlies below the second. For \\(x^2-4=2x\\) , move all terms to one side: \\(x^2-2x-4=0\\) , with exact\nintersections \\(x=1\\pm\\sqrt5\\) . Sketch \\(y=x^2-4\\) and \\(y=2x\\) ; explain graphically why \\(x^2-4<2x\\) holds between the\nintersections. image"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Applied\nmodeling case study: a feasible operating window", "url": "chapter02.html#applied-modeling-case-study-a-feasible-operating-window", "text": "Suppose a simplified engineering model gives a temperature response \\(T(t)=20+8t-t^2\\) in degrees Celsius,\nwhere \\(t\\) is hours after a process\nbegins and \\(0\\le t\\le8\\) . An operating\nprotocol requires \\(T(t)\\ge32\\) . Example 14: Determine the acceptable operating\ntimes. Find the times during which the model satisfies the\nprotocol. Worked solution. Solve \\(20+8t-t^2\\ge32\\) , or \\(t^2-8t+12\\le0\\) after multiplying by \\(-1\\) and reversing the inequality. Factor \\((t-2)(t-6)\\le0\\) . The inequality\nholds for \\(2\\le t\\le6\\) . Intersecting\nwith the model domain \\([0,8]\\) does\nnot change the result. The process is predicted to satisfy the protocol\nfrom hour \\(2\\) through hour \\(6\\) , inclusive. This is a mathematical\nmodel, not a clinical treatment recommendation. Model critique. What changes if the requirement becomes \\(T(t)>32\\) ? Why is it inappropriate to\nextend the formula to \\(t=100\\) without\nevidence? Which assumptions would need empirical validation?"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Numerical methods\nand graphical verification", "url": "chapter02.html#numerical-methods-and-graphical-verification", "text": "Some equations have no convenient closed-form solution. A numerical\nmethod can approximate a root by repeatedly narrowing an interval where\na continuous function changes sign. The bisection method begins\nwith \\(f(a)f(b)<0\\) and evaluates\nthe midpoint \\(m=(a+b)/2\\) . It retains\nthe half-interval where the sign change occurs. A sign change plus\ncontinuity guarantees a root, but a plot alone does not establish that a\ndisplayed intersection is exact. Numerical methods should report\nprecision and verify the residual \\(|f(x)|\\) . Example 15: One bisection iteration. For \\(f(x)=x^3-x-1\\) , check the interval \\([1,2]\\) and take one bisection step. Worked solution. \\(f(1)=-1\\) and \\(f(2)=5\\) , so there is a sign change. The\nmidpoint is \\(1.5\\) and \\(f(1.5)=3.375-1.5-1=0.875>0\\) . The sign\nchange is therefore on \\([1,1.5]\\) .\nFurther iterations narrow the interval."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: quadratic equations and root verification", "url": "chapter02.html#computational-laboratory-a-quadratic-equations-and-root-verification", "text": "Purpose. Compare algebraic solutions with numerical\napproximations and a graph. Predict first: Find the\ndiscriminant and estimate the roots of \\(3x^2-4x-2=0\\) before running either script.\nBoth languages use the same coefficients and plotting range. Run the R\nlisting first; then run the Python listing. Compare the printed values\nand explain why numerical agreement does not replace the quadratic\nformula derivation."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter02.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code a <- 3 ; b <- - 4 ; c0 <- - 2 D <- b ^ 2 - 4 * a * c0 roots <- c (( - b - sqrt (D)) / ( 2 * a), ( - b + sqrt (D)) / ( 2 * a)) f <- function (x) a * x ^ 2 + b * x + c0 print ( data.frame ( root= roots, residual= f (roots))) x <- seq ( - 1.5 , 3 , length.out= 400 ) plot (x, f (x), type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , col= \"steelblue\" , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"3x^2 - 4x - 2\" ) abline ( h= 0 , lty= 2 ) points (roots, f (roots), pch= 19 , col= \"firebrick\" )"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter02.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt a, b, c0 = 3 , - 4 , - 2 D = b ** 2 - 4 * a * c0 roots = np.array([( - b - np.sqrt(D)) / ( 2 * a), ( - b + np.sqrt(D)) / ( 2 * a)]) def f(x): return a * x ** 2 + b * x + c0 for root in roots: print ( f\"root= { root :.6f} , residual= { f(root) :.2e} \" ) x = np.linspace( - 1.5 , 3 , 400 ) plt.plot(x, f(x), linewidth = 2 ) plt.axhline( 0 , color = \"black\" , linestyle = \"--\" ) plt.scatter(roots, f(roots)) plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"3x^2 - 4x - 2\" ) plt.tight_layout() ; plt.show() Interpretation. Which quantities are exact algebraic expressions, and which are\nfloating-point approximations? Why may the computed residual be close\nto, but not exactly, zero?"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: inequalities and feasible regions", "url": "chapter02.html#computational-laboratory-b-inequalities-and-feasible-regions", "text": "Purpose. Visualize the solution to \\((t-2)(t-6)\\le0\\) on the domain \\(0\\le t\\le8\\) . Predict the interval first.\nThe scripts shade the feasible region and mark the boundary times."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter02.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code t <- seq ( 0 , 8 , length.out= 401 ) g <- (t - 2 ) * (t - 6 ) plot (t, g, type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , col= \"steelblue\" , xlab= \"Time (hours)\" , ylab= \"(t - 2)(t - 6)\" ) abline ( h= 0 , lty= 2 ) idx <- which (g <= 0 ) points (t[idx], g[idx], pch= 16 , cex= 0.35 , col= \"darkgreen\" ) abline ( v= c ( 2 , 6 ), lty= 3 ) print ( range (t[idx]))"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter02.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt t = np.linspace( 0 , 8 , 401 ) g = (t - 2 ) * (t - 6 ) mask = g <= 0 plt.plot(t, g, linewidth = 2 ) plt.axhline( 0 , color = \"black\" , linestyle = \"--\" ) plt.scatter(t[mask], g[mask], s = 5 ) for boundary in ( 2 , 6 ): plt.axvline(boundary, linestyle = \":\" ) print ( \"grid approximation:\" , t[mask]. min (), t[mask]. max ()) plt.xlabel( \"Time (hours)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"(t - 2)(t - 6)\" ) plt.tight_layout() ; plt.show() Interpretation. A grid approximates a feasible set but does not prove that the\nendpoints are exactly \\(2\\) and \\(6\\) . Explain why factoring does."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: bisection and a non-polynomial root", "url": "chapter02.html#computational-laboratory-c-bisection-and-a-non-polynomial-root", "text": "Purpose. Approximate a solution of \\(x^3-x-1=0\\) on \\([1,2]\\) using 25 iterations. Compare the\nresidual and final interval width. Both implementations use the same\nbisection logic."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter02.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code f <- function (x) x ^ 3 - x - 1 left <- 1 ; right <- 2 stopifnot ( f (left) * f (right) < 0 ) for (k in 1 : 25 ) { mid <- (left + right) / 2 if ( f (left) * f (mid) <= 0 ) right <- mid else left <- mid } root <- (left + right) / 2 cat ( \"root =\" , root, \"residual =\" , f (root), \"width =\" , right - left, \" \\n \" )"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter02.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code def f(x): return x ** 3 - x - 1 left, right = 1.0 , 2.0 assert f(left) * f(right) < 0 for k in range ( 25 ): mid = (left + right) / 2 if f(left) * f(mid) <= 0 : right = mid else : left = mid root = (left + right) / 2 print ( \"root =\" , root, \"residual =\" , f(root), \"width =\" , right - left) Interpretation. Why does the interval width halve each iteration? State the\ncontinuity assumption used to justify a root between the endpoints."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Guided practice\nworkshop: explain, solve, verify", "url": "chapter02.html#guided-practice-workshop-explain-solve-verify", "text": "Solve \\(7(2x-1)=3x+26\\) . Write a\ncheck in the original equation. Solve \\(2x^2+7x+3=0\\) by\nfactoring and verify using the quadratic formula. Solve \\(\\sqrt{x+10}=x-2\\) and\nreject any extraneous candidates. Solve \\(\\frac{x+1}{x-3}\\le0\\) using a sign chart; identify the excluded value. A storage system requires \\(|V-120|\\le8\\) liters. Express the allowable\nvolumes as an interval and explain the endpoints. A model \\(h(t)=-t^2+6t+7\\) is\ndefined for \\(t\\ge0\\) . Find when \\(h(t)\\ge12\\) and discuss whether all\nalgebraic solutions are in the physical domain."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Common\nmisconceptions and mathematical habits", "url": "chapter02.html#common-misconceptions-and-mathematical-habits", "text": "Dividing an inequality by a negative without reversing\nits direction. Test a simple value to detect the\nerror. Treating every candidate from a squared equation as\nvalid. Always check the original radical equation. Including a denominator zero in a rational\nsolution. Record exclusions before solving. Assuming every quadratic factors over the\nintegers. Use completing the square or the quadratic formula\nwhen needed. Confusing exact and approximate answers. \\(1+\\sqrt5\\) is exact; \\(3.236\\) is approximate. Ignoring model domains. A negative time or\nconcentration may be mathematically possible but physically\ninadmissible."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Chapter summary and\nformula reference", "url": "chapter02.html#chapter-summary-and-formula-reference", "text": "Concept Essential result Linear equation Apply reversible operations and check Quadratic formula \\(x=(-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac})/(2a)\\) Discriminant \\(D=b^2-4ac\\) determines real-root count Absolute-value tolerance \\(|x-a|\\le r\\iff\na-r\\le x\\le a+r\\) for \\(r\\ge0\\) Rational expressions Denominator zeros are excluded Radical equations Check candidates after squaring Inequalities Reverse the sign when multiplying or\ndividing by a negative Sign chart Test intervals divided by zeros and\nundefined points"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Calculus\nconnection: equations behind rates and optimization", "url": "chapter02.html#calculus-connection-equations-behind-rates-and-optimization", "text": "In calculus, derivatives lead to equations such as \\(f'(x)=0\\) and inequalities such as \\(f'(x)>0\\) . The algebraic\nskills in this chapter determine where a model increases, decreases, or\nreaches candidate extrema. Solving \\(x^2-6x+8=0\\) now prepares students to solve\nderivative equations later. Rational expressions and domain exclusions\nbecome especially important when studying continuity and limits.\nNumerical root finding is a bridge to computational calculus, but\nsymbolic reasoning remains essential."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation III: how a quadratic model changes", "url": "chapter02.html#extended-investigation-iii-how-a-quadratic-model-changes", "text": "Consider the family of models \\(P(q)=-q^2+12q-k\\) , where \\(q\\) is a nonnegative quantity and \\(k\\) is a fixed cost. A zero of \\(P\\) is a break-even quantity. The axis of\nsymmetry is always \\(q=6\\) , but the\nmaximum value depends on \\(k\\) .\nComplete the square: \\[P(q)=-(q-6)^2+36-k.\\] This form gives the\nvertex \\((6,36-k)\\) without graphing.\nIf \\(k<36\\) , the model has two\ndistinct real break-even quantities; if \\(k=36\\) , one repeated break-even quantity;\nif \\(k>36\\) , no real break-even\nquantity. A mathematical root outside the physically permitted domain \\(q\\ge0\\) must not be interpreted as a\nfeasible operating quantity. Example 16: Break-even analysis. Let \\(k=20\\) . Determine the break-even quantities\nand the quantities producing nonnegative profit. Worked solution. Set \\(-q^2+12q-20=0\\) , or \\(q^2-12q+20=0\\) . Factoring gives \\((q-2)(q-10)=0\\) , so break-even occurs at \\(q=2\\) and \\(q=10\\) . Since the parabola opens downward, \\(P(q)\\ge0\\) precisely on \\([2,10]\\) . The maximum profit is \\(36-20=16\\) at \\(q=6\\) . The units of profit depend on the\nmodel’s definition. Investigate and explain. Replace \\(k=20\\) with \\(k=36\\) and \\(k=40\\) . For each case, find the\ndiscriminant, identify the vertex, and explain whether nonnegative\nprofit is possible. Discuss how a change in fixed cost shifts the graph\nwithout changing its axis of symmetry."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Guided\npractice: a different family", "url": "chapter02.html#guided-practice-a-different-family", "text": "For \\(H(t)=-2t^2+16t+c\\) , complete\nthe square and determine the value of \\(c\\) for which the graph just touches the\nhorizontal axis. Then explain how the number of real roots changes as \\(c\\) increases. Check: \\(H(t)=-2(t-4)^2+32+c\\) ; tangency occurs when \\(c=-32\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation IV: the logic of solution sets", "url": "chapter02.html#extended-investigation-iv-the-logic-of-solution-sets", "text": "A useful way to understand equations is to view each transformation\nas a statement about solution sets. Let \\(S(E)\\) denote the set of real values\nsatisfying equation \\(E\\) . An equivalent transformation produces an equation \\(E_2\\) for which \\(S(E_1)=S(E_2)\\) . A one-way transformation\nmay only guarantee \\(S(E_1)\\subseteq\nS(E_2)\\) . Example 17: Why squaring can enlarge the solution\nset. Compare \\(\\sqrt{x+1}=x-1\\) with \\(x+1=(x-1)^2\\) . Worked solution. The squared equation becomes \\(x^2-3x=0\\) , giving candidates \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=3\\) . In the original equation, \\(x=0\\) would require \\(1=-1\\) , which is false. The original\nequation has only \\(x=3\\) . Thus the\nsquared equation has a larger solution set than the original\nequation. Example 18: Why dividing can shrink the solution\nset. Compare \\(x(x-4)=0\\) with \\(x-4=0\\) . Worked solution. The original equation has solutions \\(0\\) and \\(4\\) . Dividing by \\(x\\) assumes \\(x\\ne0\\) and removes a legitimate solution.\nThe transformed equation retains only \\(4\\) . Guided practice. For each transformation, classify it as always reversible, reversible\nunder a stated restriction, or potentially non-reversible: (a) add \\(5\\) to both sides; (b) multiply both sides\nby \\(x-2\\) ; (c) take the square root of\nboth sides; (d) divide both sides by \\(-3\\) ; (e) square both sides. Give a\ncounterexample whenever reversibility fails. Reflection. Why is a numerical check in the original equation more informative\nthan checking a transformed equation? Why does a finite list of\nsuccessful numerical checks not prove an identity for all real\nnumbers?"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation V: constraints and feasible decisions", "url": "chapter02.html#extended-investigation-v-constraints-and-feasible-decisions", "text": "A mathematical model often includes several simultaneous\nrestrictions. The feasible set is the intersection of all\nconstraints. For example, a laboratory process might require both \\(20\\le t\\le45\\) and \\(|t-32|\\le8\\) . The second condition becomes \\(24\\le t\\le40\\) , so the feasible set\nis \\([24,40]\\) . An additional strict\nrequirement \\(t>30\\) changes the\nfeasible set to \\((30,40]\\) . Example 19: Intersection of three constraints. A\nmeasurement \\(m\\) must satisfy \\(m\\ge12\\) , \\(m<20\\) , and \\(|m-15|\\le3\\) . Find the feasible set. Worked solution. The absolute-value condition\nbecomes \\(12\\le m\\le18\\) . Intersecting\nwith \\([12,\\infty)\\) and \\((-\\infty,20)\\) leaves \\(\\boxed{[12,18]}\\) . The endpoint \\(18\\) is permitted because all three\nconstraints allow it. image Guided practice. Solve the simultaneous constraints \\(|x-10|<4\\) , \\(x\\ge8\\) , and \\(x\\le13\\) . Check: \\([8,13]\\) . Modeling challenge. A storage tank must contain between \\(60\\) and \\(100\\) liters, inclusive. A sensor flags a\nwarning if its reading differs from \\(80\\) liters by more than \\(12\\) liters. Write a compound condition\ndescribing volumes that satisfy the capacity restriction but trigger a\nwarning. Explain whether the warning boundaries are included."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation VI: parameter sensitivity and root stability", "url": "chapter02.html#extended-investigation-vi-parameter-sensitivity-and-root-stability", "text": "A model is often fitted using measured coefficients rather than exact\nconstants. Consider \\(x^2-6x+c=0\\) . Its\nroots, when real, are \\(x=3\\pm\\sqrt{9-c}\\) . Near \\(c=9\\) , even a small change in \\(c\\) can cause a noticeable change in the\nroots or remove the real roots entirely. This is one reason why\nnumerical root finding and uncertainty analysis matter in\napplications. Example 20: A small parameter change. Compare the\nroots when \\(c=8.99\\) , \\(c=9\\) , and \\(c=9.01\\) . Worked solution. For \\(c=8.99\\) , the roots are \\(3\\pm\\sqrt{0.01}=2.9\\) and \\(3.1\\) . For \\(c=9\\) , there is a repeated root at \\(3\\) . For \\(c=9.01\\) , \\(9-c=-0.01\\) , so there are no real roots.\nThe model changes continuously in its coefficients, but the number of\nreal solutions can change at the discriminant threshold. Guided practice. Consider \\(x^2-10x+c=0\\) . Find the\ncritical value of \\(c\\) at which two\ndistinct real roots merge. Determine the roots for \\(c=24\\) and explain the result graphically. Check: critical \\(c=25\\) ;\nroots \\(4\\) and \\(6\\) when \\(c=24\\) . Discussion. In an experimental context, why should a root close to a\nrepeated-root threshold be interpreted cautiously? What information\nabout measurement error would help you judge the reliability of a\npredicted solution?"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation VII: an integrated algebra-to-calculus bridge", "url": "chapter02.html#extended-investigation-vii-an-integrated-algebra-to-calculus-bridge", "text": "Suppose a model gives \\(s(t)=t^2+2t\\) for position in arbitrary\nunits, with \\(t\\ge0\\) . The average rate\nof change from \\(t=2\\) to \\(t=2+h\\) is \\[\\frac{s(2+h)-s(2)}{h},\\qquad h\\ne0.\\] Compute \\(s(2)=8\\) and \\(s(2+h)=(2+h)^2+2(2+h)=8+6h+h^2\\) . Thus \\[\\frac{s(2+h)-s(2)}h=\\frac{6h+h^2}{h}=6+h,\\qquad\nh\\ne0.\\] The expression \\(6+h\\) is defined at \\(h=0\\) , but the original\nquotient is not. Later, calculus will investigate the value approached\nas \\(h\\) becomes arbitrarily small. For\nnow, the important precalculus skills are expansion, factoring,\ncancellation with a domain restriction, and numerical evaluation. Example 21: Numerical exploration. Evaluate the\nsimplified quotient for \\(h=1,0.1,0.01,-0.1\\) , and \\(-0.01\\) . Worked solution. The values are \\(7\\) , \\(6.1\\) , \\(6.01\\) , \\(5.9\\) , and \\(5.99\\) . They suggest that the average rate\napproaches \\(6\\) as \\(h\\) approaches zero, although the original\nquotient remains undefined at \\(h=0\\) . Guided practice. For \\(s(t)=3t^2-t\\) , simplify \\([s(1+h)-s(1)]/h\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) . Then evaluate it at \\(h=0.1\\) and \\(h=-0.1\\) . Check: \\(5+3h\\) ; \\(5.3\\) and \\(4.7\\) . Looking ahead. This is a preview, not a replacement for the formal definition of the\nderivative. In the single-variable calculus volume, the limiting\nbehavior of such quotients will become the foundation of\ndifferentiation."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Chapter exercises", "url": "chapter02.html#chapter-exercises", "text": "Instructions. Show algebraic reasoning, identify\ndomain restrictions, and verify candidate solutions. Graph or use\ntechnology only where requested. The following exercises progress from\nfluency to modeling and explanation."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Linear\nequations and modeling", "url": "chapter02.html#linear-equations-and-modeling", "text": "Solve or classify: \\(4(3x-2)=5x+13\\) . Solve or classify: \\(\\frac{x-2}{3}+\\frac{x+1}{2}=5\\) . Solve or classify: \\(2(x-3)+7=2x+1\\) . Solve or classify: \\(5(x-1)=5x+2\\) . Solve or classify: \\(0.15(200+x)=42\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Quadratic equations", "url": "chapter02.html#quadratic-equations", "text": "Solve or classify: \\(x^2-9x+20=0\\) . Solve or classify: \\(2x^2+x-6=0\\) . Solve or classify: \\((x-3)^2=11\\) . Solve or classify: \\(x^2+2x+7=0\\) . Solve or classify: \\(3x^2-5x-1=0\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Rational and\nradical equations", "url": "chapter02.html#rational-and-radical-equations", "text": "Solve or classify: \\(\\frac{3}{x-2}=1\\) . Solve or classify: \\(\\frac{x+1}{x-1}=2\\) . Solve or classify: \\(\\sqrt{x+6}=x\\) . Solve or classify: \\(\\sqrt{3x+4}=x+2\\) . Solve or classify: \\(\\frac{x-4}{x-4}=0\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Inequalities", "url": "chapter02.html#inequalities", "text": "Solve or classify: \\(3-5x\\ge18\\) . Solve or classify: \\(|x+2|\\le5\\) . Solve or classify: \\(|3x-1|>8\\) . Solve or classify: \\((x-1)(x+5)<0\\) . Solve or classify: \\(\\frac{x-1}{x+2}>0\\) ."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Applied and\nconceptual problems", "url": "chapter02.html#applied-and-conceptual-problems", "text": "A laboratory measurement must remain within \\(2.5\\) units of a target of \\(36\\) . Write an absolute-value inequality\nand solve it. Identify whether endpoints are allowed. A simplified revenue model is \\(R(q)=40q-q^2\\) , with \\(0\\le q\\le40\\) . Determine all quantities for\nwhich revenue is at least \\(300\\) . A rectangular enclosure has width \\(w\\) and length \\(w+5\\) meters. Its area is \\(84\\) square meters. Form a quadratic\nequation, solve it, and reject any physically invalid\ncandidate. A medication stock solution contains \\(30\\) mg in \\(100\\) mL. How much water must be added to\nobtain a concentration of \\(0.20\\) mg/mL? Explain why the total amount of medication is unchanged. Explain in words why multiplying an inequality by \\(-1\\) reverses its direction. Give a\nnumerical example. Construct a radical equation for which squaring introduces an\nextraneous candidate. Solve and explain the failure. Find an equation with exactly two distinct real roots, one\nrepeated real root, and no real roots by varying only the constant in \\(x^2-6x+c=0\\) . Determine where \\(x^2-4x-5\\) is\npositive, negative, or zero, and represent the results\ngraphically. Explain why the solutions of \\((x-1)/(x-1)=1\\) do not include \\(x=1\\) even though the simplified equation\nis \\(1=1\\) . Use either language to graph \\(y=x^2-2x-8\\) and identify approximate\nroots. Then derive the exact roots algebraically and compare."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter02.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "Simplify the difference quotient \\(\\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) . Explain why the original quotient\nis undefined at \\(h=0\\) . Solve \\(2x^2-8x=0\\) and explain\nhow the roots could become critical points in a later calculus\ncourse. Determine the domain of \\(\\frac{1}{x^2-9}\\) and solve \\(\\frac{1}{x^2-9}>0\\) . Explain the distinction between an exact root, a graphical\nestimate, and a numerical approximation. A model has feasible times determined by \\(t^2-10t+21\\le0\\) . Find the interval and\nexplain why endpoint inclusion matters."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Looking ahead to\nChapter 3", "url": "chapter02.html#looking-ahead-to-chapter-3", "text": "Equations describe where relationships agree; inequalities describe\nwhere constraints are met. In Chapter 3, coordinate geometry, slopes,\nand systems of equations will make these relationships visual and\nprepare students to interpret rates of change."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Extended\napplication: Equipment operating window", "url": "chapter02.html#extended-application-equipment-operating-window", "text": "A sensor has a simplified response that peaks at setting 5. A\ntechnician requires a response of at least 12 units. The quantities in\nthis investigation are illustrative rather than measured\nobservations."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter02.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[R(x)=16-(x-5)^2.\\] Before calculating,\nidentify the input and output, their units, and the interval on which\nthe model is being studied. Distinguish the mathematical domain from the\npractical domain. The numerical study uses inputs from \\(0\\) to \\(10\\) . Example 22: Analytical investigation. Solve \\(R(x)\\ge12\\) exactly. Explain the role of\nthe endpoints and check the setting 8. Worked solution. \\((x-5)^2\\le4\\) , hence \\(3\\le x\\le7\\) . Both endpoints qualify; \\(R(8)=7\\) does not."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter02.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Setting Response 0 -9 2.5 9.75 5 16 7.5 9.75 10 -9 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter02.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Equipment operating window # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 10 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 16 - (x -5 ) ^ 2 # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Setting\" , ylab = \"Response\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter02.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Equipment operating window import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 10 , 301 ) y = 16 - (x - 5 ) ** 2 # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Setting\" , ylabel = \"Response\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter02.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Equipment operating window", "url": "chapter02.html#chapter-project-investigating-equipment-operating-window", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter02.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "url": "chapter03.html#coordinate-geometry-lines-and-systems-of-equations", "text": ""}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Opening\ninvestigation: What does a calibration line tell us?", "url": "chapter03.html#opening-investigation-what-does-a-calibration-line-tell-us", "text": "A laboratory instrument reports signal intensity \\(S\\) for a sample with concentration \\(c\\) (mg/L). Two reference standards give \\((c,S)=(2,9)\\) and \\((8,27)\\) . Plot the two points on axes,\nlabel units, and draw the line through them. What does the rise in\nsignal per unit concentration mean? What signal would you expect at \\(c=5\\) ? Would the same model\nnecessarily remain reliable at \\(c=100\\) ? This investigation introduces\ncoordinates, slope, linear models, and the importance of checking the\ndomain of an applied model."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter03.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "After this chapter, you should be able to: (1) locate points and\ninterpret coordinates with units; (2) calculate and interpret distance\nand midpoint; (3) determine slope and equations of lines in multiple\nforms; (4) distinguish parallel and perpendicular lines; (5) model and\ninterpret linear relationships; (6) solve systems graphically,\nalgebraically, and using matrices; (7) distinguish unique, inconsistent,\nand dependent systems; (8) use R and Python for plotting and\nverification; and (9) connect secant slopes to the derivative concept\nintroduced in calculus."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Prerequisite\ndiagnostic", "url": "chapter03.html#prerequisite-diagnostic", "text": "Solve \\(3x-7=11\\) ; simplify \\(\\frac{12-4}{6-2}\\) ; and solve \\(x+y=7\\) for \\(y\\) . Answers: \\(x=6\\) , \\(2\\) , and \\(y=7-x\\) . Review Chapter 2 if solving\nequations or manipulating fractions is difficult."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "The Cartesian coordinate\nplane", "url": "chapter03.html#the-cartesian-coordinate-plane", "text": "The Cartesian plane is formed by a horizontal \\(x\\) -axis and vertical \\(y\\) -axis meeting at the origin \\((0,0)\\) . A point \\((x,y)\\) is an ordered pair : the\nfirst coordinate specifies horizontal position and the second vertical\nposition. Units and scale matter in applications. For example, \\((3,15)\\) may mean 3 hours and 15 milligrams\nper liter; swapping coordinates changes the meaning. image Visual investigation 1. For each labeled point, identify its quadrant. Reflect \\(A\\) across each axis and predict the new\ncoordinates. Explain why points on axes belong to no quadrant. Example 1: Coordinate interpretation. A measurement\nis represented by \\((4,18)\\) , with time\nin minutes horizontally and temperature in degrees Celsius vertically.\nInterpret the point and explain why \\((18,4)\\) describes a different\nobservation. Worked solution. At 4 minutes, the recorded\ntemperature is \\(18^\\circ\\) C. The\nreversed pair instead represents 18 minutes and \\(4^\\circ\\) C. The ordered pair is not\ninterchangeable. Guided practice 1. Plot \\(P(-4,1)\\) , \\(Q(0,-3)\\) , and \\(R(2,0)\\) . Which points lie on an axis?\nReflect \\(P\\) in the \\(y\\) -axis. Check: \\(Q,R\\) ; reflected point \\((4,1)\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Distance between two points", "url": "chapter03.html#distance-between-two-points", "text": "Horizontal and vertical displacements from \\(P(x_1,y_1)\\) to \\(Q(x_2,y_2)\\) are \\(\\Delta x=x_2-x_1\\) and \\(\\Delta y=y_2-y_1\\) . The Pythagorean theorem\napplied to the right triangle formed by these displacements gives \\[\\boxed{d(P,Q)=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}.}\\] Squaring the displacements ensures the distance is independent of\ndirection. The distance formula assumes both axes use compatible\nphysical units and equal geometric scaling. image Example 2: Distance and units. Find the distance\nfrom \\((-2,5)\\) to \\((4,-3)\\) . Worked solution. \\(\\Delta\nx=4-(-2)=6\\) and \\(\\Delta\ny=-3-5=-8\\) . Thus \\(d=\\sqrt{6^2+(-8)^2}=\\sqrt{100}=10\\) coordinate units. Reversing the points changes the signs of both\ndisplacements but not the distance. Guided practice 2. Find the distance between \\((1,-1)\\) and \\((7,7)\\) . Check: \\(10\\) units."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Midpoints,\npartitions, and geometric averages", "url": "chapter03.html#midpoints-partitions-and-geometric-averages", "text": "The midpoint of a segment is the point halfway between its endpoints.\nAverage the horizontal coordinates and vertical coordinates separately: \\[\\boxed{M=\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right).}\\] This formula is a coordinate-wise average, not the average of distances\nto the origin. More generally, the point a fraction \\(t\\) of the way from \\(P\\) to \\(Q\\) is \\(P+t(Q-P)\\) , or \\[(x,y)=\\bigl(x_1+t(x_2-x_1),\\;y_1+t(y_2-y_1)\\bigr),\\quad\n0\\le t\\le1.\\] This representation anticipates parametric curves\nand vectors. Example 3: Midpoint and weighted position. Find the\nmidpoint of \\(A(-4,6)\\) and \\(B(8,-2)\\) and the point one-quarter of the\nway from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) . Worked solution. The midpoint is \\((({-4+8})/2,(6-2)/2)=(2,2)\\) . For \\(t=1/4\\) , \\(A+\\frac14(B-A)=(-4,6)+\\frac14(12,-8)=(-1,4)\\) . Guided practice 3. Find the midpoint of \\((3,9)\\) and \\((11,-1)\\) . Check: \\((7,4)\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Slope as a rate of change", "url": "chapter03.html#slope-as-a-rate-of-change", "text": "For distinct points with \\(x_2\\ne\nx_1\\) , the slope of the line through them is \\[\\boxed{m=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\\frac{\\Delta\ny}{\\Delta x}.}\\] Slope compares the change in the response\nvariable with the change in the explanatory variable. It has units of\n“vertical units per horizontal unit.” Positive slope means the line\nrises as \\(x\\) increases; negative\nslope means it falls; zero slope is horizontal. A vertical line has\nundefined slope because its horizontal change is zero. image Example 4: Interpreting a laboratory slope. Standards \\((2,9)\\) and \\((8,27)\\) have concentration in mg/L and\nsignal in instrument units. Compute the slope and interpret it. Worked solution. \\(m=(27-9)/(8-2)=18/6=3\\) signal units per\nmg/L. Within the calibration range, a 1 mg/L increase in concentration\ncorresponds to a predicted increase of 3 signal units. The slope is not\n“3 mg/L”; its units are a ratio. Visual investigation 2. Draw two triangles of different sizes along the same nonvertical\nline. Compute rise/run for each and explain why the ratios agree. Then\nsketch a vertical line and explain why rise/run cannot be evaluated\nthere. Guided practice 4. Find and interpret the slope through \\((1,12)\\) and \\((5,28)\\) when \\(x\\) is days and \\(y\\) is milligrams. Check: \\(4\\) mg/day."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Equations of lines: three\nuseful forms", "url": "chapter03.html#equations-of-lines-three-useful-forms", "text": "A line can be represented in several algebraically equivalent ways,\neach emphasizing different information."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Slope-intercept form", "url": "chapter03.html#slope-intercept-form", "text": "\\[\\boxed{y=mx+b.}\\] Here \\(m\\) is the slope and \\(b\\) is the vertical intercept \\((0,b)\\) . The intercept is the model’s\nprediction at \\(x=0\\) ; it may be\nphysically meaningful, meaningless, or outside the observed range."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Point-slope form", "url": "chapter03.html#point-slope-form", "text": "\\[\\boxed{y-y_1=m(x-x_1).}\\] This\nform follows directly from the definition of slope and is particularly\nuseful when a slope and one point are known."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Standard form and special\nlines", "url": "chapter03.html#standard-form-and-special-lines", "text": "Standard form is \\(Ax+By=C\\) ,\nusually with integer coefficients. Horizontal lines are \\(y=k\\) ; vertical lines are \\(x=h\\) . A vertical line cannot be written as \\(y=mx+b\\) . Example 5: Equation from two points. Find the\nequation through \\((2,5)\\) and \\((6,13)\\) in point-slope, slope-intercept,\nand standard forms. Worked solution. The slope is \\((13-5)/(6-2)=2\\) . Point-slope form: \\(y-5=2(x-2)\\) . Expand: \\(y=2x+1\\) . Rearranging: \\(2x-y=-1\\) . Substituting \\((6,13)\\) gives \\(13=12+1\\) , confirming the equation. Example 6: Equation with undefined slope. Find the\nline through \\((3,-2)\\) and \\((3,7)\\) . Worked solution. Both points have \\(x=3\\) , so the line is \\(x=3\\) . Its slope is undefined; attempting\nto divide by \\(3-3=0\\) is invalid. Guided practice 5. Find the line through \\((-1,4)\\) with slope \\(-3\\) . Check: \\(y=-3x+1\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Intercepts and\ngraphical interpretation", "url": "chapter03.html#intercepts-and-graphical-interpretation", "text": "An \\(x\\) -intercept is a point on the\nhorizontal axis, so its \\(y\\) -coordinate is zero. A \\(y\\) -intercept is on the vertical axis, so\nits \\(x\\) -coordinate is zero. To find\nthe intercepts of \\(3x+2y=12\\) , set \\(y=0\\) to obtain \\((4,0)\\) , and set \\(x=0\\) to obtain \\((0,6)\\) . The intercepts provide a fast\ngraphing method, but do not by themselves justify extrapolating a model\nbeyond its data. Example 7: Intercepts in a budget model. A program\nhas a budget of \\(240\\) dollars. Kits\ncost \\(12\\) dollars and brochures cost \\(3\\) dollars. Interpret \\(12k+3b=240\\) in the first quadrant. Worked solution. The intercepts are \\((20,0)\\) and \\((0,80)\\) . The first represents 20 kits and\nno brochures; the second represents no kits and 80 brochures. Feasible\nquantities also require \\(k,b\\ge0\\) and, if purchases must be whole, integer values. Guided practice 6. Find both intercepts of \\(4x-5y=20\\) . Check: \\((5,0)\\) and \\((0,-4)\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Parallel and perpendicular\nlines", "url": "chapter03.html#parallel-and-perpendicular-lines", "text": "Two distinct nonvertical lines are parallel precisely when their\nslopes are equal. Two nonvertical lines with nonzero slopes are\nperpendicular precisely when \\(m_1m_2=-1\\) , so their slopes are negative\nreciprocals. A horizontal line is perpendicular to a vertical line. Be\ncareful: the negative reciprocal of \\(2/3\\) is \\(-3/2\\) , not \\(-2/3\\) . Example 8: Parallel and perpendicular through a\npoint. Find lines through \\((2,-1)\\) parallel and perpendicular to \\(3x-2y=8\\) . Worked solution. Rewrite the given line as \\(y=\\frac32x-4\\) , so \\(m=3/2\\) . The parallel line is \\(y+1=\\frac32(x-2)\\) , or \\(y=\\frac32x-4\\) . In this case \\((2,-1)\\) lies on the original line, so it\nis the same line rather than a distinct parallel line. The perpendicular\nline has slope \\(-2/3\\) : \\(y+1=-\\frac23(x-2)\\) , or \\(y=-\\frac23x+\\frac13\\) . Concept check. Why is it important to distinguish “same line” from “distinct\nparallel lines”? Draw an example. Guided practice 7. Find a line through \\((0,4)\\) perpendicular to \\(y=\\frac14x+2\\) . Check: \\(y=-4x+4\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Building and evaluating\nlinear models", "url": "chapter03.html#building-and-evaluating-linear-models", "text": "A linear model \\(y=mx+b\\) assumes a\nconstant rate of change over its intended domain. The intercept\nrepresents the predicted baseline, and the slope represents the change\nin response per unit change in predictor. A model can fit two observed\npoints exactly but still be inappropriate outside the observation range\nor when the real process is nonlinear. Example 9: Calibration curve. Using \\((c,S)=(2,9)\\) and \\((8,27)\\) , construct the calibration\nequation, predict signal at \\(c=5\\) ,\nand estimate concentration when \\(S=21\\) . Worked solution. Slope is \\(m=3\\) . Use \\((2,9)\\) : \\(9=3(2)+b\\) , so \\(b=3\\) . The calibration equation is \\(S=3c+3\\) . At \\(c=5\\) , \\(S=18\\) . For \\(S=21\\) , solve \\(21=3c+3\\) to obtain \\(c=6\\) mg/L. Both predictions are within the\nobserved concentration range \\([2,8]\\) ,\nso they are interpolations. Visual\ninvestigation 3: interpolation and extrapolation. Plot the calibration points and fitted line for \\(0\\le c\\le12\\) . Shade or label the observed\ninterval \\([2,8]\\) . Compare predictions\nat \\(c=5\\) and \\(c=11\\) . Why does the second require\nstronger assumptions? Guided practice 8. A reading changes from \\(14\\) to \\(26\\) between times \\(t=1\\) and \\(t=4\\) . Find a linear model and predict the\nreading at \\(t=3\\) . Check: \\(y=4t+10\\) ; \\(22\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Systems of two linear\nequations", "url": "chapter03.html#systems-of-two-linear-equations", "text": "A system asks for values satisfying two or more equations\nsimultaneously. Geometrically, a solution is an intersection of lines.\nTwo nonparallel lines intersect once (unique solution); distinct\nparallel lines never intersect (no solution); coincident lines have\ninfinitely many solutions."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Substitution method", "url": "chapter03.html#substitution-method", "text": "Solve one equation for one variable and substitute into the other.\nThis is especially convenient when one coefficient is \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\) . Example 10: Substitution. Solve \\(x+y=7\\) and \\(2x-y=2\\) . Worked solution. From the first equation, \\(y=7-x\\) . Substitute: \\(2x-(7-x)=2\\) , so \\(3x=9\\) and \\(x=3\\) . Then \\(y=4\\) . Check: \\(3+4=7\\) and \\(2(3)-4=2\\) . Thus \\((3,4)\\) is the unique solution."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Elimination method", "url": "chapter03.html#elimination-method", "text": "Add suitable multiples of the equations so one variable cancels.\nMultiplying an entire equation by a nonzero constant preserves its\nsolution set. Example 11: Elimination. Solve \\(2x+3y=13\\) and \\(4x-y=5\\) . Worked solution. Multiply the second equation by \\(3\\) : \\(12x-3y=15\\) . Add to the first: \\(14x=28\\) , so \\(x=2\\) . Then \\(4(2)-y=5\\) gives \\(y=3\\) . Verify \\(2(2)+3(3)=13\\) . Guided practice 9. Solve \\(3x+y=11\\) and \\(x-y=1\\) using both methods. Check: \\((3,2)\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation 4: the geometry of solution counts", "url": "chapter03.html#visual-investigation-4-the-geometry-of-solution-counts", "text": "image The blue and orange lines meet at \\((2,3)\\) . The blue and dashed green lines\nhave equal slopes but different intercepts; they never meet. A second\nequation obtained by multiplying \\(x+y=5\\) by \\(2\\) would describe exactly the same blue\nline. Use these three cases to explain why algebra can end in a\nnumerical answer, a contradiction such as \\(0=2\\) , or an identity such as \\(0=0\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Dependent and inconsistent\nsystems", "url": "chapter03.html#dependent-and-inconsistent-systems", "text": "Example 12: No solution. Solve \\(2x-4y=6\\) and \\(x-2y=5\\) . Worked solution. Doubling the second equation gives \\(2x-4y=10\\) , contradicting \\(2x-4y=6\\) . The lines are parallel and\ndistinct, so the system is inconsistent and has no solution. Example 13: Infinitely many solutions. Solve \\(2x+4y=8\\) and \\(x+2y=4\\) . Worked solution. The first equation is twice the\nsecond. They represent the same line. Every pair satisfying \\(x+2y=4\\) is a solution; for example, \\((x,y)=(4-2t,t)\\) for any real \\(t\\) . Guided practice 10. Classify \\(3x+6y=12\\) and \\(x+2y=4\\) ; then classify \\(3x+6y=12\\) and \\(x+2y=5\\) . Check: infinitely many;\nnone."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Matrices and\ndeterminants: a first look", "url": "chapter03.html#matrices-and-determinants-a-first-look", "text": "A two-variable system can be written \\(A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\\) , where \\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{pmatrix}e\\\\f\\end{pmatrix}.\\] The determinant \\(\\det(A)=ad-bc\\) helps\nclassify the system. If \\(ad-bc\\ne0\\) ,\nthe coefficient matrix is invertible and there is exactly one solution.\nIf \\(ad-bc=0\\) , there may be no\nsolution or infinitely many; additional consistency checks are required.\nFor a nonsingular \\(2\\times2\\) system,\nCramer’s rule gives \\[x=\\frac{ed-bf}{ad-bc},\\qquad\ny=\\frac{af-ec}{ad-bc}.\\] Example 14: Determinant check. For \\(2x+3y=13\\) , \\(4x-y=5\\) , calculate the determinant and\nverify the solution. Worked solution. \\(A=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}2&3\\\\4&-1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\) ,\nso \\(\\det(A)=2(-1)-3(4)=-14\\ne0\\) . Then \\(x=(13(-1)-3(5))/(-14)=2\\) , and \\(y=(2(5)-13(4))/(-14)=3\\) . Guided practice 11. Calculate the determinant for \\(x+2y=5\\) , \\(2x+4y=10\\) . Does a zero determinant alone\ntell you whether the system is inconsistent? Check: determinant\nzero; no."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Applied\ncase study: two-test calibration and mixture design", "url": "chapter03.html#applied-case-study-two-test-calibration-and-mixture-design", "text": "Suppose a laboratory prepares a mixture using two stock solutions.\nStock A contains \\(4\\) mg of a compound\nper mL and Stock B contains \\(10\\) mg\nper mL. A final mixture must have total volume \\(20\\) mL and contain \\(140\\) mg of the compound. Let \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) denote the volumes in mL. Example 15: Mixture design as a system. Determine\nthe required amounts of stocks A and B. Worked solution. Total volume gives \\(a+b=20\\) . Total compound mass gives \\(4a+10b=140\\) . Substitute \\(a=20-b\\) : \\(4(20-b)+10b=140\\) , so \\(80+6b=140\\) and \\(b=10\\) mL. Hence \\(a=10\\) mL. Check: total volume \\(20\\) mL and compound mass \\(4(10)+10(10)=140\\) mg. Both amounts are\nnonnegative, as required. Visual investigation 5:\nfeasibility. Graph \\(a+b=20\\) and \\(4a+10b=140\\) in the first quadrant. Mark\ntheir intersection. What would a negative solution mean physically? How\nwould the intersection move if the required mass were changed to 160\nmg?"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: plotting points and lines", "url": "chapter03.html#computational-laboratory-a-plotting-points-and-lines", "text": "The first laboratory compares a hand-derived calibration line with\nits computational graph. Execute the R code, then execute the Python\ncode separately. Both produce the same mathematical model; programming\nsyntax differs, but the interpretation should not."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter03.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code concentration <- c ( 2 , 8 ) signal <- c ( 9 , 27 ) slope <- diff (signal) / diff (concentration) intercept <- signal[ 1 ] - slope * concentration[ 1 ] cat ( \"Slope:\" , slope, \"Intercept:\" , intercept, \" \\n \" ) plot (concentration, signal, pch= 19 , xlim= c ( 0 , 12 ), ylim= c ( 0 , 40 ), xlab= \"Concentration (mg/L)\" , ylab= \"Signal (units)\" , main= \"Calibration line\" ) abline ( a= intercept, b= slope, col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 ) abline ( v= c ( 2 , 8 ), lty= 3 , col= \"gray\" ) cat ( \"Signal at 5 mg/L:\" , slope * 5 + intercept, \" \\n \" )"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter03.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt c = np.array([ 2.0 , 8.0 ]) s = np.array([ 9.0 , 27.0 ]) m = (s[ 1 ] - s[ 0 ]) / (c[ 1 ] - c[ 0 ]) b = s[ 0 ] - m * c[ 0 ] print ( \"Slope:\" , m, \"Intercept:\" , b) x = np.linspace( 0 , 12 , 200 ) plt.scatter(c, s, color = \"black\" , label = \"Standards\" ) plt.plot(x, m * x + b, label = \"Calibration model\" ) plt.axvline( 2 , ls = \"--\" , color = \"gray\" ) plt.axvline( 8 , ls = \"--\" , color = \"gray\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Concentration (mg/L)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Signal (units)\" ) plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() ; plt.show() print ( \"Signal at 5 mg/L:\" , m * 5 + b) Interpretation questions. What are the units of slope? Which concentrations are interpolations?\nWhat mathematical information is lost if axis labels and units are\nomitted?"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: solving and verifying systems", "url": "chapter03.html#computational-laboratory-b-solving-and-verifying-systems", "text": "This laboratory uses matrix operations to solve a two-equation system\nand checks the residual \\(A\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{b}\\) . A residual close\nto zero provides a numerical verification, not a substitute for\nexplaining the model."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter03.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code A <- matrix ( c ( 1 , 4 , 1 , 10 ), nrow= 2 , byrow= TRUE ) rhs <- c ( 20 , 140 ) solution <- solve (A, rhs) print (solution) print (A %*% solution - rhs) cat ( \"Determinant:\" , det (A), \" \\n \" )"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter03.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np A = np.array([[ 1.0 , 1.0 ],[ 4.0 , 10.0 ]]) rhs = np.array([ 20.0 , 140.0 ]) solution = np.linalg.solve(A, rhs) print ( \"Solution:\" , solution) print ( \"Residual:\" , A @ solution - rhs) print ( \"Determinant:\" , np.linalg.det(A)) Interpretation questions. Explain why the two solution coordinates are volumes. What does a\nresidual of zero mean? Change the target mass to 160 mg and interpret\nthe new result."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: slopes approaching a tangent", "url": "chapter03.html#computational-laboratory-c-slopes-approaching-a-tangent", "text": "For the curve \\(y=x^2\\) , calculate\nthe slope of the secant line through \\((2,4)\\) and \\((2+h,(2+h)^2)\\) : \\[m_h=\\frac{(2+h)^2-4}{h}=4+h,\\qquad\nh\\ne0.\\] As \\(h\\) approaches\nzero, these slopes approach \\(4\\) . This\nis a preview of the derivative, which formalizes instantaneous rate of\nchange."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter03.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code h <- c ( 1 , 0.5 , 0.1 , 0.01 , - 0.01 , - 0.1 ) secant <- (( 2 + h) ^ 2 - 4 ) / h print ( data.frame ( h= h, secant_slope= secant)) plot (h, secant, pch= 19 , xlab= \"h\" , ylab= \"Secant slope\" ) abline ( h= 4 , col= \"blue\" , lty= 2 )"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter03.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt h = np.array([ 1 , 0.5 , 0.1 , 0.01 , - 0.01 , - 0.1 ]) slopes = (( 2 + h) ** 2 - 4 ) / h for step, slope in zip (h, slopes): print ( f\"h= { step :7.3f} , secant slope= { slope :7.3f} \" ) plt.scatter(h, slopes) plt.axhline( 4 , linestyle = \"--\" , color = \"black\" ) plt.xlabel( \"h\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"Secant slope\" ) plt.tight_layout() ; plt.show() Interpretation questions. Why must \\(h\\ne0\\) in the secant\nformula? Why can the limiting slope nevertheless be \\(4\\) ? Compare the algebraic simplification\nwith the numerical output."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Guided\npractice workshop: five integrated problems", "url": "chapter03.html#guided-practice-workshop-five-integrated-problems", "text": "Geometry. Points \\(A(-2,1)\\) and \\(B(4,9)\\) represent two locations on a\nscaled map. Find distance, midpoint, and the slope of \\(AB\\) . Check: \\(10\\) , \\((1,5)\\) , and \\(4/3\\) . Model building. A quantity changes from 6 units\nto 21 units as time changes from 2 to 7 minutes. Derive the linear\nequation and predict the value at time 5. Check: \\(y=3t\\) and \\(15\\) . Line relationships. Find a line through \\((1,2)\\) perpendicular to \\(y=-2x+3\\) . Check: \\(y=\\frac12x+\\frac32\\) . System classification. Determine whether \\(x-3y=2\\) and \\(2x-6y=4\\) have zero, one, or infinitely\nmany solutions. Explain graphically. Check: infinitely\nmany. Mixtures. Two solutions have concentrations 2\nmg/mL and 8 mg/mL. Find volumes totaling 30 mL that contain 150 mg. Check: 15 mL of each."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Common\nmisconceptions and mathematical habits", "url": "chapter03.html#common-misconceptions-and-mathematical-habits", "text": "Confusing coordinates and displacements. A point\ngives location; differences of coordinates give changes. Ignoring units in slope. Always report response\nunits per predictor unit. Using the slope formula for vertical lines. Division by zero is undefined; the correct equation is \\(x=k\\) . Assuming all systems have one solution. Compare\nslopes and intercepts, or use elimination, before concluding. Confusing a zero determinant with no solution. Zero determinant means the unique-solution test fails; consistency must\nstill be checked. Overinterpreting extrapolation. A straight-line\nfit through two points does not guarantee linearity outside the observed\nrange."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Chapter summary and\nformula reference", "url": "chapter03.html#chapter-summary-and-formula-reference", "text": "Quantity Formula or principle Distance \\(\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}\\) Midpoint \\(((x_1+x_2)/2,(y_1+y_2)/2)\\) Slope \\((y_2-y_1)/(x_2-x_1)\\) when \\(x_2\\ne x_1\\) Point-slope line \\(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\\) Slope-intercept line \\(y=mx+b\\) Parallel lines Equal slopes (or both vertical) Perpendicular lines \\(m_1m_2=-1\\) when both slopes are defined\nand nonzero Two-by-two determinant \\(ad-bc\\)"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Calculus\nconnection: secants, tangents, and local change", "url": "chapter03.html#calculus-connection-secants-tangents-and-local-change", "text": "A straight line has the same slope between every pair of points. A\ncurved graph generally does not. Calculus studies the limit of secant\nslopes as the two points come together. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , the secant slope from \\(x=a\\) to \\(x=a+h\\) is \\[\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}=\\frac{(a+h)^2-a^2}{h}=2a+h,\\quad\nh\\ne0.\\] The expression approaches \\(2a\\) as \\(h\\to0\\) . At \\(a=2\\) , the instantaneous slope is \\(4\\) , consistent with Laboratory C. In a\ncalculus course, this becomes the derivative \\(f'(a)=2a\\) . The key precalculus skills\nare manipulating expressions, evaluating differences, and interpreting\nslope with units."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation I: translating and rotating the geometric picture", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-i-translating-and-rotating-the-geometric-picture", "text": "Consider \\(A(1,2)\\) and \\(B(5,5)\\) . Their displacement is \\((4,3)\\) and their distance is \\(5\\) . Translate both points by \\((2,-4)\\) to get \\(A'(3,-2)\\) and \\(B'(7,1)\\) . Calculate the new distance\nand slope. Then reflect the original points across the \\(y\\) -axis. Compare the new slope with the\noriginal slope. Translation preserves distance and slope; reflection\npreserves distance but may change the sign of slope. Explain why by\nconsidering coordinate differences. Guided practice. Translate \\((0,1)\\) and \\((3,5)\\) by \\((-2,6)\\) . Compute the original and\ntranslated distances. Check: both distances are \\(5\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation II: sensitivity of a calibration model", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-ii-sensitivity-of-a-calibration-model", "text": "Suppose the first calibration standard remains \\((2,9)\\) , but the second measurement varies\nfrom \\((8,27)\\) to \\((8,28)\\) . The slope changes from \\(3\\) to \\(19/6\\approx3.167\\) signal units per mg/L.\nThe intercept changes from \\(3\\) to \\(9-2(19/6)=8/3\\) . Compare the two\nmodels at \\(c=5\\) and \\(c=10\\) . Which prediction is more sensitive?\nDiscuss why measurement noise, multiple standards, and model validation\nmatter in scientific applications."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation III: three equations and three unknowns", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-iii-three-equations-and-three-unknowns", "text": "A system may involve more than two variables. Consider \\[x+y+z=6,\\qquad x-y+z=2,\\qquad 2x+y-z=1.\\] Subtract the second equation from the first to obtain \\(2y=4\\) , so \\(y=2\\) . Then \\(x+z=4\\) . From the third, \\(2x-z=-1\\) . Add these to get \\(3x=3\\) , hence \\(x=1\\) and \\(z=3\\) . Verify in all three original\nequations. The same elimination principle extends to larger matrices,\nalthough computational tools become increasingly useful."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation IV: piecewise linear models", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-iv-piecewise-linear-models", "text": "Many real-world relationships have different slopes in different\noperating regimes. A service might cost \\(C(t)=10+2t\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le5\\) , but \\(C(t)=20+1.5(t-5)\\) for \\(t>5\\) . Both formulas give \\(20\\) at \\(t=5\\) , so the model is continuous there,\nbut the rate of change shifts from 2 to 1.5 cost units per time unit.\nPlot both pieces and explain why a single straight line would\nmisrepresent the changing rate."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation V: constrained intersections", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-v-constrained-intersections", "text": "Consider \\(x+y=12\\) and \\(2x+5y=42\\) , with \\(x,y\\ge0\\) . Substitution gives \\(x=12-y\\) and \\(24-2y+5y=42\\) , so \\(y=6\\) and \\(x=6\\) . Now change the second equation to \\(2x+5y=75\\) . The algebraic solution is \\(y=17\\) , \\(x=-5\\) , which satisfies both equations but\nviolates nonnegativity. Explain why a mathematically correct solution\nmay be infeasible in an applied model."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation VI: linear inequalities in two variables", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-vi-linear-inequalities-in-two-variables", "text": "A linear inequality such as \\(2x+y\\le\n10\\) describes a half-plane rather than a line. First graph the\nboundary \\(2x+y=10\\) ; because equality\nis allowed, draw a solid line. Test the origin: \\(2(0)+0\\le10\\) is true, so shade the side\ncontaining \\((0,0)\\) . In a production\nmodel, we may also require \\(x\\ge0\\) and \\(y\\ge0\\) , restricting the solution\nto the first quadrant. The intersection of these three half-planes is a\ntriangular feasible region with vertices \\((0,0)\\) , \\((5,0)\\) , and \\((0,10)\\) . image Example 16: Resource feasibility. A clinic assembles\ntwo kinds of educational kits. A standard kit uses 2 units of a resource\nand a premium kit uses 1 unit. At most 10 units are available. Express\nthe feasible combinations, assuming nonnegative quantities. Worked solution. If \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are the numbers of standard and premium\nkits, respectively, then \\(2x+y\\le10\\) , \\(x\\ge0\\) , and \\(y\\ge0\\) . Whole-kit decisions also require\ninteger values. The graphical feasible region contains all continuous\nsolutions, while feasible integer decisions are its lattice points. Guided practice 12. Decide whether \\((2,5)\\) and \\((4,4)\\) satisfy \\(2x+y\\le10\\) . Check: yes; no."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation VII: an introductory optimization question", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-vii-an-introductory-optimization-question", "text": "Suppose the clinic earns 3 dollars of benefit per standard kit and 2\ndollars per premium kit, with resource constraint \\(2x+y\\le10\\) . The objective is \\(B=3x+2y\\) . On the triangular feasible\nregion, evaluate the objective at its three vertices: \\(B(0,0)=0\\) , \\(B(5,0)=15\\) , and \\(B(0,10)=20\\) . Thus the largest value occurs\nat \\((0,10)\\) , giving 20 units of\nbenefit. This illustrates a fundamental idea from linear programming: a\nlinear objective over a bounded polygon has an optimum at a vertex. More\ncomplex constraints can create many vertices, but the same geometric\nprinciple applies. Visual investigation 6. Draw the level lines \\(3x+2y=10\\) , \\(3x+2y=15\\) , and \\(3x+2y=20\\) on the feasible-region graph.\nExplain why increasing the target shifts the line away from the origin.\nWhat changes if the objective is \\(B=5x+y\\) ?"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation VIII: interpreting residuals and uncertainty", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-viii-interpreting-residuals-and-uncertainty", "text": "A line fitted to observed data rarely passes through every\nmeasurement. A residual is observed response minus predicted\nresponse: \\(e_i=y_i-\\widehat y_i\\) .\nConsider the calibration model \\(\\widehat\nS=3c+3\\) and measurements \\((2,9)\\) , \\((4,16)\\) , \\((6,20)\\) , and \\((8,27)\\) . Predicted signals are \\(9,15,21,27\\) , giving residuals \\(0,1,-1,0\\) . Residuals help us see where a\nmodel overpredicts or underpredicts. In later statistics courses,\nresidual patterns are used to assess whether a linear model is\nappropriate. Here, the key distinction is between a mathematical line\nand imperfect empirical measurements. Concentration Observed signal Predicted signal Residual 2 9 9 0 4 16 15 1 6 20 21 -1 8 27 27 0 Guided practice 13. A model predicts 18 units, but the observed reading is 16. Find the\nresidual and explain its sign. Check: \\(-2\\) ; the model overpredicts by 2\nunits."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation IX: geometric derivation of the line equation", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-ix-geometric-derivation-of-the-line-equation", "text": "Let a nonvertical line pass through \\(P(x_1,y_1)\\) with slope \\(m\\) . For any other point \\(Q(x,y)\\) on the line, the slope definition\ngives \\((y-y_1)/(x-x_1)=m\\) , provided \\(x\\ne x_1\\) . Multiplying yields \\(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\\) ; this also holds at \\(P\\) itself, where both sides are zero. Thus\nthe point-slope formula is not merely a rule to memorize: it follows\nfrom the requirement that all points on a straight line share a common\nrise-to-run ratio. Expanding gives \\(y=mx+(y_1-mx_1)\\) , so the intercept is \\(b=y_1-mx_1\\) . Example 17: Derivation in context. A line through \\((3,14)\\) has slope \\(-2\\) units per minute. Derive its\nequation. Worked solution. Constant slope means \\(y-14=-2(t-3)\\) . Therefore \\(y=-2t+20\\) . At \\(t=3\\) , the model returns \\(14\\) as required; at \\(t=0\\) , the extrapolated intercept is \\(20\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation X: why two equations can nearly agree", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-x-why-two-equations-can-nearly-agree", "text": "Consider \\(x+y=10\\) and \\(x+1.01y=10.05\\) . Subtracting gives \\(0.01y=0.05\\) , so \\(y=5\\) and \\(x=5\\) . If the second right-hand side\nchanges slightly to \\(10.06\\) , then \\(0.01y=0.06\\) , giving \\(y=6\\) and \\(x=4\\) . A change of only \\(0.01\\) in a measurement has moved each\ncoordinate by 1. This is an example of sensitivity : systems\nwith nearly parallel lines may produce substantially different\nintersections when data change slightly. The determinant is \\(1(1.01)-1(1)=0.01\\) , small but nonzero. A\nsmall determinant by itself is not a scale-invariant condition number,\nbut it alerts us to examine numerical stability and units. Guided practice 14. Solve \\(x+y=10\\) and \\(x+1.02y=10.10\\) . Check: \\((5,5)\\) ."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation XI: a three-stage model comparison", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-investigation-xi-a-three-stage-model-comparison", "text": "A simplified physiological measurement rises linearly from 5 to 17\nunits between 0 and 4 minutes, stays at 17 units until 6 minutes, and\nthen falls to 11 units by 9 minutes. Construct a piecewise linear\nfunction. The first slope is \\((17-5)/4=3\\) , so \\(y=5+3t\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le4\\) . The middle slope is zero, so \\(y=17\\) for \\(4<t\\le6\\) . The final slope is \\((11-17)/(9-6)=-2\\) , so \\(y=17-2(t-6)\\) for \\(6<t\\le9\\) . The pieces agree at their\nshared boundaries. Explain why the graph is continuous but changes slope\nabruptly at \\(t=4\\) and \\(t=6\\) . This prepares you to distinguish\ncontinuity and differentiability in calculus."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Chapter exercises", "url": "chapter03.html#chapter-exercises", "text": "Instructions. Show reasoning, label axes and units\nin applications, and verify system solutions. Technology may be used to\ncheck answers but not replace explanations."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Coordinates, distance, and\nmidpoint", "url": "chapter03.html#coordinates-distance-and-midpoint", "text": "Plot \\((-3,2)\\) , \\((4,-1)\\) , and \\((0,5)\\) ; identify quadrants or\naxes. Find the distance between \\((2,3)\\) and \\((8,11)\\) . Find the midpoint of \\((-6,4)\\) and \\((10,-2)\\) . Find the point three-quarters of the way from \\((0,0)\\) to \\((8,12)\\) . Prove using the distance formula that \\((0,0)\\) , \\((3,4)\\) , and \\((6,0)\\) form an isosceles\ntriangle. Explain why switching the axes of a measurement plot can change\nthe meaning of slope."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Slope and\nequations of lines", "url": "chapter03.html#slope-and-equations-of-lines", "text": "Find the slope through \\((1,7)\\) and \\((5,-1)\\) . Write the equation through \\((-2,3)\\) and \\((4,9)\\) in two forms. Find the intercepts of \\(5x+2y=20\\) . Write an equation for the horizontal line through \\((3,-4)\\) . Write an equation for the vertical line through \\((-5,2)\\) . Find a line through \\((2,1)\\) parallel to \\(y=3x-7\\) . Find a line through \\((2,1)\\) perpendicular to \\(y=3x-7\\) . Explain why two different lines with the same slope never\nintersect. Determine whether \\((1,2)\\) , \\((3,6)\\) , and \\((5,10)\\) are collinear."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Systems of\nequations", "url": "chapter03.html#systems-of-equations", "text": "Solve \\(x+y=9\\) and \\(x-y=3\\) by substitution. Solve \\(2x+3y=12\\) and \\(x-y=1\\) by elimination. Classify \\(2x+4y=6\\) and \\(x+2y=3\\) . Classify \\(2x+4y=6\\) and \\(x+2y=5\\) . Compute the determinant of the matrix with first row \\((3,1)\\) and second row \\((2,4)\\) . Use a matrix method to solve \\(3x+y=11\\) , \\(x-y=1\\) . Explain why a determinant of zero does not by itself imply\ninconsistency. Graph \\(y=2x+1\\) and \\(y=-x+7\\) and identify their\nintersection."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Applications and\nmathematical reasoning", "url": "chapter03.html#applications-and-mathematical-reasoning", "text": "A sensor reads 11 at concentration 2 and 29 at concentration 8.\nBuild a calibration line. Using the preceding calibration, estimate concentration when the\nsignal is 20. Explain why using the calibration line at concentration 100 is\nextrapolation. A mixture uses 3 mg/mL and 9 mg/mL stocks. Find volumes totaling\n20 mL and 120 mg. A clinic charges a fixed 25 dollars and 8 dollars per visit.\nInterpret slope and intercept. Find the average rate of change of \\(f(x)=x^2+1\\) from \\(x=1\\) to \\(x=4\\) . For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , simplify \\([f(3+h)-f(3)]/h\\) for \\(h\ne0\\) . Explain the difference between a secant slope and a tangent\nslope. Construct a piecewise linear model with slopes 2 and 5 that joins\ncontinuously at \\(x=3\\) . Choose two points with slope \\(-2\\) and explain the units in an original\napplied context."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter03.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "Derive the formula for slope from two points and explain why the\ndenominator must be nonzero. For \\(f(x)=3x^2-2x\\) , simplify \\([f(a+h)-f(a)]/h\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) . Check: \\(6a+3h-2\\) . Evaluate the preceding simplified expression as \\(h\\) approaches zero, and explain its\ngeometric meaning. Check: \\(6a-2\\) ; limiting secant slope. Explain why the rate of change of a linear function is constant\nwhile that of a quadratic is not. A calibration line has slope \\(4\\) signal units per mg/L. Interpret the\nslope as a rate and describe one reason it may not remain constant for\nall concentrations."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Looking ahead to\nChapter 4", "url": "chapter03.html#looking-ahead-to-chapter-4", "text": "Chapter 4 develops functions, graphs, and multiple representations.\nThe coordinate plane, slope, and linear models in this chapter provide\nthe language for describing how an input determines an output. You will\nextend these ideas to nonlinear functions and compare their rates of\nchange."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Extended\napplication: Comparing calibration services", "url": "chapter03.html#extended-application-comparing-calibration-services", "text": "A facility compares a service with a fixed fee of 40 dollars and 3\ndollars per sample against a second service charging 10 dollars plus 5\ndollars per sample. The quantities in this investigation are\nillustrative rather than measured observations."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter03.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[D(x)=(40+3x)-(10+5x).\\] Before\ncalculating, identify the input and output, their units, and the\ninterval on which the model is being studied. Distinguish the\nmathematical domain from the practical domain. The numerical study uses\ninputs from \\(0\\) to \\(30\\) . Example 18: Analytical investigation. Find the\nbreak-even sample count and explain the sign of \\(D\\) on either side. Identify the slope and\nits units. Worked solution. \\(30-2x=0\\) gives 15 samples. The first\nservice costs more below 15 and less above 15. Slope is \\(-2\\) dollars per sample."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter03.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Samples Cost difference (dollars) 0 30 7.5 15 15 0 22.5 -15 30 -30 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter03.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Comparing calibration services # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 30 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 30-2 * x # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Samples\" , ylab = \"Cost difference (dollars)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter03.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Comparing calibration services import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 30 , 301 ) y = 30 - 2 * x # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Samples\" , ylabel = \"Cost difference (dollars)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter03.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Comparing calibration services", "url": "chapter03.html#chapter-project-investigating-comparing-calibration-services", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter03.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "url": "chapter04.html#functions-graphs-and-multiple-representations", "text": ""}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Opening\ninvestigation: How does a quantity change?", "url": "chapter04.html#opening-investigation-how-does-a-quantity-change", "text": "A laboratory records the concentration of a substance at several\ntimes. A table gives individual observations, a graph reveals overall\ntrends, and a formula can provide predictions. Yet all three\ndescriptions are useful only if they connect each allowed input to a\nsingle output. In this chapter, we develop the language of functions,\nstudy their graphs and rates of change, and learn to move among\nsymbolic, numerical, graphical, and verbal representations."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter04.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "By the end of this chapter, you should be able to (1) determine\nwhether a relation is a function; (2) find and interpret domains and\nranges; (3) evaluate functions and interpret notation; (4) apply the\nvertical-line test; (5) identify zeros, intercepts, signs, and monotonic\nintervals; (6) compute average rates of change; (7) translate between\ntables, equations, graphs, and verbal descriptions; (8) evaluate\npiecewise functions; (9) identify symmetry; and (10) use R and Python to\ninvestigate functions and models."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Prerequisite\ndiagnostic", "url": "chapter04.html#prerequisite-diagnostic", "text": "Evaluate \\(3(2)^2-5\\) ; solve \\(2x+3=11\\) ; determine the slope between \\((1,4)\\) and \\((5,12)\\) ; and state why \\(1/(x-2)\\) is undefined at \\(x=2\\) . Answers: \\(7\\) , \\(x=4\\) , slope \\(2\\) , and division by zero. Review Chapters\n1–3 as needed."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "A visual roadmap", "url": "chapter04.html#a-visual-roadmap", "text": "image The arrows emphasize that translating representations is an active\nreasoning process: we must preserve units, domain restrictions, and\nmeaning."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Functions as\nmathematical relationships", "url": "chapter04.html#functions-as-mathematical-relationships", "text": "A function assigns exactly one output to each allowable input. We\nwrite \\(f:D\\to\\mathbb R\\) when \\(D\\) is the domain and \\(f(x)\\) is the output associated with input \\(x\\) . A relation may contain several\nordered pairs with the same first coordinate; it is a function only when\nthose pairs all have the same second coordinate. The notation \\(f(x)\\) means the value of the function at \\(x\\) , not multiplication of \\(f\\) and \\(x\\) . Visual investigation. Plot \\((1,2),(2,4),(3,6)\\) and compare with \\((1,2),(1,5),(3,6)\\) . Which set represents a\nfunction? Explain using input-output language. Example 1: Testing a relation. Does \\(\\{(-2,4),(0,0),(2,4)\\}\\) represent a\nfunction? Worked solution. Yes. Every input occurs with\nexactly one output. Repeated outputs are permitted: both \\(-2\\) and \\(2\\) map to \\(4\\) . Guided practice 1. Decide whether the pairs \\((0,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) define a function and\nexplain why."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Domain, range, and\nrestrictions", "url": "chapter04.html#domain-range-and-restrictions", "text": "The domain consists of all inputs for which a formula or\ncontextual model is defined. The range consists of all attained\noutputs. For real-valued algebraic functions, denominators cannot be\nzero and even-indexed radicals require nonnegative radicands. A\ncontextual domain may be narrower: time since a treatment begins cannot\nbe negative. For \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x-2}/(x-5)\\) , we\nrequire \\(x-2\\ge0\\) and \\(x\\ne5\\) , giving \\([2,5)\\cup(5,\\infty)\\) . The range is not\nobtained merely by copying the domain. Example 2: Two restrictions. Find the domain of \\(g(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{9-x^2}}{x-1}\\) . Worked solution. The radicand requires \\(-3\\le x\\le3\\) ; the denominator excludes \\(x=1\\) . Thus the domain is \\([-3,1)\\cup(1,3]\\) . Guided practice 2. Find the domain of \\(h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}/(x-2)\\) ."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Function notation and\nevaluation", "url": "chapter04.html#function-notation-and-evaluation", "text": "Evaluation means replacing each occurrence of the input variable with\nthe specified number or expression. Parentheses are essential: if \\(f(x)=x^2-3x+1\\) , then \\(f(a+h)=(a+h)^2-3(a+h)+1\\) , which expands to \\(a^2+2ah+h^2-3a-3h+1\\) . This\nexpression is not \\(f(a)+f(h)\\) in\ngeneral. Guided investigation. Evaluate \\(f(-2)\\) , \\(f(0)\\) , and \\(f(3)\\) ; then compare \\(f(1+2)\\) with \\(f(1)+f(2)\\) . Example 3: Input expressions. If \\(f(x)=2x^2-x\\) , find \\(f(3)\\) and \\(f(t+1)\\) . Worked solution. \\(f(3)=2(9)-3=15\\) . Also \\(f(t+1)=2(t+1)^2-(t+1)=2t^2+3t+1\\) . Guided practice 3. For \\(f(x)=x^2-2x\\) , expand \\(f(a+h)\\) and simplify."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Graphs and the vertical-line\ntest", "url": "chapter04.html#graphs-and-the-vertical-line-test", "text": "The graph of \\(y=f(x)\\) is the set\nof all points \\((x,f(x))\\) . Every\nvertical line intersects a function graph at most once. This vertical-line test is a geometric restatement of the\none-output-per-input definition. It does not test whether a function is\none-to-one; that later question uses horizontal lines. Visual investigation. Compare \\(y=x^2\\) and \\(x=y^2\\) . For the second relation, \\(x=4\\) corresponds to \\(y=2\\) and \\(y=-2\\) , so it fails the vertical-line\ntest. Example 4: A circle is not a function of \\(x\\) . Does \\(x^2+y^2=9\\) define \\(y\\) as a function of \\(x\\) ? Worked solution. No. At \\(x=0\\) , both \\(y=3\\) and \\(y=-3\\) satisfy the equation. The upper\nsemicircle \\(y=\\sqrt{9-x^2}\\) , however,\nis a function on \\([-3,3]\\) . Guided practice 4. Explain why \\(y^2=x+1\\) is not a\nfunction of \\(x\\) on its full\nrelation."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Intercepts, zeros, and signs", "url": "chapter04.html#intercepts-zeros-and-signs", "text": "A zero of \\(f\\) is an input \\(c\\) satisfying \\(f(c)=0\\) ; its graph meets the horizontal\naxis at \\((c,0)\\) . The vertical\nintercept is \\((0,f(0))\\) , provided \\(0\\) belongs to the domain. Zeros need\nnot be sign changes: \\(f(x)=(x-2)^2\\) touches the axis at \\(2\\) but stays\nnonnegative. Visual investigation. Sketch \\(x-2\\) , \\((x-2)^2\\) , and \\((x-2)^3\\) near \\(x=2\\) . Compare whether each crosses or\ntouches the axis. Example 5: Zeros and signs. Determine the zeros and\nsign intervals of \\(f(x)=(x-1)(x+3)\\) . Worked solution. Zeros are \\(-3\\) and \\(1\\) . Test \\(x=-4,0,2\\) : the signs are positive,\nnegative, positive. Thus \\(f>0\\) on \\((-\\infty,-3)\\cup(1,\\infty)\\) and \\(f<0\\) on \\((-3,1)\\) . Guided practice 5. Find the zeros of \\(x^2-4x\\) and\nidentify where the function is negative."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Increasing,\ndecreasing, and constant behavior", "url": "chapter04.html#increasing-decreasing-and-constant-behavior", "text": "A function is increasing on an interval when larger inputs always\ngive larger outputs, and decreasing when larger inputs always give\nsmaller outputs. These descriptions refer to intervals, not isolated\npoints. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , values\ndecrease as \\(x\\) moves toward zero\nfrom the left and increase as \\(x\\) moves away from zero on the right. Its minimum value is \\(0\\) at \\(x=0\\) . Guided investigation. Create a table of \\(x^2\\) for \\(x=-3,-2,\\ldots,3\\) . Identify the two\nintervals of monotonicity and explain the turning point. Example 6: Reading a quadratic. For \\(q(x)=-(x-2)^2+5\\) , identify its maximum and\nincreasing/decreasing intervals. Worked solution. The vertex is \\((2,5)\\) , giving maximum \\(5\\) . The function increases on \\((-\\infty,2)\\) and decreases on \\((2,\\infty)\\) . Guided practice 6. Identify the vertex and intervals of increase/decrease for \\((x+1)^2-3\\) ."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Average rate of change", "url": "chapter04.html#average-rate-of-change", "text": "For \\(a\\ne b\\) , the average rate of\nchange of \\(f\\) from \\(a\\) to \\(b\\) is \\(\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}\\) . It is the slope of\nthe secant line connecting \\((a,f(a))\\) and \\((b,f(b))\\) . In applications,\nattach units: if \\(f(t)\\) is\nconcentration in mg/L and \\(t\\) is time\nin hours, the rate has units mg/(L hour). Average rate is not\nnecessarily the instantaneous rate at either endpoint. Visual investigation. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , compare secant slopes over \\([0,2]\\) , \\([1,2]\\) , and \\([1.9,2]\\) . What number do they\napproach? Example 7: Secant slope. Find the average rate of\nchange of \\(f(x)=x^2+1\\) on \\([1,4]\\) . Worked solution. \\(f(1)=2\\) , \\(f(4)=17\\) , so the average rate is \\((17-2)/(4-1)=5\\) output units per input\nunit. Guided practice 7. Find the average rate of change of \\(x^2\\) over \\([2,5]\\) ."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Multiple representations", "url": "chapter04.html#multiple-representations", "text": "A function can be expressed verbally, by a table, as an equation, or\nby a graph. Each representation highlights different information: a\ntable shows observed values, an equation supports exact calculation, a\ngraph displays trends, and a verbal description supplies meaning and\nunits. Translating between representations requires checking that the\nsame inputs and outputs are being described. Visual investigation. A sample cools according to \\(T(t)=22+58(0.8)^t\\) degrees Celsius.\nMake a table for \\(t=0,1,2,3,4\\) , graph\nthe points, and describe the trend in words. Explain why the model\nshould not be interpreted as linear. Example 8: From table to rule. A table lists \\((0,7),(1,10),(2,13),(3,16)\\) . Suggest a\nrule. Worked solution. Outputs increase by \\(3\\) for each unit of input, so \\(f(x)=3x+7\\) matches the data. A finite\ntable does not uniquely determine a rule without assumptions about the\nmodel family. Guided practice 8. Build a table for \\(f(x)=4x-1\\) at \\(x=0,1,2,3\\) and describe its\ngraph."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Piecewise-defined functions", "url": "chapter04.html#piecewise-defined-functions", "text": "A piecewise function uses different rules on different portions of\nits domain. The conditions are part of the definition; the correct rule\nmust be selected before substitution. Endpoints require particular care: \\(x<2\\) excludes \\(2\\) , whereas \\(x\\ge2\\) includes it. Piecewise models arise\nin dosing thresholds, billing schedules, and decision rules. Visual investigation. Sketch \\(f(x)=\\begin{cases}x+2,&x<1\\\\3,&x\\ge1\\end{cases}\\) and mark open or closed endpoints. Example 9: Threshold rule. Let \\(p(x)=\\begin{cases}2x,&0\\le\nx<5\\\\10+1.5(x-5),&x\\ge5.\\end{cases}\\) Find \\(p(4)\\) , \\(p(5)\\) , and \\(p(8)\\) . Worked solution. \\(p(4)=8\\) , \\(p(5)=10\\) , and \\(p(8)=10+1.5(3)=14.5\\) . Guided practice 9. Evaluate \\(f(2)\\) for \\(f(x)=x^2\\) when \\(x<2\\) and \\(f(x)=x+2\\) when \\(x\\ge2\\) ."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Even, odd, and symmetric\nfunctions", "url": "chapter04.html#even-odd-and-symmetric-functions", "text": "An even function satisfies \\(f(-x)=f(x)\\) and has symmetry about the\nvertical axis. An odd function satisfies \\(f(-x)=-f(x)\\) and has symmetry about the\norigin. The domain must itself be symmetric about zero for either\nproperty to hold globally. Many functions are neither even nor odd. Visual investigation. Plot \\(x^2\\) , \\(x^3\\) , and \\(x^2+x\\) on symmetric intervals. Predict the\nsymmetry algebraically before checking the graph. Example 10: Algebraic symmetry test. Classify \\(f(x)=x^4-2x^2\\) . Worked solution. \\(f(-x)=(-x)^4-2(-x)^2=x^4-2x^2=f(x)\\) , so \\(f\\) is even. Guided practice 10. Classify \\(x^5+x\\) as even, odd, or\nneither."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Mathematical modeling and\nlimitations", "url": "chapter04.html#mathematical-modeling-and-limitations", "text": "A useful model identifies input, output, units, domain, parameters,\nand assumptions. Fitting an equation to data is only one stage:\npredictions must be interpreted in context and checked for plausibility.\nInterpolation within observed conditions is usually more defensible than\nextrapolation far beyond them. Measurement uncertainty and omitted\nvariables may limit conclusions. Applied investigation. A clinic observes heart-rate\nrecovery after exercise. Discuss why time is a plausible input and heart\nrate an output, why negative time may be excluded, and why a simple\nlinear fit might fail over a long interval. Example 11: Interpreting a model. A temperature\nmodel is \\(T(t)=37+2e^{-0.5t}\\) for \\(t\\ge0\\) . Find \\(T(0)\\) and describe the long-term\nprediction. Worked solution. \\(T(0)=39^\\circ\\) C. As \\(t\\) grows, \\(e^{-0.5t}\\) approaches zero, so \\(T(t)\\) approaches \\(37^\\circ\\) C. This is a model prediction,\nnot a clinical guarantee. Guided practice 11. For \\(C(t)=12e^{-0.2t}\\) , explain\nthe meaning of \\(C(0)\\) and the domain \\(t\\ge0\\) ."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation: comparing function families", "url": "chapter04.html#visual-investigation-comparing-function-families", "text": "image Explore. Compare intercepts, signs, symmetry, and\naverage rate of change. Find the average rate on \\([0,1]\\) and \\([1,2]\\) for each curve. Explain why a\nconstant slope distinguishes the line from the other graphs."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation: secants approaching a tangent", "url": "chapter04.html#visual-investigation-secants-approaching-a-tangent", "text": "image The secant through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,4)\\) has slope \\(3\\) . The dashed line has slope \\(4\\) and touches the parabola at \\((2,4)\\) ; calculus will formalize the\nlimiting process behind such tangent slopes. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , the secant slope from \\(2\\) to \\(2+h\\) is \\(\\frac{(2+h)^2-4}{h}=4+h\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) . As \\(h\\) approaches zero, this approaches \\(4\\) ."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: function tables and graphs", "url": "chapter04.html#computational-laboratory-a-function-tables-and-graphs", "text": "Goal. Compare a linear and quadratic function using\nidentical input values. Predict the graph before running either program.\nExecute the R code first, then the Python code separately."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter04.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code x <- seq ( - 3 , 3 , by = 0.5 ) linear <- 2 * x + 1 quadratic <- x ^ 2 - 2 * x print ( data.frame (x, linear, quadratic)) plot (x, quadratic, type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , col= \"blue\" , xlab= \"Input x\" , ylab= \"Function value\" , main= \"Comparing function families\" ) lines (x, linear, lwd= 2 , col= \"red\" ) legend ( \"topleft\" , legend= c ( \"Quadratic\" , \"Linear\" ), col= c ( \"blue\" , \"red\" ), lty= 1 , lwd= 2 )"}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter04.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt x = np.arange( - 3 , 3.01 , 0.5 ) linear = 2 * x + 1 quadratic = x ** 2 - 2 * x for a, b, c in zip (x, linear, quadratic): print ( f\" { a :5.1f} { b :8.2f} { c :10.2f} \" ) plt.plot(x, quadratic, label = \"Quadratic\" ) plt.plot(x, linear, label = \"Linear\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Input x\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Function value\" ) plt.title( \"Comparing function families\" ) plt.legend() plt.grid( True ) plt.show() Interpretation. Where do the graphs intersect? Why\ncan a quadratic have two intersections with a line? Solve \\(x^2-2x=2x+1\\) algebraically and compare\nwith the graph."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: domains and piecewise models", "url": "chapter04.html#computational-laboratory-b-domains-and-piecewise-models", "text": "Goal. Avoid plotting invalid inputs and investigate\na threshold model."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter04.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code x <- seq ( - 4 , 8 , length.out= 500 ) y <- ifelse (x >= 2 & abs (x -5 ) > 1e-8 , sqrt ( pmax (x -2 , 0 )) / (x -5 ), NA_real_ ) plot (x, y, type= \"l\" , col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"f(x)\" , ylim= c ( - 4 , 4 )) abline ( v= 5 , lty= 2 , col= \"red\" )"}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter04.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt x = np.linspace( - 4 , 8 , 500 ) y = np.full_like(x, np.nan) valid = (x >= 2 ) & (np. abs (x - 5 ) > 1e-8 ) y[valid] = np.sqrt(x[valid] - 2 ) / (x[valid] - 5 ) plt.plot(x, y) plt.axvline( 5 , linestyle = \"--\" ) plt.ylim( - 4 , 4 ) plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) plt.ylabel( \"f(x)\" ) plt.grid( True ) plt.show() Interpretation. Explain why the graph begins at \\(x=2\\) and has a break at \\(x=5\\) . Explain why the numerical plot\ncannot replace the algebraic domain argument."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: average rates and calculus readiness", "url": "chapter04.html#computational-laboratory-c-average-rates-and-calculus-readiness", "text": "Goal. Observe secant slopes as the second point\napproaches the first."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter04.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code f <- function (x) x ^ 2 h <- c ( 1 , 0.5 , 0.1 , 0.01 , 0.001 ) slope <- ( f ( 2 + h) - f ( 2 )) / h print ( data.frame (h, slope))"}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter04.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code def f(x): return x ** 2 for h in [ 1 , 0.5 , 0.1 , 0.01 , 0.001 ]: slope = (f( 2 + h) - f( 2 )) / h print ( f\"h= { h :7.3f} ; secant slope= { slope :.5f} \" ) Interpretation. Predict the limiting slope and prove\nit by simplifying the difference quotient. Why must \\(h\\ne0\\) in the original expression?"}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended guided workshops", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-guided-workshops", "text": ""}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Workshop 1:\nMedication concentration and domain", "url": "chapter04.html#workshop-1-medication-concentration-and-domain", "text": "A model \\(C(t)=18/(t+2)\\) describes\nconcentration for \\(0\\le t\\le10\\) hours. Evaluate \\(C(0),C(2),C(10)\\) ;\nidentify the contextual domain and range; calculate the average rate\nover \\([0,2]\\) ; explain why the rate is\nnegative. Reflect. State which representation (table, graph, formula, or words) was most\nuseful for each part and why."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Workshop\n2: Temperature thresholds", "url": "chapter04.html#workshop-2-temperature-thresholds", "text": "A storage unit has temperature \\(T(t)=4+3\\sin(\\pi t/6)\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le12\\) . Determine \\(T(0),T(3),T(6),T(9)\\) and sketch the graph;\nidentify the maximum and minimum; explain the role of the time\ndomain. Reflect. State which representation (table, graph, formula, or words) was most\nuseful for each part and why."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Workshop 3: Piecewise\ntransport costs", "url": "chapter04.html#workshop-3-piecewise-transport-costs", "text": "A delivery service charges \\(F(d)=8+2d\\) for \\(0\\le d\\le5\\) and \\(F(d)=18+1.5(d-5)\\) for \\(d>5\\) . Evaluate \\(F(0),F(5),F(8)\\) , draw the graph, and\ndiscuss whether the model is continuous at \\(d=5\\) . Reflect. State which representation (table, graph, formula, or words) was most\nuseful for each part and why."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Workshop 4:\nFrom data to models", "url": "chapter04.html#workshop-4-from-data-to-models", "text": "A study records \\((t,y)=(0,10),(1,15),(2,22),(3,31)\\) .\nCompute first differences and second differences, propose a quadratic\nrule, verify it, and explain why the table alone cannot prove the rule\nis unique. Reflect. State which representation (table, graph, formula, or words) was most\nuseful for each part and why."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Workshop 5: A\ndifference-quotient preview", "url": "chapter04.html#workshop-5-a-difference-quotient-preview", "text": "For \\(f(x)=3x^2-2x\\) , derive \\([f(a+h)-f(a)]/h\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) . Simplify fully and explain what\nhappens as \\(h\\) becomes very\nsmall. Reflect. State which representation (table, graph, formula, or words) was most\nuseful for each part and why."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Chapter review exercises", "url": "chapter04.html#chapter-review-exercises", "text": "Instructions. Show reasoning, label axes and units\nwhen appropriate, and verify solutions using a second representation\nwhen possible. Determine whether \\(\\{(-1,3),(0,3),(2,4)\\}\\) is a function.\nExplain. Determine whether \\(\\{(-1,3),(-1,4),(2,4)\\}\\) is a\nfunction. Find the domain of \\(\\sqrt{2x-6}\\) . Find the domain of \\(1/(x^2-9)\\) . Find the domain of \\(\\sqrt{x+1}/(x-4)\\) . Find the domain of \\(\\sqrt{16-x^2}\\) . For \\(f(x)=2x^2-3x+1\\) , compute \\(f(-2)\\) and \\(f(1/2)\\) . For \\(f(x)=x^2+4x\\) , simplify \\(f(a+h)-f(a)\\) . Find the zeros and \\(y\\) -intercept of \\(f(x)=x^2-5x+6\\) . Determine the sign intervals of \\((x-4)(x+2)\\) . Explain why \\(x^2+y^2=16\\) does\nnot define \\(y\\) as a function of \\(x\\) . Identify the maximum or minimum of \\(f(x)=(x-3)^2+2\\) . Find the average rate of change of \\(x^2+2x\\) on \\([1,3]\\) . Find the average rate of change of \\(1/x\\) on \\([1,2]\\) . Classify \\(x^6+2\\) as even, odd,\nor neither. Classify \\(x^3-4x\\) as even,\nodd, or neither. For \\(f(x)=\\begin{cases}x+1,&x<0\\\\x^2+1,&x\\ge0\\end{cases}\\) ,\ncompute \\(f(-2),f(0),f(3)\\) . Construct a table for \\(y=3x-2\\) at \\(x=-2,-1,0,1,2\\) . A function has points \\((0,5),(1,8),(2,11)\\) . Suggest a linear rule\nand explain the assumption. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , find secant\nslopes on \\([1,2]\\) , \\([1,1.5]\\) , and \\([1,1.1]\\) . A clinic model is \\(H(t)=70+20e^{-t/3}\\) for \\(t\\ge0\\) . Interpret \\(H(0)\\) and the long-term\nprediction. Explain the difference between the domain of a formula and the\ncontextual domain of a model. Give an example of a function that has two different inputs with\nthe same output. Give an example of a relation that fails the vertical-line\ntest. Explain why a table of five observations cannot establish a\nunique formula. For \\(g(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}\\) ,\ndetermine the domain and range. For \\(f(x)=|x-2|\\) , identify the\nminimum and intervals of decrease/increase. For \\(f(x)=2x+3\\) , show\nalgebraically that the average rate of change is always \\(2\\) . Describe a plausible real-world situation modeled by a piecewise\nfunction. Write a short explanation of how a secant slope leads toward the\nderivative concept."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter04.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "Simplify \\(\\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) and explain why direct substitution \\(h=0\\) is initially invalid. A position model is \\(s(t)=t^2+3t\\) . Find its average velocity\nfrom \\(t=1\\) to \\(t=3\\) . Describe the difference between average rate of change over an\ninterval and instantaneous rate of change at one input. Sketch a function with a jump at \\(x=2\\) . Explain why a graph can communicate\ninformation that a table might miss. Explain why understanding domain restrictions matters before\nstudying limits and derivatives."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Further\ninvestigations and mathematical reasoning", "url": "chapter04.html#further-investigations-and-mathematical-reasoning", "text": "These investigations extend the chapter beyond routine substitution.\nThey are suitable for collaborative classroom work, written homework, or\ncomputational follow-up."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation 1: Input-output reasoning and common misconceptions", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-investigation-1-input-output-reasoning-and-common-misconceptions", "text": "For each relation, explain whether the rule is single-valued, whether\ntwo inputs can share an output, and whether the stated domain changes\nthe conclusion: (a) \\(y=|x|\\) on all\nreal numbers; (b) \\(y=\\pm\\sqrt{x}\\) for \\(x\\ge0\\) ; (c) \\(y=1/x\\) for \\(x\\ne0\\) ; (d) \\(y=\\sqrt{x^2}\\) for all real \\(x\\) . In case (d), note that \\(\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|\\) , not always \\(x\\) . Construct a table for \\(x=-2,-1,0,1,2\\) and compare the two\nformulas. Reasoning prompt. A student says that \\(f(2)=f(-2)\\) proves that \\(f\\) is not a function. Explain the error by\nreferring to the definition. Write your explanation as if addressing a\nclassmate. Write and explain. Record your calculations, a sketch or table where appropriate, and a\ntwo- to three-sentence interpretation. Compare the strengths and\nlimitations of the representations you used."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation 2: Domains from combined operations", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-investigation-2-domains-from-combined-operations", "text": "Consider \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{6-x}\\) and \\(g(x)=1/(x+2)\\) . Find the domains of \\(f+g\\) , \\(fg\\) , and \\(f/g\\) . Explain why the domain of \\(f/g\\) must also exclude inputs at which \\(g(x)=0\\) . For these particular\nformulas, \\(g(x)\\) never equals zero on\nits domain, so all three combined expressions have domain \\((-\\infty,-2)\\cup(-2,6]\\) . Extension. Replace \\(g(x)\\) with \\(h(x)=x-1\\) and find the domain of \\(f/h\\) . Contrast the excluded input \\(x=1\\) with the square-root endpoint \\(x=6\\) . Write and explain. Record your calculations, a sketch or table where appropriate, and a\ntwo- to three-sentence interpretation. Compare the strengths and\nlimitations of the representations you used."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation 3: Recovering a formula from a graph", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-investigation-3-recovering-a-formula-from-a-graph", "text": "A line crosses the vertical axis at \\((0,4)\\) and passes through \\((3,10)\\) . Use the slope formula to derive \\(f(x)=2x+4\\) . Then identify the zero,\ncalculate \\(f(5)\\) , and interpret the\nline as a possible calibration equation with appropriate units. A parabola has vertex \\((1,-4)\\) and\npasses through \\((3,4)\\) . Use vertex\nform \\(g(x)=a(x-1)^2-4\\) to find \\(a\\) . Since \\(4=4a-4\\) , we obtain \\(a=2\\) . Verify that \\(g(x)=2(x-1)^2-4\\) has the stated vertex and\npoint. Discuss how a graph provides structural clues but may not supply\nexact coordinates when drawn by hand. Write and explain. Record your calculations, a sketch or table where appropriate, and a\ntwo- to three-sentence interpretation. Compare the strengths and\nlimitations of the representations you used."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation 4: Average rates in a changing process", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-investigation-4-average-rates-in-a-changing-process", "text": "Suppose the distance traveled by a vehicle is \\(s(t)=5t^2\\) meters for \\(0\\le t\\le4\\) seconds. Find average velocity\non \\([0,1]\\) , \\([1,2]\\) , \\([2,3]\\) , and \\([3,4]\\) . The results are \\(5,15,25,35\\) m/s. Explain why a single\nconstant-slope line would not describe the entire motion. Investigation. Plot the four secant lines or draw\ntheir rise/run triangles. Describe the pattern in the slopes. Then\ncompute the slope from \\(t=2\\) to \\(t=2+h\\) and simplify to \\(20+5h\\) . This expression suggests a\nlimiting slope of \\(20\\) m/s as \\(h\\) approaches zero, preparing the way for\nthe derivative. Write and explain. Record your calculations, a sketch or table where appropriate, and a\ntwo- to three-sentence interpretation. Compare the strengths and\nlimitations of the representations you used."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation 5: Graphical resolution and numerical sampling", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-investigation-5-graphical-resolution-and-numerical-sampling", "text": "A computer plot is made by evaluating a function at a finite\ncollection of input values and joining the resulting points. For a\nrapidly oscillating function or a function with a narrow discontinuity,\na coarse grid can conceal important behavior. Compare sampling \\(f(x)=\\sin(10x)\\) on \\([0,2\\pi]\\) at 10 equally spaced points and\nat 500 points. Explain why a visually smooth line drawn through too few\nsamples may misrepresent the underlying function. Discussion. How could you detect a possible hole or\nasymptote by checking the symbolic formula, rather than relying solely\non plotting? What would happen if the graphing software automatically\nconnected points on opposite sides of an undefined input? Write and explain. Record your calculations, a sketch or table where appropriate, and a\ntwo- to three-sentence interpretation. Compare the strengths and\nlimitations of the representations you used."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation 6: Interpreting a real data table", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-investigation-6-interpreting-a-real-data-table", "text": "A health-science team records the following illustrative values for a\nrecovery index after an intervention: at 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4 hours, the\nindex is 82, 75, 70, 67, and 65. Construct a labeled table and\nscatterplot. Calculate the successive hourly changes and explain why a\nlinear model may be a poor long-term description. Modeling questions. Is the input continuous even\nthough measurements were taken only at whole hours? Does the observed\ntable establish that the index will eventually reach zero? What\nadditional evidence would you want before using the model for\npatient-level decisions? Explain why the values should be treated as\nillustrative rather than clinical recommendations. Write and explain. Record your calculations, a sketch or table where appropriate, and a\ntwo- to three-sentence interpretation. Compare the strengths and\nlimitations of the representations you used."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation 7: Comparing exact and approximate answers", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-investigation-7-comparing-exact-and-approximate-answers", "text": "The zeros of \\(f(x)=x^2-2\\) are\nexactly \\(\\pm\\sqrt2\\) , whereas a\nnumerical plot may suggest approximately \\(\\pm1.414\\) . Verify by substitution that the\nexact answers satisfy the equation. Explain why rounding a root before\nsubstitution can create a small residual. Investigation. Estimate the positive root using the\ntable \\(x=1.4,1.41,1.414,1.415,1.42\\) .\nCompute \\(x^2-2\\) for each. Use the\nsign change to bracket the root, and explain how this idea relates to\nthe bisection method introduced in Chapter 2. Write and explain. Record your calculations, a sketch or table where appropriate, and a\ntwo- to three-sentence interpretation. Compare the strengths and\nlimitations of the representations you used."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation 8: Piecewise endpoints and continuity intuition", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-investigation-8-piecewise-endpoints-and-continuity-intuition", "text": "Compare \\(f(x)=\\begin{cases}2x+1,&x<2\\\\5,&x\\ge2\\end{cases}\\) and \\(g(x)=\\begin{cases}2x+1,&x<2\\\\6,&x\\ge2\\end{cases}\\) .\nEvaluate each at \\(x=2\\) , and compute\nthe value approached from inputs just below \\(2\\) . In the first model, both sides meet at\nheight \\(5\\) ; in the second, the rule\njumps from a value near \\(5\\) to \\(6\\) . Visual investigation. Draw open and filled circles\nat the endpoints and explain their meanings. State, without formal limit\nnotation, what it means for a graph to be unbroken at an input. Write and explain. Record your calculations, a sketch or table where appropriate, and a\ntwo- to three-sentence interpretation. Compare the strengths and\nlimitations of the representations you used."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\ninvestigation 9: Parameter sensitivity in function models", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-investigation-9-parameter-sensitivity-in-function-models", "text": "Consider the family \\(f_a(x)=ax^2+1\\) for \\(a=-2,-1,0,1,2\\) . Sketch all five graphs on\nthe same axes. Describe the effects of the sign and magnitude of \\(a\\) . For \\(a=0\\) , the model becomes constant; for\npositive \\(a\\) , the vertex is a\nminimum; for negative \\(a\\) , it is a\nmaximum. Applied reflection. Parameters are not always\nmeasured without error. If \\(a\\) is\nestimated from data, how might uncertainty in \\(a\\) affect predictions at large \\(|x|\\) ? Compare the impact at \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=10\\) by calculating the difference\nbetween models with \\(a=1\\) and \\(a=1.1\\) . Write and explain. Record your calculations, a sketch or table where appropriate, and a\ntwo- to three-sentence interpretation. Compare the strengths and\nlimitations of the representations you used."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Guided-practice checks\nand explanations", "url": "chapter04.html#guided-practice-checks-and-explanations", "text": "The repeated input \\(1\\) has two\ndifferent outputs, \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) , so the relation is not a function. The\nfailure concerns repeated inputs, not repeated outputs. Require \\(x+4\\ge0\\) and \\(x\\ne2\\) . Thus \\([-4,2)\\cup(2,\\infty)\\) . \\(f(a+h)=(a+h)^2-2(a+h)=a^2+2ah+h^2-2a-2h\\) .\nExpanding first prevents common substitution errors. At any \\(x>-1\\) , the relation\ngives two values \\(y=\\pm\\sqrt{x+1}\\) ,\nso it is not single-valued. Restricting to \\(y\\ge0\\) gives a function. \\(x^2-4x=x(x-4)\\) vanishes at \\(0\\) and \\(4\\) and is negative for \\(0<x<4\\) . The vertex is \\((-1,-3)\\) ; the\nparabola decreases on \\((-\\infty,-1)\\) and increases on \\((-1,\\infty)\\) . \\([f(5)-f(2)]/(5-2)=(25-4)/3=7\\) . The table values are \\(-1,3,7,11\\) ; the graph is a line with slope \\(4\\) and intercept \\(-1\\) . The input \\(2\\) belongs to the\nsecond branch, so \\(f(2)=2+2=4\\) . \\(f(-x)=(-x)^5+(-x)=-(x^5+x)\\) ,\nso the function is odd. \\(C(0)=12\\) is the initial\nconcentration in the model’s units; \\(t\\ge0\\) excludes times before the chosen\nstarting event. Self-assessment. Identify any errors you made in the original practice set. For each,\nwrite whether the cause was an algebraic manipulation, a domain\nrestriction, a graph-reading issue, or a conceptual misunderstanding.\nCorrect the reasoning rather than merely copying the numerical\nanswer."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Cumulative\napplication: interpreting a function from four viewpoints", "url": "chapter04.html#cumulative-application-interpreting-a-function-from-four-viewpoints", "text": "A laboratory device is calibrated with the illustrative model \\(S(c)=2c+5\\) for concentrations \\(0\\le c\\le12\\) mg/L, where \\(S\\) is a signal in arbitrary instrument\nunits. Work through the following stages before consulting software. Verbal: Explain what the input, output, slope,\nand intercept mean. Distinguish the physical interpretation of a signal\nintercept from a concentration intercept. Tabular: Construct a table for \\(c=0,2,4,6,8,10,12\\) and identify the\nconstant change in signal per 2 mg/L. Graphical: Plot the ordered pairs and draw the\nline segment over the stated domain. Label both axes with units. Mark\nthe endpoints as included. Symbolic: Solve \\(S(c)=17\\) and interpret the solution. Find\nthe average rate of change from \\(c=2\\) to \\(c=8\\) . Critical reasoning: A student uses the formula\nto predict the signal at \\(c=50\\) .\nExplain why the arithmetic is possible but the scientific interpretation\nis uncertain. Computational: Reproduce the graph using either\nR or Python. Compare your plot with the hand-drawn graph, and verify\nthat the code respects the restricted domain. Solution outline. The slope is \\(2\\) signal units per\nmg/L and the intercept is \\(5\\) signal\nunits at zero concentration. The requested signal \\(17\\) occurs at \\(c=6\\) mg/L. The average rate over \\([2,8]\\) is \\(2\\) signal units per mg/L. A prediction at \\(c=50\\) is extrapolation outside the\ncalibration interval, so it needs independent validation."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Extended\napplication: Water tank monitoring", "url": "chapter04.html#extended-application-water-tank-monitoring", "text": "A tank initially contains 20 liters. It fills at 4 liters per minute\nuntil it reaches 60 liters, then stays at that level. The quantities in\nthis investigation are illustrative rather than measured\nobservations."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter04.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[V(t)=\\min(20+4t,60).\\] Before calculating,\nidentify the input and output, their units, and the interval on which\nthe model is being studied. Distinguish the mathematical domain from the\npractical domain. The numerical study uses inputs from \\(0\\) to \\(20\\) . Example 12: Analytical investigation. Write the\npiecewise formula and find the average rate on \\([5,15]\\) . Identify the range. Worked solution. The transition is at 10 min. The\nrate is \\((60-40)/10=2\\) L/min; range\nis \\([20,60]\\) ."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter04.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Time (min) Volume (L) 0 20 5 40 10 60 15 60 20 60 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter04.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Water tank monitoring # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 20 , length.out = 301 ) y <- pmin ( 20 + 4 * x, 60 ) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Time (min)\" , ylab = \"Volume (L)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter04.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Water tank monitoring import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 20 , 301 ) y = np.minimum( 20 + 4 * x, 60 ) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Time (min)\" , ylabel = \"Volume (L)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter04.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Water tank monitoring", "url": "chapter04.html#chapter-project-investigating-water-tank-monitoring", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter04.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations", "section": "Chapter summary", "url": "chapter04.html#chapter-summary", "text": "A function assigns one output to each input in its domain. Its\nequation, table, graph, and verbal description are complementary\nrepresentations. Domain and range, intercepts, signs, symmetry,\nmonotonicity, and piecewise behavior describe different aspects of the\nsame relationship. Average rate of change measures secant slope and\nprovides the conceptual bridge to differential calculus. R and Python\nsupport exploration, but mathematical justification remains\nessential."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "url": "chapter05.html#transformations-compositions-and-inverse-functions", "text": ""}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Opening\ninvestigation: How do mathematical models change?", "url": "chapter05.html#opening-investigation-how-do-mathematical-models-change", "text": "Suppose a laboratory instrument reports a measurement with a fixed\nbaseline offset. Another laboratory measures the same quantity using a\ndifferent scale. These changes correspond to vertical translations and\nstretches of a function. When a model uses the output of one calculation\nas the input to another, we compose functions; when we recover an input\nfrom a measured output, we use an inverse. These operations are central\nto scientific modeling and later calculus."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter05.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "After completing this chapter, you should be able to describe and\ngraph translations, reflections, and stretches; map points under\ncombined transformations; combine functions while respecting domains;\nconstruct and interpret compositions; determine domains of compositions;\ntest whether a function is one-to-one; find, verify, and graph inverses;\nand implement these ideas using R and Python."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Prerequisite check", "url": "chapter05.html#prerequisite-check", "text": "Before proceeding, review function notation, domains and ranges,\ngraphing, and slope from Chapters 3–4. Evaluate \\(f(-2)\\) for \\(f(x)=x^2+1\\) ; state the domain of \\(\\sqrt{x-3}\\) ; and identify the vertex of \\((x-2)^2+5\\) . Answers: \\(5\\) , \\([3,\\infty)\\) , and \\((2,5)\\) ."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Chapter roadmap", "url": "chapter05.html#chapter-roadmap", "text": "Transformations change a known function; arithmetic combines two\nfunctions; composition chains them; inversion reverses a one-to-one\nrelationship. Each operation changes what inputs are allowed and what\noutputs mean."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "From parent functions to\nfamilies", "url": "chapter05.html#from-parent-functions-to-families", "text": "A parent function provides a familiar reference graph. Common parents\ninclude \\(f(x)=x\\) , \\(x^2\\) , \\(|x|\\) , \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) , \\(1/x\\) , and \\(x^3\\) . Transformations create related\ngraphs without plotting every point from scratch. The essential skill is\nto distinguish a change to the input from a change to the output . A change to the output affects vertical coordinates; a\nchange to the input affects horizontal coordinates. Investigation. Starting from \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , list the points \\((-2,4),(-1,1),(0,0),(1,1),(2,4)\\) . Predict\nthe points on \\(y=f(x)+3\\) and \\(y=f(x-3)\\) before sketching. Example 1: Parent-function recognition. Identify the\nparent of \\(g(x)=\\sqrt{x+2}-1\\) and\ndescribe its starting point. Worked solution. The parent is \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\) . Moving left two and down\none sends the starting point \\((0,0)\\) to \\((-2,-1)\\) . Guided practice 1. Identify the parent and describe \\(y=|x-4|+2\\) . Explain each step, and check\nyour result against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Vertical translations", "url": "chapter05.html#vertical-translations", "text": "For \\(g(x)=f(x)+k\\) , every point \\((x,y)\\) on the graph of \\(f\\) becomes \\((x,y+k)\\) on the graph of \\(g\\) . A positive \\(k\\) shifts upward and a negative \\(k\\) shifts downward. The domain remains the\nsame, while the range is shifted by \\(k\\) . In a measurement model, a vertical\nshift can represent a baseline correction or a constant offset. Investigation. Compare \\(x^2\\) , \\(x^2+2\\) , and \\(x^2-3\\) on the same axes. Track the vertex\nand the minimum output. Example 2: Baseline adjustment. A sensor model is \\(S(t)=2t+5\\) . After calibration, every\nreading increases by \\(1.5\\) units.\nFind the adjusted model. Worked solution. \\(A(t)=S(t)+1.5=2t+6.5\\) . The slope remains \\(2\\) because only the baseline\nchanges. Guided practice 2. Find the vertex and range of \\(y=(x+1)^2-4\\) . Explain each step, and check\nyour result against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Horizontal translations", "url": "chapter05.html#horizontal-translations", "text": "For \\(g(x)=f(x-h)\\) , the graph\nshifts \\(h\\) units to the right. The\napparent reversal is explained by solving \\(x-h=a\\) : to obtain the original output \\(f(a)\\) , the new input must be \\(x=a+h\\) . Thus a point \\((a,f(a))\\) moves to \\((a+h,f(a))\\) . If \\(h<0\\) , the shift is left. Domain\nrestrictions move with the graph. Investigation. Compare \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) , \\(\\sqrt{x-4}\\) , and \\(\\sqrt{x+2}\\) ; write each domain before\ngraphing. Example 3: Shift and domain. Find the domain and\nvertex of \\(g(x)=(x-4)^2+1\\) . Worked solution. Every real input is allowed, so the\ndomain is \\(\\mathbb R\\) . The vertex\nmoves from \\((0,0)\\) to \\((4,1)\\) . Guided practice 3. Find the domain of \\(y=\\sqrt{x+5}\\) .\nExplain each step, and check your result against the graph when\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Reflections across\ncoordinate axes", "url": "chapter05.html#reflections-across-coordinate-axes", "text": "The graph of \\(g(x)=-f(x)\\) is the\nreflection of \\(f\\) across the\nhorizontal axis: \\((x,y)\\) becomes \\((x,-y)\\) . The graph of \\(h(x)=f(-x)\\) reflects across the vertical\naxis: \\((x,y)\\) becomes \\((-x,y)\\) . For even functions, horizontal\nreflection produces the same graph; for odd functions, reflecting\nhorizontally or vertically produces the same result. Investigation. Compare \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) , \\(-\\sqrt{x}\\) , and \\(\\sqrt{-x}\\) . Explain their different\ndomains and ranges. Example 4: Reflection and domain. Describe \\(g(x)=\\sqrt{-x}\\) and determine its\ndomain. Worked solution. Reflect \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) across the vertical axis. The\nradicand requires \\(-x\\ge0\\) , hence \\(x\\le0\\) . The range is \\([0,\\infty)\\) . Guided practice 4. Compare the graphs of \\(y=-|x|\\) and \\(y=|-x|\\) . Explain each step, and\ncheck your result against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Vertical stretching and\ncompression", "url": "chapter05.html#vertical-stretching-and-compression", "text": "For \\(g(x)=af(x)\\) , each output is\nmultiplied by \\(a\\) . If \\(|a|>1\\) , vertical distances from the\nhorizontal axis increase; if \\(0<|a|<1\\) , they decrease. A negative \\(a\\) also reflects across the\nhorizontal axis. Vertical scaling changes units and magnitude, but it\ndoes not change the allowable input values. Investigation. Compare \\(x^2\\) , \\(3x^2\\) , and \\(\\frac14x^2\\) . For each, find the output at \\(x=2\\) and discuss which graph appears\nnarrower. Example 5: Scaled quadratic. Describe \\(g(x)=-2(x-1)^2+3\\) and find its\nmaximum. Worked solution. Shift right one, stretch vertically\nby factor two, reflect across the horizontal axis, and shift up three.\nThe vertex \\((1,3)\\) is a maximum. Guided practice 5. Find the vertex and maximum of \\(y=-3(x-2)^2+7\\) . Explain each step, and\ncheck your result against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Horizontal stretching and\ncompression", "url": "chapter05.html#horizontal-stretching-and-compression", "text": "For \\(g(x)=f(bx)\\) with \\(b\\ne0\\) , a point \\((a,f(a))\\) moves to \\((a/b,f(a))\\) . Thus the horizontal scale\nfactor is \\(1/|b|\\) , not \\(|b|\\) . When \\(|b|>1\\) the graph is compressed\nhorizontally; when \\(0<|b|<1\\) it\nis stretched. A negative \\(b\\) also\nreflects across the vertical axis. Investigation. Compare \\(|x|\\) , \\(|2x|\\) , and \\(|x/2|\\) . Explain why the scale factor\nappears reciprocal. Example 6: Horizontal scale. Describe \\(g(x)=\\sqrt{3x}\\) and find its domain. Worked solution. The graph of \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) is compressed horizontally by\nfactor \\(1/3\\) . Since \\(3x\\ge0\\) , its domain remains \\([0,\\infty)\\) . Guided practice 6. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , compare \\(f(3x)\\) and \\(3f(x)\\) . Explain each step, and check your\nresult against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Combining\ntransformations systematically", "url": "chapter05.html#combining-transformations-systematically", "text": "A useful general form is \\(g(x)=a\nf(b(x-h))+k\\) . Begin by identifying the reference graph and the\nroles of \\(a,b,h,k\\) . The point-mapping\nformula is \\((u,v)\\mapsto(h+u/b,\\,k+av)\\) for \\(b\\ne0\\) . This formula handles reflections,\nstretches, and translations simultaneously and avoids ambiguity about\nthe order of operations. Always check the transformed domain. Investigation. Use the points \\((0,0),(1,1),(4,2)\\) on \\(y=\\sqrt{x}\\) to generate points on \\(g(x)=-2\\sqrt{3(x-1)}+4\\) . Example 7: Point mapping. If \\((2,5)\\) is on \\(y=f(x)\\) , find the corresponding point on \\(y=3f(2(x-4))-1\\) . Worked solution. Solve \\(2(x-4)=2\\) to obtain \\(x=5\\) . The output is \\(3(5)-1=14\\) , so the new point is \\((5,14)\\) . Guided practice 7. Map \\((2,3)\\) under \\(y=-2f(4(x-1))+5\\) . Explain each step, and\ncheck your result against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Function arithmetic and\ndomains", "url": "chapter05.html#function-arithmetic-and-domains", "text": "Given functions \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) , define \\((f+g)(x)=f(x)+g(x)\\) , \\((fg)(x)=f(x)g(x)\\) , and \\((f/g)(x)=f(x)/g(x)\\) wherever both original\nexpressions are defined. Division additionally requires \\(g(x)\\ne0\\) . The domain of a combination is\ntherefore the intersection of the original domains, with further\nexclusions for division. Simplifying an expression does not restore\nexcluded inputs. Investigation. Let \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}\\) and \\(g(x)=1/(x-3)\\) . Determine the domains of \\(f+g\\) , \\(fg\\) , and \\(f/g\\) . Example 8: A removable-looking restriction. Let \\(f(x)=x^2-1\\) and \\(g(x)=x-1\\) . Find \\((f/g)(x)\\) and its domain. Worked solution. For \\(x\\ne1\\) , \\((f/g)(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1)=x+1\\) . The original\nquotient is undefined at \\(x=1\\) , so\nthe domain excludes \\(1\\) . Guided practice 8. If \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\) and \\(g(x)=x-2\\) , determine the domain of \\(f/g\\) . Explain each step, and check your\nresult against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Composition of functions", "url": "chapter05.html#composition-of-functions", "text": "The composition \\((f\\circ\ng)(x)=f(g(x))\\) means apply \\(g\\) first, then use its output as the input\nto \\(f\\) . The order matters: \\(f\\circ g\\) and \\(g\\circ f\\) generally differ. A composition\nis defined only where \\(x\\) belongs to\nthe domain of \\(g\\) and \\(g(x)\\) belongs to the domain of \\(f\\) . This input-output chain is useful for\nmultistage models, such as converting elapsed time into dosage and then\ndosage into predicted response. Investigation. Draw an input-output diagram for \\(g(x)=2x+1\\) and \\(f(x)=x^2\\) ; compare \\(f(g(2))\\) with \\(g(f(2))\\) . Example 9: Two orders of composition. For \\(f(x)=x^2+1\\) and \\(g(x)=3x-2\\) , compute both compositions. Worked solution. \\((f\\circ\ng)(x)=(3x-2)^2+1=9x^2-12x+5\\) . \\((g\\circ f)(x)=3(x^2+1)-2=3x^2+1\\) . Guided practice 9. For \\(f(x)=2x+1\\) and \\(g(x)=x^2\\) , compute \\((f\\circ g)(-2)\\) and \\((g\\circ f)(-2)\\) . Explain each step, and\ncheck your result against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Domains of composite\nfunctions", "url": "chapter05.html#domains-of-composite-functions", "text": "The domain of \\(f\\circ g\\) is \\(\\{x\\in\\operatorname{Dom}(g):g(x)\\in\\operatorname{Dom}(f)\\}\\) .\nA frequent mistake is to check only the inner function. For example, \\(\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\) requires \\(1-x^2\\ge0\\) , not merely that the polynomial \\(1-x^2\\) exists. If a denominator\noccurs in either stage, it must also be nonzero. Investigation. For \\(f(u)=\\sqrt{u}\\) and \\(g(x)=2-x\\) , identify the domain of \\(f\\circ g\\) and explain why \\(g\\circ f\\) has a different domain. Example 10: Composite domain. If \\(f(u)=1/(u-4)\\) and \\(g(x)=x^2\\) , find the domain of \\(f\\circ g\\) . Worked solution. \\((f\\circ\ng)(x)=1/(x^2-4)\\) , which requires \\(x\\ne-2\\) and \\(x\\ne2\\) . Guided practice 10. Find the domain of \\(\\sqrt{9-x^2}\\) .\nExplain each step, and check your result against the graph when\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "One-to-one\nfunctions and the horizontal-line test", "url": "chapter05.html#one-to-one-functions-and-the-horizontal-line-test", "text": "A function is one-to-one if different inputs always produce different\noutputs. Equivalently, \\(f(a)=f(b)\\) implies \\(a=b\\) . A graph passes the\nhorizontal-line test if every horizontal line intersects it at most\nonce. A one-to-one function can be reversed without ambiguity. The\nquadratic \\(x^2\\) fails this test on\nall real numbers, but it passes when its domain is restricted to \\([0,\\infty)\\) . Investigation. Compare \\(x^2\\) and \\(x^3\\) . Explain which has a unique input for\nevery output and how domain restriction changes the answer. Example 11: Restricting a domain. Is \\(f(x)=(x-2)^2\\) one-to-one on \\([2,\\infty)\\) ? Worked solution. Yes. On \\(x\\ge2\\) , the function is strictly\nincreasing, so no output is repeated. On all real numbers, it is not\none-to-one. Guided practice 11. Explain why \\(f(x)=|x|\\) is not\none-to-one on \\(\\mathbb R\\) . Explain\neach step, and check your result against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Inverse functions and their\nmeaning", "url": "chapter05.html#inverse-functions-and-their-meaning", "text": "If \\(f\\) is one-to-one, its inverse \\(f^{-1}\\) reverses the input-output\nrelationship: \\(f(a)=b\\) exactly when \\(f^{-1}(b)=a\\) . The domain of \\(f^{-1}\\) is the range of \\(f\\) , and the range of \\(f^{-1}\\) is the domain of \\(f\\) . The notation \\(f^{-1}\\) does not mean \\(1/f\\) . Graphs of inverse functions are\nreflections across \\(y=x\\) . Investigation. Make a table for \\(f(x)=2x+3\\) and reverse the input and\noutput columns. Plot both sets of points with \\(y=x\\) . Example 12: Inverse of an affine model. Find the\ninverse of \\(f(x)=5x-7\\) . Worked solution. Write \\(y=5x-7\\) , interchange variables to get \\(x=5y-7\\) , then solve \\(y=(x+7)/5\\) . Thus \\(f^{-1}(x)=(x+7)/5\\) . Guided practice 12. Find the inverse of \\(f(x)=3x+8\\) .\nExplain each step, and check your result against the graph when\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Finding and\nchecking inverses algebraically", "url": "chapter05.html#finding-and-checking-inverses-algebraically", "text": "To find an inverse, write \\(y=f(x)\\) , exchange \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) , solve for \\(y\\) , and record the necessary domain and\nrange restrictions. Verify by computing \\(f(f^{-1}(x))=x\\) and \\(f^{-1}(f(x))=x\\) on the appropriate\ndomains. If the original function is not one-to-one, first restrict its\ndomain to a suitable interval. Investigation. Derive the inverse of \\(f(x)=(x-1)^2\\) on \\([1,\\infty)\\) and test both\ncompositions. Example 13: Quadratic inverse. Find the inverse of \\(f(x)=(x+2)^2+1\\) on \\([-2,\\infty)\\) . Worked solution. From \\(y=(x+2)^2+1\\) , obtain \\(x=-2+\\sqrt{y-1}\\) using the nonnegative\nbranch. Hence \\(f^{-1}(x)=-2+\\sqrt{x-1}\\) , defined for \\(x\\ge1\\) . Guided practice 13. Find the inverse of \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}\\) and its domain. Explain\neach step, and check your result against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Inverse models, units,\nand interpretation", "url": "chapter05.html#inverse-models-units-and-interpretation", "text": "In applications, an inverse answers a different question from the\noriginal model. If a calibration equation converts concentration (mg/L)\ninto sensor signal (mV), the inverse converts signal back into\nconcentration. The physical domain and range must remain meaningful;\nalgebraic inverses can produce implausible predictions when used outside\nthe calibration interval. Investigation. A signal model is \\(S(c)=0.8c+2\\) for \\(0\\le c\\le20\\) . Find the range of \\(S\\) , its inverse, and the concentration\ncorresponding to a signal of \\(10\\) mV. Example 14: Calibration inverse. A sensor reads \\(S(c)=1.2c+0.5\\) . Estimate \\(c\\) when \\(S=8.9\\) . Worked solution. \\(c=(8.9-0.5)/1.2=7\\) . The numerical answer\nhas concentration units, assuming the model applies at that reading. Guided practice 14. Invert \\(S(c)=2.5c+1\\) and interpret \\(S^{-1}(11)\\) . Explain each step, and\ncheck your result against the graph when appropriate."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation I: the geometry of transformations", "url": "chapter05.html#visual-investigation-i-the-geometry-of-transformations", "text": "image Questions. Locate the vertex of each curve;\ncalculate the image of \\((1,1)\\) ;\ndistinguish a vertical stretch from a horizontal translation; and\nidentify how the minimum output changes. Explain which transformations\npreserve the domain and which alter the range."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation II: composition as a two-stage process", "url": "chapter05.html#visual-investigation-ii-composition-as-a-two-stage-process", "text": "image At \\(x=2\\) , the inner function\nreturns \\(5\\) and the outer returns \\(25\\) . Reversing the order gives \\(g(f(2))=9\\) . Draw a second flowchart for\nthe reverse order and discuss why order is mathematically\nconsequential."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation III: reflecting inverse graphs", "url": "chapter05.html#visual-investigation-iii-reflecting-inverse-graphs", "text": "image For each point \\((a,b)\\) on the\noriginal line, find \\((b,a)\\) on the\ninverse. Verify that the two graphs are mirror images across \\(y=x\\) . Explain how this reflection\nexchanges the domain and range."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation IV: why domain restriction matters", "url": "chapter05.html#visual-investigation-iv-why-domain-restriction-matters", "text": "image The horizontal line \\(y=4\\) intersects \\(y=x^2\\) twice. Restricting\nthe domain to \\(x\\ge0\\) removes the\nnegative branch and permits the inverse \\(f^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\) on \\(x\\ge0\\) . Why is \\(-\\sqrt{x}\\) the inverse for the restriction \\(x\\le0\\) instead?"}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: exploring transformations", "url": "chapter05.html#computational-laboratory-a-exploring-transformations", "text": "Graph a parent quadratic and a transformed quadratic. Compare\nvertices, reflection, and vertical scaling. Predict the output before\nrunning each program. Run the R code first, followed by Python."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter05.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code x <- seq ( - 4 , 6 , length.out= 500 ) f <- function (x) x ^ 2 plot (x, f (x), type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , col= \"blue\" , ylim= c ( - 4 , 12 ), xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"Output\" , main= \"Function transformations\" ) lines (x, - 2 * f (x -2 ) + 5 , col= \"red\" , lwd= 2 ) legend ( \"topright\" , c ( \"x^2\" , \"-2(x-2)^2+5\" ), col= c ( \"blue\" , \"red\" ), lty= 1 , lwd= 2 )"}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter05.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt x = np.linspace( - 4 , 6 , 500 ) f = lambda z: z ** 2 plt.plot(x, f(x), label = \"x^2\" ) plt.plot(x, - 2 * f(x - 2 ) + 5 , label = \"-2(x-2)^2+5\" ) plt.ylim( - 4 , 12 ) plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"Output\" ) plt.legend() ; plt.grid( True ) ; plt.show() Interpretation and\nreflection. Identify the maximum of the transformed graph. What happens if the\ncoefficient \\(-2\\) becomes \\(-1/2\\) ? Compare numerical output with your\nalgebraic reasoning."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: composition and valid domains", "url": "chapter05.html#computational-laboratory-b-composition-and-valid-domains", "text": "Construct \\(f(g(x))=\\sqrt{3-x^2}\\) and ensure the square root is evaluated only on valid inputs. Predict\nthe output before running each program. Run the R code first, followed\nby Python."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter05.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code f <- function (x) sqrt (x) g <- function (x) 3 - x ^ 2 x <- seq ( - 2 , 2 , length.out= 401 ) y <- rep ( NA_real_ , length (x)) valid <- g (x) >= 0 y[valid] <- f ( g (x[valid])) plot (x, y, type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , col= \"blue\" , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"f(g(x))\" , main= \"Composite function with restricted domain\" ) print ( c ( - sqrt ( 3 ), sqrt ( 3 )))"}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter05.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt x = np.linspace( - 2 , 2 , 401 ) g = lambda z: 3 - z ** 2 f = lambda u: np.sqrt(u) y = np.full_like(x, np.nan) valid = g(x) >= 0 y[valid] = f(g(x[valid])) plt.plot(x, y) plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"f(g(x))\" ) plt.grid( True ) ; plt.show() print ( \"Domain:\" , - np.sqrt( 3 ), np.sqrt( 3 )) Interpretation and\nreflection. Explain why the composite domain is \\([-\\sqrt3,\\sqrt3]\\) . Why does a plotting\nprogram need an explicit validity mask? Compare numerical output with\nyour algebraic reasoning."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: inverse calibration", "url": "chapter05.html#computational-laboratory-c-inverse-calibration", "text": "Convert concentration to signal, invert the model, and verify the\nround trip. Predict the output before running each program. Run the R\ncode first, followed by Python."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter05.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code signal <- function (c) 0.8 * c + 2 inverse <- function (s) (s -2 ) / 0.8 c <- seq ( 0 , 20 , length.out= 101 ) s <- signal (c) plot (c, s, type= \"l\" , col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 , xlab= \"Concentration (mg/L)\" , ylab= \"Signal (mV)\" ) print ( data.frame ( signal= c ( 2 , 6 , 10 , 18 ), concentration= inverse ( c ( 2 , 6 , 10 , 18 )))) stopifnot ( all ( abs ( inverse ( signal (c)) - c) < 1e-10 ))"}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter05.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt signal = lambda c: 0.8 * c + 2 inverse = lambda s: (s - 2 ) / 0.8 c = np.linspace( 0 , 20 , 101 ) plt.plot(c, signal(c)) plt.xlabel( \"Concentration (mg/L)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Signal (mV)\" ) ; plt.grid( True ) ; plt.show() for s in [ 2 , 6 , 10 , 18 ]: print ( f\"Signal { s :.1f} -> concentration { inverse(s) :.2f} \" ) assert np.allclose(inverse(signal(c)), c) Interpretation and\nreflection. Find the signal range for concentrations between \\(0\\) and \\(20\\) mg/L. Explain why extrapolating\noutside this interval needs validation. Compare numerical output with\nyour algebraic reasoning."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Workshop 1:\ntranslating a dose-response curve", "url": "chapter05.html#workshop-1-translating-a-dose-response-curve", "text": "A hypothetical response score is \\(R(d)=-(d-4)^2+16\\) for \\(0\\le d\\le8\\) . (a) Find the dose that\nmaximizes the score. (b) Define \\(A(d)=R(d)+3\\) and interpret the shift. (c)\nDefine \\(B(d)=R(d-1)\\) ; determine the\nmathematical domain inherited from the original model and explain why an\nextended physical domain would need justification. (d) Compare the two\nmodels at \\(d=4\\) . (e) Sketch the\ngraphs with labeled units. Guided solution. The vertex of \\(R\\) is \\((4,16)\\) , so the maximum is \\(16\\) at \\(d=4\\) . \\(A\\) adds three points to every output and\nhas maximum \\(19\\) . For \\(B\\) , the condition \\(0\\le d-1\\le8\\) gives \\(1\\le d\\le9\\) ; the curve shifts right one\nunit. \\(A(4)=19\\) and \\(B(4)=R(3)=15\\) . In practice, a transformed\nresponse curve is a modeling hypothesis, not evidence of treatment\neffectiveness."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Workshop 2:\ntwo-stage laboratory processing", "url": "chapter05.html#workshop-2-two-stage-laboratory-processing", "text": "A specimen concentration \\(c\\) (mg/L) produces a signal \\(g(c)=1.5c+2\\) mV. A second instrument\nreports an adjusted value \\(f(s)=0.4s-0.8\\) . (a) Write \\(f\\circ g\\) and simplify. (b) Compute the\nfinal report at \\(c=6\\) . (c) Reverse\nthe operations algebraically and compare \\(g\\circ f\\) . (d) Describe the units at each\nstage. (e) Explain why the two compositions are not interchangeable. Guided solution. \\(f(g(c))=0.4(1.5c+2)-0.8=0.6c\\) , so at \\(c=6\\) the report is \\(3.6\\) in the second instruments output\nunits. By contrast, \\(g(f(c))=1.5(0.4c-0.8)+2=0.6c+0.8\\) , a\ndifferent formula that may be physically meaningless if \\(f\\) expects a signal rather than a\nconcentration. Function order must respect input and output units."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Workshop\n3: recovering an input from a measured output", "url": "chapter05.html#workshop-3-recovering-an-input-from-a-measured-output", "text": "A calibration model is \\(S(c)=2+0.8c\\) for \\(0\\le c\\le20\\) , with signal \\(S\\) in mV and concentration \\(c\\) in mg/L. (a) Find the range of \\(S\\) . (b) Find \\(S^{-1}\\) . (c) Recover \\(c\\) when \\(S=11.6\\) . (d) Verify both compositions. (e)\nExplain why a reading of \\(25\\) mV\nshould not be used for unvalidated extrapolation. Guided solution. The range is \\([2,18]\\) mV. The inverse is \\(S^{-1}(s)=(s-2)/0.8\\) for \\(2\\le s\\le18\\) . Thus \\(S^{-1}(11.6)=12\\) mg/L. Algebraically, \\(S^{-1}(S(c))=c\\) for \\(c\\in[0,20]\\) and \\(S(S^{-1}(s))=s\\) for \\(s\\in[2,18]\\) . A signal of \\(25\\) lies outside the validated range."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Workshop 4: restricted\nquadratic inverses", "url": "chapter05.html#workshop-4-restricted-quadratic-inverses", "text": "A hypothetical cost model is \\(C(q)=(q-3)^2+5\\) . (a) Explain why it is not\none-to-one on \\(\\mathbb R\\) . (b)\nRestrict the domain to \\(q\\ge3\\) . (c)\nFind the inverse and its domain. (d) Find the input corresponding to\noutput \\(14\\) . (e) Sketch the original\nrestricted graph, inverse, and \\(y=x\\) . Guided solution. The unrestricted model gives equal\ncosts at \\(q=3-t\\) and \\(q=3+t\\) . On \\(q\\ge3\\) it is increasing. Solving \\(y=(q-3)^2+5\\) with \\(q\\ge3\\) gives \\(q=3+\\sqrt{y-5}\\) . Thus \\(C^{-1}(y)=3+\\sqrt{y-5}\\) for \\(y\\ge5\\) , and \\(C^{-1}(14)=6\\) ."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Workshop 5:\ndiagnosing transformation errors", "url": "chapter05.html#workshop-5-diagnosing-transformation-errors", "text": "A student claims that \\(f(2x)\\) stretches a graph horizontally by factor \\(2\\) , and that \\(f^{-1}(x)=1/f(x)\\) . (a) Use \\(f(x)=x^2\\) to disprove the first claim\nusing a point. (b) Use \\(f(x)=2x+1\\) to\ndisprove the second. (c) State the correct horizontal scaling rule. (d)\nFind and verify the actual inverse of \\(2x+1\\) . (e) Explain how graphing can help\ndetect both errors. Guided solution. The point \\((2,4)\\) on \\(y=x^2\\) corresponds to \\((1,4)\\) on \\(y=f(2x)\\) , so the graph compresses by\nfactor \\(1/2\\) . For \\(f(x)=2x+1\\) , \\(1/f(x)=1/(2x+1)\\) while \\(f^{-1}(x)=(x-1)/2\\) . Inverse graphs reflect\nacross \\(y=x\\) ; reciprocal graphs\ngenerally do not."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Chapter review: key\nideas and common errors", "url": "chapter05.html#chapter-review-key-ideas-and-common-errors", "text": "Operation Rule and interpretation Vertical shift \\(f(x)+k\\) : outputs change by \\(k\\) Horizontal shift \\(f(x-h)\\) : shift right by \\(h\\) Vertical scaling \\(af(x)\\) : output distances scaled by \\(|a|\\) Horizontal scaling \\(f(bx)\\) : input coordinates scaled by \\(1/|b|\\) Composition \\((f\\circ\ng)(x)=f(g(x))\\) ; apply \\(g\\) first Inverse \\(f^{-1}(f(x))=x\\) on the original\ndomain Common errors to avoid. Confusing \\(f(x-h)\\) with a left shift; treating \\(f(2x)\\) as a horizontal stretch by two;\ncomposing functions in the wrong order; forgetting domain restrictions;\nassuming every function has an inverse; confusing \\(f^{-1}(x)\\) with \\(1/f(x)\\) ; and using inverse models outside\nthe measured range."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Chapter exercises", "url": "chapter05.html#chapter-exercises", "text": "Complete the following exercises without technology first. Then use R\nor Python to verify selected graphs or calculations. Explain the domain\nof every function you construct."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Transformations and graph\ninterpretation", "url": "chapter05.html#transformations-and-graph-interpretation", "text": "Describe all transformations taking \\(y=x^2\\) to \\(y=3(x-2)^2-4\\) . State the vertex and\nrange. Graph \\(y=-|x+3|+2\\) using five\ntransformed points. State the maximum. Find the domain and range of \\(y=\\sqrt{x-5}+1\\) . Describe the transformation from \\(y=1/x\\) to \\(y=1/(x+2)-3\\) and identify both\nasymptotes. Compare \\(f(3x)\\) and \\(3f(x)\\) for \\(f(x)=x^2\\) . If \\((4,-2)\\) lies on \\(y=f(x)\\) , find its image on \\(y=-3f(2(x-1))+5\\) . Explain why the graph of \\(f(-x)\\) reflects across the vertical\naxis. Find the domain of \\(g(x)=2\\sqrt{4-x}-1\\) and state its\nendpoint. Determine whether \\(y=-2(x+1)^2+6\\) has a maximum or minimum,\nand find its value. For \\(f(x)=|x|\\) , compare \\(f(x-2)+1\\) and \\(f(x+1)-2\\) using vertices. Let \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\) and \\(g(x)=x-4\\) . Find the domain of \\(f+g\\) . For the same functions, determine the domain of \\(f/g\\) . Let \\(f(x)=x^2+1\\) and \\(g(x)=2x-3\\) . Find \\(f\\circ g\\) and \\(g\\circ f\\) . For \\(f(x)=1/(x-2)\\) and \\(g(x)=x^2\\) , determine the domain of \\(f\\circ g\\) . For \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}\\) and \\(g(x)=3-x\\) , determine the domain of \\(f\\circ g\\) . Explain in words why \\((f\\circ\ng)(x)\\) is generally not equal to \\((g\\circ f)(x)\\) . Find \\((f\\circ g)(2)\\) when \\(f(u)=u^3\\) and \\(g(x)=x-5\\) . If \\(f(x)=x+2\\) and \\(g(x)=1/x\\) , compute both compositions and\nstate their domains. Determine whether \\(f(x)=x^3-1\\) is one-to-one on \\(\\mathbb R\\) .\nJustify. Explain why \\(f(x)=x^2-4\\) is\nnot one-to-one on \\(\\mathbb\nR\\) . Find the inverse of \\(f(x)=4x-9\\) and verify by\ncomposition. Find the inverse of \\(f(x)=(x-1)/3\\) and state both\ndomains. Find the inverse of \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x+2}\\) and give its domain and\nrange. Find the inverse of \\(f(x)=(x-4)^2\\) on \\([4,\\infty)\\) . Find the inverse of \\(f(x)=(x-4)^2\\) on \\((-\\infty,4]\\) . Explain the\ndifference. A calibration function is \\(S(c)=0.5c+1\\) for \\(0\\le c\\le30\\) . Find its inverse and valid\nsignal range. Use the inverse from Exercise 26 to find the concentration at\nsignal \\(9\\) . For \\(f(x)=2x+3\\) , graph \\(f\\) , \\(f^{-1}\\) , and \\(y=x\\) on common axes. Explain why a horizontal line can intersect a graph twice even\nwhen it passes the vertical-line test. Find \\(f^{-1}(f(7))\\) for a\none-to-one \\(f\\) whose domain includes \\(7\\) . Explain without knowing the\nformula."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter05.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , simplify \\(\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) . Explain why the resulting\nexpression is useful when \\(h\\) becomes\nvery small. If \\(g(x)=f(3x-6)\\) , identify\nthe input to \\(f\\) and explain how the\ngraph changes. This anticipates the chain rule. A concentration model is \\(C(t)=e^{-0.2t}\\) for \\(t\\ge0\\) . Describe what an inverse function\nwould answer and why the output range matters. Show algebraically that \\(f^{-1}(f(x))=x\\) for \\(f(x)=5x+2\\) and discuss the meaning of\nreversing a model. Explain how restricting a quadratic to a monotonic interval makes\nan inverse possible and how this prepares for inverse trigonometric\nfunctions."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Looking ahead", "url": "chapter05.html#looking-ahead", "text": "Chapter 6 studies polynomial and rational functions in greater depth.\nTransformations will help us graph their families, composition will help\nus analyze complex formulas, and inverse-function reasoning will later\nconnect exponential and logarithmic functions and calculus."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Extended\napplication: Temperature sensor conversion", "url": "chapter05.html#extended-application-temperature-sensor-conversion", "text": "A sensor reports a raw voltage. The calibration first subtracts 0.5\nvolts and then multiplies by 20 to obtain a temperature in degrees\nCelsius. The quantities in this investigation are illustrative rather\nthan measured observations."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter05.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[T(v)=20(v-0.5).\\] Before calculating,\nidentify the input and output, their units, and the interval on which\nthe model is being studied. Distinguish the mathematical domain from the\npractical domain. The numerical study uses inputs from \\(0.5\\) to \\(3\\) . Example 15: Analytical investigation. Express the\ncalibration as a composition. Derive its inverse and find the voltage\nfor 30 degrees Celsius. Worked solution. Compose \\(g(v)=v-0.5\\) with \\(f(u)=20u\\) . The inverse is \\(v=T/20+0.5\\) , giving 2 V."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter05.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Voltage (V) Temperature (C) 0.5 0 1.12 12.5 1.75 25 2.38 37.5 3 50 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter05.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Temperature sensor conversion # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0.5 , 3 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 20 * (x -0.5 ) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Voltage (V)\" , ylab = \"Temperature (C)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter05.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Temperature sensor conversion import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0.5 , 3 , 301 ) y = 20 * (x - 0.5 ) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Voltage (V)\" , ylabel = \"Temperature (C)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter05.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Temperature sensor conversion", "url": "chapter05.html#chapter-project-investigating-temperature-sensor-conversion", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter05.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "url": "chapter06.html#polynomial-and-rational-functions", "text": ""}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Opening context:\ninterpreting nonlinear response", "url": "chapter06.html#opening-context-interpreting-nonlinear-response", "text": "A laboratory response may rise and then fall, or it may increase\nrapidly before leveling off. Polynomials provide flexible curves, while\nrational functions can model saturation and discontinuities. In this\nchapter, algebraic structure, graphical features, and computational\nchecks work together. The central questions are: Where is a model zero?\nWhere is it defined? How does it behave near a critical input or far\nfrom the origin? And what does that behavior mean in context?"}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter06.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "After completing this chapter, students should be able to classify\nand evaluate polynomials; determine end behavior; factor and solve\npolynomial equations; use synthetic division and the remainder and\nfactor theorems; interpret zeros and multiplicities; solve polynomial\ninequalities; determine domains and discontinuities of rational\nfunctions; locate holes and asymptotes; graph rational models; solve\nrational inequalities; and use R and Python to check algebraic and\ngraphical conclusions."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Prerequisite check", "url": "chapter06.html#prerequisite-check", "text": "Review factoring, quadratic equations, function domains,\ntransformations, and interval notation. Factor \\(x^2-9\\) ; solve \\((x-1)(x+4)=0\\) ; and state why \\(1/(x-2)\\) is undefined at \\(x=2\\) . Answers: \\((x-3)(x+3)\\) , \\(x=1,-4\\) , and division by zero is\nundefined."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Roadmap", "url": "chapter06.html#roadmap", "text": "We begin with polynomial structure and zeros, move from algebraic\nfactors to graphs and inequalities, then study rational functions and\ntheir excluded inputs, holes, asymptotes, and applications. Each step is\npaired with a visual or computational investigation."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Polynomials: structure\nand terminology", "url": "chapter06.html#polynomials-structure-and-terminology", "text": "A polynomial has the form \\(P(x)=a_nx^n+\\cdots+a_1x+a_0\\) , where \\(n\\) is a nonnegative integer, \\(a_n\\ne0\\) , and the coefficients are real.\nThe degree is \\(n\\) , the leading\ncoefficient is \\(a_n\\) , and \\(a_0\\) is the constant term. Polynomial\nfunctions are defined and continuous for every real input. A polynomial\nis not the same as a rational function: \\(x^{-1}\\) , \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) , and \\(1/(x-2)\\) are not polynomial terms. Example 1: Polynomials: structure and terminology. Classify \\(P(x)=4-3x^2+2x^5\\) by degree\nand leading coefficient. Worked solution. Rewriting in descending powers\ngives \\(2x^5-3x^2+4\\) . Its degree is \\(5\\) and leading coefficient is \\(2\\) . Guided practice 1. Classify \\(-7x^4+2x-9\\) and explain\nwhy \\(x^{1/2}+3\\) is not a polynomial.\nShow each algebraic step, check any excluded values, and interpret your\nconclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Polynomial\nevaluation and the remainder theorem", "url": "chapter06.html#polynomial-evaluation-and-the-remainder-theorem", "text": "Use substitution or Horner’s method to evaluate a polynomial.\nDividing \\(P(x)\\) by \\(x-c\\) produces a remainder equal to \\(P(c)\\) ; this is the remainder theorem.\nHorner’s method efficiently evaluates a polynomial while avoiding\nrepeated powers. For a model, always interpret the input units and the\nunits of the predicted output. Example 2: Polynomial evaluation and the remainder\ntheorem. Evaluate \\(P(x)=2x^3-3x^2+4x-5\\) at \\(x=2\\) . Worked solution. \\(P(2)=16-12+8-5=7\\) . Thus the remainder on\ndivision by \\(x-2\\) is \\(7\\) . Guided practice 2. Use the remainder theorem to find the remainder when \\(x^3-4x+1\\) is divided by \\(x+2\\) . Show each algebraic step, check any\nexcluded values, and interpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "End behavior and\nleading-term dominance", "url": "chapter06.html#end-behavior-and-leading-term-dominance", "text": "For sufficiently large positive or negative inputs, the leading term \\(a_nx^n\\) dominates lower-degree\nterms. Even-degree polynomials have ends pointing in the same direction;\nodd-degree polynomials have ends pointing in opposite directions. The\nsign of \\(a_n\\) determines whether the\nright end rises or falls. This is a statement about long-run behavior,\nnot necessarily the shape near the origin. Example 3: End behavior and leading-term dominance. Describe both ends of \\(P(x)=-2x^5+7x^2-1\\) . Worked solution. Because the leading term is \\(-2x^5\\) , \\(P(x)\\to+\\infty\\) as \\(x\\to-\\infty\\) and \\(P(x)\\to-\\infty\\) as \\(x\\to+\\infty\\) . Guided practice 3. Describe the end behavior of \\(3x^4-2x^3+1\\) and \\(-x^6+2x\\) . Show each algebraic step, check\nany excluded values, and interpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Zeros, factors, and\nthe zero-product property", "url": "chapter06.html#zeros-factors-and-the-zero-product-property", "text": "A real number \\(c\\) is a zero of \\(P\\) exactly when \\(P(c)=0\\) . By the factor theorem, this\nhappens exactly when \\(x-c\\) is a\nfactor. In a factored polynomial, apply the zero-product property: a\nproduct is zero if at least one factor is zero. A zero corresponds to an \\(x\\) -intercept of the graph. The\nnumber of distinct real zeros can be less than the degree. Example 4: Zeros, factors, and the zero-product\nproperty. Find the zeros of \\(P(x)=(x-2)(x+1)(x-4)\\) . Worked solution. The zeros are \\(2,-1,4\\) . Each gives an \\(x\\) -intercept \\((c,0)\\) . Guided practice 4. Find the zeros of \\(x(x-3)^2(x+5)\\) and list the distinct intercepts. Show each algebraic step, check any\nexcluded values, and interpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Multiplicity and\ncrossing versus touching", "url": "chapter06.html#multiplicity-and-crossing-versus-touching", "text": "If \\((x-c)^m\\) divides \\(P\\) but \\((x-c)^{m+1}\\) does not, \\(c\\) has multiplicity \\(m\\) . An odd multiplicity generally makes\nthe graph cross the horizontal axis; an even multiplicity makes it touch\nand turn. Higher multiplicities often flatten the graph near the\nintercept. Multiplicity counts toward the degree, whereas the number of\ndistinct intercepts does not. Example 5: Multiplicity and crossing versus\ntouching. Describe the graph near each zero of \\((x+2)^2(x-1)^3\\) . Worked solution. At \\(-2\\) (multiplicity \\(2\\) ), the graph touches and turns. At \\(1\\) (multiplicity \\(3\\) ), it crosses with flattening. Guided practice 5. Explain how \\(x^2(x-3)\\) behaves\nnear each zero. Show each algebraic step, check any excluded values, and\ninterpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Factoring and\nsolving polynomial equations", "url": "chapter06.html#factoring-and-solving-polynomial-equations", "text": "Useful techniques include common factors, grouping, difference of\nsquares, perfect-square trinomials, and substitution in quadratic form.\nAlways verify a factorization by multiplying it out. Some higher-degree\nequations cannot be solved by elementary factoring; numerical\napproximation and graphing then become useful, but an approximate root\nshould not be presented as exact. Example 6: Factoring and solving polynomial\nequations. Solve \\(x^4-5x^2+4=0\\) by substitution. Worked solution. Let \\(u=x^2\\) . Then \\((u-1)(u-4)=0\\) , so \\(x^2=1\\) or \\(x^2=4\\) . Hence \\(x=\\pm1,\\pm2\\) . Guided practice 6. Solve \\(2x^3-8x=0\\) and verify each\nsolution. Show each algebraic step, check any excluded values, and\ninterpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Polynomial division\nand synthetic division", "url": "chapter06.html#polynomial-division-and-synthetic-division", "text": "Long division expresses \\(P(x)=D(x)Q(x)+R(x)\\) with \\(\\deg R<\\deg D\\) . Synthetic division is a\nstreamlined method when the divisor is \\(x-c\\) . It is useful for testing candidate\nzeros and reducing the degree after one factor is known. The quotient\nand remainder must always satisfy the division identity. Example 7: Polynomial division and synthetic\ndivision. Divide \\(x^3-2x^2-5x+6\\) by \\(x-1\\) . Worked solution. Synthetic division with \\(1\\) and coefficients \\(1,-2,-5,6\\) yields quotient \\(x^2-x-6\\) and remainder \\(0\\) . Thus \\(P(x)=(x-1)(x-3)(x+2)\\) . Guided practice 7. Divide \\(x^3+2x^2-3x+4\\) by \\(x+1\\) and check the remainder. Show each\nalgebraic step, check any excluded values, and interpret your\nconclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Rational and complex zeros", "url": "chapter06.html#rational-and-complex-zeros", "text": "For an integer-coefficient polynomial, any rational zero in lowest\nterms \\(p/q\\) has numerator dividing\nthe constant term and denominator dividing the leading coefficient. This\ntheorem generates candidates, not guarantees. Complex nonreal zeros\noccur in conjugate pairs when the coefficients are real. A degree- \\(n\\) polynomial has exactly \\(n\\) complex zeros counting multiplicity\n(fundamental theorem of algebra). Example 8: Rational and complex zeros. List possible\nrational zeros of \\(2x^3-3x^2-8x+12\\) . Worked solution. Candidates are \\(\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm3,\\pm4,\\pm6,\\pm12\\) and \\(\\pm\\frac12,\\pm\\frac32\\) . Test candidates\nbefore claiming any are roots. Guided practice 8. List the possible rational zeros of \\(3x^3+2x^2-5x-2\\) . Show each algebraic step,\ncheck any excluded values, and interpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Polynomial graphs and\nturning points", "url": "chapter06.html#polynomial-graphs-and-turning-points", "text": "A degree- \\(n\\) polynomial has at\nmost \\(n-1\\) turning points. To sketch\na polynomial, identify the degree and leading coefficient, intercepts\nand multiplicities, and selected points between zeros. A graph is\nevidence for a conjecture, but algebra establishes exact intercepts and\nsigns. Graphing windows can hide distant roots or turning points. Example 9: Polynomial graphs and turning points. Sketch \\(P(x)=(x+1)(x-2)^2\\) using its\nzeros and leading term. Worked solution. The graph crosses at \\(-1\\) , touches at \\(2\\) , and rises to the right because the\nleading term is \\(x^3\\) . Its left end\nfalls. Guided practice 9. Sketch \\(-(x+2)(x-1)^2\\) and state\nhow it differs from the preceding example. Show each algebraic step,\ncheck any excluded values, and interpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Polynomial inequalities\nand sign charts", "url": "chapter06.html#polynomial-inequalities-and-sign-charts", "text": "To solve \\(P(x)>0\\) or \\(P(x)\\le0\\) , factor when possible and mark\nall real zeros on a number line. Test the sign on each interval. At a\nzero of odd multiplicity, the sign changes; at a zero of even\nmultiplicity, it does not. Include zeros for non-strict inequalities and\nexclude them for strict inequalities. Example 10: Polynomial inequalities and sign charts. Solve \\((x+2)(x-1)>0\\) . Worked solution. The sign is positive on \\((-\\infty,-2)\\) and \\((1,\\infty)\\) , so the solution is their\nunion. Guided practice 10. Solve \\(x(x-2)^2\\le0\\) with a sign\nchart. Show each algebraic step, check any excluded values, and\ninterpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Rational functions and\ndomains", "url": "chapter06.html#rational-functions-and-domains", "text": "A rational function has the form \\(R(x)=P(x)/Q(x)\\) , where \\(P,Q\\) are polynomials and \\(Q\\not\\equiv0\\) . Its domain excludes every\nreal zero of the original denominator. Even if a factor cancels\nalgebraically, the excluded input remains excluded: the simplified\nexpression agrees only where the original was defined. Distinguish an\nalgebraic simplification from an extension of a function. Example 11: Rational functions and domains. Find the\ndomain of \\(R(x)=(x^2-4)/(x^2-x-6)\\) . Worked solution. The denominator factors as \\((x-3)(x+2)\\) , so the domain is \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-2,3\\}\\) . Cancellation\nwith \\(x+2\\) does not restore \\(x=-2\\) . Guided practice 11. Find the domain of \\((x^2-9)/(x^2-5x+6)\\) . Show each algebraic\nstep, check any excluded values, and interpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Holes and vertical\nasymptotes", "url": "chapter06.html#holes-and-vertical-asymptotes", "text": "If a denominator factor cancels with the numerator, the original\nfunction may have a removable discontinuity, shown as a hole. A\ndenominator factor that remains after cancellation typically produces a\nvertical asymptote. To locate a hole, simplify and evaluate the\nsimplified expression at the excluded input. To analyze a vertical\nasymptote, examine the signs of factors on either side. Example 12: Holes and vertical asymptotes. Analyze \\(R(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1)\\) . Worked solution. For \\(x\\ne1\\) , \\(R(x)=x+1\\) . The graph is the line \\(y=x+1\\) with a hole at \\((1,2)\\) , not a vertical asymptote. Guided practice 12. Identify the hole and vertical asymptote of \\((x^2-4)/[(x-2)(x-3)]\\) . Show each algebraic\nstep, check any excluded values, and interpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Horizontal and slant\nasymptotes", "url": "chapter06.html#horizontal-and-slant-asymptotes", "text": "Compare the degrees of numerator and denominator. If the numerator\ndegree is smaller, the horizontal asymptote is \\(y=0\\) . If degrees are equal, it is the\nratio of leading coefficients. If the numerator degree is exactly one\ngreater, polynomial division gives a slant (oblique) asymptote. A\nhorizontal asymptote describes end behavior and can be crossed by the\ngraph. Example 13: Horizontal and slant asymptotes. Find\nthe horizontal asymptote of \\((3x^2+1)/(2x^2-5)\\) . Worked solution. Both degrees are \\(2\\) , so the horizontal asymptote is \\(y=3/2\\) . Guided practice 13. Find the end-behavior asymptote of \\((x^2+1)/(x-1)\\) using division. Show each\nalgebraic step, check any excluded values, and interpret your\nconclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Graphing rational\nfunctions systematically", "url": "chapter06.html#graphing-rational-functions-systematically", "text": "Start with the original domain, factor numerator and denominator,\nmark zeros and excluded inputs, classify holes and asymptotes, compute\nintercepts, and use interval sign tests. The behavior on either side of\na vertical asymptote may differ. Do not draw a continuous curve through\nan excluded input. Confirm with numerical tables close to the\ndiscontinuities. Example 14: Graphing rational functions\nsystematically. Sketch \\(R(x)=(x+1)/(x-2)\\) and identify its\nintercepts and asymptotes. Worked solution. The \\(x\\) -intercept is \\(-1\\) , the \\(y\\) -intercept is \\(-1/2\\) , the vertical asymptote is \\(x=2\\) , and the horizontal asymptote is \\(y=1\\) . Guided practice 14. Analyze \\(R(x)=(2x-4)/(x+3)\\) using\na domain, intercept, and asymptote checklist. Show each algebraic step,\ncheck any excluded values, and interpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Rational inequalities\nand critical points", "url": "chapter06.html#rational-inequalities-and-critical-points", "text": "To solve a rational inequality, bring everything to one side and\nidentify both zeros of the numerator and zeros of the denominator. Use a\nsign chart on the resulting intervals. Include numerator zeros when the\ninequality permits equality; never include denominator zeros.\nMultiplying an inequality by an expression of unknown sign can reverse\nthe inequality, so avoid that shortcut. Example 15: Rational inequalities and critical\npoints. Solve \\((x-1)/(x+2)\\ge0\\) . Worked solution. The critical points are \\(-2\\) (excluded) and \\(1\\) (included). The solution is \\((-\\infty,-2)\\cup[1,\\infty)\\) . Guided practice 15. Solve \\((x+3)/(x-2)<0\\) and\nexplain endpoint exclusions. Show each algebraic step, check any\nexcluded values, and interpret your conclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Applications:\nresponse, saturation, and limits", "url": "chapter06.html#applications-response-saturation-and-limits", "text": "Polynomial models can approximate a response over a bounded interval;\nrational models can represent saturation. For example \\(S(c)=120c/(8+c)\\) models a response\napproaching \\(120\\) as concentration \\(c\\) grows, with \\(c\\ge0\\) physically meaningful. The\nmathematical domain excludes \\(-8\\) ,\nbut the scientific domain is narrower. Interpret asymptotes in the units\nof the model and avoid extrapolating beyond the range supported by\nobservations. Example 16: Applications: response, saturation, and\nlimits. For \\(S(c)=120c/(8+c)\\) , find \\(S(8)\\) and the long-run response. Worked solution. \\(S(8)=60\\) . As \\(c\\to\\infty\\) , \\(S(c)\\to120\\) , a horizontal saturation\nlevel. Guided practice 16. For \\(S(c)=90c/(5+c)\\) , find the\nhalf-saturation concentration and explain its meaning. Show each\nalgebraic step, check any excluded values, and interpret your\nconclusion. Visual investigation. Create a small table of values near the relevant intercepts, turning\npoints, or domain boundaries. Sketch the behavior before using software,\nthen compare the sketch with a graph. Explain one feature the table\nreveals and one feature it cannot establish on its own."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation I: zeros and multiplicity", "url": "chapter06.html#visual-investigation-i-zeros-and-multiplicity", "text": "image Investigate. Identify the zeros and their\nmultiplicities. Why does the curve cross at one intercept but touch at\nthe other? What changes when the polynomial is multiplied by \\(-1\\) ? Estimate the sign of each function on\nthe intervals determined by the zeros."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation II: holes versus asymptotes", "url": "chapter06.html#visual-investigation-ii-holes-versus-asymptotes", "text": "image Investigate. Why is \\((1,2)\\) an excluded point on one graph,\nwhile \\(x=2\\) is a vertical asymptote\non the other? Evaluate both expressions at inputs approaching their\nexcluded values from the left and right. Explain why cancellation does\nnot change the original domain."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation III: rational end behavior", "url": "chapter06.html#visual-investigation-iii-rational-end-behavior", "text": "image Investigate. Use polynomial division to rewrite the\nrational expression as \\(3-5/(x+2)\\) .\nPredict which side of \\(y=3\\) the curve\noccupies for large positive and negative \\(x\\) . Explain why the horizontal asymptote\nis not a bound on the range."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Extended\nworkshop A: polynomial design and sensitivity", "url": "chapter06.html#extended-workshop-a-polynomial-design-and-sensitivity", "text": "A response curve is modeled by \\(P(t)=-(t-1)(t-5)(t-8)\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le9\\) , where \\(t\\) is hours. (a) Find the three\nzero-response times. (b) Determine the sign of \\(P\\) on each interval. (c) Explain why\nnegative predicted responses may indicate the model is unsuitable in\npart of its domain. (d) Compare \\(P(t)\\) with \\(P(t)+2\\) and discuss the effect of a\nbaseline adjustment. (e) Use numerical tables to estimate turning\npoints, making clear that these are approximations. Worked discussion. The zeros are \\(1,5,8\\) . The leading\ncoefficient is negative, so the graph falls to the right. Sign tests at \\(t=0,3,6,9\\) establish the alternating\nsigns. A constant baseline adjustment shifts every output up by two\nwithout changing the input domain, but it generally changes the zeros. A\nmathematical model must be checked against physical constraints."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Extended\nworkshop B: rational calibration and physical domains", "url": "chapter06.html#extended-workshop-b-rational-calibration-and-physical-domains", "text": "A response model is \\(S(c)=100c/(10+c)\\) , where \\(c\\ge0\\) is concentration in mg/L and \\(S\\) is signal units. (a) Find the\nmathematical domain and the physical domain. (b) Compute \\(S(0)\\) , \\(S(10)\\) , and \\(S(30)\\) . (c) Find the horizontal asymptote\nand interpret it. (d) Solve \\(S(c)=80\\) . (e) Explain why the vertical\nasymptote at \\(c=-10\\) is not\nphysically relevant. Worked solution. The mathematical domain is \\(\\mathbb\nR\\setminus\\{-10\\}\\) , and the physical domain is \\([0,\\infty)\\) . The signals are \\(0\\) , \\(50\\) , and \\(75\\) . The saturation level is \\(100\\) . Solving \\(100c/(10+c)=80\\) gives \\(100c=800+80c\\) , hence \\(c=40\\) mg/L. Negative concentration is not\npart of the intended model."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Extended workshop C:\ncomparing two models", "url": "chapter06.html#extended-workshop-c-comparing-two-models", "text": "Consider \\(P(c)=12c-c^2\\) and \\(R(c)=72c/(6+c)\\) for \\(0\\le c\\le12\\) . (a) Graph both on the same\naxes. (b) Find where \\(P(c)=0\\) and\nwhere \\(R(c)=0\\) . (c) Compare their\nlong-run mathematical behavior outside the stated interval. (d) Explain\nwhy neither extrapolation is automatically justified by a good fit over \\([0,12]\\) . (e) Use a table at \\(c=0,3,6,9,12\\) to compare predicted\nresponses. Worked discussion. The polynomial has zeros \\(0\\) and \\(12\\) ; the rational model has zero \\(0\\) . The polynomial eventually\ndecreases without bound as \\(c\\to+\\infty\\) , while the rational model\napproaches \\(72\\) . The models represent\ndifferent scientific assumptions, and neither should be extrapolated\nwithout evidence."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: polynomial zeros and multiplicity", "url": "chapter06.html#computational-laboratory-a-polynomial-zeros-and-multiplicity", "text": "Purpose. Plot a factored polynomial and verify its\nzeros numerically. First predict the crossings and touches from the\nfactors."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter06.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code # Chapter 6 computational laboratories - run from top to bottom # LAB A: polynomial zeros and multiplicity p <- function (x) (x + 1 ) * (x -2 ) ^ 2 x <- seq ( - 3 , 4 , length.out= 600 ) plot (x, p (x), type= \"l\" , col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"P(x)\" , main= \"Polynomial zeros and multiplicity\" ) abline ( h= 0 , lty= 2 ) points ( c ( - 1 , 2 ), c ( 0 , 0 ), pch= 19 , col= \"red\" ) print ( data.frame ( zero= c ( - 1 , 2 ), value= p ( c ( - 1 , 2 )))) # LAB B: rational function and vertical asymptote rfun <- function (x) (x + 1 ) / (x -2 ) left <- seq ( - 5 , 1.85 , length.out= 300 ) right <- seq ( 2.15 , 7 , length.out= 300 ) plot (left, rfun (left), type= \"l\" , col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 , xlim= c ( - 5 , 7 ), ylim= c ( - 12 , 12 ), xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"R(x)\" , main= \"Rational function with asymptotes\" ) lines (right, rfun (right), col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 ) abline ( v= 2 , h= 1 , col= \"red\" , lty= 2 ) # LAB C: saturation model S <- function (c) 100 * c / ( 10 + c) c <- seq ( 0 , 100 , length.out= 300 ) plot (c, S (c), type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , col= \"blue\" , xlab= \"Concentration (mg/L)\" , ylab= \"Signal\" , main= \"Saturation response\" ) abline ( h= 100 , col= \"red\" , lty= 2 ) print ( data.frame ( concentration= c ( 0 , 10 , 30 , 40 ), signal= S ( c ( 0 , 10 , 30 , 40 )))) stopifnot ( abs ( S ( 40 ) - 80 ) < 1e-10 )"}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter06.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code # Chapter 6 computational laboratories - run from top to bottom import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # LAB A: polynomial zeros and multiplicity def P(x): return (x + 1 ) * (x - 2 ) ** 2 x = np.linspace( - 3 , 4 , 600 ) plt.figure() ; plt.plot(x,P(x),label = \"(x+1)(x-2)^2\" ) plt.scatter([ - 1 , 2 ],[ 0 , 0 ]) ; plt.axhline( 0 ,color = \"gray\" ,linestyle = \"--\" ) plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"P(x)\" ) ; plt.grid( True ) ; plt.legend() ; plt.show() print ( \"Exact zeros:\" ,[ - 1 , 2 ], \"Values:\" ,P(np.array([ - 1 , 2 ]))) # LAB B: rational function and vertical asymptote def R(x): return (x + 1 ) / (x - 2 ) left = np.linspace( - 5 , 1.85 , 300 ) ; right = np.linspace( 2.15 , 7 , 300 ) plt.figure() ; plt.plot(left,R(left),color = \"blue\" ) ; plt.plot(right,R(right),color = \"blue\" ) plt.axvline( 2 ,color = \"red\" ,linestyle = \"--\" ,label = \"x=2\" ) plt.axhline( 1 ,color = \"gray\" ,linestyle = \"--\" ,label = \"y=1\" ) plt.ylim( - 12 , 12 ) ; plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"R(x)\" ) ; plt.legend() ; plt.grid( True ) ; plt.show() # LAB C: saturation model def S(c): return 100 * c / ( 10 + c) c = np.linspace( 0 , 100 , 300 ) plt.figure() ; plt.plot(c,S(c)) ; plt.axhline( 100 ,color = \"red\" ,linestyle = \"--\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Concentration (mg/L)\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"Signal\" ) ; plt.grid( True ) ; plt.show() for value in [ 0 , 10 , 30 , 40 ]: print (value, S(value)) assert abs (S( 40 ) - 80 ) < 1e-10 Interpretation questions. Compare exact factored zeros with numerical output. Explain why a\nnumerical grid may fail to land exactly on a zero of even multiplicity.\nWhat information does a plot reveal about end behavior?"}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: rational discontinuities", "url": "chapter06.html#computational-laboratory-b-rational-discontinuities", "text": "The scripts also plot a rational function on two separate intervals\naround its vertical asymptote and draw the asymptote explicitly. Use the\nalgebraic domain to explain why the plot must not connect across the\nexcluded input. Change the denominator and repeat."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: applied saturation", "url": "chapter06.html#computational-laboratory-c-applied-saturation", "text": "The scripts evaluate \\(S(c)=100c/(10+c)\\) on \\(0\\le c\\le100\\) , plot the horizontal\nsaturation level, and calculate the concentration that produces \\(80\\) signal units. Explain the distinction\nbetween a numerical estimate and the exact algebraic solution."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Calculus\nreadiness: behavior near an excluded input", "url": "chapter06.html#calculus-readiness-behavior-near-an-excluded-input", "text": "The rational function \\(R(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1)\\) equals \\(x+1\\) whenever \\(x\\ne1\\) , but it is not defined at \\(x=1\\) . A later calculus course asks what\nvalues \\(R(x)\\) approaches as \\(x\\) gets close to \\(1\\) ; this question is different from\nevaluating \\(R(1)\\) . Likewise, a\nhorizontal asymptote describes what happens as the input grows without\nbound. These ideas motivate limits and continuity. Concept check 1. Explain, in words, the difference\nbetween \\(R(1)\\) and the value\napproached by \\(R(x)\\) as \\(x\\) approaches \\(1\\) for \\(R(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1)\\) ."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Chapter review exercises", "url": "chapter06.html#chapter-review-exercises", "text": "Identify the degree and leading coefficient of \\(5x^6-2x^4+x-7\\) . Evaluate \\(2x^3-5x+1\\) at \\(x=-2\\) . State the end behavior of \\(-3x^4+7x^2\\) . State the end behavior of \\(2x^5-x^2+1\\) . Factor \\(x^3-4x\\) and list its\nreal zeros. Solve \\(x^4-10x^2+9=0\\) . Identify zeros and multiplicities of \\((x-1)^2(x+3)^3\\) . Sketch \\(-(x+2)(x-4)^2\\) using\nintercepts and end behavior. Find the remainder on division of \\(x^3-2x+5\\) by \\(x-2\\) . Use synthetic division to divide \\(x^3-4x^2+x+6\\) by \\(x-2\\) . List rational-zero candidates for \\(2x^3+x^2-8x-4\\) . Solve \\((x-2)(x+5)\\le0\\) . Solve \\(x(x-1)^2>0\\) . Determine the domain of \\((x^2-4)/(x^2-x-6)\\) . Determine whether \\((x^2-9)/(x-3)\\) has a hole or a vertical\nasymptote at \\(3\\) . Find all vertical asymptotes of \\((x+1)/(x^2-9)\\) . Find the horizontal asymptote of \\((5x^2+3)/(2x^2+1)\\) . Find the slant asymptote of \\((x^2+3x+1)/(x+1)\\) . Solve \\((x-4)/(x+2)\\ge0\\) . Solve \\((x+1)/(x-3)<0\\) . For \\(S(c)=80c/(4+c)\\) ,\ncalculate \\(S(4)\\) and the saturation\nlevel. For \\(S(c)=120c/(8+c)\\) , solve \\(S(c)=90\\) . Explain why a graphing window can hide a polynomial\nzero. Explain why canceling factors does not restore excluded\ninputs. Compare a polynomial model and a rational saturation model for\nlarge inputs. Create an R or Python plot of \\((x-2)^2(x+1)\\) and annotate its\nzeros. Use R or Python to compare \\((x^2-1)/(x-1)\\) and \\(x+1\\) near \\(x=1\\) . Construct a rational model with a hole at \\(x=2\\) and a vertical asymptote at \\(x=-1\\) . Describe an example in which the mathematical domain differs from\nthe scientifically meaningful domain. Write a short explanation of how rational functions prepare\nstudents for the calculus concept of limits."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Extended\napplication: Designing a rectangular enclosure", "url": "chapter06.html#extended-application-designing-a-rectangular-enclosure", "text": "A laboratory garden has 40 meters of fencing for a rectangle. Let the\nwidth be variable and use all the fencing. The quantities in this\ninvestigation are illustrative rather than measured observations."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter06.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[A(w)=w(20-w).\\] Before calculating,\nidentify the input and output, their units, and the interval on which\nthe model is being studied. Distinguish the mathematical domain from the\npractical domain. The numerical study uses inputs from \\(0\\) to \\(20\\) . Example 17: Analytical investigation. Find the zeros\nand use completing the square to find the largest area. Explain the\nphysical domain. Worked solution. \\(A=100-(w-10)^2\\) ; zeros 0 and 20 are\ndegenerate. Maximum 100 square meters at width 10 m."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter06.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Width (m) Area (square m) 0 0 5 75 10 100 15 75 20 0 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter06.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Designing a rectangular enclosure # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 20 , length.out = 301 ) y <- x * ( 20 - x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Width (m)\" , ylab = \"Area (square m)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter06.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Designing a rectangular enclosure import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 20 , 301 ) y = x * ( 20 - x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Width (m)\" , ylabel = \"Area (square m)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter06.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Designing a rectangular enclosure", "url": "chapter06.html#chapter-project-investigating-designing-a-rectangular-enclosure", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter06.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions", "section": "Chapter summary", "url": "chapter06.html#chapter-summary", "text": "Polynomial factors reveal zeros and multiplicities; degree and\nleading coefficient determine end behavior. Rational functions introduce\nexcluded inputs, holes, and asymptotes. A complete analysis integrates\nsymbolic algebra, numerical checks, graphs, and interpretation in\ncontext. Looking ahead. Chapter 7 develops exponential and\nlogarithmic functions, providing models for growth, decay, and\nmultiplicative change."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "url": "chapter07.html#exponential-and-logarithmic-functions", "text": ""}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Opening\ncontext: change that depends on the current amount", "url": "chapter07.html#opening-context-change-that-depends-on-the-current-amount", "text": "A medicine may be eliminated from the bloodstream at a rate\nproportional to how much remains. A bacterial culture may increase by a\nnearly constant percentage over equal time intervals. Neither process is\nwell described by a line over a long interval. Exponential models\ncapture repeated multiplication; logarithms answer the inverse question: how long until a target is reached? We will connect formulas,\ntables, graphs, units, and computational experiments."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter07.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "By the end of this chapter you should be able to distinguish additive\nfrom multiplicative change; evaluate and graph exponential functions;\nidentify growth and decay factors; solve exponential equations;\ninterpret the natural exponential function; define logarithms as\ninverses; apply logarithm laws with appropriate domain restrictions;\nsolve logarithmic equations and reject extraneous answers; build and\ncritique growth, decay, and saturation models; interpret doubling times\nand half-lives; and use R and Python to verify predictions."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Prerequisite check", "url": "chapter07.html#prerequisite-check", "text": "Review exponent rules, function composition, inverses, domains, and\nthe meaning of a percent. For example, a 12% increase multiplies a\nquantity by \\(1.12\\) , whereas a 12%\ndecrease multiplies it by \\(0.88\\) . The\ninverse of \\(f(x)=3x+2\\) is \\(f^{-1}(x)=(x-2)/3\\) . Explain why \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) is real only for \\(x\\ge0\\) ."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Chapter roadmap", "url": "chapter07.html#chapter-roadmap", "text": "We begin with repeated percentage change, develop exponential\nfunctions and their inverses, learn to solve equations analytically, and\nend with models in which assumptions and units matter. Each\ncomputational laboratory presents the complete R code first and the\ncorresponding Python code second."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Repeated\nmultiplication versus repeated addition", "url": "chapter07.html#repeated-multiplication-versus-repeated-addition", "text": "A linear sequence adds the same amount at every step; an exponential\nsequence multiplies by the same positive factor. If a quantity starts at \\(A_0\\) and grows by a fraction \\(r\\) each period, then \\(A_n=A_0(1+r)^n\\) . A decrease of fraction \\(d\\) uses \\(A_n=A_0(1-d)^n\\) . The percentage applies to\nthe current amount, not the original amount. A negative base is\nnot suitable for a continuous real-input exponential model. Example 1: Repeated multiplication versus repeated\naddition. A culture begins with 200 cells and grows 25% per\nhour. Find the predicted population after three hours. Worked solution. \\(A(3)=200(1.25)^3=390.625\\) . The fractional\nprediction is a model expectation, not a literal count of partial\ncells. Guided practice 1. An initial measurement is 800 units and decreases by 15% each day.\nWrite a model and compute the value after four days. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Exponential functions\nand their domains", "url": "chapter07.html#exponential-functions-and-their-domains", "text": "For \\(a>0\\) , \\(a\\ne1\\) , the function \\(f(x)=a^x\\) is defined for every real \\(x\\) , takes only positive values, and passes\nthrough \\((0,1)\\) . When \\(a>1\\) , it is increasing; when \\(0<a<1\\) , it is decreasing. Its\nhorizontal asymptote is \\(y=0\\) .\nNegative exponents mean reciprocals: \\(a^{-x}=1/a^x\\) . The restriction \\(a>0\\) allows a consistent real-valued\ndefinition for irrational exponents. Example 2: Exponential functions and their domains. Compare \\(2^x\\) and \\((1/2)^x\\) at \\(x=-2,0,2\\) . Worked solution. For \\(2^x\\) the values are \\(1/4,1,4\\) ; for \\((1/2)^x\\) they are \\(4,1,1/4\\) . Both are positive, and both pass\nthrough \\((0,1)\\) . Guided practice 2. Explain the difference between \\(3^{-2}\\) and \\((-3)^2\\) . State why \\((-3)^x\\) is not a real-valued exponential\nfunction for every real \\(x\\) . Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Transformations of\nexponential graphs", "url": "chapter07.html#transformations-of-exponential-graphs", "text": "The graph \\(g(x)=A a^{x-h}+k\\) is a\nshifted and scaled exponential graph. \\(h\\) shifts horizontally, \\(k\\) shifts vertically, and \\(A\\) scales or reflects the vertical\ndisplacement. The horizontal asymptote is \\(y=k\\) . If \\(A>0\\) , the range is \\((k,\\infty)\\) ; if \\(A<0\\) , the range is \\((-\\infty,k)\\) . A horizontal translation\ninside the exponent must be read with care. Example 3: Transformations of exponential graphs. Identify the asymptote and range of \\(g(x)=3\\cdot2^{x-1}-4\\) . Worked solution. The graph of \\(2^x\\) moves right one unit, stretches\nvertically by 3, and moves down four units. Its asymptote is \\(y=-4\\) and its range is \\((-4,\\infty)\\) . Guided practice 3. Sketch \\(-2(1/3)^{x+1}+5\\) , labeling\nits horizontal asymptote and one convenient point. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "The natural exponential\nfunction", "url": "chapter07.html#the-natural-exponential-function", "text": "The constant \\(e\\approx2.71828\\) arises naturally from continuous compounding and many continuous-change\nprocesses. The function \\(e^x\\) has the\nsame positive-domain and increasing properties as other growth\nexponentials. We write \\(A(t)=A_0e^{kt}\\) , where \\(k\\) is the continuous growth rate per unit\ntime. Positive \\(k\\) indicates growth;\nnegative \\(k\\) indicates decay. The\nunits of \\(k\\) are reciprocal time\nunits. Example 4: The natural exponential function. A\nsubstance has initial mass 50 mg and follows \\(M(t)=50e^{-0.2t}\\) , where \\(t\\) is in hours. Find the predicted mass\nafter 5 hours. Worked solution. \\(M(5)=50e^{-1}\\approx18.39\\) mg. The\nnegative exponent reflects decay, and the result retains mass units. Guided practice 4. For \\(N(t)=120e^{0.08t}\\) , evaluate \\(N(10)\\) and explain the units of \\(0.08\\) when \\(t\\) is measured in days. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Compound interest\nand compounding frequency", "url": "chapter07.html#compound-interest-and-compounding-frequency", "text": "If principal \\(P\\) earns nominal\nannual rate \\(r\\) compounded \\(n\\) times per year, then \\(A(t)=P(1+r/n)^{nt}\\) . Continuous\ncompounding yields \\(A(t)=Pe^{rt}\\) .\nThe nominal annual rate is not necessarily the effective annual\npercentage yield: the latter is \\((1+r/n)^n-1\\) . Financial models illustrate\nexponentials, although real investments may involve fees, taxes, and\nvariable returns. Example 5: Compound interest and compounding\nfrequency. Compare one year of 6% nominal interest on \\(\\$1{,}000\\) compounded monthly with\ncontinuous compounding. Worked solution. Monthly: \\(1000(1+0.06/12)^{12}\\approx1061.68\\) .\nContinuous: \\(1000e^{0.06}\\approx1061.84\\) . The\ndifference is small but nonzero. Guided practice 5. Calculate the effective annual rate for a nominal 8% compounded\nquarterly. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Logarithms as inverse\nfunctions", "url": "chapter07.html#logarithms-as-inverse-functions", "text": "For \\(a>0\\) and \\(a\\ne1\\) , \\(\\log_a(x)=y\\) means exactly \\(a^y=x\\) . The logarithm is defined only for \\(x>0\\) and can return any real\nvalue. The graph of \\(\\log_a x\\) is the\nreflection of \\(a^x\\) across \\(y=x\\) ; its vertical asymptote is \\(x=0\\) . The inverse identities are \\(a^{\\log_a x}=x\\) for \\(x>0\\) and \\(\\log_a(a^x)=x\\) for all real \\(x\\) . Example 6: Logarithms as inverse functions. Evaluate \\(\\log_2(32)\\) and \\(\\log_3(1/9)\\) . Worked solution. Since \\(2^5=32\\) , \\(\\log_2(32)=5\\) . Since \\(3^{-2}=1/9\\) , \\(\\log_3(1/9)=-2\\) . Guided practice 6. Rewrite \\(\\log_5(125)=3\\) exponentially and evaluate \\(\\log_{10}(0.001)\\) . Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Common\nlogarithms, natural logarithms, and change of base", "url": "chapter07.html#common-logarithms-natural-logarithms-and-change-of-base", "text": "The common logarithm is \\(\\log\nx=\\log_{10}x\\) and the natural logarithm is \\(\\ln x=\\log_e x\\) . Calculators and software\nusually provide both. For any valid bases, \\(\\log_a x=\\ln x/\\ln a\\) . This is the\nchange-of-base formula. Logarithmic axes can reveal multiplicative\npatterns, but a straight line on a semilog plot does not prove the model\nis scientifically appropriate. Example 7: Common logarithms, natural logarithms, and change\nof base. Approximate \\(\\log_2(7)\\) using natural logarithms. Worked solution. \\(\\log_2(7)=\\ln7/\\ln2\\approx2.8074\\) . Because \\(2^2<7<2^3\\) , the result should\nlie between 2 and 3. Guided practice 7. Approximate \\(\\log_3(20)\\) to three\ndecimals and bound the answer between consecutive integers. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Logarithm laws and their\nlimitations", "url": "chapter07.html#logarithm-laws-and-their-limitations", "text": "For positive \\(u,v\\) and valid base \\(a\\) , \\(\\log_a(uv)=\\log_a u+\\log_a v\\) , \\(\\log_a(u/v)=\\log_a u-\\log_a v\\) , and \\(\\log_a(u^r)=r\\log_a u\\) when the\nexpressions are defined. There is no rule \\(\\log(u+v)=\\log u+\\log v\\) . When expanding\nlogarithms of algebraic expressions, retain the original domain: for\ninstance, \\(\\ln(x^2)\\) is defined for \\(x\\ne0\\) , and equals \\(2\\ln|x|\\) , not \\(2\\ln x\\) for all nonzero real \\(x\\) . Example 8: Logarithm laws and their limitations. Expand \\(\\ln(5x^2/y)\\) for \\(x\\ne0\\) and \\(y>0\\) . Worked solution. \\(\\ln(5x^2/y)=\\ln5+2\\ln|x|-\\ln y\\) . The\nabsolute value is essential for negative \\(x\\) . Guided practice 8. Condense \\(2\\ln x-\\ln(x+1)\\) into\none logarithm and state the domain. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Solving exponential\nequations", "url": "chapter07.html#solving-exponential-equations", "text": "When both sides can be expressed using the same positive base, equate\nexponents. Otherwise isolate the exponential term and take a logarithm.\nKeep units in the interpretation, and verify numerically. For \\(A_0e^{kt}=T\\) , the time is \\(t=\\ln(T/A_0)/k\\) , provided \\(A_0,T>0\\) and \\(k\\ne0\\) . The sign of the resulting time\nshould make sense in context. Example 9: Solving exponential equations. Solve \\(3e^{0.4t}=12\\) . Worked solution. Divide by 3 to obtain \\(e^{0.4t}=4\\) . Take natural logs: \\(0.4t=\\ln4\\) , so \\(t=\\ln4/0.4\\approx3.466\\) . Guided practice 9. Solve \\(250(0.8)^t=100\\) using\nlogarithms, and check your answer by substitution. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Solving logarithmic\nequations", "url": "chapter07.html#solving-logarithmic-equations", "text": "Every logarithmic argument must be positive. Solve by combining\nlogarithms when appropriate, converting to exponential form, and\nchecking candidates in the original equation. Algebraic\nmanipulation may produce extraneous roots. For example, \\(\\ln(x-1)+\\ln(x+1)\\) requires \\(x>1\\) , even though the product \\(x^2-1\\) is positive also when \\(x<-1\\) . Example 10: Solving logarithmic equations. Solve \\(\\ln(x-1)+\\ln(x+1)=\\ln8\\) . Worked solution. The original domain requires \\(x>1\\) . Combining gives \\(\\ln(x^2-1)=\\ln8\\) , so \\(x^2=9\\) . Candidates are \\(\\pm3\\) , but only \\(x=3\\) satisfies the domain and original\nequation. Guided practice 10. Solve \\(\\log_2(x-2)=3\\) and state\nthe domain before solving. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Doubling time and half-life", "url": "chapter07.html#doubling-time-and-half-life", "text": "For \\(A(t)=A_0e^{kt}\\) with \\(k>0\\) , the doubling time is \\(T_d=\\ln2/k\\) . For \\(k<0\\) , the half-life is \\(T_{1/2}=\\ln2/|k|\\) . These times do not\ndepend on the starting amount when the constant-rate exponential model\napplies. A half-life does not imply the amount becomes exactly zero\nafter finitely many half-lives. Example 11: Doubling time and half-life. A\nmedication concentration follows \\(C(t)=24e^{-0.1733t}\\) mg/L. Estimate the\nhalf-life. Worked solution. \\(T_{1/2}=\\ln2/0.1733\\approx4.00\\) hours.\nAfter about four hours the predicted concentration is 12 mg/L, assuming\nthe model applies. Guided practice 11. A culture grows as \\(N(t)=500e^{0.12t}\\) with \\(t\\) in hours. Compute its doubling\ntime. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Fitting an\nexponential model from two observations", "url": "chapter07.html#fitting-an-exponential-model-from-two-observations", "text": "Suppose \\(A(t)=A_0e^{kt}\\) and\nmeasurements \\(A(t_1),A(t_2)\\) are\npositive. Taking a ratio gives \\(A(t_2)/A(t_1)=e^{k(t_2-t_1)}\\) , so \\(k=\\ln[A(t_2)/A(t_1)]/(t_2-t_1)\\) . Then \\(A_0=A(t_1)e^{-kt_1}\\) . Two\nobservations determine a curve under the exponential assumption; they do\nnot establish that the process is truly exponential. Example 12: Fitting an exponential model from two\nobservations. A measured concentration is 40 mg/L at 0 hours\nand 10 mg/L at 6 hours. Find an exponential model. Worked solution. \\(k=\\ln(10/40)/6=\\ln(1/4)/6\\approx-0.2310\\) per hour. Thus \\(C(t)=40e^{-0.2310t}\\) mg/L, with half-life about 3 hours. Guided practice 12. A population rises from 300 to 450 in five days. Estimate \\(k\\) and write a continuous-growth\nmodel. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Logarithmic\nscales and scientific measurements", "url": "chapter07.html#logarithmic-scales-and-scientific-measurements", "text": "Logarithmic scales compress wide ranges by recording powers or\nratios. The pH scale is \\(\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log_{10}([H^+]/(1\\\n\\mathrm{mol/L}))\\) , treating the concentration ratio as\ndimensionless in an introductory approximation. A one-unit pH decrease\ncorresponds to ten times the hydrogen-ion concentration. In chemistry,\nactivity rather than raw concentration is required for precise pH, so\nthe simple formula is an instructional model. Example 13: Logarithmic scales and scientific\nmeasurements. Compare hydrogen-ion concentrations at pH 5 and\npH 7 under the introductory concentration approximation. Worked solution. At pH 5, \\([H^+]\\approx10^{-5}\\) mol/L; at pH 7, \\([H^+]\\approx10^{-7}\\) mol/L. The first is\n100 times the second. Guided practice 13. Under the same approximation, determine the pH of a solution with \\([H^+]=2\\times10^{-4}\\) mol/L. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Model\ninterpretation, assumptions, and limitations", "url": "chapter07.html#model-interpretation-assumptions-and-limitations", "text": "Exponential growth cannot continue indefinitely in most biological\nsettings because resources, competition, treatment changes, and other\nconstraints intervene. An exponential decline may fail when a drug\nfollows multiple compartments or nonlinear clearance. Check residual\npatterns, measurement uncertainty, time units, and whether predictions\nextrapolate beyond the observed interval. A model is a conditional\ndescription, not a guarantee. Example 14: Model interpretation, assumptions, and\nlimitations. A model predicts that a culture doubles every two\nhours from 100 cells. What does it predict after 24 hours, and why\nshould we be cautious? Worked solution. The prediction is \\(100\\cdot2^{12}=409{,}600\\) cells. It\nassumes an unchanged doubling time over 12 cycles; limited nutrients and\nspace may invalidate this assumption. Guided practice 14. Explain why a drug-decay model fitted over 0–8 hours may not reliably\npredict concentrations after 10 days. Think, sketch, verify. Before using software, predict the direction of change, important\nintercepts or asymptotes, and whether the result is physically\nmeaningful. Then use a table or graph to test your prediction."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation I: linear versus exponential change", "url": "chapter07.html#visual-investigation-i-linear-versus-exponential-change", "text": "Consider \\(L(t)=100+20t\\) and \\(E(t)=100(1.2)^t\\) . Both start at 100, but\nthe first adds 20 each step and the second adds 20% of its current\namount. Complete the table, sketch both curves, and explain why the two\nmodels agree at \\(t=0\\) and \\(t=1\\) but diverge thereafter. \\(t\\) 0 1 2 3 4 5 \\(L(t)\\) 100 120 140 160 180 200 \\(E(t)\\) 100 120 144 172.8 207.36 248.832 image Investigation prompts. Which model has constant first differences? Which has constant\nsuccessive ratios? At what time does the exponential prediction first\nexceed the linear prediction by at least 20 units? Estimate from the\ngraph, then verify numerically."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation II: growth, decay, and horizontal asymptotes", "url": "chapter07.html#visual-investigation-ii-growth-decay-and-horizontal-asymptotes", "text": "The graphs of \\(2^x\\) , \\((1/2)^x\\) , and \\(2^x-3\\) show how the base and vertical\nshift affect monotonicity, range, and asymptotes. Record the \\(y\\) -intercept and range of each curve.\nDescribe what happens as \\(x\\to-\\infty\\) and \\(x\\to+\\infty\\) . image"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Visual investigation\nIII: inverse graphs", "url": "chapter07.html#visual-investigation-iii-inverse-graphs", "text": "Plot \\(y=2^x\\) , \\(y=\\log_2 x\\) , and \\(y=x\\) . The first two are reflections across\nthe diagonal. Make a table of matching points: \\((0,1)\\) on the exponential becomes \\((1,0)\\) on the logarithm; \\((3,8)\\) becomes \\((8,3)\\) . Why does the logarithm have a\nvertical rather than horizontal asymptote? image"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation IV: interpreting a semilog plot", "url": "chapter07.html#visual-investigation-iv-interpreting-a-semilog-plot", "text": "Suppose \\(C(t)=50e^{-0.25t}\\) .\nTaking natural logs gives \\(\\ln\nC(t)=\\ln50-0.25t\\) . Therefore a plot of \\(\\ln C\\) against \\(t\\) is a line with slope \\(-0.25\\) . Calculate concentrations at \\(t=0,2,4,6,8\\) ; graph both \\(C(t)\\) and \\(\\ln\nC(t)\\) ; then explain how the slope of the transformed plot\nrelates to the original model. Do not take a logarithm of a\nnonpositive measurement."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Extended workshop A:\nmedication elimination", "url": "chapter07.html#extended-workshop-a-medication-elimination", "text": "A hypothetical medication has concentration \\(C(t)=32e^{-0.18t}\\) mg/L, with time in\nhours. (a) Find \\(C(0)\\) and \\(C(6)\\) . (b) Determine the half-life. (c)\nFind when the model first predicts \\(C(t)=5\\) mg/L. (d) Interpret the units and\ndiscuss why a single exponential may not describe all patients. Worked solution. \\(C(0)=32\\) mg/L; \\(C(6)=32e^{-1.08}\\approx10.87\\) mg/L. The\nhalf-life is \\(\\ln2/0.18\\approx3.85\\) hours. Solving \\(32e^{-0.18t}=5\\) gives \\(t=\\ln(32/5)/0.18\\approx10.31\\) hours.\nThis is a mathematical prediction, not medical dosing advice."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Extended\nworkshop B: microbial growth and model breakdown", "url": "chapter07.html#extended-workshop-b-microbial-growth-and-model-breakdown", "text": "A culture starts at 250 cells and is observed to reach 400 cells\nafter three hours. Assume exponential growth for the exercise. (a)\nEstimate \\(k\\) . (b) Predict the amount\nat six hours. (c) Find doubling time. (d) Explain why extrapolation to a\nmonth is unreasonable. Worked solution. \\(k=\\ln(400/250)/3\\approx0.1567\\) per hour.\nThe six-hour prediction is \\(250e^{6k}=250(1.6)^2=640\\) cells. Doubling\ntime is \\(\\ln2/k\\approx4.42\\) hours.\nNutrient limits and changing growth phases can invalidate the long-term\nmodel."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Extended\nworkshop C: logarithmic interpretation", "url": "chapter07.html#extended-workshop-c-logarithmic-interpretation", "text": "Under the introductory concentration-based pH approximation, compare\nsolutions with \\([H^+]=10^{-3}\\) mol/L\nand \\([H^+]=10^{-5}\\) mol/L. Compute\npH, concentration ratio, and the effect of a tenfold dilution of the\nfirst solution (assuming the simple concentration model remains\napplicable). Worked solution. Their pH values are 3 and 5; the first\nhas 100 times the hydrogen-ion concentration of the second. A tenfold\ndilution changes \\(10^{-3}\\) to \\(10^{-4}\\) mol/L, corresponding to pH 4\nunder the stated approximation."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: exponential growth and decay", "url": "chapter07.html#computational-laboratory-a-exponential-growth-and-decay", "text": "Purpose. Compare linear and exponential\ntrajectories, generate a graph, and interpret the difference between\nfixed increments and fixed percentages. Run the R listing first, then\nthe Python listing. Both scripts are standalone and require no input\nfiles."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter07.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code # LAB A: Linear and exponential change period <- 0 : 10 linear <- 100 + 20 * period exponential <- 100 * 1.2 ^ period results <- data.frame (period, linear, exponential) print (results) plot (period, exponential, type = \"b\" , col = \"blue\" , pch = 19 , xlab = \"Period\" , ylab = \"Amount\" , main = \"Change models\" ) lines (period, linear, type = \"b\" , col = \"red\" , pch = 17 ) legend ( \"topleft\" , legend = c ( \"Exponential\" , \"Linear\" ), col = c ( \"blue\" , \"red\" ), lty = 1 , pch = c ( 19 , 17 )) cat ( \"At t=5:\" , exponential[ 6 ], \"versus\" , linear[ 6 ], \" \\n \" ) # End laboratory A # LAB B: Half-life and inverse calculation"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter07.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code # LAB A: Linear and exponential change import math import matplotlib.pyplot as plt period = list ( range ( 11 )) linear = [ 100 + 20 * t for t in period] exponential = [ 100 * 1.2 ** t for t in period] for t, l, e in zip (period, linear, exponential): print ( f\"t= { t :2d} : linear= { l :.2f} , exponential= { e :.2f} \" ) plt.figure() plt.plot(period, exponential, \"o-\" , label = \"Exponential\" ) plt.plot(period, linear, \"s-\" , label = \"Linear\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Period\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Amount\" ) plt.title( \"Change models\" ) plt.legend() plt.savefig( \"chapter07_models.png\" , dpi = 180 ) plt.close() Interpretation. Which model is larger at \\(t=5\\) ?\nWhat changes if the growth rate is reduced from 20% to 5%?"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: half-life and inverse calculations", "url": "chapter07.html#computational-laboratory-b-half-life-and-inverse-calculations", "text": "Purpose. Use logarithms to determine the time\nrequired to cross a target concentration, and check by substituting into\nthe original model."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter07.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code k <- 0.18 target <- 5 half_life <- log ( 2 ) / k time_to_target <- log (C0 / target) / k check <- C0 * exp ( - k * time_to_target) print ( c ( half_life = half_life, time_to_target = time_to_target, checked_concentration = check)) # End laboratory B # LAB C: Log-linear fitting and residual inspection"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter07.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code # LAB B: Half-life and inverse calculation C0 = 32 k = 0.18 target = 5 half_life = math.log( 2 ) / k time_to_target = math.log(C0 / target) / k check = C0 * math.exp( - k * time_to_target) print ( \"Half-life:\" , round (half_life, 4 )) print ( \"Time to target:\" , round (time_to_target, 4 )) Interpretation. Explain why the logarithm of the ratio \\(C_0/C_{\\rm target}\\) is dimensionless."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: fitting a model and checking residuals", "url": "chapter07.html#computational-laboratory-c-fitting-a-model-and-checking-residuals", "text": "Purpose. Estimate an exponential model from\nobservations, visualize measured versus predicted values, and inspect\nresiduals. This is an exploratory fit, not a claim of causal\nmechanism."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter07.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code measured <- c ( 40 , 26 , 17 , 10.8 , 7.4 ) fit <- lm ( log (measured) ~ time) intercept <- coef (fit)[ 1 ] rate <- coef (fit)[ 2 ] predicted <- exp ( predict (fit)) residual <- measured - predicted print ( data.frame (time, measured, predicted, residual)) cat ( \"Estimated A0:\" , exp (intercept), \"rate:\" , rate, \" \\n \" ) plot (time, measured, pch = 19 , xlab = \"Time (hours)\" , ylab = \"Concentration (mg/L)\" , main = \"Exponential fit\" ) lines (time, predicted, col = \"blue\" , lwd = 2 ) plot (time, residual, pch = 19 , xlab = \"Time (hours)\" , ylab = \"Observed - predicted\" , main = \"Residuals\" ) abline ( h = 0 , lty = 2 ) # End laboratory C"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter07.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code # End laboratory B # LAB C: Log-linear fitting and residual inspection import numpy as np time = np.array([ 0 , 2 , 4 , 6 , 8 ], dtype = float ) measured = np.array([ 40 , 26 , 17 , 10.8 , 7.4 ], dtype = float ) rate, intercept = np.polyfit(time, np.log(measured), 1 ) predicted = np.exp(intercept + rate * time) residual = measured - predicted for t, observed, fitted, error in zip (time, measured, predicted, residual): print ( f\" { t :.0f} : observed= { observed :.2f} , predicted= { fitted :.2f} , residual= { error :.2f} \" ) print ( \"Estimated A0:\" , round (math.exp(intercept), 4 ), \"rate:\" , round (rate, 4 )) plt.figure() plt.plot(time, measured, \"o\" , label = \"Observed\" ) plt.plot(time, predicted, \"-\" , label = \"Fitted\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Time (hours)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Concentration (mg/L)\" ) plt.legend() Interpretation. A fitted straight line in log-space corresponds to multiplicative\nerrors on the original scale. What pattern in residuals might make you\nquestion the model?"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Calculus readiness:\ninstantaneous change", "url": "chapter07.html#calculus-readiness-instantaneous-change", "text": "For \\(A(t)=A_0e^{kt}\\) , the average\nrate of change over \\([t,t+h]\\) is \\([A(t+h)-A(t)]/h=A_0e^{kt}(e^{kh}-1)/h\\) . As \\(h\\) approaches zero, this expression\napproaches \\(kA(t)\\) ; calculus will\nestablish this limit rigorously. For a decay model \\(k<0\\) , the instantaneous rate is\nnegative and its magnitude decreases as the amount declines. Compare\naverage rates using \\(h=1,0.1,0.01\\) and predict the limiting value."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Guided\nproblem-solving clinic: choosing the right method", "url": "chapter07.html#guided-problem-solving-clinic-choosing-the-right-method", "text": ""}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Case\n1: Identifying a model from a table", "url": "chapter07.html#case-1-identifying-a-model-from-a-table", "text": "Consider the observations \\(100,130,169,219.7\\) at times \\(0,1,2,3\\) . Subtract successive entries and\ncompare the differences: \\(30,39,50.7\\) are not constant. Now divide successive entries: each ratio is \\(1.3\\) . Thus \\(A(t)=100(1.3)^t\\) is an exact model for the\ntable. Explain why the data alone do not guarantee the same rate in\nfuture periods. Your turn. A second sequence is \\(500,425,361.25,307.0625\\) . Determine the\ncommon ratio and write a model. Estimate the first period at which the\nvalue falls below 200."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Case 2: An\nexponential equation with a baseline", "url": "chapter07.html#case-2-an-exponential-equation-with-a-baseline", "text": "Suppose a response is modeled by \\(R(t)=8+40e^{-0.3t}\\) . Find the time when \\(R(t)=18\\) . First subtract the\nbaseline: \\(10=40e^{-0.3t}\\) . Divide by\n40: \\(1/4=e^{-0.3t}\\) . Take logarithms: \\(-0.3t=\\ln(1/4)\\) . Therefore \\(t=\\ln4/0.3\\approx4.62\\) . The response\napproaches 8 but never reaches 8 at finite time. Your turn. Solve \\(5+25e^{-0.4t}=10\\) . Why\nwould setting \\(25e^{-0.4t}=10\\) be\nincorrect?"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Case\n3: Checking a logarithmic solution", "url": "chapter07.html#case-3-checking-a-logarithmic-solution", "text": "Solve \\(\\log_3(x+1)+\\log_3(x-1)=2\\) .\nThe original domain is \\(x>1\\) .\nCombine logs: \\(\\log_3(x^2-1)=2\\) .\nConvert: \\(x^2-1=9\\) , so \\(x=\\pm\\sqrt{10}\\) . Only \\(x=\\sqrt{10}\\) is in the original domain.\nSubstituting confirms that \\((x+1)(x-1)=9\\) . Your turn. Solve \\(\\ln(x-2)+\\ln x=\\ln15\\) with\na complete domain check."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Case 4: Model parameters\nfrom a half-life", "url": "chapter07.html#case-4-model-parameters-from-a-half-life", "text": "Suppose a tracer has a half-life of 9 hours and an initial amount of\n75 units. The factor after \\(t\\) hours\nis \\(2^{-t/9}\\) , so \\(A(t)=75\\cdot2^{-t/9}=75e^{-(\\ln2/9)t}\\) .\nBoth forms describe the same model. At 18 hours, two half-lives have\nelapsed and the predicted amount is \\(75/4=18.75\\) units. Your turn. Write a model for an initial 120 mg with half-life 6 hours. Find the\npredicted amount after 15 hours and the time required to reach 10\nmg."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Further\nvisual investigation: parameter sensitivity", "url": "chapter07.html#further-visual-investigation-parameter-sensitivity", "text": "Compare \\(C_1(t)=30e^{-0.1t}\\) , \\(C_2(t)=30e^{-0.2t}\\) , and \\(C_3(t)=30e^{-0.4t}\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le15\\) . All share an initial value\nof 30, but their rates and half-lives differ. Before plotting, rank\ntheir concentrations at \\(t=10\\) and\ntheir half-lives. After plotting, explain why a steeper early decline\ncorresponds to a shorter half-life. image Interpretation prompts. Explain what happens to the predicted concentration as \\(t\\to\\infty\\) . Why is the model’s zero\nasymptote not a statement that the drug is measurable forever? How might\na detection threshold affect the practical interpretation?"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Chapter review exercises", "url": "chapter07.html#chapter-review-exercises", "text": "A quantity grows by 7% per month from 400. Write a model and find\nthe value after eight months. A quantity declines by 9% per week from 900. Find the value after\nsix weeks. Distinguish \\(200+15t\\) from \\(200(1.15)^t\\) using successive\ndifferences and ratios. Find the domain, range, intercept, and asymptote of \\(f(x)=4^x\\) . Find the domain, range, and asymptote of \\(g(x)=2\\cdot3^{x+1}-5\\) . Explain why \\((-2)^x\\) is not a\nreal-valued exponential function on all of \\(\\mathbb R\\) . Evaluate \\(\\log_4 64\\) , \\(\\log_2(1/16)\\) , and \\(\\ln(e^{-3})\\) . Convert \\(\\log_7(49)=2\\) to\nexponential form. Compute \\(\\log_5(12)\\) using\nchange of base. Expand \\(\\ln(3x^2/y)\\) with a\nmathematically correct domain statement. Condense \\(\\ln x+2\\ln(x+2)\\) into one logarithm and give its domain. Explain with a numerical counterexample why \\(\\ln(a+b)\\ne\\ln a+\\ln b\\) in\ngeneral. Solve \\(2^{x+1}=32\\) exactly. Solve \\(7e^{0.3t}=20\\) and\nverify numerically. Solve \\(100(0.85)^t=35\\) for \\(t\\) . Solve \\(\\ln(x-4)=2\\) and check\nthe domain. Solve \\(\\ln(x-1)+\\ln(x+2)=\\ln10\\) and reject\ninvalid candidates. Determine the half-life for \\(C(t)=60e^{-0.14t}\\) . Determine the doubling time for \\(N(t)=120e^{0.06t}\\) . Find the exponential model through \\((0,80)\\) and \\((4,20)\\) . A culture grows from 600 to 750 in two hours. Estimate its\ncontinuous rate. Compute the amount after five years on \\(\\$2{,}000\\) at 4% compounded\nquarterly. Compare annual and continuous compounding at a nominal 5%\nrate. Under the simplified concentration-based pH formula, find pH when \\([H^+]=4\\times10^{-6}\\) mol/L. Two solutions differ by 3 pH units. What is their hydrogen-ion\nconcentration ratio under the approximation? Interpret the slope and intercept of \\(\\ln C(t)=3.5-0.2t\\) . Make a table and graph comparing \\(500+30t\\) and \\(500(1.06)^t\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le10\\) . Write R or Python code to compute a decay-model half-life and\nverify the half-value. Fit a two-point exponential model to observations \\((0,30)\\) and \\((6,12)\\) ; explain a limitation. For \\(A(t)=40e^{-0.1t}\\) ,\ncompute average rates on \\([2,3]\\) , \\([2,2.1]\\) , and \\([2,2.01]\\) ; predict the instantaneous rate\nat \\(t=2\\) ."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Extended\napplication: Decay of a fluorescent signal", "url": "chapter07.html#extended-application-decay-of-a-fluorescent-signal", "text": "A synthetic fluorescent signal starts at 80 units and retains 75\npercent of its intensity every hour. The quantities in this\ninvestigation are illustrative rather than measured observations."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter07.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[I(t)=80(0.75)^t.\\] Before calculating,\nidentify the input and output, their units, and the interval on which\nthe model is being studied. Distinguish the mathematical domain from the\npractical domain. The numerical study uses inputs from \\(0\\) to \\(12\\) . Example 15: Analytical investigation. Derive the\nhalf-life and the time at which intensity reaches 10 units. Explain why\nthe model never reaches zero. Worked solution. \\(t_{1/2}=\\ln(0.5)/\\ln(0.75)\\) and \\(t_{10}=\\ln(1/8)/\\ln(0.75)\\) . Positive\nexponential factors never vanish."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter07.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Time (h) Intensity 0 80 3 33.75 6 14.24 9 6.007 12 2.534 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter07.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Decay of a fluorescent signal # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 12 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 80 * 0.75 ^ x # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Time (h)\" , ylab = \"Intensity\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter07.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Decay of a fluorescent signal import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 12 , 301 ) y = 80 * 0.75 ** x # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Time (h)\" , ylabel = \"Intensity\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter07.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Decay of a fluorescent signal", "url": "chapter07.html#chapter-project-investigating-decay-of-a-fluorescent-signal", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter07.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Chapter summary", "url": "chapter07.html#chapter-summary", "text": "Exponential models describe constant proportional change, while\nlogarithms reverse exponentiation and solve time-to-threshold questions.\nDomains, positive arguments, units, and model assumptions are essential.\nGraphs, tables, and computation provide checks, but do not replace\nmathematical explanation or validation against observations."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions", "section": "Looking ahead", "url": "chapter07.html#looking-ahead", "text": "The next chapter uses radian measure and the unit circle to develop\nperiodic phenomena. The habits practiced here—matching formulas to\ngraphs, identifying domains, interpreting parameters, and checking\nassumptions—remain central throughout trigonometry and calculus."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "url": "chapter08.html#angles-radian-measure-and-the-unit-circle", "text": ""}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Opening context:\nmeasuring rotation in science", "url": "chapter08.html#opening-context-measuring-rotation-in-science", "text": "A laboratory centrifuge rotates thousands of times per minute, an\nimaging arm sweeps through a sector, and a point on a wheel travels\nalong a circular arc. All three situations require a common mathematical\nlanguage for rotation. This chapter builds that language, beginning with\ndirected angles and radians and ending with unit-circle coordinates,\nexact trigonometric values, and applied circular-motion models."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter08.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "After completing this chapter, students should be able to convert\nbetween degrees and radians; identify coterminal and reference angles;\ncalculate arc length and sector area; derive exact unit-circle\ncoordinates; evaluate all six trigonometric functions and identify where\nthey are undefined; use symmetry and periodicity; model angular and\ntangential speed; and explore these ideas in R and Python."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Prerequisite check", "url": "chapter08.html#prerequisite-check", "text": "Review the distance formula, the Pythagorean theorem, similarity of\ntriangles, coordinate quadrants, and elementary function notation. In\nparticular, the equation \\(x^2+y^2=r^2\\) describes a circle of radius \\(r\\) centered at the origin."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "How to use the\ncomputational laboratories", "url": "chapter08.html#how-to-use-the-computational-laboratories", "text": "Predict and calculate by hand first. Then run the complete R listing\nand the corresponding Python listing, which are presented sequentially\nrather than side by side. Compare exact and approximate answers and\ninterpret units."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Angles as rotations and\ndirected angles", "url": "chapter08.html#angles-as-rotations-and-directed-angles", "text": "An angle measures rotation from an initial ray to a terminal ray.\nCounterclockwise rotation is positive and clockwise rotation is\nnegative. The same terminal ray can be reached after any integer number\nof full turns: angles differing by \\(360^\\circ\nk\\) are coterminal. This distinction matters when an angle\ndescribes accumulated rotation rather than just position. Example 1. A wheel rotates \\(-450^\\circ\\) . Find a positive coterminal\nangle and the number of clockwise revolutions. Worked solution. Adding \\(720^\\circ\\) gives \\(270^\\circ\\) . The signed number of\nrevolutions is \\(-450/360=-1.25\\) . The\nterminal position alone does not preserve the total rotation. Guided practice 1. Find two positive and two negative angles coterminal with \\(135^\\circ\\) . Explain whether they represent\nidentical motions. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Degree and radian measure", "url": "chapter08.html#degree-and-radian-measure", "text": "A radian is the central angle that subtends an arc whose length\nequals the circle radius. One full revolution is \\(2\\pi\\) radians, so \\(180^\\circ=\\pi\\) radians. To convert degrees\nto radians multiply by \\(\\pi/180\\) ; to\nconvert radians to degrees multiply by \\(180/\\pi\\) . Radians are dimensionless\nratios, but explicitly labeling them avoids confusion. Example 2. Convert \\(225^\\circ\\) to radians and \\(-5\\pi/6\\) radians to degrees. Worked solution. \\(225(\\pi/180)=5\\pi/4\\) radians, while \\((-5\\pi/6)(180/\\pi)=-150^\\circ\\) . Guided practice 2. Convert \\(72^\\circ\\) , \\(-315^\\circ\\) , and \\(11\\pi/12\\) radians. Explain why the radian\nconversion is exact. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Arc length and the\ngeometry of radians", "url": "chapter08.html#arc-length-and-the-geometry-of-radians", "text": "If a circle of radius \\(r\\) has\ncentral angle \\(\\theta\\) measured in\nradians, the signed arc displacement is \\(r\\theta\\) and the physical arc length is \\(s=r|\\theta|\\) . This formula follows\nfrom the proportion \\(s/(2\\pi\nr)=|\\theta|/(2\\pi)\\) . It is not correct to insert a degree\nmeasure directly into \\(s=r\\theta\\) . Example 3. A pulley of radius \\(12\\) cm rotates through \\(150^\\circ\\) . How far does a point on its\nrim travel? Worked solution. Convert \\(150^\\circ=5\\pi/6\\) rad. Thus \\(s=12(5\\pi/6)=10\\pi\\approx31.42\\) cm. Guided practice 3. A wheel of radius \\(0.35\\) m turns \\(3.5\\) radians. Find the rim\ndisplacement. Repeat for a clockwise rotation of \\(-3.5\\) radians. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Area of a circular sector", "url": "chapter08.html#area-of-a-circular-sector", "text": "A sector occupies the fraction \\(|\\theta|/(2\\pi)\\) of a full disk.\nConsequently its area is \\(A=\\frac12r^2|\\theta|\\) for \\(\\theta\\) in radians. The area is\nnonnegative even when the directed angle is negative. The relationship\nis geometric, not a new definition of radians. Example 4. Find the area of a sector with radius \\(8\\) cm and angle \\(3\\pi/4\\) . Worked solution. \\(A=\\frac12(8)^2(3\\pi/4)=24\\pi\\approx75.40\\) cm \\(^2\\) . Guided practice 4. A sector of radius \\(6\\) cm has area \\(9\\pi\\) cm \\(^2\\) . Determine its central angle in\nradians and degrees. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Standard\nposition, quadrants, and reference angles", "url": "chapter08.html#standard-position-quadrants-and-reference-angles", "text": "An angle in standard position starts on the positive horizontal axis.\nIts terminal side may lie in one of four quadrants or on an axis. A\nreference angle is the acute angle between the terminal side and the\nhorizontal axis. It is useful for determining signs and exact\ntrigonometric values without memorizing separate triangles in every\nquadrant. Example 5. Find the reference angle for \\(210^\\circ\\) and \\(-45^\\circ\\) . Worked solution. \\(210^\\circ\\) lies in quadrant III, so its\nreference angle is \\(210^\\circ-180^\\circ=30^\\circ\\) . The angle \\(-45^\\circ\\) lies in quadrant IV and\nhas reference angle \\(45^\\circ\\) . Guided practice 5. Find the quadrant and reference angle for \\(7\\pi/6\\) , \\(5\\pi/3\\) , and \\(-2\\pi/3\\) . Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "The unit circle and\ncoordinate definitions", "url": "chapter08.html#the-unit-circle-and-coordinate-definitions", "text": "On the unit circle \\(x^2+y^2=1\\) ,\nthe point reached by angle \\(\\theta\\) has coordinates \\((\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)\\) . Thus cosine is\nthe horizontal coordinate and sine is the vertical coordinate. This\ndefinition extends the right-triangle ratios beyond acute angles. The\nidentity \\(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1\\) follows\nimmediately from the circle equation. Example 6. Find \\((\\cos(2\\pi/3),\\sin(2\\pi/3))\\) . Worked solution. The reference angle is \\(\\pi/3\\) and the terminal side lies in\nquadrant II, so the point is \\((-1/2,\\sqrt3/2)\\) . Guided practice 6. Find the unit-circle coordinates at \\(5\\pi/4\\) and \\(11\\pi/6\\) , then verify the Pythagorean\nidentity. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Special\ntriangles and exact trigonometric values", "url": "chapter08.html#special-triangles-and-exact-trigonometric-values", "text": "A \\(45^\\circ\\) – \\(45^\\circ\\) – \\(90^\\circ\\) triangle has side ratios \\(1:1:\\sqrt2\\) . A \\(30^\\circ\\) – \\(60^\\circ\\) – \\(90^\\circ\\) triangle has ratios \\(1:\\sqrt3:2\\) . Dividing the legs by the\nhypotenuse gives the exact unit-circle coordinates for multiples of \\(\\pi/6\\) and \\(\\pi/4\\) . Exact values communicate structure\nthat decimal approximations can hide. Example 7. Compute \\(\\sin(5\\pi/6)\\) and \\(\\cos(7\\pi/4)\\) exactly. Worked solution. The first is in quadrant II with\nreference angle \\(\\pi/6\\) , giving \\(1/2\\) . The second is in quadrant IV with\nreference angle \\(\\pi/4\\) , giving \\(\\sqrt2/2\\) . Guided practice 7. Evaluate \\(\\sin(4\\pi/3)\\) , \\(\\cos(3\\pi/4)\\) , and \\(\\tan(\\pi/6)\\) exactly. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Tangent and the\nremaining trigonometric ratios", "url": "chapter08.html#tangent-and-the-remaining-trigonometric-ratios", "text": "Where \\(\\cos\\theta\\ne0\\) , define \\(\\tan\\theta=\\sin\\theta/\\cos\\theta\\) .\nIts reciprocal is cotangent where sine is nonzero; secant and cosecant\nare the reciprocals of cosine and sine, respectively. Undefined values\nare not zero: \\(\\tan(\\pi/2)\\) is\nundefined because its denominator is zero. Example 8. Find \\(\\tan(2\\pi/3)\\) and state whether \\(\\sec(\\pi/2)\\) exists. Worked solution. \\(\\tan(2\\pi/3)=(\\sqrt3/2)/(-1/2)=-\\sqrt3\\) .\nSince \\(\\cos(\\pi/2)=0\\) , \\(\\sec(\\pi/2)\\) is undefined. Guided practice 8. Determine the exact values of \\(\\tan(5\\pi/4)\\) and \\(\\csc(7\\pi/6)\\) and identify all denominator\nrestrictions. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Symmetry,\nperiodicity, and coterminal angles", "url": "chapter08.html#symmetry-periodicity-and-coterminal-angles", "text": "A full rotation returns to the same point, so sine and cosine repeat\nafter \\(2\\pi\\) : \\(\\sin(\\theta+2\\pi k)=\\sin\\theta\\) and \\(\\cos(\\theta+2\\pi k)=\\cos\\theta\\) .\nReflection across the horizontal axis shows that cosine is even and sine\nis odd. Tangent has period \\(\\pi\\) where defined. These properties reduce large and negative angles to\nmanageable ones. Example 9. Evaluate \\(\\sin(-11\\pi/6)\\) without a calculator. Worked solution. Adding \\(2\\pi\\) gives \\(\\pi/6\\) , so \\(\\sin(-11\\pi/6)=1/2\\) . Guided practice 9. Simplify \\(\\cos(-7\\pi/3)\\) and \\(\\tan(13\\pi/4)\\) using periodicity. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Angles from\ncoordinates and inverse trigonometry", "url": "chapter08.html#angles-from-coordinates-and-inverse-trigonometry", "text": "If \\((x,y)\\) lies on a circle of\nradius \\(r>0\\) , then \\(\\cos\\theta=x/r\\) and \\(\\sin\\theta=y/r\\) . An inverse sine or cosine\nreturns a principal angle, not necessarily the angle describing the\noriginal point’s quadrant. The two-argument arctangent function (called atan2 in R and Python) uses both coordinates to recover the\nappropriate direction. Example 10. A point is \\((-3,4)\\) relative to the origin. Find its\nradius, sine, cosine, and approximate direction. Worked solution. \\(r=5\\) , so \\(\\sin\\theta=4/5\\) and \\(\\cos\\theta=-3/5\\) . The direction is in\nquadrant II: \\(\\theta\\approx126.87^\\circ\\) . Guided practice 10. A displacement vector is \\((5,-12)\\) . Determine its radius, sine,\ncosine, and standard-position angle. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Angular and linear speed", "url": "chapter08.html#angular-and-linear-speed", "text": "Angular speed is \\(\\omega=\\Delta\\theta/\\Delta t\\) in radians\nper unit time. A point at radius \\(r\\) has tangential speed \\(v=r|\\omega|\\) .\nRevolutions per minute must be converted to radians per second when SI\nunits are used. This relationship connects trigonometry to rotating\nmachinery, laboratory centrifuges, and motion models. Example 11. A rotor turns at 120 revolutions per\nminute. Find its angular speed in rad/s and the speed of a point \\(0.10\\) m from its center. Worked solution. \\(120/60=2\\) rev/s, hence \\(\\omega=4\\pi\\) rad/s and \\(v=0.10(4\\pi)=0.4\\pi\\approx1.257\\) m/s. Guided practice 11. A disk of radius \\(0.18\\) m spins at\n75 rpm. Find angular and rim speeds, carefully tracking units. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Circular motion as a\nparametric model", "url": "chapter08.html#circular-motion-as-a-parametric-model", "text": "For uniform circular motion of radius \\(r\\) and angular speed \\(\\omega\\) , position can be written \\(x(t)=r\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)\\) and \\(y(t)=r\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)\\) . The phase \\(\\phi\\) determines the starting\nlocation. The resulting path is a circle, although each coordinate\nvaries periodically in time. This is an important bridge to sinusoidal\nfunctions and parametric curves. Example 12. A point travels on a circle of radius 3\nwith \\(\\omega=\\pi/2\\) rad/s, starting\nat \\((3,0)\\) . Find its position at \\(t=1\\) second. Worked solution. \\(x(1)=3\\cos(\\pi/2)=0\\) and \\(y(1)=3\\sin(\\pi/2)=3\\) , so the point is \\((0,3)\\) . Guided practice 12. Model a point moving clockwise on a circle of radius 2, starting at \\((0,2)\\) with angular speed \\(\\pi\\) rad/s. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Applications and model\ninterpretation", "url": "chapter08.html#applications-and-model-interpretation", "text": "A mathematical model must state whether an angle is a rotation, a\ndirection, or a measured central angle; whether the radius is constant;\nand whether a signed or unsigned quantity is needed. In a biomedical\nimaging system, a scanning arm may sweep through a sector; in a\ncentrifuge, the radial position affects tangential speed. The formulas\nare useful only when the assumptions match the physical situation. Example 13. A scanning device sweeps through \\(110^\\circ\\) at a fixed radius of 0.4 m.\nCompute arc length and sector area. Worked solution. \\(\\theta=11\\pi/18\\) radians. Then \\(s=0.4(11\\pi/18)\\approx0.768\\) m and \\(A=\\frac12(0.4)^2(11\\pi/18)\\approx0.154\\) m \\(^2\\) . Guided practice 13. A scanning arm doubles its radius while retaining the same angular\nsweep. Predict and verify the factors by which arc length and sector\narea change. Reasoning check. Before using a calculator, predict the quadrant, sign, units, and\nwhether the result is exact or approximate. Explain one common mistake a\nstudent could make here."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Visual investigations", "url": "chapter08.html#visual-investigations", "text": ""}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Investigation 1:\nOne full turn and the radian scale", "url": "chapter08.html#investigation-1-one-full-turn-and-the-radian-scale", "text": "image Trace the circle counterclockwise. Explain why one revolution is \\(2\\pi\\) radians and why a negative\nangle reverses the direction. Mark \\(-\\pi/2\\) and \\(5\\pi/2\\) on the same diagram and discuss\ntheir different accumulated rotations."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Investigation\n2: Arc length grows linearly in radius", "url": "chapter08.html#investigation-2-arc-length-grows-linearly-in-radius", "text": "image At fixed \\(\\theta=\\pi/3\\) , compare\nradii \\(r=1,2,3\\) . Build a table of \\(s=r\\theta\\) and \\(A=r^2\\theta/2\\) . Which quantity doubles and\nwhich quadruples when the radius doubles?"}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Investigation 3:\nExact unit-circle coordinates", "url": "chapter08.html#investigation-3-exact-unit-circle-coordinates", "text": "image Use the \\(30\\) – \\(60\\) – \\(90\\) triangle to derive the coordinates at \\(\\pi/3\\) . Reflect the point across the\nvertical and horizontal axes to obtain three other exact coordinates.\nVerify \\(x^2+y^2=1\\) in every case."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Investigation\n4: Periodic motion and coordinate traces", "url": "chapter08.html#investigation-4-periodic-motion-and-coordinate-traces", "text": "Sketch \\(x(t)=2\\cos t\\) and \\(y(t)=2\\sin t\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le 2\\pi\\) . On a separate set of axes\nsketch the parametric path \\((x(t),y(t))\\) . Why do two wave-like\ncoordinate graphs describe one circular path?"}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Problem-solving workshops", "url": "chapter08.html#problem-solving-workshops", "text": ""}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Workshop\nA: Centrifuge calculations", "url": "chapter08.html#workshop-a-centrifuge-calculations", "text": "A rotor spins at 3,000 rpm and a specimen lies 8 cm from the center.\n(a) Convert to revolutions per second. (b) Determine angular speed in\nrad/s. (c) Find tangential speed in m/s. (d) Describe how speed changes\nif the specimen is moved to 12 cm. Worked solution. The rotor makes \\(50\\) rev/s, so \\(\\omega=100\\pi\\) rad/s. At \\(r=0.08\\) m, \\(v=8\\pi\\approx25.13\\) m/s. At \\(r=0.12\\) m, speed is \\(12\\pi\\approx37.70\\) m/s, a 50%\nincrease."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Workshop B:\nImaging geometry", "url": "chapter08.html#workshop-b-imaging-geometry", "text": "A scanning arm of radius 30 cm sweeps through \\(240^\\circ\\) . Determine the arc length and\nsector area; state why the sign of the angle does not change the\nphysical area. Worked solution. The angle is \\(4\\pi/3\\) radians. Arc length is \\(30(4\\pi/3)=40\\pi\\) cm. Sector area is \\((1/2)(30)^2(4\\pi/3)=600\\pi\\) cm \\(^2\\) . The area is nonnegative."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Workshop C: Finding\ndirection from coordinates", "url": "chapter08.html#workshop-c-finding-direction-from-coordinates", "text": "A robot moves from \\((2,1)\\) to \\((-4,9)\\) . Determine its displacement\nvector, length, and counterclockwise direction from the positive\nhorizontal axis. Worked solution. The displacement is \\((-6,8)\\) , its length is \\(\\sqrt{36+64}=10\\) , and the direction is \\(\\operatorname{atan2}(8,-6)\\approx126.87^\\circ\\) ."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: Unit-circle coordinates and exact-angle checks", "url": "chapter08.html#computational-laboratory-a-unit-circle-coordinates-and-exact-angle-checks", "text": "The code samples \\(\\theta\\) around\nthe unit circle, checks \\(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1\\) numerically,\nand draws the circle. First predict the coordinates for \\(0\\) , \\(\\pi/6\\) , \\(\\pi/4\\) , \\(\\pi/3\\) , and \\(\\pi/2\\) ."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter08.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code # Chapter 8: Angles, radians, and the unit circle # Laboratory A: unit-circle coordinates angles <- c ( 0 , pi / 6 , pi / 4 , pi / 3 , pi / 2 , pi, 3 * pi / 2 , 2 * pi) results <- data.frame ( theta= angles, x= cos (angles), y= sin (angles)) results $ identity_error <- results $ x ^ 2 + results $ y ^ 2 - 1 print (results) theta <- seq ( 0 , 2 * pi, length.out= 401 ) plot ( cos (theta), sin (theta), type= \"l\" , asp= 1 , xlab= \"cos(theta)\" , ylab= \"sin(theta)\" , main= \"Unit circle\" ) points (results $ x, results $ y, pch= 19 ) # Laboratory B: arc length and sector area radii <- c ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) angles_b <- c (pi / 6 , pi / 3 , pi / 2 ) model <- expand.grid ( radius= radii, theta= angles_b) model $ arc_length <- model $ radius * abs (model $ theta) model $ sector_area <- 0.5 * model $ radius ^ 2 * abs (model $ theta) print (model) # Laboratory C: circular motion time <- seq ( 0 , 4 , length.out= 401 ) r <- 2 ; omega <- pi / 2 ; phase <- pi / 4 x <- r * cos (omega * time + phase) y <- r * sin (omega * time + phase) par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 2 )) plot (time, x, type= \"l\" , ylim= c ( - r,r), xlab= \"Time (s)\" , ylab= \"x-coordinate\" , main= \"Horizontal coordinate\" ) plot (x, y, type= \"l\" , asp= 1 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"y\" , main= \"Circular path\" ) par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 1 ))"}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter08.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code # Chapter 8: Angles, radians, and the unit circle import math import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # Laboratory A: unit-circle coordinates angles = np.array([ 0 , math.pi / 6 , math.pi / 4 , math.pi / 3 , math.pi / 2 , math.pi, 3 * math.pi / 2 , 2 * math.pi]) for angle in angles: x, y = math.cos(angle), math.sin(angle) print ( f\"theta= { angle :.5f} , x= { x :.5f} , y= { y :.5f} , error= { x * x + y * y - 1 :.2e} \" ) theta = np.linspace( 0 , 2 * math.pi, 401 ) plt.figure() plt.plot(np.cos(theta), np.sin(theta)) plt.scatter(np.cos(angles), np.sin(angles)) plt.axis( 'equal' ) ; plt.xlabel( 'cos(theta)' ) ; plt.ylabel( 'sin(theta)' ) plt.title( 'Unit circle' ) ; plt.tight_layout() ; plt.savefig( 'unit_circle_python.png' ) ; plt.close() # Laboratory B: arc length and sector area for radius in [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]: for angle in [math.pi / 6 , math.pi / 3 , math.pi / 2 ]: print ( f\"r= { radius } , theta= { angle :.4f} , arc= { radius * abs (angle) :.4f} , area= { 0.5 * radius ** 2 * abs (angle) :.4f} \" ) # Laboratory C: circular motion time = np.linspace( 0 , 4 , 401 ) radius, omega, phase = 2 , math.pi / 2 , math.pi / 4 x = radius * np.cos(omega * time + phase) y = radius * np.sin(omega * time + phase) plt.figure() ; plt.plot(time, x) ; plt.xlabel( 'Time (s)' ) ; plt.ylabel( 'x-coordinate' ) plt.title( 'Horizontal coordinate' ) ; plt.tight_layout() ; plt.savefig( 'circular_coordinate_python.png' ) ; plt.close() plt.figure() ; plt.plot(x,y) ; plt.axis( 'equal' ) ; plt.xlabel( 'x' ) ; plt.ylabel( 'y' ) plt.title( 'Circular path' ) ; plt.tight_layout() ; plt.savefig( 'circular_path_python.png' ) ; plt.close() Interpretation. Why are numerical errors typically close to, but not exactly, zero?\nWhy is an exact expression such as \\(\\sqrt3/2\\) often preferable to its decimal\napproximation?"}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: Arc length and sector area", "url": "chapter08.html#computational-laboratory-b-arc-length-and-sector-area", "text": "Modify the radius and angle in the scripts. Make a table for three\nradii and three angles. Compare the linear dependence of arc length on\nradius with the quadratic dependence of sector area on radius. Use\nradians in every formula."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: Circular motion", "url": "chapter08.html#computational-laboratory-c-circular-motion", "text": "The scripts generate the coordinate traces and the circular\ntrajectory of a moving point. Change the angular speed and phase, and\npredict which properties change: radius, period, starting point, and\ndirection."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Extended\nguided practice: reasoning with the unit circle", "url": "chapter08.html#extended-guided-practice-reasoning-with-the-unit-circle", "text": ""}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Investigation\n5: Building a complete exact-value table", "url": "chapter08.html#investigation-5-building-a-complete-exact-value-table", "text": "Start with the first-quadrant angles \\(0\\) , \\(\\pi/6\\) , \\(\\pi/4\\) , \\(\\pi/3\\) , and \\(\\pi/2\\) . Construct a table of sine and\ncosine values using special triangles rather than decimal\napproximations. Then reflect each first-quadrant point into the other\nthree quadrants. Complete the table below, recording the signs before\ninserting exact magnitudes. Angle Reference angle \\(\\cos\\theta\\) \\(\\sin\\theta\\) \\(2\\pi/3\\) \\(\\pi/3\\) \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\) \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\) \\(3\\pi/4\\) \\(\\pi/4\\) \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\) \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\) \\(7\\pi/6\\) \\(\\pi/6\\) \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\) \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\) \\(5\\pi/3\\) \\(\\pi/3\\) \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\) \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1.5cm}}\\) Discuss. Which values are determined by the reference angle, and which by the\nquadrant? Why is the unit-circle approach more general than defining\nsine and cosine only in a right triangle?"}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Investigation\n6: Radian approximation and the small-angle idea", "url": "chapter08.html#investigation-6-radian-approximation-and-the-small-angle-idea", "text": "Consider a unit circle and a small positive central angle \\(h\\) . The chord connecting the two points\nhas length \\(2\\sin(h/2)\\) , while the\narc length is \\(h\\) . For small angles,\nthe chord and arc are close. Evaluate the following expressions\nnumerically for \\(h=0.5\\) , \\(0.1\\) , and \\(0.01\\) radians: \\[\\frac{2\\sin(h/2)}{h},\\qquad \\frac{\\sin\nh}{h}.\\] Describe the trend and explain why angles must be\nexpressed in radians. Do not treat numerical agreement as a formal proof\nof a limit."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Investigation\n7: Signed displacement versus distance traveled", "url": "chapter08.html#investigation-7-signed-displacement-versus-distance-traveled", "text": "A wheel of radius \\(0.25\\) m turns\ncounterclockwise through \\(3\\pi\\) radians and then clockwise through \\(\\pi\\) radians. Find (a) the net angular\ndisplacement, (b) the total angle swept, (c) the net signed rim\ndisplacement, and (d) the total distance traveled by a marked point\nalong the circumference. Distinguish displacement from distance. Worked solution. Net angle is \\(2\\pi\\) rad, while total swept angle is \\(4\\pi\\) rad. Signed arc displacement\nis \\(0.25(2\\pi)=\\pi/2\\) m, while total\ndistance is \\(0.25(4\\pi)=\\pi\\) m. The\npoint returns to its original physical location after a net full\nrevolution, so its straight-line displacement vector is zero."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Further worked\nexamples and diagnostic practice", "url": "chapter08.html#further-worked-examples-and-diagnostic-practice", "text": "Example 14: An angle given in mixed units. A\nmechanical arm rotates \\(45^\\circ\\) counterclockwise and then \\(\\pi/3\\) radians clockwise. Determine the net angle in both degrees and\nradians. Worked solution. Convert \\(\\pi/3\\) rad to \\(60^\\circ\\) . Net angle is \\(45^\\circ-60^\\circ=-15^\\circ\\) , or \\(-\\pi/12\\) rad. The negative sign means the\nnet rotation is clockwise. Try it. Reverse the order of the two rotations. Does the net angle change?\nDoes the path taken by the arm necessarily remain the same? Example 15: Finding an angle from sector area. A\nsector of radius \\(15\\) cm has area \\(45\\pi\\) cm \\(^2\\) . Find its central angle, and then\ncalculate the arc length. Worked solution. Using \\(45\\pi=\\frac12(15)^2\\theta\\) , we obtain \\(\\theta=\\frac{90\\pi}{225}=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}\\) rad. Therefore \\(s=15(2\\pi/5)=6\\pi\\) cm. Checking dimensions, area uses cm \\(^2\\) and arc length uses cm. Try it. If the radius were doubled while sector area stayed fixed, what would\nhappen to the central angle? Example 16: Exact values from a point on the terminal\nside. An angle has terminal side passing through \\((-7,-24)\\) . Find all six trigonometric\nfunctions. Worked solution. The radius is \\(r=\\sqrt{(-7)^2+(-24)^2}=25\\) . Hence \\(\\sin\\theta=-24/25\\) , \\(\\cos\\theta=-7/25\\) , and \\(\\tan\\theta=24/7\\) . Taking reciprocals gives \\(\\csc\\theta=-25/24\\) , \\(\\sec\\theta=-25/7\\) , and \\(\\cot\\theta=7/24\\) . The signs agree with\nquadrant III. Try it. Repeat with the point \\((7,-24)\\) and compare the signs. Example 17: Choosing an inverse-trigonometric\ndirection. A displacement has components \\((-2,-2\\sqrt3)\\) . Find its direction between \\(0\\) and \\(2\\pi\\) . Worked solution. The vector is in quadrant III. Its\nreference angle is \\(\\pi/3\\) because \\(|y/x|=\\sqrt3\\) . Thus the direction is \\(\\pi+\\pi/3=4\\pi/3\\) . A single-argument\narctangent alone would return an angle in the wrong quadrant unless\ncorrected. Try it. Find the direction for \\((2,-2\\sqrt3)\\) and explain the\ndifference."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Applied\ninvestigation: comparing two rotating systems", "url": "chapter08.html#applied-investigation-comparing-two-rotating-systems", "text": "Two laboratory rotors have radii \\(r_1=0.06\\) m and \\(r_2=0.10\\) m. The first rotates at 2,400\nrpm and the second at 1,800 rpm. Convert both rotation rates to radians per second, showing each\nunit conversion. Calculate tangential speeds at the outer edges and identify the\nfaster moving point. How long does each rotor take to complete one revolution? State\nyour answer in seconds. A point on each rotor starts on the positive horizontal axis.\nWrite its \\((x(t),y(t))\\) model\nassuming counterclockwise motion. At \\(t=0.01\\) seconds, calculate\nboth positions numerically and explain whether the points have completed\nan integer number of revolutions. Worked solution. The angular speeds are \\(80\\pi\\) rad/s and \\(60\\pi\\) rad/s, respectively. Their edge\nspeeds are \\(4.8\\pi\\approx15.08\\) m/s\nand \\(6\\pi\\approx18.85\\) m/s; the\nlarger rotor has greater tangential speed despite its lower rpm. Periods\nare \\(1/40=0.025\\) s and \\(1/30\\approx0.0333\\) s. The position models\nare \\((0.06\\cos(80\\pi t),0.06\\sin(80\\pi\nt))\\) and \\((0.10\\cos(60\\pi\nt),0.10\\sin(60\\pi t))\\) . At \\(t=0.01\\) , the first angle is \\(0.8\\pi\\) and the second is \\(0.6\\pi\\) ; neither represents a full\nrevolution."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Conceptual questions for\ndiscussion", "url": "chapter08.html#conceptual-questions-for-discussion", "text": "Why does \\(\\sin\\theta\\) have the\nsame sign for all angles in quadrant II, regardless of their\nmagnitude? Is \\(\\theta=0\\) a reference\nangle? Explain how axis angles differ from quadrant angles. Why does \\(\\tan\\theta\\) repeat\nafter \\(\\pi\\) while sine and cosine\nrequire \\(2\\pi\\) ? Can a sector have negative area? How should a clockwise rotation\nbe handled? What information is lost when a directed angle is reduced to a\ncoterminal angle between \\(0\\) and \\(2\\pi\\) ? Why might a model of constant angular speed be inappropriate for\na real motor as it starts or stops?"}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Chapter review exercises", "url": "chapter08.html#chapter-review-exercises", "text": ""}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Core mathematical\nskills", "url": "chapter08.html#core-mathematical-skills", "text": "Convert \\(315^\\circ\\) to\nradians. Convert \\(-7\\pi/12\\) to\ndegrees. Give three angles coterminal with \\(-80^\\circ\\) . Find the reference angle of \\(11\\pi/6\\) . Find the reference angle of \\(-5\\pi/4\\) . A circle has radius \\(9\\) cm and\nangle \\(2\\pi/5\\) . Find the arc\nlength. A circle has radius \\(4\\) m and\nsector angle \\(135^\\circ\\) . Find its\narea. An arc has length \\(15\\) cm on a\ncircle of radius \\(6\\) cm. Find the\nangle in radians. Evaluate \\(\\sin(7\\pi/6)\\) exactly. Evaluate \\(\\cos(5\\pi/3)\\) exactly. Evaluate \\(\\tan(3\\pi/4)\\) exactly. Find \\(\\sec(2\\pi/3)\\) and state\nthe relevant restriction. Explain why \\(\\tan(\\pi/2)\\) is\nundefined. Find \\(\\sin(-13\\pi/6)\\) using\nperiodicity. Find \\(\\cos(17\\pi/4)\\) using\nperiodicity. Verify \\(\\sin^2(5\\pi/6)+\\cos^2(5\\pi/6)=1\\) . Find sine and cosine of an angle passing through \\((-5,12)\\) . Find the quadrant of the point \\((\\cos(4\\pi/3),\\sin(4\\pi/3))\\) . Find the principal direction of the vector \\((-8,-6)\\) in degrees between \\(0\\) and \\(360\\) . Explain the difference between \\(\\arctan(y/x)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{atan2}(y,x)\\) . A wheel rotates \\(90\\) rpm.\nExpress its angular speed in rad/s. A point 20 cm from a rotation axis has angular speed \\(6\\) rad/s. Find its tangential\nspeed. A wheel has radius \\(0.5\\) m.\nHow many revolutions cover a path length of \\(10\\pi\\) m without slipping? Write parametric equations for radius \\(4\\) , speed \\(2\\) rad/s, and initial angle \\(\\pi/2\\) . Find the period of \\(x(t)=3\\cos(5t)\\) . Explain why \\(s=r\\theta\\) requires radians. Compare arc lengths for radii \\(r\\) and \\(3r\\) at a fixed angle. Compare sector areas for radii \\(r\\) and \\(3r\\) at a fixed angle. A scanning arm of radius 0.6 m sweeps \\(75^\\circ\\) . Compute arc length and sector\narea. Explain the calculus significance of the ratio \\(\\sin h/h\\) as \\(h\\) approaches zero in radians."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter08.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "Radians become especially important in calculus because the\nfundamental limit \\(\\lim_{h\\to0}(\\sin\nh)/h=1\\) holds when \\(h\\) is\nmeasured in radians. In later chapters, this result helps explain\nderivatives of sine and cosine. For now, calculate \\(\\sin(h)/h\\) for \\(h=0.5,0.1,0.01\\) radians, compare it with\nthe same numerical inputs interpreted as degrees, and explain why the\nunits matter."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Extended\napplication: Rotating imaging platform", "url": "chapter08.html#extended-application-rotating-imaging-platform", "text": "A marker sits 12 cm from the center of a rotating platform. Its angle\nis measured counterclockwise from the positive horizontal axis. The\nquantities in this investigation are illustrative rather than measured\nobservations."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter08.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[X(\\theta)=12\\cos\\theta.\\] Before\ncalculating, identify the input and output, their units, and the\ninterval on which the model is being studied. Distinguish the\nmathematical domain from the practical domain. The numerical study uses\ninputs from \\(0\\) to \\(6.28\\) . Example 18: Analytical investigation. Find the arc\nlength at \\(\\theta=\\pi/3\\) and both\ncoordinates. Identify all angles in one revolution where the horizontal\ncoordinate is zero. Worked solution. Arc length \\(4\\pi\\) cm; coordinates \\((6,6\\sqrt3)\\) cm. Zeros at \\(\\pi/2\\) and \\(3\\pi/2\\) ."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter08.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Angle (rad) Horizontal position (cm) 0 12 1.57 -1.241e-12 3.14 -12 4.71 3.717e-12 6.28 12 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter08.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Rotating imaging platform # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 6.28318530718 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 12 * cos (x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Angle (rad)\" , ylab = \"Horizontal position (cm)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter08.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Rotating imaging platform import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 6.28318530718 , 301 ) y = 12 * np.cos(x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Angle (rad)\" , ylabel = \"Horizontal position (cm)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter08.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Rotating imaging platform", "url": "chapter08.html#chapter-project-investigating-rotating-imaging-platform", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter08.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle", "section": "Chapter summary", "url": "chapter08.html#chapter-summary", "text": "One full revolution is \\(360^\\circ=2\\pi\\) radians. Arc length and sector area are \\(s=r|\\theta|\\) and \\(A=\\frac12r^2|\\theta|\\) for\nradians. Unit-circle coordinates are \\((\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)\\) and satisfy \\(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1\\) . Reference angles and quadrant signs determine exact trigonometric\nvalues. Angular and tangential speeds satisfy \\(v=r|\\omega|\\) . Uniform circular motion can be modeled parametrically with sine\nand cosine."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "url": "chapter09.html#trigonometric-functions-and-their-graphs", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Opening\ncontext: periodic signals in health sciences", "url": "chapter09.html#opening-context-periodic-signals-in-health-sciences", "text": "Breathing, heart rhythms, daily temperature cycles, and rotating\nsensors motivate the study of periodic functions. This chapter develops\ngraph-based and algebraic models from the unit-circle ideas of Chapter\n8."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter09.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "Students will sketch and interpret sine, cosine, tangent, cotangent,\nsecant, and cosecant; identify amplitude, midline, period, and phase\nshift; construct periodic models from data; solve basic trigonometric\nequations; and use R and Python to investigate model behavior."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Prerequisite check", "url": "chapter09.html#prerequisite-check", "text": "Review radians, the unit circle, exact values at multiples of \\(\\pi/6\\) and \\(\\pi/4\\) , and function transformations from\nChapter 5."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Computational\nworkflow", "url": "chapter09.html#computational-workflow", "text": "For each investigation, first predict the mathematics, then run the R\nlisting, followed by the equivalent Python listing. The listings are\ndeliberately sequential rather than side-by-side."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "From circular motion\nto periodic functions", "url": "chapter09.html#from-circular-motion-to-periodic-functions", "text": "A point rotating counterclockwise on the unit circle through angle \\(t\\) has horizontal and vertical\ncoordinates \\(\\cos t\\) and \\(\\sin t\\) . Plotting these coordinates\nagainst \\(t\\) creates the cosine and\nsine graphs. A periodic function satisfies \\(f(t+P)=f(t)\\) for all inputs in its domain\nand some \\(P>0\\) ; the smallest such\npositive \\(P\\) , when it exists, is the\nfundamental period. For sine and cosine, \\(P=2\\pi\\) . Unlike the circle itself, the\ngraph of sine has horizontal input measured in radians and vertical\noutput measured as a coordinate. Example. Find \\(\\sin(13\\pi/6)\\) and explain the repetition.\nSince \\(13\\pi/6=2\\pi+\\pi/6\\) ,\nperiodicity gives \\(\\sin(13\\pi/6)=1/2\\) . Guided practice. Use periodicity to evaluate \\(\\cos(-11\\pi/3)\\) and \\(\\sin(17\\pi/4)\\) without a calculator. Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Graphing sine and cosine", "url": "chapter09.html#graphing-sine-and-cosine", "text": "The five quarter-period points of \\(y=\\sin\nx\\) on \\([0,2\\pi]\\) are \\((0,0)\\) , \\((\\pi/2,1)\\) , \\((\\pi,0)\\) , \\((3\\pi/2,-1)\\) , and \\((2\\pi,0)\\) . For \\(y=\\cos x\\) , the corresponding outputs are \\(1,0,-1,0,1\\) . Smoothness between\nthese points is important: connecting them with straight lines would not\nreproduce the trigonometric functions. Both have domain \\(\\mathbb R\\) and range \\([-1,1]\\) . Sine is odd, \\(\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x\\) ; cosine is even, \\(\\cos(-x)=\\cos x\\) . Example. Locate all maxima of \\(\\cos x\\) on \\([-2\\pi,2\\pi]\\) . They occur at \\(-2\\pi,0,2\\pi\\) . Guided practice. Sketch two full cycles of sine and\ncosine; label zeros, extrema, and the period. Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate. image"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Amplitude, midline, and\nvertical changes", "url": "chapter09.html#amplitude-midline-and-vertical-changes", "text": "Consider \\(y=A\\sin x+D\\) or \\(y=A\\cos x+D\\) . The amplitude is \\(|A|\\) and the midline is \\(y=D\\) . When \\(A\\ne0\\) , the range is \\([D-|A|,D+|A|]\\) . A negative \\(A\\) reflects the basic curve about its\nmidline. Amplitude is a nonnegative distance, never a signed\nquantity. Example. For \\(y=-3\\cos\nx+2\\) , the amplitude is \\(3\\) ,\nmidline \\(2\\) , maximum \\(5\\) , and minimum \\(-1\\) . At \\(x=0\\) , the output is \\(-1\\) . Guided practice. Determine amplitude, midline,\nmaximum, and minimum for \\(y=4\\sin\nx-1\\) and \\(y=-\\tfrac12\\cos\nx+3\\) . Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Period and horizontal\nscaling", "url": "chapter09.html#period-and-horizontal-scaling", "text": "For \\(y=A\\sin(Bx)+D\\) and \\(y=A\\cos(Bx)+D\\) with \\(B\\ne0\\) , the fundamental period is \\(P=2\\pi/|B|\\) . The parameter \\(B\\) controls the horizontal scale, not the\namplitude. Larger \\(|B|\\) means more\ncycles in the same interval. The quarter-period is \\(P/4\\) , useful when constructing graphs by\nhand. Example. For \\(y=2\\sin(3x)\\) , the period is \\(2\\pi/3\\) and quarter-period \\(\\pi/6\\) . Starting at \\(x=0\\) , the five key outputs are \\(0,2,0,-2,0\\) . Guided practice. Find the periods of \\(\\cos(x/4)\\) and \\(\\sin(-5x)\\) and sketch one cycle of\neach. Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Phase shifts and\nthe complete sinusoidal model", "url": "chapter09.html#phase-shifts-and-the-complete-sinusoidal-model", "text": "A flexible model is \\(f(x)=A\\sin(B(x-C))+D\\) or \\(A\\cos(B(x-C))+D\\) . Here \\(C\\) shifts the graph horizontally to the\nright when positive. The period remains \\(2\\pi/|B|\\) ; \\(D\\) sets the midline. The equivalent\nexpression \\(A\\sin(Bx+\\phi)+D\\) has\nphase shift \\(-\\phi/B\\) (when \\(B\\ne0\\) ). It is essential to factor \\(B\\) before reading the shift. Example. Rewrite \\(2\\sin(3x-\\pi)+1\\) as \\(2\\sin(3(x-\\pi/3))+1\\) . Its amplitude is \\(2\\) , period \\(2\\pi/3\\) , right shift \\(\\pi/3\\) , and midline \\(1\\) . Guided practice. Identify the four parameters of \\(-4\\cos(2(x+\\pi/6))-3\\) and draw a\nkey-point table. Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate. image"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Constructing a\nsinusoid from a graph or data", "url": "chapter09.html#constructing-a-sinusoid-from-a-graph-or-data", "text": "When a graph has maximum \\(M\\) and\nminimum \\(m\\) , compute \\(A=(M-m)/2\\) and \\(D=(M+m)/2\\) . Measure the horizontal\nseparation between consecutive peaks to obtain \\(P\\) , then set \\(|B|=2\\pi/P\\) . Choose sine or cosine and\ndetermine a shift using a known maximum or midline crossing. Multiple\nequivalent formulas may represent the same function. Example. A periodic measurement has maximum \\(9\\) , minimum \\(3\\) , and successive maxima at \\(t=2\\) and \\(t=10\\) . Then \\(A=3\\) , \\(D=6\\) , \\(P=8\\) , and a cosine model is \\(f(t)=6+3\\cos(\\pi(t-2)/4)\\) . Guided practice. Build a cosine model for maximum \\(12\\) , minimum \\(4\\) , and peaks at \\(t=1\\) and \\(t=7\\) . Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Tangent and cotangent graphs", "url": "chapter09.html#tangent-and-cotangent-graphs", "text": "Since \\(\\tan x=\\sin x/\\cos x\\) ,\ntangent is undefined at \\(x=\\pi/2+k\\pi\\) . These are vertical\nasymptotes. Tangent has period \\(\\pi\\) ,\nrange \\(\\mathbb R\\) , and zeros at \\(k\\pi\\) . The cotangent function \\(\\cot x=\\cos x/\\sin x\\) is undefined at \\(k\\pi\\) and has zeros at \\(\\pi/2+k\\pi\\) . Both require careful domain\nanalysis; asymptotes are not points on the graph. Example. For \\(y=\\tan(2x)\\) , the period is \\(\\pi/2\\) , and asymptotes satisfy \\(2x=\\pi/2+k\\pi\\) , giving \\(x=\\pi/4+k\\pi/2\\) . Guided practice. Find all zeros and asymptotes of \\(y=\\tan(x-\\pi/4)\\) on \\([-\\pi,\\pi]\\) . Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate. image"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Secant and cosecant graphs", "url": "chapter09.html#secant-and-cosecant-graphs", "text": "The reciprocal functions are \\(\\sec\nx=1/\\cos x\\) and \\(\\csc x=1/\\sin\nx\\) . They have no outputs strictly between \\(-1\\) and \\(1\\) . Their domains exclude zeros of the\ndenominator, creating vertical asymptotes. Their graph branches touch \\(y=\\pm1\\) at extrema of the\ncorresponding sine or cosine graph. Example. \\(\\csc x\\) is undefined at \\(k\\pi\\) , has value \\(1\\) at \\(\\pi/2+2k\\pi\\) , and value \\(-1\\) at \\(3\\pi/2+2k\\pi\\) . Guided practice. Explain why \\(\\sec x\\) cannot equal \\(0.5\\) for any real \\(x\\) . Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate. image"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Inverse\ntrigonometric functions and restricted domains", "url": "chapter09.html#inverse-trigonometric-functions-and-restricted-domains", "text": "Sine, cosine, and tangent are not one-to-one on their full natural\ndomains, so inverse functions require restricted branches. \\(\\arcsin:[-1,1]\\to[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]\\) , \\(\\arccos:[-1,1]\\to[0,\\pi]\\) , and \\(\\arctan:\\mathbb R\\to(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)\\) . These\nreturn principal values; solving an equation like \\(\\sin x=a\\) requires periodic families of\nsolutions. Example. \\(\\arcsin(1/2)=\\pi/6\\) , but \\(\\sin x=1/2\\) has solutions \\(x=\\pi/6+2k\\pi\\) or \\(x=5\\pi/6+2k\\pi\\) . Guided practice. Evaluate \\(\\arccos(-1/2)\\) and solve \\(\\cos x=-1/2\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Modeling periodic\nhealth-science measurements", "url": "chapter09.html#modeling-periodic-health-science-measurements", "text": "Periodic models approximate repeating measurements such as breathing\ndisplacement, circadian variation, and instrument oscillations.\nParameters must have units: \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) share the response unit, \\(B\\) has radians per unit time, and \\(C\\) has time units. A sinusoid is a\ndescriptive approximation; actual biological signals may be noisy,\nasymmetric, and nonstationary. Example. A simplified respiratory displacement is \\(d(t)=1.5\\sin(\\pi t/2)\\) cm. Its\namplitude is \\(1.5\\) cm and period \\(4\\) seconds, corresponding to \\(15\\) cycles per minute. Guided practice. A model \\(T(t)=37+0.4\\cos(\\pi(t-4)/12)\\) gives\ntemperature in degrees Celsius with \\(t\\) in hours. Identify its period, midline,\nmaximum, and time of a peak. Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Average rate of\nchange and calculus preparation", "url": "chapter09.html#average-rate-of-change-and-calculus-preparation", "text": "For any function, the average rate of change over \\([a,b]\\) is \\([f(b)-f(a)]/(b-a)\\) . A sinusoid may have a\npositive average rate over an interval even when it decreases elsewhere.\nSmall changes in input can be studied with a difference quotient \\([\\sin(x+h)-\\sin x]/h\\) . The limit as \\(h\\to0\\) leads to the derivative of sine in\ncalculus, and the radian convention is crucial for the familiar\nderivative formula. Example. For \\(f(t)=2\\sin(\\pi t/4)\\) , the average rate on \\([0,2]\\) is \\((2-0)/2=1\\) unit per second. Guided practice. Calculate the average rate of\nchange of \\(\\cos x\\) on \\([0,\\pi/2]\\) and explain its sign. Reasoning check. Explain which conclusions follow from exact mathematics and which\ndepend on a modeling assumption. State the domain and units where\nappropriate."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Visual\ninvestigations and guided workshops", "url": "chapter09.html#visual-investigations-and-guided-workshops", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation 1: Tracing\nthe unit circle", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-1-tracing-the-unit-circle", "text": "Complete a table for angles \\(0,\\pi/6,\\pi/4,\\pi/3,\\pi/2,\\pi,3\\pi/2,2\\pi\\) .\nPlot sine and cosine separately and explain why the vertical coordinate\nof the circle becomes a sinusoidal graph. Reflection. Write a short explanation in words, a symbolic conclusion, and a\ngraphical interpretation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation 2:\nTransformations in stages", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-2-transformations-in-stages", "text": "Start with \\(y=\\sin x\\) . Apply a\nvertical stretch by 2, horizontal compression by 3, right shift by \\(\\pi/6\\) , and upward shift by 1. Write the\nresulting equation and give five key points. Reflection. Write a short explanation in words, a symbolic conclusion, and a\ngraphical interpretation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation\n3: Amplitude and period from observations", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-3-amplitude-and-period-from-observations", "text": "A sensor oscillates between 2 and 10 units, with maxima 6 seconds\napart. Derive a cosine model with a peak at \\(t=1\\) and evaluate the prediction at \\(t=4\\) . Reflection. Write a short explanation in words, a symbolic conclusion, and a\ngraphical interpretation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation 4: The\nmeaning of asymptotes", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-4-the-meaning-of-asymptotes", "text": "Make a table of \\(an x\\) for values\napproaching \\(\\pi/2\\) from both sides.\nExplain why \\(an(\\pi/2)\\) cannot be\nassigned a finite value. Reflection. Write a short explanation in words, a symbolic conclusion, and a\ngraphical interpretation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation 5:\nRespiration and model limitations", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-5-respiration-and-model-limitations", "text": "A simplified respiratory signal is \\(R(t)=2+0.8\\sin(\\pi t/3)\\) liters. Find the\nbaseline, range, cycle duration, and predicted breaths per minute.\nIdentify two reasons real measurements might depart from this model. Reflection. Write a short explanation in words, a symbolic conclusion, and a\ngraphical interpretation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation 6:\nComparing periodic models", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-6-comparing-periodic-models", "text": "Compare \\(f(t)=2\\sin(\\pi t/4)\\) and \\(g(t)=2\\sin(\\pi t/4+\\pi/2)\\) .\nDetermine whether their amplitudes and periods differ; explain the\nhorizontal displacement. Reflection. Write a short explanation in words, a symbolic conclusion, and a\ngraphical interpretation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation 7: Rates of\nchange", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-7-rates-of-change", "text": "Compute the secant slope of \\(\\sin\nx\\) on \\([0,h]\\) for \\(h=0.5,0.1,0.01\\) radians and compare to the\nsame calculations using degree inputs. Reflection. Write a short explanation in words, a symbolic conclusion, and a\ngraphical interpretation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: Sine and cosine transformations", "url": "chapter09.html#computational-laboratory-a-sine-and-cosine-transformations", "text": "Explore how amplitude, period, phase, and midline change a graph.\nPredict the extrema and period before running the code."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter09.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory A: Sine and cosine transformations x <- seq ( - 2 * pi, 2 * pi, length.out= 600 ) f <- 2 * sin ( 3 * (x - pi / 6 )) + 1 plot (x, f, type= \"l\" , col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 , xlab= \"x (radians)\" , ylab= \"f(x)\" , main= \"Transformed sine\" ) abline ( h= 1 , lty= 2 ) cat ( \"Amplitude:\" , 2 , \"Period:\" , 2 * pi / 3 , \"Midline:\" , 1 , \" \\n \" ) # Five key points, one cycle key_x <- pi / 6 + ( 0 : 4 ) * ( 2 * pi / 3 ) / 4 key_y <- 2 * sin ( 3 * (key_x - pi / 6 )) + 1 print ( data.frame ( x= key_x, y= key_y)) # Save a portable image png ( \"chapter09_transformation_R.png\" ) plot (x, f, type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"f(x)\" ) abline ( h= 1 , lty= 2 ) dev.off () # Laboratory B: tangent and reciprocal graphs x <- seq ( - 2 * pi, 2 * pi, length.out= 3000 )"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter09.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory A: Sine and cosine transformations import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt x = np.linspace( - 2 * np.pi, 2 * np.pi, 600 ) f = 2 * np.sin( 3 * (x - np.pi / 6 )) + 1 plt.figure() plt.plot(x,f,label = \"Transformed sine\" ) plt.axhline( 1 ,linestyle = \"--\" ,label = \"Midline\" ) plt.xlabel( \"x (radians)\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"f(x)\" ) plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"chapter09_transformation_python.png\" ) ; plt.close() print ( \"Amplitude:\" , 2 , \"Period:\" , 2 * np.pi / 3 , \"Midline:\" , 1 ) key_x = np.pi / 6 + np.arange( 5 ) * ( 2 * np.pi / 3 ) / 4 key_y = 2 * np.sin( 3 * (key_x - np.pi / 6 )) + 1 for a,b in zip (key_x,key_y): print ( f\"x= { a :.4f} , y= { b :.4f} \" ) # Compare the exact period with measured peak separation print ( \"Period in radians:\" , 2 * np.pi / 3 ) # Laboratory B: tangent and reciprocal graphs Interpretation. Compare the graph against the key-point table constructed by\nhand."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: Tangent and reciprocal functions", "url": "chapter09.html#computational-laboratory-b-tangent-and-reciprocal-functions", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter09.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code sec_y <- 1 / cos (x) tan_y[ abs ( cos (x)) < 0.035 ] <- NA sec_y[ abs ( cos (x)) < 0.035 ] <- NA png ( \"chapter09_reciprocal_R.png\" ) plot (x,tan_y, type= \"l\" , ylim= c ( - 4 , 4 ), col= \"blue\" , xlab= \"x (radians)\" , ylab= \"y\" , main= \"Tangent and secant\" ) lines (x,sec_y, col= \"red\" ) legend ( \"topright\" , c ( \"tangent\" , \"secant\" ), col= c ( \"blue\" , \"red\" ), lty= 1 ) dev.off () cat ( \"Tangent asymptotes: pi/2 + k*pi \\n \" ) # Excluded points are not zero values print ( c ( tan (pi / 4 ), 1 / cos (pi / 3 ))) # Laboratory C: fit a sinusoidal model set.seed ( 9 ) t <- seq ( 0 , 24 , by= 1 ) observed <- 37 + 0.4 * cos ( 2 * pi * (t -4 ) / 24 ) + rnorm ( length (t), 0 , 0.06 )"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter09.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code cosx = np.cos(x) tan_y = np.divide(np.sin(x),cosx) sec_y = np.divide( 1.0 ,cosx) tan_y[np. abs (cosx) < 0.035 ] = np.nan sec_y[np. abs (cosx) < 0.035 ] = np.nan plt.figure() plt.plot(x,tan_y,label = \"tangent\" ) plt.plot(x,sec_y,label = \"secant\" ) plt.ylim( - 4 , 4 ) ; plt.xlabel( \"x (radians)\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"y\" ) plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"chapter09_reciprocal_python.png\" ) ; plt.close() print ( \"Tangent asymptotes: pi/2 + k*pi\" ) print ( \"tan(pi/4):\" ,np.tan(np.pi / 4 )) print ( \"sec(pi/3):\" , 1 / np.cos(np.pi / 3 )) # Laboratory C: periodic model fitting rng = np.random.default_rng( 9 ) t = np.arange( 25 ) Interpretation. Identify excluded input values and explain why plotting routines must\nnot join points across asymptotes."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: Periodic model fitting", "url": "chapter09.html#computational-laboratory-c-periodic-model-fitting", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter09.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code pred <- predict (fit) residual <- observed - pred print ( summary (fit)) cat ( \"RMSE:\" , sqrt ( mean (residual ^ 2 )), \" \\n \" ) png ( \"chapter09_model_R.png\" ) plot (t,observed, pch= 19 , xlab= \"Time (hours)\" , ylab= \"Temperature (C)\" ) lines (t,pred, col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 ) dev.off ()"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter09.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code X = np.column_stack([np.ones( len (t)),np.cos( 2 * np.pi * t / 24 ),np.sin( 2 * np.pi * t / 24 )]) coef = np.linalg.lstsq(X,observed,rcond = None )[ 0 ] pred = X @ coef residual = observed - pred print ( \"Coefficients:\" ,coef) print ( \"RMSE:\" ,np.sqrt(np.mean(residual ** 2 ))) plt.figure() plt.scatter(t,observed,label = \"Observed\" ) plt.plot(t,pred,label = \"Fitted\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Time (hours)\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"Temperature (C)\" ) plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"chapter09_model_python.png\" ) ; plt.close() Interpretation. Compare predicted and observed values; a small residual does not\nprove a model is scientifically appropriate."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Further\nworked examples and problem-solving strategies", "url": "chapter09.html#further-worked-examples-and-problem-solving-strategies", "text": "Example 1: Finding a model from extrema. A signal\nhas maximum \\(14\\) , minimum \\(6\\) , and peaks at \\(t=3\\) and \\(t=11\\) . Worked solution. \\(A=(14-6)/2=4\\) , \\(D=(14+6)/2=10\\) , and \\(P=8\\) , so \\(B=\\pi/4\\) . One model is \\(10+4\\cos(\\pi(t-3)/4)\\) . Example 2: Finding the phase shift. Analyze \\(-2\\sin(4x+\\pi)+5\\) . Worked solution. Factor the argument as \\(4(x+\\pi/4)\\) . Amplitude \\(2\\) , period \\(\\pi/2\\) , left shift \\(\\pi/4\\) , midline \\(5\\) , range \\([3,7]\\) . Example 3: Solving a trigonometric equation. Solve \\(2\\sin x-1=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Worked solution. \\(\\sin\nx=1/2\\) , hence \\(x=\\pi/6\\) or \\(x=5\\pi/6\\) . Example 4: Locating tangent asymptotes. Find the\nvertical asymptotes of \\(y=\\tan(3x-\\pi/2)\\) . Worked solution. Set \\(3x-\\pi/2=\\pi/2+k\\pi\\) , obtaining \\(x=(k+1)\\pi/3\\) , \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\) . Example 5: Distinguishing amplitude from vertical\nshift. Interpret \\(y=-5\\cos(x/2)-2\\) . Worked solution. Amplitude is \\(5\\) (not \\(-5\\) ), midline \\(-2\\) , period \\(4\\pi\\) , range \\([-7,3]\\) . Example 6: Inverse trig and full solution sets. Solve \\(\\cos x=\\sqrt2/2\\) for all real \\(x\\) . Worked solution. The principal inverse value is \\(\\pi/4\\) , but the full solution is \\(x=2k\\pi\\pm\\pi/4\\) , \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\) . Example 7: Periodic model interpretation. A\nheart-rate model is \\(H(t)=72+6\\cos(2\\pi\nt/24)\\) with \\(t\\) in hours. Worked solution. The baseline is \\(72\\) beats/minute, amplitude \\(6\\) beats/minute, and period \\(24\\) hours. The model is illustrative and\nnot a clinical prediction."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Extended\nguided practice: constructing and checking graphs", "url": "chapter09.html#extended-guided-practice-constructing-and-checking-graphs", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation 8: A\nfive-point graphing algorithm", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-8-a-five-point-graphing-algorithm", "text": "For \\(f(x)=A\\sin(B(x-C))+D\\) with \\(B>0\\) , start at \\(x=C\\) and add the quarter-period \\(P/4=\\pi/(2B)\\) successively. The five\noutputs are \\(D,D+A,D,D-A,D\\) . This\ngives a reliable graphing procedure without relying on a calculator. For\ncosine, use \\(D+A,D,D-A,D,D+A\\) instead. The points must be connected by a smooth curve. A negative\nvalue of \\(A\\) reverses the orientation\nof the cycle. Example 8. Build a five-point table for \\(f(x)=-2\\sin(2(x-\\pi/4))+3\\) . Worked solution. Here \\(A=-2\\) , \\(B=2\\) , \\(C=\\pi/4\\) , and \\(D=3\\) . The period is \\(\\pi\\) and quarter-period is \\(\\pi/4\\) . The input points are \\(\\pi/4,\\pi/2,3\\pi/4,\\pi,5\\pi/4\\) , with\noutputs \\(3,1,3,5,3\\) . The range is \\([1,5]\\) . Guided practice. Make a five-point table for \\(g(x)=3\\cos(\\tfrac12(x+\\pi))-2\\) . State the\nlocation of its first maximum and minimum."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation\n9: Equivalent phase-shift descriptions", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-9-equivalent-phase-shift-descriptions", "text": "The identity \\(\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos\nx\\) illustrates that different formulas can describe exactly the\nsame graph. A phase shift is not always uniquely determined unless the\nchoice of sine or cosine and the sign of the amplitude are specified.\nCheck equivalence algebraically using known identities or graphically\nover more than one cycle. Example 9. Compare \\(f(x)=4\\sin(x+\\pi/2)\\) and \\(g(x)=4\\cos x\\) . Worked solution. By the angle-addition identity, \\(\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\sin x\\cos(\\pi/2)+\\cos\nx\\sin(\\pi/2)=\\cos x\\) . Thus \\(f=g\\) for all real \\(x\\) . Guided practice. Express \\(3\\sin(x-\\pi/2)\\) as a\ncosine function and verify at \\(x=0,\\pi/2,\\pi\\) ."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation\n10: Recovering a model from a data table", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-10-recovering-a-model-from-a-data-table", "text": "Suppose a periodic process has observed maximum \\(18\\) at time \\(t=2\\) and observed minimum \\(6\\) at \\(t=7\\) . If these are successive extrema,\nhalf a period is \\(5\\) time units, so\nthe full period is \\(10\\) . The\namplitude is \\((18-6)/2=6\\) and the\nmidline is \\((18+6)/2=12\\) . A\ncompatible model is \\[f(t)=12+6\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{5}(t-2)\\right).\\] Verify that \\(f(2)=18\\) , \\(f(7)=6\\) , and \\(f(12)=18\\) . If the observed extrema are not\nsuccessive, the period cannot be inferred uniquely from these two\ntimes. Guided practice. A temperature record has consecutive peaks at 4 and 28 hours, maximum \\(37.4\\) and minimum \\(36.6\\) degrees Celsius. Construct a model,\nstate the units of each parameter, and explain why its predictions are\napproximate."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation 11:\nSolving by graph intersections", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-11-solving-by-graph-intersections", "text": "The equation \\(2\\sin x=1\\) asks\nwhere the graph of \\(y=2\\sin x\\) intersects the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) .\nDividing by \\(2\\) gives \\(\\sin x=1/2\\) . On one cycle \\([0,2\\pi)\\) , the intersections occur at \\(\\pi/6\\) and \\(5\\pi/6\\) . A graph can suggest solutions,\nbut exact unit-circle reasoning establishes them. Guided practice. Solve \\(3\\cos(2x)=3/2\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) , listing every solution. Check\nyour answer by graphing both sides."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Investigation 12:\nSampling and apparent frequency", "url": "chapter09.html#investigation-12-sampling-and-apparent-frequency", "text": "A computer displays a continuous periodic signal using discrete\nsamples. If a signal is sampled too sparsely, different frequencies can\nappear indistinguishable at the sampled times, an effect known as\naliasing. For example, at integer times \\(t\\) , \\(\\sin(2\\pi\nt)=0\\) and \\(\\sin(4\\pi t)=0\\) ,\nalthough the continuous functions have different periods. Therefore a\ngraph of sample points alone may not reveal the underlying\noscillation. Guided practice. Evaluate \\(\\sin(\\pi t)\\) and \\(\\sin(3\\pi t)\\) at integer \\(t\\) and at half-integer \\(t\\) . Discuss which sampling schedule\ndistinguishes the two functions."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Additional worked\napplications", "url": "chapter09.html#additional-worked-applications", "text": "Example 10: Respiratory cycle and frequency. A\nsimplified signal has the form \\(V(t)=2.4+0.6\\sin(\\pi t/2)\\) liters, with \\(t\\) in seconds. Determine its range\nand number of cycles per minute. Worked solution. The midline is \\(2.4\\) liters and amplitude is \\(0.6\\) liters, so the range is \\([1.8,3.0]\\) liters. Its period is \\(2\\pi/(\\pi/2)=4\\) seconds, corresponding to \\(60/4=15\\) cycles per minute. This\nmodel is illustrative and does not establish a clinical breathing rate\nfor an individual. Example 11: Tidal or circadian phase. A model \\(T(t)=36.9+0.3\\cos(\\pi(t-5)/12)\\) has \\(t\\) measured in hours. Find the first\nmaximum at or after \\(t=0\\) and the\nnext minimum. Worked solution. Cosine is \\(1\\) when its argument is an integer\nmultiple of \\(2\\pi\\) . Thus maxima occur\nat \\(t=5+24k\\) , so the first\nnonnegative maximum is at \\(t=5\\) . The\nnext minimum occurs half a period later at \\(t=17\\) . The maximum is \\(37.2\\) and minimum is \\(36.6\\) degrees Celsius. Example 12: An oscillating sensor. A sensor output\nis modeled by \\(S(t)=5-2\\cos(4\\pi t)\\) volts. Find the period, first minimum, and first maximum after \\(t=0\\) . Worked solution. The period is \\(2\\pi/(4\\pi)=0.5\\) seconds. At \\(t=0\\) , cosine equals \\(1\\) , so the signal starts at its minimum \\(3\\) volts. Half a period later, \\(t=0.25\\) seconds, the signal reaches its\nmaximum \\(7\\) volts. Example 13: Model consistency check. A student\nclaims that \\(f(t)=10+2\\sin(\\pi t/3)\\) has a period of \\(3\\) seconds because\nthe denominator is \\(3\\) . Assess the\nclaim. Worked solution. The coefficient of \\(t\\) is \\(B=\\pi/3\\) , hence \\(P=2\\pi/(\\pi/3)=6\\) seconds. The student has\nconfused the expression inside the sine with the period formula."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Conceptual\ndiscussion and common misconceptions", "url": "chapter09.html#conceptual-discussion-and-common-misconceptions", "text": "Why is the amplitude of \\(-3\\sin\nx\\) equal to \\(3\\) rather than \\(-3\\) ? Explain why \\(\\tan x\\) has\nperiod \\(\\pi\\) while sine and cosine\nhave period \\(2\\pi\\) . Can two sinusoidal functions have the same period and amplitude\nbut different graphs? Give an example. Explain why \\(\\arcsin(\\sin x)\\) is not always equal to \\(x\\) . Why should a scientific model include units for the phase-shift\nparameter? Distinguish a graph’s vertical asymptote from a removable\ndiscontinuity. Why is it misleading to connect sampled measurements with a\nsmooth sinusoid without considering the sampling interval? Explain how the secant slope of sine prepares students for\ninstantaneous rate of change in calculus."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Instructor-guided\nexploration: parameter sensitivity", "url": "chapter09.html#instructor-guided-exploration-parameter-sensitivity", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Exploration A:\nChanging one parameter at a time", "url": "chapter09.html#exploration-a-changing-one-parameter-at-a-time", "text": "Start with \\(f(x)=\\sin x\\) and\ncompare it to \\(2\\sin x\\) , \\(\\sin(2x)\\) , \\(\\sin(x-\\pi/3)\\) , and \\(\\sin x+2\\) . Draw each graph on the same\nhorizontal interval \\([-2\\pi,2\\pi]\\) but use separate coordinate systems to prevent visual clutter. For each\ncomparison, explain what stays invariant: the domain, period, range,\nzeros, or midline. Record your findings in the table below. Function Changed feature Features preserved \\(2\\sin\nx\\) Vertical scale Period, midline \\(\\sin(2x)\\) Horizontal scale Amplitude, midline \\(\\sin(x-\\pi/3)\\) Horizontal location Amplitude, period \\(\\sin\nx+2\\) Vertical location Amplitude, period Interpretation prompt. Is the amplitude of \\(\\sin x+2\\) equal to \\(3\\) because the maximum is \\(3\\) ? Explain carefully using the\nmidline."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Exploration\nB: A graph with two different representations", "url": "chapter09.html#exploration-b-a-graph-with-two-different-representations", "text": "Consider \\(f(x)=2\\cos(3x)\\) . Use the\nidentity \\(\\cos u=\\sin(u+\\pi/2)\\) to\nobtain \\(f(x)=2\\sin(3x+\\pi/2)\\) . Factor\nthe sine argument to show a left shift of \\(\\pi/6\\) . Both formulas have amplitude \\(2\\) , period \\(2\\pi/3\\) , and midline \\(0\\) . Sketch both and explain why the curves\ncoincide even though one description uses cosine and the other sine."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Exploration C: Why\ntangent is not a sinusoid", "url": "chapter09.html#exploration-c-why-tangent-is-not-a-sinusoid", "text": "Although tangent is periodic, it is unbounded and has vertical\nasymptotes. Compare its range with that of \\(A\\sin(B(x-C))+D\\) for finite \\(A\\) . The latter always has a bounded range,\nso no such sinusoidal formula can reproduce tangent on its entire\ndomain. This distinction matters when selecting a model for a scientific\nsignal."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Independent\npractice: interpret, calculate, justify", "url": "chapter09.html#independent-practice-interpret-calculate-justify", "text": "A graph has range \\([-4,10]\\) and period \\(5\\) . Find its amplitude\nand midline, and give one possible sine model. A sinusoidal curve crosses its midline upward at \\(t=2\\) and repeats every \\(12\\) units. Write a model with amplitude \\(3\\) and midline \\(8\\) . A cosine graph reaches a maximum at \\(t=0\\) and its first minimum at \\(t=3\\) . Determine the period and a formula\nwith maximum \\(5\\) and minimum \\(-1\\) . Explain why the period of \\(\\cos(\\pi\nx/6)\\) is \\(12\\) rather than \\(6\\) . Sketch \\(y=\\sec x\\) on \\([-\\pi,\\pi]\\) , marking its asymptotes and\nbranch extrema. Determine all solutions to \\(\\sin(2x)=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) and check how many cycles the\nargument completes. A periodic sensor reading is \\(S(t)=4+2\\sin(\\pi t/5)\\) . Find the first\ntime after \\(t=0\\) when the reading\nreaches its maximum. A student claims that changing \\(C\\) in \\(A\\sin(B(x-C))+D\\) changes the period.\nExplain why this is false. Compare \\(\\arcsin(\\sin(5\\pi/6))\\) with \\(5\\pi/6\\) and explain the\ndifference. Describe how you would test a sinusoidal model using a residual\nplot rather than merely checking whether the graph looks\nsmooth. Study strategy. For each exercise, produce a symbolic answer, a labeled sketch or\ntable, and one sentence interpreting the result. Where relevant, confirm\nthe result with R or Python, but retain the hand calculation as the\nprimary mathematical justification."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Chapter review exercises", "url": "chapter09.html#chapter-review-exercises", "text": "Show algebraic steps and explain graph-based conclusions. Use radians\nunless degrees are explicitly indicated."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Core mathematical\nskills", "url": "chapter09.html#core-mathematical-skills", "text": "Sketch \\(y=\\sin x\\) on \\([-2\\pi,2\\pi]\\) and label all\nextrema. Sketch \\(y=\\cos x\\) on \\([-\\pi,3\\pi]\\) and identify its\nzeros. Find the amplitude and period of \\(3\\sin(2x)\\) . Find the amplitude, midline, and range of \\(-4\\cos x+1\\) . Find the period of \\(\\sin(x/5)\\) . Find the period of \\(\\cos(7x)\\) . Write \\(2\\sin(3x-\\pi)\\) in\nshifted form. Find all key points of one cycle of \\(\\sin(2x-\\pi/2)\\) . Find the zeros of \\(\\sin x\\) on \\([0,4\\pi]\\) . Find the zeros of \\(\\cos x\\) on \\([-\\pi,\\pi]\\) . Locate the vertical asymptotes of \\(\\tan x\\) on \\([-2\\pi,2\\pi]\\) . Find the period and asymptotes of \\(\\tan(4x)\\) . Explain the domain of \\(\\csc\nx\\) . Explain why \\(\\sec x\\) cannot\nhave values in \\((-1,1)\\) . Solve \\(\\sin x=\\sqrt3/2\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Solve \\(\\cos x=-1/2\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Solve \\(\\tan x=1\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Compute \\(\\arcsin(-1/2)\\) and\nexplain the principal range. Compute \\(\\arccos(0)\\) and\nexplain the principal range. Find a sinusoid with maximum \\(8\\) , minimum \\(2\\) , and period \\(6\\) . Construct a cosine model with maximum at \\(t=3\\) , period \\(10\\) , amplitude \\(4\\) , and midline \\(7\\) . Find the average rate of change of \\(\\sin x\\) on \\([0,\\pi/2]\\) . Compare \\(\\sin(x+\\pi)\\) and \\(-\\sin x\\) graphically and\nalgebraically. Explain why \\(\\sin x\\) is odd\nand \\(\\cos x\\) is even. For \\(f(x)=3\\cos(2(x-1))+4\\) ,\nfind its range and period. Describe the transformations in \\(g(x)=-\\tfrac12\\sin(3(x+2))-1\\) . A periodic signal repeats every 12 seconds. Find its angular\nfrequency in radians per second. A wheel completes 90 rotations per minute. Find its angular\nfrequency in radians per second. A temperature model has midline \\(37\\) and amplitude \\(0.3\\) . Find its predicted range. Explain why fitting a sinusoid to noisy measurements requires\nexamining residuals."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter09.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "For \\(f(x)=\\sin x\\) , calculate \\([f(h)-f(0)]/h\\) at \\(h=0.5,0.1,0.01\\) radians. Predict the\nlimiting slope. Explain why radians, not degrees, are the natural input\nunit for calculus."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Extended\napplication: Daily environmental cycle", "url": "chapter09.html#extended-application-daily-environmental-cycle", "text": "A controlled room has a synthetic daily temperature cycle centered at\n22 degrees Celsius with amplitude 3 and a maximum at hour 6. The\nquantities in this investigation are illustrative rather than measured\nobservations."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter09.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[T(t)=22+3\\cos(\\pi(t-6)/12).\\] Before\ncalculating, identify the input and output, their units, and the\ninterval on which the model is being studied. Distinguish the\nmathematical domain from the practical domain. The numerical study uses\ninputs from \\(0\\) to \\(48\\) . Example 14: Analytical investigation. Identify\nperiod, midline, maximum and minimum. Find the first two midline\ncrossings after hour 0. Worked solution. Period 24 h; midline 22; extrema 25\nand 19. Crossings after 0 occur at 12 and 24 h."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter09.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Time (h) Temperature (C) 0 22 12 22 24 22 36 22 48 22 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter09.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Daily environmental cycle # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 48 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 22 + 3 * cos (pi * (x -6 ) / 12 ) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Time (h)\" , ylab = \"Temperature (C)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter09.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Daily environmental cycle import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 48 , 301 ) y = 22 + 3 * np.cos(np.pi * (x - 6 ) / 12 ) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Time (h)\" , ylabel = \"Temperature (C)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter09.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Daily environmental cycle", "url": "chapter09.html#chapter-project-investigating-daily-environmental-cycle", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter09.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs", "section": "Chapter summary", "url": "chapter09.html#chapter-summary", "text": "Sine and cosine convert circular motion into periodic coordinate\ngraphs. Amplitude, period, phase shift, and midline describe sinusoidal\ntransformations. Tangent, cotangent, secant, and cosecant introduce\ndomain restrictions and asymptotes. These ideas support mathematical\nmodels of repeated processes and prepare students to study trigonometric\nlimits and derivatives."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "url": "chapter10.html#trigonometric-identities-and-equations", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Opening context:\nsimplifying periodic models", "url": "chapter10.html#opening-context-simplifying-periodic-models", "text": "A rotating sensor, an oscillating instrument, or a periodic\nphysiological measurement can often be represented by several apparently\ndifferent trigonometric expressions. Identities explain why those\nexpressions agree for every admissible input; equations identify the\nparticular times or angles at which a measurement reaches a prescribed\nvalue. The distinction between an identity and an equation is central to\nthis chapter."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter10.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "By the end of this chapter, students should be able to (1)\ndistinguish identities from equations; (2) use reciprocal, quotient,\nPythagorean, cofunction, and parity identities; (3) prove identities\nwith logically reversible algebra and domain checks; (4) derive and\napply angle-sum, angle-difference, double-angle, and half-angle\nformulas; (5) solve trigonometric equations over intervals and over all\nreal numbers; (6) interpret extraneous solutions and numerical\napproximations; and (7) investigate identities and equations with R and\nPython."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Prerequisite check", "url": "chapter10.html#prerequisite-check", "text": "Review the unit circle and exact values (Chapter 8), graphs and\nperiods of trigonometric functions (Chapter 9), and factoring, quadratic\nequations, and rational expressions (Chapters 1–2 and 6). All angles in\nthis chapter are measured in radians unless otherwise indicated."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Computational\nworkflow", "url": "chapter10.html#computational-workflow", "text": "For each laboratory, first make an algebraic prediction, then execute\nthe complete R listing, followed by the equivalent Python listing. Code\nis presented sequentially, never in side-by-side columns. Numerical\nagreement is evidence for a conjecture, not a proof of an identity."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Identities versus equations", "url": "chapter10.html#identities-versus-equations", "text": "An identity is an equality true for every input where both\nsides are defined. An equation may hold only at selected\ninputs. For example, \\(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1\\) is an identity, whereas \\(\\sin x=1/2\\) holds only for certain\nangles. The common domain matters: \\(\\tan\nx=\\sin x/\\cos x\\) is an identity only when \\(\\cos x\\ne0\\) . Numerical testing can reveal\na false conjecture but cannot establish an identity for infinitely many\ninputs. Example 1. Decide whether \\(\\sin(2x)=2\\sin x\\) is an identity. At \\(x=\\pi/2\\) , the left side is \\(0\\) and the right side is \\(2\\) , so the statement is false. Guided practice 1. Classify \\(\\cos^2x=1-\\sin^2x\\) and \\(\\cos x=0\\) ; state their domains and, for\nthe equation, give two solutions. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Reciprocal and quotient\nidentities", "url": "chapter10.html#reciprocal-and-quotient-identities", "text": "The reciprocal identities are \\(\\csc\nx=1/\\sin x\\) , \\(\\sec x=1/\\cos\nx\\) , and \\(\\cot x=1/\\tan x\\) where the indicated denominators are nonzero. The quotient identities\nare \\(\\tan x=\\sin x/\\cos x\\) and \\(\\cot x=\\cos x/\\sin x\\) . They are\nparticularly useful for rewriting complicated expressions in sine and\ncosine. Always carry exclusions from the original expression: canceling\na factor does not restore a point at which the original expression was\nundefined. Example 2. Simplify \\(\\frac{\\tan x}{\\sec x}\\) . Writing the\nquotient in sine and cosine gives \\(\\frac{\\sin\nx/\\cos x}{1/\\cos x}=\\sin x\\) for \\(\\cos\nx\\ne0\\) . The simplified formula extends to additional inputs, but\nthe original does not. Guided practice 2. Simplify \\(\\frac{\\csc x-\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\) and identify\nevery excluded input. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Pythagorean\nidentities and geometric derivation", "url": "chapter10.html#pythagorean-identities-and-geometric-derivation", "text": "A point \\((\\cos x,\\sin x)\\) on the\nunit circle satisfies \\(u^2+v^2=1\\) ,\nhence \\(\\cos^2x+\\sin^2x=1\\) . Dividing\nby \\(\\cos^2x\\) (when \\(\\cos x\\ne0\\) ) gives \\(1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x\\) . Dividing by \\(\\sin^2x\\) (when \\(\\sin x\\ne0\\) ) gives \\(1+\\cot^2x=\\csc^2x\\) . These identities\nconvert sums of squares into single functions, but square roots require\nattention to signs. Example 3. Given \\(\\sin\nx=3/5\\) and \\(x\\) in quadrant\nII, the Pythagorean identity yields \\(\\cos^2x=16/25\\) . Since cosine is negative\nin quadrant II, \\(\\cos x=-4/5\\) and \\(\\tan x=-3/4\\) . Guided practice 3. If \\(\\tan x=-5/12\\) and \\(x\\) lies in quadrant IV, find \\(\\sin x\\) , \\(\\cos\nx\\) , and \\(\\sec x\\) . Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Even–odd, periodic,\nand cofunction identities", "url": "chapter10.html#evenodd-periodic-and-cofunction-identities", "text": "The unit circle gives \\(\\sin(-x)=-\\sin\nx\\) and \\(\\cos(-x)=\\cos x\\) ;\ntangent is odd wherever defined. Periodicity gives \\(\\sin(x+2\\pi)=\\sin x\\) , \\(\\cos(x+2\\pi)=\\cos x\\) , and \\(\\tan(x+\\pi)=\\tan x\\) . Complementary-angle\nrelationships include \\(\\sin(\\pi/2-x)=\\cos\nx\\) and \\(\\cos(\\pi/2-x)=\\sin\nx\\) . These formulas reduce unfamiliar angles to reference\nangles. Example 4. Evaluate \\(\\sin(-7\\pi/6)\\) . Oddness gives \\(-\\sin(7\\pi/6)=-(-1/2)=1/2\\) . Guided practice 4. Evaluate \\(\\cos(-5\\pi/3)\\) and \\(\\sin(\\pi/2-\\pi/6)\\) exactly. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "A reliable\nstrategy for verifying identities", "url": "chapter10.html#a-reliable-strategy-for-verifying-identities", "text": "Begin with the more complicated side and transform it into the other\nside. Useful moves include factoring, finding common denominators,\nmultiplying by a conjugate, or rewriting in sine and cosine. Do not\nassume the identity by setting the two sides equal and performing steps\nthat might not be reversible. Record restrictions introduced by\ndivision. A proof is a chain of justified equalities on the common\ndomain. Example 5. Verify \\(\\frac{1-\\cos^2x}{\\sin x}=\\sin x\\) for \\(\\sin x\\ne0\\) . Substitute \\(1-\\cos^2x=\\sin^2x\\) ; dividing by \\(\\sin x\\) gives \\(\\sin x\\) . Guided practice 5. Verify \\(\\frac{1-\\sin^2x}{\\cos x}=\\cos x\\) and state\nwhy \\(\\cos x\\ne0\\) is required. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Factoring and\nconjugates in identity proofs", "url": "chapter10.html#factoring-and-conjugates-in-identity-proofs", "text": "The factorization \\(a^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)\\) appears frequently in\ntrigonometric proofs. Multiplying by the conjugate can convert \\(1-\\cos x\\) into \\(\\sin^2x/(1+\\cos x)\\) , provided \\(1+\\cos x\\ne0\\) . A successful simplification\nshould preserve the domain of the original expression even when its\nfinal form looks simpler. Example 6. Show that \\(\\frac{1-\\cos x}{\\sin x}=\\frac{\\sin x}{1+\\cos\nx}\\) on their common domain. Multiply the left numerator and\ndenominator by \\(1+\\cos x\\) : \\(\\frac{1-\\cos^2x}{\\sin x(1+\\cos x)}=\\frac{\\sin\nx}{1+\\cos x}\\) . Guided practice 6. Use a conjugate to simplify \\(\\frac{\\sin x}{1-\\cos x}\\) and state\nrestrictions. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Angle-sum and\nangle-difference formulas", "url": "chapter10.html#angle-sum-and-angle-difference-formulas", "text": "The fundamental addition formulas are \\(\\sin(a\\pm b)=\\sin a\\cos b\\pm\\cos a\\sin b\\) and \\(\\cos(a\\pm b)=\\cos a\\cos b\\mp\\sin a\\sin\nb\\) . They can be motivated geometrically by rotations: applying a\nrotation through \\(b\\) after a rotation\nthrough \\(a\\) has the same effect as a\nrotation through \\(a+b\\) . The signs are\ndifferent in the cosine formula; memorizing the pattern without\nunderstanding it is a common source of error. Example 7. Find \\(\\sin(5\\pi/12)=\\sin(\\pi/4+\\pi/6)=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\frac12=\\frac{\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2}{4}\\) . Guided practice 7. Find \\(\\cos(\\pi/12)\\) exactly using \\(\\pi/4-\\pi/6\\) . Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "The tangent addition formula", "url": "chapter10.html#the-tangent-addition-formula", "text": "Dividing the sine addition formula by the cosine addition formula\ngives \\(\\tan(a+b)=\\frac{\\tan a+\\tan b}{1-\\tan\na\\tan b}\\) whenever all expressions are defined. Similarly, \\(\\tan(a-b)=\\frac{\\tan a-\\tan b}{1+\\tan a\\tan\nb}\\) . The formula cannot be applied blindly at angles where\ntangent is undefined or where the resulting denominator vanishes. Example 8. Compute \\(\\tan(\\pi/12)=\\tan(\\pi/4-\\pi/6)=\\frac{1-1/\\sqrt3}{1+1/\\sqrt3}=2-\\sqrt3\\) . Guided practice 8. Use the tangent sum formula to\ncompute \\(\\tan(5\\pi/12)\\) . Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Double-angle identities", "url": "chapter10.html#double-angle-identities", "text": "Set \\(a=b=x\\) in the addition\nformulas to obtain \\(\\sin2x=2\\sin x\\cos\nx\\) and \\(\\cos2x=\\cos^2x-\\sin^2x=1-2\\sin^2x=2\\cos^2x-1\\) .\nFor tangent, \\(\\tan2x=2\\tan\nx/(1-\\tan^2x)\\) where defined. Choose the cosine form that\nmatches the known information. These formulas also explain why the\nfrequency of a periodic signal can double when it is squared. Example 9. If \\(\\sin\nx=3/5\\) and \\(\\cos x=4/5\\) , then \\(\\sin2x=24/25\\) and \\(\\cos2x=16/25-9/25=7/25\\) . Guided practice 9. Given \\(\\cos x=-5/13\\) and \\(x\\) in quadrant II, calculate \\(\\sin2x\\) and \\(\\cos2x\\) . Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Half-angle and\npower-reduction identities", "url": "chapter10.html#half-angle-and-power-reduction-identities", "text": "From \\(\\cos2u=1-2\\sin^2u\\) and \\(\\cos2u=2\\cos^2u-1\\) , set \\(u=x/2\\) to obtain \\(\\sin^2(x/2)=(1-\\cos x)/2\\) and \\(\\cos^2(x/2)=(1+\\cos x)/2\\) . Thus \\(\\sin(x/2)=\\pm\\sqrt{(1-\\cos x)/2}\\) and \\(\\cos(x/2)=\\pm\\sqrt{(1+\\cos x)/2}\\) ;\nthe quadrant of \\(x/2\\) determines the\nsign. Power-reduction formulas \\(\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos2x)/2\\) and \\(\\cos^2x=(1+\\cos2x)/2\\) will later support\nintegration. Example 10. Find \\(\\sin(\\pi/8)\\) . Since \\(\\pi/8\\) is in quadrant I, \\(\\sin(\\pi/8)=\\sqrt{(1-\\cos(\\pi/4))/2}=\\frac12\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt2}\\) . Guided practice 10. Find \\(\\cos(\\pi/8)\\) and explain the sign\nchoice. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Product-to-sum and\nsum-to-product identities", "url": "chapter10.html#product-to-sum-and-sum-to-product-identities", "text": "Adding and subtracting angle-sum formulas yields \\(2\\sin a\\cos b=\\sin(a+b)+\\sin(a-b)\\) and \\(2\\cos a\\cos b=\\cos(a+b)+\\cos(a-b)\\) .\nAnother useful identity is \\(2\\sin a\\sin\nb=\\cos(a-b)-\\cos(a+b)\\) . These transformations are useful for\nperiodic signal analysis and later integration, but they do not change\nthe physical meaning of the underlying measurements. Example 11. Rewrite \\(\\sin3x\\cos x=\\tfrac12[\\sin4x+\\sin2x]\\) . The\nright side exposes two frequencies in a product of periodic signals. Guided practice 11. Rewrite \\(\\cos4x\\cos2x\\) as a sum and identify its\ntwo angular frequencies. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Solving basic\ntrigonometric equations", "url": "chapter10.html#solving-basic-trigonometric-equations", "text": "To solve \\(\\sin x=c\\) or \\(\\cos x=c\\) , first determine whether \\(c\\in[-1,1]\\) , then find the reference angle\nand the appropriate quadrants. For tangent, solutions repeat every \\(\\pi\\) . Give either all solutions using an\ninteger parameter \\(k\\) or a complete\nlist on the specified interval. Checking endpoints matters when the\ninterval is closed. Example 12. Solve \\(\\sin\nx=\\sqrt3/2\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) .\nThe reference angle is \\(\\pi/3\\) , and\nsine is positive in quadrants I and II, giving \\(x=\\pi/3,2\\pi/3\\) . For all real \\(x\\) , add \\(2k\\pi\\) to each family. Guided practice 12. Solve \\(\\cos x=-1/2\\) on \\([-\\pi,\\pi]\\) and solve \\(\\tan x=1\\) for all real \\(x\\) . Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Factoring and\nquadratic trigonometric equations", "url": "chapter10.html#factoring-and-quadratic-trigonometric-equations", "text": "Treat a trigonometric expression such as \\(\\sin x\\) as an algebraic variable when the\nequation is polynomial in that expression. Factor or use the quadratic\nformula, reject impossible values outside \\([-1,1]\\) , and then solve each remaining\ntrigonometric equation. Dividing by \\(\\sin\nx\\) or \\(\\cos x\\) can\naccidentally discard solutions when that factor is zero; factoring is\nsafer. Example 13. Solve \\(2\\sin^2x-\\sin x-1=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Factor \\((2\\sin x+1)(\\sin x-1)=0\\) . Thus \\(\\sin x=-1/2\\) or \\(1\\) , yielding \\(x=7\\pi/6,11\\pi/6,\\pi/2\\) . Guided practice 13. Solve \\(2\\cos^2x+\\cos x-1=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Equations\ninvolving multiple angles and restrictions", "url": "chapter10.html#equations-involving-multiple-angles-and-restrictions", "text": "For equations such as \\(\\sin(2x)=0\\) , solve first for the entire\ninner angle \\(u=2x\\) , then divide to\nrecover \\(x\\) . When solving on an\ninterval, map its endpoints through the substitution so no roots are\nomitted. Squaring both sides can introduce extraneous solutions, and\nmultiplying by a potentially zero expression can alter the solution set.\nVerify candidates in the original equation. Example 14. Solve \\(\\cos(2x)=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Since \\(2x=\\pi/2+k\\pi\\) , \\(x=\\pi/4+k\\pi/2\\) . The interval gives \\(x=\\pi/4,3\\pi/4,5\\pi/4,7\\pi/4\\) . Guided practice 14. Solve \\(\\sin(3x)=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) and explain how many solutions\nthere are. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Applications:\ntiming and periodic thresholds", "url": "chapter10.html#applications-timing-and-periodic-thresholds", "text": "Suppose a simplified respiratory sensor follows \\(S(t)=2+0.8\\sin(\\pi t/3)\\) , with \\(t\\) in seconds and output in arbitrary\nsensor units. To find times at which \\(S(t)=2.4\\) , solve \\(\\sin(\\pi t/3)=1/2\\) . This yields \\(t=0.5+6k\\) or \\(t=2.5+6k\\) seconds. In the first \\(12\\) seconds, the threshold occurs at \\(0.5,2.5,6.5,8.5\\) . These are mathematical\npredictions; real sensor noise may shift observed crossing times. Example 15. A rotating laboratory marker has height \\(h(t)=5+2\\cos(\\pi t/4)\\) cm. Solve \\(h(t)=6\\) for \\(0\\le t<8\\) . We need \\(\\cos(\\pi t/4)=1/2\\) , so \\(\\pi t/4=\\pi/3,5\\pi/3\\) , giving \\(t=4/3\\) and \\(20/3\\) seconds. Guided practice 15. A periodic temperature model is \\(T(t)=37+0.5\\sin(\\pi t/12)\\) . Find all \\(t\\) in \\([0,24)\\) for which \\(T(t)=37.25\\) and interpret the units. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Preparation\nfor calculus: identities and difference quotients", "url": "chapter10.html#preparation-for-calculus-identities-and-difference-quotients", "text": "The angle-addition formula provides the key algebraic step in the\nderivative of sine. For \\(h\\ne0\\) , \\(\\frac{\\sin(x+h)-\\sin x}{h}=\\sin x\\frac{\\cos\nh-1}{h}+\\cos x\\frac{\\sin h}{h}\\) . In calculus, the radian-based\nlimits \\(\\lim_{h\\to0}(\\sin h)/h=1\\) and \\(\\lim_{h\\to0}(\\cos h-1)/h=0\\) lead to\nthe derivative \\(\\cos x\\) . At this\nstage, students should understand the exact algebraic decomposition; the\nlimit arguments belong to calculus. Example 16. At \\(x=0\\) , the difference quotient for sine\nbecomes \\(\\sin h/h\\) . Evaluate it for \\(h=0.1,0.01,0.001\\) (radians) and\nobserve its approach to \\(1\\) . Guided practice 16. Use the cosine addition formula\nto expand \\([\\cos(x+h)-\\cos x]/h\\) without taking a limit. Concept check. Explain the role of the domain, show intermediate algebraic steps,\nand distinguish an exact identity from a numerical approximation."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Visual investigations", "url": "chapter10.html#visual-investigations", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Investigation\n1: The Pythagorean identity on the unit circle", "url": "chapter10.html#investigation-1-the-pythagorean-identity-on-the-unit-circle", "text": "Draw a unit circle with a point \\(P=(\\cos\nx,\\sin x)\\) in quadrant II. Construct the right triangle to the\nhorizontal axis and label its signed coordinates. Explain why the\nsquared coordinates sum to one regardless of the quadrant. Compare this\ngeometric proof with testing five numerical angles. image"}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Investigation 2: Angle\naddition as rotation", "url": "chapter10.html#investigation-2-angle-addition-as-rotation", "text": "Sketch two consecutive rotations through \\(a=\\pi/6\\) and \\(b=\\pi/4\\) . Compare the final unit-circle\ncoordinates with \\(\\cos(a+b)\\) and \\(\\sin(a+b)\\) from the addition\nformulas. Explain why \\(\\sin(a+b)\\) is\nnot generally \\(\\sin a+\\sin b\\) ."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Investigation 3:\nDouble-angle frequency", "url": "chapter10.html#investigation-3-double-angle-frequency", "text": "Graph \\(y=\\sin x\\) and \\(y=\\sin2x\\) for \\(0\\le x\\le2\\pi\\) . Mark zeros and peaks and\ncompare the periods. Then graph \\(y=\\sin^2x\\) and \\(y=(1-\\cos2x)/2\\) ; explain the perfect\noverlap algebraically. image"}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Investigation 4:\nA sign trap in half-angle formulas", "url": "chapter10.html#investigation-4-a-sign-trap-in-half-angle-formulas", "text": "Evaluate \\(\\cos x\\) at \\(x=4\\pi/3\\) and use it to calculate \\(\\sin(x/2)\\) . Explain why the positive\nsquare root is correct even though \\(\\cos\nx\\) is negative. Repeat for \\(x=10\\pi/3\\) and identify the appropriate\nsign."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Investigation\n5: Equation roots versus identity graphs", "url": "chapter10.html#investigation-5-equation-roots-versus-identity-graphs", "text": "On the same axes, plot \\(y=\\sin x\\) and \\(y=1/2\\) over \\([0,4\\pi]\\) . Count the intersections, report\ntheir exact coordinates, and compare with the general solution families.\nThen compare \\(y=\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x\\) with \\(y=1\\) ; explain why this is a\ndifferent kind of equality. image"}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Investigation 6:\nModel crossing times and units", "url": "chapter10.html#investigation-6-model-crossing-times-and-units", "text": "For \\(S(t)=2+0.8\\sin(\\pi t/3)\\) ,\nconstruct a table every half-second over \\([0,12]\\) . Graph the sensor output and\nhorizontal threshold \\(2.4\\) . Identify\ncrossings graphically, then derive exact times. Describe why numerical\ngrid resolution may miss a crossing."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Extended guided workshops", "url": "chapter10.html#extended-guided-workshops", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Workshop A:\nVerify or refute", "url": "chapter10.html#workshop-a-verify-or-refute", "text": "For each statement, decide whether it is an identity. If true, give a\nproof on the common domain; if false, provide an exact counterexample:\n(a) \\(\\sin(a+b)=\\sin a+\\sin b\\) ; (b) \\(\\sec^2x-\\tan^2x=1\\) ; (c) \\(\\frac{\\sin2x}{2\\sin x}=\\cos x\\) ; (d) \\(\\cos^2x=(1+\\cos2x)/2\\) . Worked\ndiscussion. (a) is false at \\(a=b=\\pi/2\\) . (b) follows by dividing the\nunit-circle identity by \\(\\cos^2x\\) ;\nexclude \\(\\cos x=0\\) . (c) follows from\nthe double-angle identity but only where \\(\\sin x\\ne0\\) . (d) is the power-reduction\nidentity and holds for all real \\(x\\) ."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Workshop B: Exact\nvalues without a calculator", "url": "chapter10.html#workshop-b-exact-values-without-a-calculator", "text": "Find \\(\\sin75^\\circ\\) , \\(\\cos15^\\circ\\) , \\(\\tan15^\\circ\\) , and \\(\\sin22.5^\\circ\\) . Show the decomposition of\neach angle and explain the correct quadrant/sign. Worked\ndiscussion. The respective values are \\((\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2)/4\\) , \\((\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2)/4\\) , \\(2-\\sqrt3\\) , and \\(\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt2}/2\\) ."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Workshop C: A complete\nsolution-set audit", "url": "chapter10.html#workshop-c-a-complete-solution-set-audit", "text": "Solve \\(2\\cos^2x-3\\cos x+1=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi]\\) , listing endpoints\nexplicitly. Compare solving by factoring with dividing by \\(\\cos x\\) . Worked\ndiscussion. \\((2\\cos x-1)(\\cos\nx-1)=0\\) , so \\(\\cos x=1/2\\) or \\(1\\) . The solutions are \\(0,\\pi/3,5\\pi/3,2\\pi\\) . Both \\(0\\) and \\(2\\pi\\) belong to the closed interval."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Workshop D: Physiological\nsignal threshold", "url": "chapter10.html#workshop-d-physiological-signal-threshold", "text": "A simplified sensor reads \\(R(t)=10+3\\cos(\\pi t/6)\\) units. Determine\nits period, find all times in \\([0,24)\\) when \\(R(t)=11.5\\) , and explain how the result\nchanges if the sensor clock is shifted by two seconds. Worked\ndiscussion. The period is \\(12\\) s. The equation becomes \\(\\cos(\\pi t/6)=1/2\\) , giving \\(t=2,10,14,22\\) s. A two-second right shift\nadds two seconds modulo the observation window."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Workshop E: From\nidentity to a calculus formula", "url": "chapter10.html#workshop-e-from-identity-to-a-calculus-formula", "text": "Starting from \\(\\sin(x+h)=\\sin x\\cos h+\\cos\nx\\sin h\\) , subtract \\(\\sin x\\) ,\ndivide by \\(h\\ne0\\) , and group terms.\nInterpret the numerical values of \\(\\sin\nh/h\\) and \\((\\cos h-1)/h\\) for\nsmall positive and negative \\(h\\) . Worked discussion. The result is \\(\\sin x(\\cos h-1)/h+\\cos x\\sin h/h\\) . In\nradians, the two numerical factors approach \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) respectively, suggesting the derivative\nof sine."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Computational\nLaboratory A: Checking identities numerically", "url": "chapter10.html#computational-laboratory-a-checking-identities-numerically", "text": "Goal. Compare both sides of Pythagorean and\ndouble-angle identities over a grid, and investigate floating-point\nerror. Exact algebraic proof remains necessary."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter10.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory A: identities and numerical precision x <- seq ( - 2 * pi, 2 * pi, length.out = 1001 ) pythagorean_error <- sin (x) ^ 2 + cos (x) ^ 2 - 1 double_angle_error <- sin ( 2 * x) - 2 * sin (x) * cos (x) cat ( \"Max Pythagorean error:\" , max ( abs (pythagorean_error)), \" \\n \" ) cat ( \"Max double-angle error:\" , max ( abs (double_angle_error)), \" \\n \" ) plot (x, pythagorean_error, type= \"l\" , col= \"blue\" , xlab= \"Angle (radians)\" , ylab= \"Numerical difference\" , main= \"Floating-point identity check\" ) abline ( h= 0 , lty= 2 )"}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter10.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory A: identities and numerical precision import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt x = np.linspace( - 2 * np.pi, 2 * np.pi, 1001 ) e1 = np.sin(x) ** 2 + np.cos(x) ** 2 - 1 e2 = np.sin( 2 * x) - 2 * np.sin(x) * np.cos(x) print ( \"Max Pythagorean error:\" , np. max (np. abs (e1))) print ( \"Max double-angle error:\" , np. max (np. abs (e2))) plt.plot(x, e1) plt.axhline( 0 , linestyle = \"--\" , color = \"black\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Angle (radians)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Numerical difference\" ) plt.title( \"Floating-point identity check\" ) plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"labA_python.png\" , dpi = 160 ) plt.close() Interpretation. Explain why a maximum discrepancy\nnear machine precision is expected and why numerical agreement at\nfinitely many inputs is not a proof."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Computational\nLaboratory B: Solving trigonometric equations", "url": "chapter10.html#computational-laboratory-b-solving-trigonometric-equations", "text": "Goal. Plot a trigonometric function and a target\nthreshold; compare grid-based approximations with exact roots. The\nbisection routine brackets roots only where the function changes sign,\nso tangent roots and even-multiplicity roots need additional care."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter10.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory B: solving sin(x) = 1/2 numerically f <- function (x) sin (x) - 0.5 x <- seq ( 0 , 4 * pi, length.out= 2001 ) plot (x, sin (x), type= \"l\" , col= \"blue\" , xlab= \"x (radians)\" , ylab= \"y\" , main= \"Sine and target threshold\" ) abline ( h= 0.5 , col= \"red\" , lty= 2 ) roots <- c (pi / 6 , 5 * pi / 6 , 13 * pi / 6 , 17 * pi / 6 ) points (roots, sin (roots), pch= 19 ) print ( data.frame ( root= roots, residual= f (roots)))"}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter10.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory B: solving sin(x) = 1/2 numerically import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt x = np.linspace( 0 , 4 * np.pi, 2001 ) roots = np.array([np.pi / 6 , 5 * np.pi / 6 , 13 * np.pi / 6 , 17 * np.pi / 6 ]) print ( \"Root and residual:\" ) for r in roots: print ( f\" { r :.9f} { np . sin(r) - 0.5 :.2e} \" ) plt.plot(x, np.sin(x), label = \"sin(x)\" ) plt.axhline( 0.5 , color = \"red\" , linestyle = \"--\" , label = \"target\" ) plt.scatter(roots, np.sin(roots), color = \"black\" ) plt.xlabel( \"x (radians)\" ) plt.legend() plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"labB_python.png\" , dpi = 160 ) plt.close() Interpretation. Compare numerical roots with \\(\\pi/6,5\\pi/6,13\\pi/6,17\\pi/6\\) for \\(\\sin x=1/2\\) on \\([0,4\\pi]\\) ."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Computational\nLaboratory C: Periodic sensor thresholds", "url": "chapter10.html#computational-laboratory-c-periodic-sensor-thresholds", "text": "Goal. Simulate a simplified periodic measurement,\nmark its threshold crossings, and verify them algebraically. All\nquantities are illustrative, not clinical recommendations."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter10.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory C: periodic sensor and threshold times sensor <- function (t) 2 + 0.8 * sin (pi * t / 3 ) t <- seq ( 0 , 12 , length.out= 1201 ) crossings <- c ( 0.5 , 2.5 , 6.5 , 8.5 ) plot (t, sensor (t), type= \"l\" , col= \"blue\" , xlab= \"Time (seconds)\" , ylab= \"Sensor units\" , main= \"Illustrative periodic sensor\" ) abline ( h= 2.4 , col= \"red\" , lty= 2 ) points (crossings, sensor (crossings), pch= 19 ) print ( data.frame ( time= crossings, sensor= sensor (crossings)))"}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter10.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory C: periodic sensor and threshold times import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt def sensor(t): return 2 + 0.8 * np.sin(np.pi * t / 3 ) t = np.linspace( 0 , 12 , 1201 ) crossings = np.array([ 0.5 , 2.5 , 6.5 , 8.5 ]) for time in crossings: print ( f\"t= { time :.1f} s, sensor= { sensor(time) :.3f} \" ) plt.plot(t, sensor(t), label = \"sensor\" ) plt.axhline( 2.4 , color = \"red\" , linestyle = \"--\" , label = \"threshold\" ) plt.scatter(crossings, sensor(crossings), color = \"black\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Time (seconds)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Sensor units\" ) plt.legend() plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"labC_python.png\" , dpi = 160 ) plt.close() Interpretation. Describe the period, units,\nthreshold, and the limitations of a noiseless sinusoidal model."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Additional\nguided practice: a structured problem-solving clinic", "url": "chapter10.html#additional-guided-practice-a-structured-problem-solving-clinic", "text": ""}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Clinic 1: What does a\ncomplete proof require?", "url": "chapter10.html#clinic-1-what-does-a-complete-proof-require", "text": "Consider \\(\\frac{\\sec x-\\cos x}{\\tan\nx}=\\sin x\\) . A first attempt might substitute numerical angles\nand observe agreement. A valid proof instead begins with the left side,\nrewrites \\(\\sec x-\\cos x=(1-\\cos^2x)/\\cos\nx=\\sin^2x/\\cos x\\) , and divides by \\(\\tan x=\\sin x/\\cos x\\) to obtain \\(\\sin x\\) . This proof is valid only when \\(\\sin x\\ne0\\) and \\(\\cos x\\ne0\\) , which are precisely the\nrestrictions required by the original left side. The right side is\ndefined everywhere, but the identity is asserted only on the common\ndomain. Try it. Verify \\(\\frac{\\csc x-\\sin x}{\\cot x}=\\cos x\\) .\nBefore simplifying, write down all excluded angles. Then ask whether the\nsimplified expression extends beyond the original domain."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Clinic\n2: Choosing a useful identity", "url": "chapter10.html#clinic-2-choosing-a-useful-identity", "text": "A common difficulty is deciding which identity to apply first. When\nan expression contains \\(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x\\) , use the unit-circle\nidentity. When it contains \\(\\sin x\\cos\nx\\) , consider \\(\\sin2x\\) . When a\nfraction contains \\(1-\\cos x\\) or \\(1+\\cos x\\) , try a conjugate. When an angle\nis a sum of special angles, use an addition formula. When a\ntrigonometric equation contains a square, factor as an algebraic\nquadratic before using the unit circle. Try it. For each expression, name the first identity\nyou would use, then simplify: (a) \\(2\\sin\nx\\cos x\\) ; (b) \\(1-2\\sin^2x\\) ;\n(c) \\(\\sin(\\pi/3+\\pi/4)\\) ; (d) \\(\\frac{1-\\cos^2x}{1+\\cos x}\\) . State the\nrestrictions for (d)."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Clinic 3:\nCounting solutions without missing endpoints", "url": "chapter10.html#clinic-3-counting-solutions-without-missing-endpoints", "text": "Suppose \\(\\sin(2x)=1/2\\) on \\([0,2\\pi]\\) . Set \\(u=2x\\) , so \\(u\\in[0,4\\pi]\\) . Within this interval, \\(u=\\pi/6,5\\pi/6,13\\pi/6,17\\pi/6\\) . Therefore \\(x=\\pi/12,5\\pi/12,13\\pi/12,17\\pi/12\\) .\nSimply solving for \\(u\\) on \\([0,2\\pi]\\) would miss half the answers. The\nsubstitution must transform the interval as well as the equation. Try it. Solve \\(\\cos(3x)=1/2\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) by first solving for \\(u=3x\\) on \\([0,6\\pi)\\) . Verify the number of roots with\na graph."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Clinic 4: Exact\nanswers versus approximate answers", "url": "chapter10.html#clinic-4-exact-answers-versus-approximate-answers", "text": "Exact answers such as \\(\\pi/6\\) preserve mathematical structure. A decimal approximation such as \\(0.523599\\) can be useful for a time\nmeasurement, but it is not identical to the exact number. In\napplications, state the input units and round only after solving. If the\nmodel is approximate, do not report excessive precision merely because\nthe calculator supplies many digits. Try it. A sensor has model \\(S(t)=3+\\sin(\\pi t/4)\\) , with \\(t\\) measured in seconds. Find every\ncrossing of \\(S(t)=3.5\\) in \\([0,16)\\) . Report exact times and then\napproximate to the nearest hundredth of a second. Explain why these are\nmodel-based crossing times."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Collaborative\ninvestigation: build and critique a periodic model", "url": "chapter10.html#collaborative-investigation-build-and-critique-a-periodic-model", "text": "In groups, choose a simplified periodic context (a rotating marker, a\nlaboratory oscillator, or a repeated temperature cycle). Specify a\nbaseline, amplitude, and period with units. Construct a sinusoidal model\nand select a threshold that is neither the maximum nor the minimum.\nSolve the threshold equation exactly if possible, then plot the model\nand confirm the crossing times. Finally, critique the model: Which\nassumptions make the sinusoidal form plausible? What kinds of noise,\ndrift, or asymmetry would make the prediction less reliable? Submit the\nequation, a labeled graph, a complete solution set on a specified\ninterval, and a short paragraph distinguishing exact mathematics from\nassumptions about the real-world process."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Chapter summary and\nformula reference", "url": "chapter10.html#chapter-summary-and-formula-reference", "text": "Concept Formula or principle Pythagorean \\(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1\\) ; \\(1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x\\) Reciprocal \\(\\sec x=1/\\cos\nx\\) , \\(\\csc x=1/\\sin x\\) Sine addition \\(\\sin(a\\pm\nb)=\\sin a\\cos b\\pm\\cos a\\sin b\\) Cosine addition \\(\\cos(a\\pm\nb)=\\cos a\\cos b\\mp\\sin a\\sin b\\) Double angle \\(\\sin2x=2\\sin\nx\\cos x\\) ; \\(\\cos2x=1-2\\sin^2x\\) Power reduction \\(\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos2x)/2\\) Equation solving Find reference angle, quadrants, period,\ninterval, and verify"}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Chapter review exercises", "url": "chapter10.html#chapter-review-exercises", "text": "Part I: Conceptual reasoning. Provide explanations\nand domain restrictions. Explain why testing an identity at one hundred angles does not\nprove it. State the domain of \\(\\tan x=\\sin\nx/\\cos x\\) . Give an example of an identity and an equation that look similar\nbut have different meanings. Explain why \\(\\sqrt{\\cos^2x}=|\\cos\nx|\\) , not necessarily \\(\\cos\nx\\) . Describe how a periodic solution family differs from a principal\ninverse-trigonometric value. Explain why dividing \\(\\sin x\\cos\nx=0\\) by \\(\\sin x\\) can lose\nsolutions. Part II: Identities and exact values. Simplify \\((1-\\sin^2x)/\\cos x\\) on its domain. Prove \\((\\sec x-\\cos x)/\\sin x=\\sin\nx/\\cos x\\) on the common domain. Evaluate \\(\\sin(\\pi/12)\\) exactly. Evaluate \\(\\cos(5\\pi/12)\\) exactly. Evaluate \\(\\tan(5\\pi/12)\\) exactly. Find \\(\\sin2x\\) when \\(\\sin x=5/13\\) and \\(x\\) is in quadrant II. Find \\(\\cos2x\\) when \\(\\cos x=3/5\\) and \\(x\\) is in quadrant IV. Find \\(\\cos(\\pi/8)\\) using a\nhalf-angle formula. Rewrite \\(\\sin5x\\cos2x\\) as a\nsum. Rewrite \\(\\sin^2(3x)\\) in terms\nof a cosine. Prove \\(\\frac{1}{1+\\cos\nx}+\\frac{1}{1-\\cos x}=2\\csc^2x\\) on the common domain. Prove \\(\\tan x+\\cot x=\\sec x\\csc\nx\\) on the common domain. Part III: Solve exactly. Solve \\(\\sin x=-\\sqrt2/2\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Solve \\(\\cos x=\\sqrt3/2\\) on \\([-\\pi,\\pi]\\) . Solve \\(\\tan x=-1\\) for all real \\(x\\) . Solve \\(2\\sin^2x+\\sin x-1=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Solve \\(2\\cos^2x-\\cos x-1=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Solve \\(\\sin2x=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Solve \\(\\cos3x=1\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Solve \\(\\sin x\\cos x=0\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) without dividing by either\nfactor. Part IV: Modeling and calculus readiness. For \\(S(t)=4+2\\sin(\\pi t/5)\\) ,\nfind all \\(t\\in[0,20)\\) with \\(S(t)=5\\) . For \\(H(t)=8+3\\cos(\\pi t/4)\\) ,\nfind all \\(t\\in[0,16)\\) with \\(H(t)=9.5\\) . Expand \\([\\sin(x+h)-\\sin x]/h\\) using an addition identity. Evaluate \\(\\sin h/h\\) for \\(h=0.1,0.01,0.001\\) and explain the role of\nradians."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter10.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "Without using derivative rules, rewrite \\([\\cos(x+h)-\\cos x]/h\\) as a sum of two\nterms involving \\(\\sin h/h\\) and \\((\\cos h-1)/h\\) . Explain how the\ntrigonometric identities developed in this chapter make this possible.\nThen discuss why a graphical or computational experiment suggests, but\ndoes not establish, the limiting result."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Looking ahead", "url": "chapter10.html#looking-ahead", "text": "Chapter 11 applies trigonometry to triangles, navigation, periodic\nprocesses, and real-world modeling. The identity and equation techniques\nhere will also reappear in single-variable calculus, particularly when\ndifferentiating and integrating trigonometric functions."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Extended\napplication: Power in an oscillating signal", "url": "chapter10.html#extended-application-power-in-an-oscillating-signal", "text": "A normalized oscillating signal has instantaneous squared amplitude\ngiven by the square of its sine component. The quantities in this\ninvestigation are illustrative rather than measured observations."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter10.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[P(\\theta)=\\sin^2\\theta.\\] Before\ncalculating, identify the input and output, their units, and the\ninterval on which the model is being studied. Distinguish the\nmathematical domain from the practical domain. The numerical study uses\ninputs from \\(0\\) to \\(6.28\\) . Example 17: Analytical investigation. Use a\ndouble-angle identity to rewrite \\(P\\) .\nSolve \\(P=1/4\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Worked solution. \\(P=(1-\\cos 2\\theta)/2\\) . Solutions are \\(\\pi/6,5\\pi/6,7\\pi/6,11\\pi/6\\) ."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter10.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Phase (rad) Squared amplitude 0 0 1.57 1 3.14 4.278e-26 4.71 1 6.28 1.711e-25 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter10.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Power in an oscillating signal # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 6.28318530718 , length.out = 301 ) y <- sin (x) ^ 2 # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Phase (rad)\" , ylab = \"Squared amplitude\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter10.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Power in an oscillating signal import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 6.28318530718 , 301 ) y = np.sin(x) ** 2 # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Phase (rad)\" , ylabel = \"Squared amplitude\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter10.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Power in an oscillating signal", "url": "chapter10.html#chapter-project-investigating-power-in-an-oscillating-signal", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter10.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "url": "chapter11.html#applications-of-trigonometry-and-periodic-models", "text": ""}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Opening context:\nmeasuring what cannot be reached", "url": "chapter11.html#opening-context-measuring-what-cannot-be-reached", "text": "A clinician may infer a distance from imaging angles, a surveyor may\nestimate the width of a river without crossing it, and a researcher may\nmodel the daily rise and fall of a physiological signal. Trigonometry\nconnects measurable angles and lengths to otherwise inaccessible\nquantities. In this chapter we move beyond graphing trigonometric\nfunctions to selecting, solving, and critically evaluating mathematical\nmodels."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter11.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "After completing this chapter, students should be able to: (1) solve\nright and oblique triangles; (2) derive and use the Laws of Sines and\nCosines; (3) recognize the ambiguous SSA case; (4) compute triangle area\nfrom two sides and an included angle; (5) model bearings, elevation, and\nnavigation; (6) fit and interpret periodic models; (7) interpret units\nand assess uncertainty; and (8) implement equivalent investigations in R\nand Python."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Prerequisite check", "url": "chapter11.html#prerequisite-check", "text": "Review exact unit-circle values (Chapter 8), sinusoidal graphs and\ntransformations (Chapter 9), and trigonometric identities and equations\n(Chapter 10). Unless explicitly stated otherwise, angles in triangle\nproblems are measured in degrees and arguments in computational\ntrigonometric functions are converted to radians."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Working with R and\nPython", "url": "chapter11.html#working-with-r-and-python", "text": "Each computational laboratory begins with a mathematical prediction\nand presents a complete R listing followed by a complete Python listing.\nThe listings are sequential and full width. Numerical results should be\nchecked against geometric constraints; software is a tool for\ninvestigation, not a substitute for mathematical reasoning."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Right-triangle\nreview and choosing a ratio", "url": "chapter11.html#right-triangle-review-and-choosing-a-ratio", "text": "For a right triangle with acute angle \\(\\theta\\) , the sine is opposite over\nhypotenuse, cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, and tangent is opposite\nover adjacent. These ratios are independent of the size of a similar\ntriangle. Before calculating, sketch the situation, label the known\nangle, and decide which ratio connects the known and unknown lengths.\nInverse trigonometric functions recover an angle from a ratio, but the\nphysical geometry determines which angle is meaningful. Example 1. A ramp rises \\(1.2\\) m over a horizontal run of \\(8.0\\) m. Its angle of inclination satisfies \\(\\tan\\theta=1.2/8.0=0.15\\) , hence \\(\\theta=\\arctan(0.15)\\approx8.53^\\circ\\) .\nThe ramp length is \\(\\sqrt{8^2+1.2^2}\\approx8.09\\) m. Guided practice 1. A medical imaging platform rises\n0.9 m over a horizontal run of 6 m. Find its angle and sloped\nlength. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Angles of elevation and\ndepression", "url": "chapter11.html#angles-of-elevation-and-depression", "text": "An angle of elevation is measured upward from a horizontal line of\nsight; an angle of depression is measured downward. Parallel horizontal\nlines imply that an angle of depression from an observer equals the\ncorresponding angle of elevation from a target. A correct diagram must\ndistinguish the observer height from the height being estimated. For a\nlevel baseline \\(d\\) and vertical\nheight difference \\(h\\) , \\(\\tan\\theta=h/d\\) . Example 2. From a point \\(30\\) m from a building, the angle of\nelevation to its roof is \\(38^\\circ\\) .\nIf the instrument is \\(1.5\\) m above\nground, the building height is \\(H=1.5+30\\tan38^\\circ\\approx24.94\\) m.\nIgnoring instrument height would systematically underestimate \\(H\\) . Guided practice 2. A technician observes the top of\na tower at 27 degrees from a station 45 m away. The instrument is 1.7 m\nhigh. Find tower height. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Oblique triangles and\nnotation", "url": "chapter11.html#oblique-triangles-and-notation", "text": "An oblique triangle has no right angle. Standard notation assigns\nsides \\(a,b,c\\) opposite angles \\(A,B,C\\) , respectively. The interior angles\nsum to \\(180^\\circ\\) . The Law of Sines\nis efficient when an opposite side-angle pair is known; the Law of\nCosines is efficient for SAS or SSS information. A common error is\nmatching a side to an adjacent rather than opposite angle. Example 3. In triangle \\(ABC\\) , suppose \\(A=50^\\circ\\) , \\(B=65^\\circ\\) , and \\(a=12\\) cm. Then \\(C=65^\\circ\\) . The equal angles \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) imply \\(b=c\\) ; the Law of Sines will determine\ntheir common length. Guided practice 3. Draw a triangle with A=42 degrees\nand B=73 degrees; identify C and which sides could be equal. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Deriving the Law of Sines", "url": "chapter11.html#deriving-the-law-of-sines", "text": "Drop an altitude \\(h\\) from vertex \\(C\\) to side \\(AB\\) . When the altitude lies inside the\ntriangle, \\(h=b\\sin A=a\\sin B\\) , giving \\(a/\\sin A=b/\\sin B\\) . Repeating with\nanother altitude yields \\[\\boxed{\\frac{a}{\\sin A}=\\frac{b}{\\sin\nB}=\\frac{c}{\\sin C}}.\\] The same relation remains valid for\nobtuse triangles because \\(\\sin(180^\\circ-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta\\) . The\nsine law relates opposite pairs, not neighboring ones. Example 4. Given \\(A=50^\\circ\\) , \\(B=65^\\circ\\) , \\(a=12\\) cm, compute \\(C=65^\\circ\\) and \\(b=12\\sin65^\\circ/\\sin50^\\circ\\approx14.20\\) cm; similarly \\(c\\approx14.20\\) cm.\nBoth longer sides oppose larger angles, a useful reasonableness\ncheck. Guided practice 4. Given A=35 degrees, B=75 degrees,\nand a=9 cm, determine C, b, and c. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "The ambiguous SSA case", "url": "chapter11.html#the-ambiguous-ssa-case", "text": "Knowing two sides and an angle opposite one of them (SSA) may yield\nzero, one, or two triangles. If \\(A\\) is acute, with known \\(a\\) opposite \\(A\\) and known adjacent side \\(b\\) , the altitude is \\(h=b\\sin A\\) . When \\(a<h\\) , no triangle exists; when \\(a=h\\) , one right triangle exists; when \\(h<a<b\\) , two triangles exist;\nwhen \\(a\\ge b\\) , one triangle exists.\nFor an obtuse \\(A\\) , a triangle is\npossible only if \\(a>b\\) . An\ninverse-sine calculation gives a principal acute angle, but its\nsupplement may also satisfy the original data. Example 5. Let \\(A=30^\\circ\\) , \\(a=7\\) , and \\(b=10\\) . Then \\(h=10\\sin30^\\circ=5\\) and \\(5<7<10\\) , so two triangles exist.\nSince \\(\\sin B=10\\sin30^\\circ/7=5/7\\) , \\(B_1\\approx45.58^\\circ\\) or \\(B_2\\approx134.42^\\circ\\) . Both give\npositive remaining angles: \\(C_1\\approx104.42^\\circ\\) and \\(C_2\\approx15.58^\\circ\\) . Guided practice 5. For A=40 degrees, a=6, b=8,\nclassify the SSA case and compute all possible B angles. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Deriving the Law of Cosines", "url": "chapter11.html#deriving-the-law-of-cosines", "text": "Place a triangle in the coordinate plane with \\(A=(0,0)\\) , \\(B=(c,0)\\) , and \\(C=(b\\cos A,b\\sin A)\\) . The squared distance\nfrom \\(B\\) to \\(C\\) is \\[a^2=(b\\cos A-c)^2+(b\\sin A)^2=b^2+c^2-2bc\\cos\nA.\\] Therefore \\(\\boxed{a^2=b^2+c^2-2bc\\cos A}\\) . Cyclic\npermutations produce formulas for \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) . When \\(A=90^\\circ\\) , the cosine term vanishes,\nrecovering the Pythagorean theorem. Example 6. Two sides of a triangular plot measure \\(8\\) m and \\(11\\) m and enclose an angle of \\(60^\\circ\\) . The third side satisfies \\(a^2=8^2+11^2-2(8)(11)\\cos60^\\circ=97\\) , so \\(a=\\sqrt{97}\\approx9.85\\) m. Guided practice 6. Find the third side of a triangle\nwith b=7, c=13, and A=110 degrees. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Solving SSS triangles", "url": "chapter11.html#solving-sss-triangles", "text": "When all three side lengths are known, the Law of Cosines recovers an\nangle: \\[\\cos\nA=\\frac{b^2+c^2-a^2}{2bc}.\\] Check the triangle inequalities \\(a+b>c\\) , \\(a+c>b\\) , and \\(b+c>a\\) before using inverse cosine. The\nangle opposite the longest side must be the largest. Calculate two\nangles and obtain the third from the angle sum to reduce rounding\nerror. Example 7. For sides \\(a=5\\) , \\(b=7\\) , and \\(c=9\\) , \\(\\cos\nC=(5^2+7^2-9^2)/(2\\cdot5\\cdot7)=-0.1\\) . Thus \\(C\\approx95.74^\\circ\\) . The longest side \\(c\\) indeed opposes the obtuse\nangle. Guided practice 7. Determine whether lengths 4, 6,\n11 can form a triangle. Then find the largest angle for sides 6, 8,\n10. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Area from trigonometry", "url": "chapter11.html#area-from-trigonometry", "text": "The familiar area formula \\(K=\\tfrac12\nbh\\) becomes more useful when the height is not directly\nmeasured. If sides \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) enclose angle \\(A\\) , the perpendicular height relative to \\(c\\) is \\(b\\sin A\\) , hence \\[\\boxed{K=\\tfrac12bc\\sin A=\\tfrac12ac\\sin\nB=\\tfrac12ab\\sin C}.\\] This formula is valid for acute and obtuse\nincluded angles. Since sine is dimensionless, area units are the square\nof the length units. Example 8. Two survey baselines of \\(40\\) m and \\(55\\) m meet at \\(72^\\circ\\) . The triangular area is \\(K=\\tfrac12(40)(55)\\sin72^\\circ\\approx1046.16\\) m \\(^2\\) . Guided practice 8. Find the area of a triangular\nfield with sides 18 m and 25 m enclosing 125 degrees. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Bearings and navigation", "url": "chapter11.html#bearings-and-navigation", "text": "A bearing is a direction relative to a reference axis. In many\nnavigation contexts, an azimuth is measured clockwise from north: north\nis \\(0^\\circ\\) , east is \\(90^\\circ\\) , south is \\(180^\\circ\\) , and west is \\(270^\\circ\\) . For a displacement of\nmagnitude \\(d\\) at azimuth \\(\\alpha\\) , east and north components are \\(E=d\\sin\\alpha\\) and \\(N=d\\cos\\alpha\\) . State the convention\nbefore computing; measuring angles counterclockwise from east produces\ndifferent component formulas. Example 9. A research vessel travels \\(12\\) km on azimuth \\(60^\\circ\\) and then \\(8\\) km on azimuth \\(150^\\circ\\) . Its east displacement is \\(12\\sin60^\\circ+8\\sin150^\\circ\\approx14.39\\) km; north displacement is \\(12\\cos60^\\circ+8\\cos150^\\circ\\approx-0.93\\) km. The resultant distance is approximately \\(14.42\\) km. Guided practice 9. A drone travels 5 km at azimuth\n30 degrees and 7 km at azimuth 120 degrees. Find its net east and north\ndisplacements. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Triangulation and\nindirect measurement", "url": "chapter11.html#triangulation-and-indirect-measurement", "text": "Triangulation uses a known baseline and two observed directions to\nlocate an inaccessible point. Suppose stations \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are \\(d\\) units apart and the measured interior\nangles toward target \\(T\\) are \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) . Then \\(T=180^\\circ-A-B\\) and \\[AT=\\frac{d\\sin B}{\\sin T},\\qquad BT=\\frac{d\\sin\nA}{\\sin T}.\\] Small changes in angles can have large effects when \\(T\\) is near zero; nearly collinear\nsight lines make triangulation unstable. Example 10. Stations \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are \\(100\\) m apart. The angles to a landmark are \\(A=48^\\circ\\) and \\(B=67^\\circ\\) . Then \\(T=65^\\circ\\) and \\(AT=100\\sin67^\\circ/\\sin65^\\circ\\approx101.57\\) m. Guided practice 10. A baseline is 80 m with observed\nbase angles 55 and 60 degrees. Find both distances to the target. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Periodic phenomena and\nmodel parameters", "url": "chapter11.html#periodic-phenomena-and-model-parameters", "text": "A sinusoidal model has the form \\[y(t)=D+A\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{P}(t-h)\\right),\\] where \\(D\\) is the midline, \\(|A|\\) the amplitude, \\(P>0\\) the period, and \\(h\\) a horizontal shift. For a measured\nmaximum \\(M\\) and minimum \\(m\\) , estimate \\(D=(M+m)/2\\) and \\(|A|=(M-m)/2\\) . A peak-to-peak interval\nestimates \\(P\\) . The units of \\(2\\pi/P\\) are radians per unit time. Example 11. A simplified temperature pattern ranges\nfrom \\(36.5^\\circ\\) C to \\(37.5^\\circ\\) C over a 24-hour cycle. Its\nmidline is \\(37.0^\\circ\\) C and\namplitude is \\(0.5^\\circ\\) C. A\ncandidate model is \\(T(t)=37+0.5\\sin(2\\pi\nt/24)\\) , provided \\(t=0\\) corresponds to a midline upward crossing. Guided practice 11. A periodic measurement varies\nfrom 62 to 78 units every 12 hours. State its midline, amplitude,\nangular frequency, and one possible sine model. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Determining phase from data", "url": "chapter11.html#determining-phase-from-data", "text": "A periodic curve cannot be specified by amplitude and period alone.\nThe phase determines where maxima, minima, and midline crossings occur.\nFor a cosine model with a maximum at \\(t=t_{\\max}\\) , a convenient form is \\(y(t)=D+A\\cos[2\\pi(t-t_{\\max})/P]\\) with \\(A>0\\) . If the observation starts\nat a minimum, use a negative cosine or shift by half a period. More than\none algebraic representation can describe the same function. Example 12. A laboratory sensor has maximum reading \\(8\\) at \\(t=2\\) s, minimum \\(2\\) , and period \\(10\\) s. Then \\(D=5\\) , \\(A=3\\) , and \\(S(t)=5+3\\cos[2\\pi(t-2)/10]\\) . At \\(t=2\\) , the cosine is \\(1\\) , giving the required maximum. Guided practice 12. A respiration model has minimum\n10 at t=3 s, maximum 18, and period 8 s. Write a cosine model. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Fitting a\nsinusoidal model and checking residuals", "url": "chapter11.html#fitting-a-sinusoidal-model-and-checking-residuals", "text": "A model fitted to data should be judged by its residuals \\(e_i=y_i-\\widehat y_i\\) . Random-looking\nresiduals of modest magnitude support the model’s descriptive\nusefulness; systematic waves or drifting residuals suggest missing\nstructure. The root-mean-square error is \\(\\mathrm{RMSE}=\\sqrt{n^{-1}\\sum_i e_i^2}\\) .\nA small RMSE does not guarantee valid extrapolation. For known period \\(P\\) , rewrite \\(D+A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)\\) as \\(D+B\\sin(\\omega t)+C\\cos(\\omega t)\\) , where \\(\\omega=2\\pi/P\\) . This is linear in\nthe coefficients \\(D,B,C\\) and can be\nfitted by least squares. Example 13. Suppose a 24-hour cycle is modeled by \\(y(t)=70+6\\sin(2\\pi t/24)+2\\cos(2\\pi\nt/24)\\) . The amplitude is \\(\\sqrt{6^2+2^2}=\\sqrt{40}\\approx6.32\\) . Its\nphase \\(\\phi=\\operatorname{atan2}(2,6)\\) , since \\(B=A\\cos\\phi\\) and \\(C=A\\sin\\phi\\) . Guided practice 13. Given B=4 and C=-3 in a harmonic\nmodel, compute amplitude and phase, and explain the role of atan2. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Model limitations,\nuncertainty, and units", "url": "chapter11.html#model-limitations-uncertainty-and-units", "text": "A trigonometric formula is an idealization. Physical signals can have\nnoise, changing amplitude, drift, irregular cycles, or abrupt events. A\npredicted value should be reported with appropriate units and precision,\nand parameter uncertainty should be acknowledged. When measuring a\ndistance \\(d=h/\\tan\\theta\\) , small\nangular errors are especially influential for shallow angles. Compare\npredictions under plausible alternative measurements rather than\nreporting an implausibly precise answer. Example 14. At an elevation angle of \\(20^\\circ\\) , a horizontal distance estimate\nfor a height difference of \\(10\\) m is \\(d=10/\\tan20^\\circ\\approx27.47\\) m. If\nthe angle is \\(19^\\circ\\) or \\(21^\\circ\\) , the estimates are approximately \\(29.04\\) m and \\(26.05\\) m. A one-degree error changes the\ninferred distance substantially. Guided practice 14. Estimate the distance to a 15\nm-high target using angles of elevation 24, 25, and 26 degrees. Discuss\nsensitivity. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Preparation for\ncalculus: instantaneous change", "url": "chapter11.html#preparation-for-calculus-instantaneous-change", "text": "For \\(y(t)=D+A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)\\) ,\nthe average rate of change over \\([t,t+h]\\) is \\([y(t+h)-y(t)]/h\\) . By the angle-addition\nidentity, the numerator can be rearranged into terms involving \\(\\sin(\\omega h)\\) and \\(\\cos(\\omega h)-1\\) . Calculus shows that the\ninstantaneous rate is \\(y'(t)=A\\omega\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)\\) when \\(t\\) is measured in compatible units.\nThe derivative is largest in magnitude at midline crossings and zero at\nsmooth maxima and minima. Example 15. For \\(y(t)=5+2\\sin(\\pi t/6)\\) , the instantaneous\nrate at \\(t=0\\) is \\(y'(0)=2(\\pi/6)\\cos0=\\pi/3\\) units per\nsecond. The factor \\(\\pi/6\\) is\nessential: omitting it confuses amplitude with rate. Guided practice 15. Write the average rate of change\nfor y(t)=10+3 cos(pi t/4) over [0,h], and predict the limiting\nvalue. Reasoning checkpoint. State the applicable formula, explain why it applies, show\nintermediate steps, and interpret the result with units and appropriate\nprecision."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Visual investigations", "url": "chapter11.html#visual-investigations", "text": ""}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Investigation 1:\nAltitude and the Law of Sines", "url": "chapter11.html#investigation-1-altitude-and-the-law-of-sines", "text": "Draw an oblique triangle with \\(A=40^\\circ\\) , \\(B=65^\\circ\\) , and baseline \\(c=8\\) . Drop an altitude from \\(C\\) and label the two right triangles.\nDerive the Law of Sines from the two expressions for the altitude.\nExplain how the derivation extends to an obtuse triangle. image"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Investigation\n2: Two triangles from the same SSA measurements", "url": "chapter11.html#investigation-2-two-triangles-from-the-same-ssa-measurements", "text": "Construct a ray making \\(30^\\circ\\) with a horizontal baseline. Mark a fixed side of length \\(10\\) along the baseline, then draw a circle\nof radius \\(7\\) centered at its\nendpoint. Explain why the circle intersects the ray twice. Compare both\npossible triangles with the numerical calculations in Section 5."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Investigation\n3: The cosine rule as a distance formula", "url": "chapter11.html#investigation-3-the-cosine-rule-as-a-distance-formula", "text": "Plot \\(A=(0,0)\\) , \\(B=(6,0)\\) , and \\(C=(4\\cos60^\\circ,4\\sin60^\\circ)\\) . Use the\ndistance formula to calculate \\(BC\\) and compare with the Law of Cosines. Repeat with \\(A=120^\\circ\\) and describe the effect of an\nobtuse included angle."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Investigation\n4: Triangulation and near-parallel sight lines", "url": "chapter11.html#investigation-4-triangulation-and-near-parallel-sight-lines", "text": "Fix baseline \\(AB=100\\) m. Compare\ntarget distances when \\((A,B)=(50^\\circ,60^\\circ)\\) and when \\((A,B)=(5^\\circ,6^\\circ)\\) . Which\narrangement is more sensitive to small angular errors? Support your\nconclusion with calculations."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Investigation\n5: Changing amplitude, period, and phase", "url": "chapter11.html#investigation-5-changing-amplitude-period-and-phase", "text": "On one set of axes graph \\(\\sin t\\) , \\(2\\sin t\\) , \\(\\sin(2t)\\) , and \\(\\sin(t-\\pi/3)\\) over \\(0\\le t\\le4\\pi\\) . For each curve identify\namplitude, period, and first positive maximum. Explain why a horizontal\nshift is not the same as a vertical shift. image"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Investigation\n6: A periodic health-science measurement", "url": "chapter11.html#investigation-6-a-periodic-health-science-measurement", "text": "A simulated physiological signal follows \\(R(t)=16+3\\cos[2\\pi(t-2)/8]\\) cycles per\nminute. Determine its range, period, and first maximum. Construct a\ntable at one-second intervals, graph the result, and explain why the\nmodel should not be used to make clinical decisions without\nvalidation."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Investigation 7: Residual\npatterns", "url": "chapter11.html#investigation-7-residual-patterns", "text": "Generate observations from a sinusoid with a slowly increasing trend.\nFit a pure sinusoid and plot residuals versus time. Describe the\npattern. Explain why an apparently good fit can still omit a\nscientifically important process."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Extended applied workshops", "url": "chapter11.html#extended-applied-workshops", "text": ""}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Workshop A: Indirect height\nmeasurement", "url": "chapter11.html#workshop-a-indirect-height-measurement", "text": "Two observation stations lie on level ground \\(35\\) m apart. From the farther station the\nangle of elevation to a tower top is \\(28^\\circ\\) ; from the nearer station it is \\(41^\\circ\\) . Both instruments are \\(1.6\\) m high. Let \\(x\\) be the horizontal distance from the\nnearer station to the tower. (a) Draw and label a diagram. (b) Derive \\(x\\tan41^\\circ=(x+35)\\tan28^\\circ\\) .\n(c) Solve for \\(x\\) and the tower\nheight. (d) Describe how a one-degree angle error might affect the\nresult."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Workshop B: Navigating to\na research site", "url": "chapter11.html#workshop-b-navigating-to-a-research-site", "text": "A field team travels \\(9\\) km at\nazimuth \\(35^\\circ\\) and then \\(13\\) km at azimuth \\(125^\\circ\\) . (a) Calculate east and north\ncomponents. (b) Find straight-line distance from the starting point. (c)\nDetermine the return azimuth. (d) Explain why a diagram without an angle\nconvention can be misleading."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Workshop C:\nTriangle ambiguity in imaging geometry", "url": "chapter11.html#workshop-c-triangle-ambiguity-in-imaging-geometry", "text": "An idealized imaging setup has \\(A=35^\\circ\\) , opposite length \\(a=7\\) cm, and adjacent length \\(b=10\\) cm. (a) Compute the altitude. (b)\nDetermine whether zero, one, or two triangles are possible. (c) Solve\nfor each feasible triangle. (d) Explain why an inverse-sine calculator\nresult alone is insufficient."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Workshop D: A periodic\nenvironmental sensor", "url": "chapter11.html#workshop-d-a-periodic-environmental-sensor", "text": "An environmental monitor has a minimum of \\(12\\) units at 3 AM and maximum of \\(28\\) units at 3 PM. Assume a 24-hour cycle.\n(a) Determine amplitude and midline. (b) Write a cosine model using\nhours after midnight. (c) Predict the reading at 9 AM. (d) Identify the\ntimes at which the model crosses its midline. (e) Discuss why real\nobservations may deviate."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Workshop E:\nHarmonic regression", "url": "chapter11.html#workshop-e-harmonic-regression", "text": "At times \\(t=0,3,6,9,12,15,18,21\\) hours, simulated readings are \\([72,77,80,76,70,65,64,68]\\) . (a) Plot the\ndata. (b) Fit \\(D+B\\sin(2\\pi t/24)+C\\cos(2\\pi\nt/24)\\) . (c) Report amplitude and phase. (d) Plot residuals and\ncalculate RMSE. (e) Explain why a single day of data cannot establish a\nstable long-term biological cycle."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Computational\nLaboratory A: Solving triangles and checking ambiguity", "url": "chapter11.html#computational-laboratory-a-solving-triangles-and-checking-ambiguity", "text": "Before coding. Predict how many triangles are\npossible for \\(A=30^\\circ\\) , \\(a=7\\) , \\(b=10\\) . Explain the altitude test. Run the\nR listing, then the Python listing, and compare both sets of angles."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter11.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory A: the ambiguous SSA case A <- 30 * pi / 180 ; a <- 7 ; b <- 10 s <- b * sin (A) / a if ( abs (s) > 1 ) { print ( \"No triangle\" ) } else { B_candidates <- c ( asin (s), pi - asin (s)) for (B in B_candidates) { C <- pi - A - B if (C > 0 ) { c_side <- a * sin (C) / sin (A) cat ( sprintf ( \"B=%.2f deg, C=%.2f deg, c=%.3f \\n \" , B * 180 / pi, C * 180 / pi, c_side)) } } }"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter11.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory A: the ambiguous SSA case import math A = math.radians( 30 ) ; a = 7 ; b = 10 s = b * math.sin(A) / a if abs (s) > 1 : print ( \"No triangle\" ) else : for B in (math.asin(s), math.pi - math.asin(s)): C = math.pi - A - B if C > 0 : c_side = a * math.sin(C) / math.sin(A) print ( f\"B= { math . degrees(B) :.2f} deg, \" f\"C= { math . degrees(C) :.2f} deg, c= { c_side :.3f} \" ) Interpretation. Why do two values of \\(B\\) satisfy the sine law? How can you\nreject an angle when the sum of interior angles would exceed \\(180^\\circ\\) ?"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Computational\nLaboratory B: Navigation and triangulation", "url": "chapter11.html#computational-laboratory-b-navigation-and-triangulation", "text": "Before coding. Predict the signs of east and north\ndisplacement for an azimuth of \\(150^\\circ\\) . Compare a component-based\nsolution with a triangle-based solution."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter11.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory B: navigation and triangulation d <- c ( 12 , 8 ); az <- c ( 60 , 150 ) * pi / 180 east <- sum (d * sin (az)); north <- sum (d * cos (az)) distance <- sqrt (east ^ 2 + north ^ 2 ) azimuth <- ( atan2 (east, north) * 180 / pi) %% 360 cat ( sprintf ( \"East=%.3f, North=%.3f km \\n \" , east, north)) cat ( sprintf ( \"Distance=%.3f km, azimuth=%.2f deg \\n \" , distance, azimuth)) baseline <- 100 ; A <- 48 * pi / 180 ; B <- 67 * pi / 180 T <- pi - A - B cat ( sprintf ( \"AT=%.3f m, BT=%.3f m \\n \" , baseline * sin (B) / sin (T), baseline * sin (A) / sin (T)))"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter11.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory B: navigation and triangulation import math legs = [( 12 , 60 ), ( 8 , 150 )] east = sum (d * math.sin(math.radians(az)) for d,az in legs) north = sum (d * math.cos(math.radians(az)) for d,az in legs) distance = math.hypot(east, north) azimuth = math.degrees(math.atan2(east, north)) % 360 print ( f\"East= { east :.3f} , North= { north :.3f} km\" ) print ( f\"Distance= { distance :.3f} km, azimuth= { azimuth :.2f} deg\" ) A, B = map (math.radians, ( 48 , 67 )) T = math.pi - A - B ; baseline = 100 print ( f\"AT= { baseline * math . sin(B) / math . sin(T) :.3f} m, \" f\"BT= { baseline * math . sin(A) / math . sin(T) :.3f} m\" ) Interpretation. Explain how the coordinate\nconvention determines the sine and cosine assignments. How would\nuncertainty in bearings affect the final location?"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Computational\nLaboratory C: Fitting and evaluating a periodic model", "url": "chapter11.html#computational-laboratory-c-fitting-and-evaluating-a-periodic-model", "text": "Before coding. For a 24-hour signal, compute angular\nfrequency and predict the qualitative shape of a residual plot when the\nobservations include a trend."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter11.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory C: periodic regression and residuals t <- seq ( 0 , 23 , by= 1 ); omega <- 2 * pi / 24 set.seed ( 11 ) y <- 70 + 6 * sin (omega * t) + 2 * cos (omega * t) + 0.12 * t + rnorm ( length (t), sd= 0.5 ) fit <- lm (y ~ sin (omega * t) + cos (omega * t)) co <- coef (fit); B <- co[ 2 ]; C <- co[ 3 ] amp <- sqrt (B ^ 2 + C ^ 2 ); phase <- atan2 (C,B) rmse <- sqrt ( mean ( residuals (fit) ^ 2 )) cat ( sprintf ( \"Midline=%.3f, amplitude=%.3f, phase=%.3f rad, RMSE=%.3f \\n \" , co[ 1 ],amp,phase,rmse)) png ( \"periodic_fit_R.png\" , width= 900 , height= 500 ) plot (t,y, pch= 16 , xlab= \"Time (hours)\" , ylab= \"Sensor reading\" ) lines (t, fitted (fit), col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 ) dev.off () png ( \"periodic_residuals_R.png\" , width= 900 , height= 500 ) plot (t, residuals (fit), pch= 16 , xlab= \"Time (hours)\" , ylab= \"Residual\" ) abline ( h= 0 , lty= 2 ) dev.off ()"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter11.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory C: periodic regression and residuals import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt rng = np.random.default_rng( 11 ) t = np.arange( 24 , dtype = float ) ; omega = 2 * np.pi / 24 y = 70 + 6 * np.sin(omega * t) + 2 * np.cos(omega * t) + 0.12 * t y = y + rng.normal( 0 , 0.5 , len (t)) X = np.column_stack((np.ones( len (t)), np.sin(omega * t), np.cos(omega * t))) D, B, C = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond = None )[ 0 ] fitted = X @ np.array([D,B,C]) ; residuals = y - fitted amp = np.hypot(B,C) ; phase = np.arctan2(C,B) rmse = np.sqrt(np.mean(residuals ** 2 )) print ( f\"Midline= { D :.3f} , amplitude= { amp :.3f} , \" f\"phase= { phase :.3f} rad, RMSE= { rmse :.3f} \" ) plt.figure(figsize = ( 8 , 4 )) ; plt.scatter(t,y,label = \"Observed\" ) plt.plot(t,fitted,label = \"Fitted\" ) ; plt.xlabel( \"Time (hours)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Sensor reading\" ) ; plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"periodic_fit_python.png\" ,dpi = 160 ) ; plt.close() plt.figure(figsize = ( 8 , 4 )) ; plt.scatter(t,residuals) plt.axhline( 0 ,ls = \"--\" ) ; plt.xlabel( \"Time (hours)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Residual\" ) ; plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"periodic_residuals_python.png\" ,dpi = 160 ) ; plt.close() Interpretation. Compare the fitted amplitude,\nmidline, and RMSE. Identify limitations of a one-period fit and explain\nwhy a smooth-looking curve does not imply a validated clinical\nmodel."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "A step-by-step\nproblem-solving clinic", "url": "chapter11.html#a-step-by-step-problem-solving-clinic", "text": ""}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Case study 1:\nSelecting the correct triangle law", "url": "chapter11.html#case-study-1-selecting-the-correct-triangle-law", "text": "A team measures two sides of a triangular study region as \\(14\\) m and \\(19\\) m, with an included angle of \\(47^\\circ\\) . The goal is to determine the\nremaining side and the area. Step 1: Organize\ninformation. The angle is between the known sides, so the data\nare SAS. Step 2: Choose a method. The Law of Cosines\ndetermines the third side, and the sine area formula gives the area\ndirectly. Step 3: Set up exact expressions: \\[a=\\sqrt{14^2+19^2-2(14)(19)\\cos47^\\circ},\\qquad\nK=\\frac12(14)(19)\\sin47^\\circ.\\] Step 4: Check. The third side must lie between \\(19-14=5\\) m and \\(19+14=33\\) m. The area must be less than \\(\\tfrac12(14)(19)=133\\) m \\(^2\\) because \\(\\sin47^\\circ<1\\) . Step 5:\nInterpret. Report the results with units and precision\nconsistent with the measurements. Try it: Repeat the\ncalculations with the included angle increased to \\(48^\\circ\\) and compare sensitivity."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Case study 2: A model\nwith a delayed maximum", "url": "chapter11.html#case-study-2-a-model-with-a-delayed-maximum", "text": "A simulated sensor oscillates between \\(30\\) and \\(50\\) units with a period of \\(12\\) minutes. Its maximum occurs at \\(t=4\\) minutes. Step 1: Determine\nparameters. \\(D=(50+30)/2=40\\) , \\(A=(50-30)/2=10\\) , and \\(\\omega=2\\pi/12=\\pi/6\\) radians per minute. Step 2: Align phase. A cosine reaches its maximum when\nits argument is zero, so \\[S(t)=40+10\\cos\\left[\\frac{\\pi}{6}(t-4)\\right].\\] Step 3: Verify. \\(S(4)=50\\) ; six minutes later, \\(S(10)=30\\) . Step 4: Solve a\nthreshold question. \\(S(t)=45\\) means \\(\\cos[\\pi(t-4)/6]=1/2\\) . Thus \\(\\pi(t-4)/6=\\pm\\pi/3+2k\\pi\\) , or \\(t=4\\pm2+12k\\) . Over \\([0,12)\\) the crossings are \\(t=2\\) and \\(t=6\\) minutes. Try it: Determine all crossings of the midline over \\([0,24)\\) ."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Case\nstudy 3: Interpreting uncertainty rather than hiding it", "url": "chapter11.html#case-study-3-interpreting-uncertainty-rather-than-hiding-it", "text": "A target is observed from a horizontal distance of \\(25\\) m at an elevation angle reported as \\(30^\\circ\\pm1^\\circ\\) . The instrument\nis \\(1.5\\) m above the ground. A\ncentral estimate for height is \\(H=1.5+25\\tan30^\\circ\\) . To represent the\nstated angular uncertainty, compute \\(H_{\\min}=1.5+25\\tan29^\\circ\\) and \\(H_{\\max}=1.5+25\\tan31^\\circ\\) . Explain why\nthe central estimate alone would imply more certainty than the\nmeasurement supports. Try it: Repeat for a distance of \\(50\\) m and compare the width of the\nheight interval."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Common errors and\ndiagnostic questions", "url": "chapter11.html#common-errors-and-diagnostic-questions", "text": "Degrees versus radians: A student evaluates \\(\\sin30\\) on a calculator set to\nradians and concludes that the Law of Sines is incorrect. Identify the\nerror and explain how to prevent it in both R and Python. Mismatched sides and angles: A student writes \\(a/\\sin B=b/\\sin A\\) . Draw a labeled\ntriangle and correct the expression. Lost SSA solution: A student reports only \\(B=\\arcsin(5/7)\\) in the ambiguous-case\nexample. Explain the second candidate and the angle-sum check. Unclear bearing convention: Two students obtain\ndifferent eastward displacements from the same stated angle. Explain how\ndefining the zero direction and positive rotation resolves the\ndisagreement. Period confused with frequency: A model has \\(\\omega=\\pi/4\\) rad/s. Explain why its\nperiod is \\(8\\) seconds rather than \\(4\\) seconds. Overinterpreting a fitted curve: A sinusoidal\nmodel closely matches 24 simulated measurements. Explain why this does\nnot prove that a physiological process is exactly periodic."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Chapter review and\nself-assessment", "url": "chapter11.html#chapter-review-and-self-assessment", "text": "Instructions. Show calculations, draw diagrams where\nappropriate, and give units. Use exact values when possible and round\nmeasured results appropriately. A ladder of length 5 m makes an angle of \\(68^\\circ\\) with the ground. Find its\nvertical reach. A ramp rises 0.75 m over a run of 9 m. Find its slope\nangle. An observer 40 m from a building measures an elevation angle of \\(33^\\circ\\) from a height of 1.5 m.\nEstimate building height. In triangle \\(ABC\\) , \\(A=42^\\circ\\) , \\(B=68^\\circ\\) , and \\(a=11\\) . Find \\(C,b,c\\) . Determine the number of triangles for \\(A=25^\\circ\\) , \\(a=4\\) , \\(b=9\\) . Determine the number of triangles for \\(A=30^\\circ\\) , \\(a=6\\) , \\(b=10\\) . Solve all possible triangles with \\(A=40^\\circ\\) , \\(a=8\\) , \\(b=10\\) . Explain why \\(\\arcsin\\) alone\ncannot resolve every SSA triangle. Two sides of length 7 and 12 enclose \\(55^\\circ\\) . Find the third side. Find the angle opposite side 13 in a triangle with sides 5, 12,\n13. Determine whether lengths 3, 5, 9 form a triangle. Find the area of a triangle with sides 14 and 18 enclosing \\(75^\\circ\\) . Find the area of a triangle with sides 8 and 9 enclosing \\(120^\\circ\\) . Derive the Law of Cosines from the distance formula. Explain how the Pythagorean theorem is a special case of the Law\nof Cosines. A ship travels 10 km at azimuth \\(45^\\circ\\) . Find its east and north\ncomponents. A drone travels 4 km at \\(90^\\circ\\) then 3 km at \\(180^\\circ\\) . Find resultant distance and\nazimuth. A survey baseline is 120 m with sight-line angles \\(52^\\circ\\) and \\(61^\\circ\\) . Find both target\ndistances. Explain why nearly parallel sight lines are undesirable for\ntriangulation. A periodic signal has maximum 20, minimum 8, and period 6 s. Find\namplitude, midline, and angular frequency. Write a cosine model with maximum 15 at \\(t=3\\) and minimum 5 at \\(t=7\\) , assuming the maximum and minimum are\nconsecutive. For \\(y=3+2\\sin(\\pi t/4)\\) , find\nrange and period. Find the first positive maximum of \\(y=10+4\\cos[\\pi(t-2)/6]\\) . Convert \\(D+4\\sin(\\omega\nt)-3\\cos(\\omega t)\\) to amplitude-phase form. Explain why \\(\\operatorname{atan2}\\) is preferable to\nordinary arctangent for determining phase. Define a residual and RMSE in a periodic model. Describe two residual patterns that suggest model\nmisspecification. A model estimates a 10 m height difference using an elevation\nangle of \\(15^\\circ\\) . Calculate\nhorizontal distance. Recalculate the preceding distance for \\(14^\\circ\\) and \\(16^\\circ\\) ; discuss uncertainty. For \\(y=2+3\\sin(\\pi t/5)\\) ,\nwrite a difference quotient and predict its derivative at \\(t=0\\) ."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter11.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "Explain why radians are the natural units in the derivative of a sine\nfunction. For \\(y(t)=D+A\\sin(\\omega\nt+\\phi)\\) , identify the units of \\(A\\) , \\(\\omega\\) , and \\(y'(t)\\) . Explain geometrically why a\nsinusoid’s rate of change vanishes at its peaks and troughs. Finally,\ndescribe how the secant-slope idea from Chapter 3 connects to the\ninstantaneous rate of change of a periodic model."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Looking ahead", "url": "chapter11.html#looking-ahead", "text": "Chapter 12 turns to sequences and series. Periodic signals can be\nsampled at equally spaced times, producing numerical sequences; patterns\nin those samples can reveal both useful structure and misleading\naliases."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Extended\napplication: Indirect height measurement", "url": "chapter11.html#extended-application-indirect-height-measurement", "text": "A surveyor stands 30 meters from a vertical tower on level ground.\nThe instrument is 1.5 meters above the ground. The quantities in this\ninvestigation are illustrative rather than measured observations."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter11.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[H(\\theta)=1.5+30\\tan\\theta.\\] Before\ncalculating, identify the input and output, their units, and the\ninterval on which the model is being studied. Distinguish the\nmathematical domain from the practical domain. The numerical study uses\ninputs from \\(0.1\\) to \\(1.1\\) . Example 16: Analytical investigation. Find the\nheight for \\(\\theta=\\pi/4\\) . Explain\nwhy angles close to \\(\\pi/2\\) make the\nestimate sensitive. Worked solution. Height 31.5 m. Tangent grows\nwithout bound near \\(\\pi/2\\) , so small\nangle errors can cause large height errors."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter11.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Elevation angle (rad) Tower height (m) 0.1 4.51 0.35 12.45 0.6 22.02 0.85 35.65 1.1 60.44 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter11.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Indirect height measurement # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0.1 , 1.1 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 1.5 + 30 * tan (x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Elevation angle (rad)\" , ylab = \"Tower height (m)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter11.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Indirect height measurement import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0.1 , 1.1 , 301 ) y = 1.5 + 30 * np.tan(x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Elevation angle (rad)\" , ylabel = \"Tower height (m)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter11.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Indirect height measurement", "url": "chapter11.html#chapter-project-investigating-indirect-height-measurement", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter11.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "url": "chapter12.html#sequences-series-and-mathematical-patterns", "text": ""}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Opening context:\nrepeated change in health sciences", "url": "chapter12.html#opening-context-repeated-change-in-health-sciences", "text": "A clinic purchases replacement sensors every month, a laboratory\ngrows a cell culture in repeated generations, and a public-health\nprogram tracks vaccinations week by week. Each process generates a sequence : an ordered list whose position matters. Adding\nobservations produces a series . The central questions are: What\npattern describes the observations? How can we predict a later term?\nWhat does the total represent? When is extrapolation defensible?"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter12.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "By the end of the chapter, students should be able to (1) interpret\nsequences as functions on integer domains; (2) distinguish explicit and\nrecursive descriptions; (3) derive arithmetic and geometric formulas;\n(4) evaluate finite sums with sigma notation; (5) use geometric sums and\ndiscuss convergence; (6) analyze compound-growth and repeated-dose\nmodels; (7) distinguish exact patterns from empirical approximations;\n(8) explore recurrence and sums in R and Python; and (9) connect\ndifference quotients and discrete accumulation to calculus."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Prerequisite\nconnections", "url": "chapter12.html#prerequisite-connections", "text": "Chapter 4 introduced functions and multiple representations; Chapter\n7 developed exponential models. In this chapter the input is usually an\ninteger index, and the outputs form a discrete pattern. Unless stated\notherwise, the first term has index \\(n=1\\) . In computer code we label indices\nexplicitly to avoid the common zero-indexing mismatch."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Working with R and\nPython", "url": "chapter12.html#working-with-r-and-python", "text": "Every laboratory includes a prediction, a full-width R\ncode listing, a full-width Python code listing, and interpretation questions. Execute the two programs\nindependently; compare their results with hand calculations."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Sequences as\nfunctions on integer domains", "url": "chapter12.html#sequences-as-functions-on-integer-domains", "text": "A sequence \\(\\{a_n\\}_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\) is a function\nwhose domain is the positive integers. Its values \\(a_1,a_2,\\ldots\\) are called terms. The\norder is essential: \\(2,4,8,16\\) is not\nthe same sequence as \\(16,8,4,2\\) . A\nfinite sequence has a final index; an infinite sequence is defined for\nevery positive integer, even though we cannot list all terms. Example 1. For \\(a_n=3n-1\\) , the first four terms are \\(2,5,8,11\\) . The term \\(a_{20}=59\\) is found by substitution; it is\nnot necessary to write all previous terms. Guided practice 1. Evaluate \\(a_1,a_2,a_5,a_{12}\\) for \\(a_n=n^2-2n\\) . Identify whether the first\nfour terms are increasing. Visual\ninvestigation 1: discrete versus continuous. Plot \\(a_n=2n+1\\) at \\(n=1,2,3,4,5\\) . Then draw the line \\(y=2x+1\\) on the same axes. The dots belong\nto the sequence; the continuous line is an extension. Explain why a\nvalue at \\(x=2.5\\) is not a term of the\noriginal sequence. image"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Explicit formulas,\ntables, and recursion", "url": "chapter12.html#explicit-formulas-tables-and-recursion", "text": "An explicit formula computes \\(a_n\\) directly from \\(n\\) . A recurrence defines later\nterms using earlier terms and requires an initial condition. For example \\(a_1=4\\) and \\(a_{n+1}=a_n+3\\) generates \\(4,7,10,13,\\ldots\\) . A recurrence without an\ninitial condition does not specify a unique sequence. Tables and graphs\nhelp identify patterns, but a few observations never prove a rule for\nall indices. Example 2. Given \\(a_1=2\\) and \\(a_{n+1}=2a_n+1\\) , successive terms are \\(2,5,11,23\\) . Solving \\(a_{n+1}+1=2(a_n+1)\\) suggests \\(a_n+1=3\\cdot2^{n-1}\\) , hence \\(a_n=3\\cdot2^{n-1}-1\\) . Verify the initial\ncondition and recurrence. Guided practice 2. Generate five terms from \\(b_1=10\\) , \\(b_{n+1}=0.8b_n\\) . Explain the meaning of\nmultiplying by \\(0.8\\) each step."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Arithmetic sequences:\nconstant differences", "url": "chapter12.html#arithmetic-sequences-constant-differences", "text": "A sequence is arithmetic when \\(a_{n+1}-a_n=d\\) is constant. Starting at \\(a_1\\) , we add \\(d\\) once to obtain \\(a_2\\) , twice to obtain \\(a_3\\) , and \\(n-1\\) times to obtain \\(a_n\\) . Thus \\[\\boxed{a_n=a_1+(n-1)d}.\\] A negative \\(d\\) gives a decreasing sequence, and \\(d=0\\) gives a constant sequence. Arithmetic\nchange is the discrete counterpart of a linear function: every unit\nincrease in the index changes the output by the same amount. Example 3. A laboratory has \\(240\\) test strips at the start of week 1\nand uses \\(18\\) strips each week. The\ninventory at the start of week \\(n\\) is \\(a_n=240-18(n-1)\\) , as long as no new\nstock arrives. At the start of week 8, \\(a_8=114\\) . The mathematical formula becomes\nphysically invalid after the stock is exhausted. Guided practice 3. An arithmetic sequence has \\(a_4=17\\) and \\(a_{10}=41\\) . Subtract the two equations to\ndetermine \\(d\\) ; then find \\(a_1\\) and \\(a_{25}\\) . Visual investigation\n2: first differences. Create a table of \\(n\\) , \\(a_n\\) , and \\(a_{n+1}-a_n\\) for \\(a_n=12-2n\\) . Explain how the constant\ndifferences appear as a fixed vertical step between consecutive plotted\npoints."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Arithmetic\nseries: accumulating a constant difference", "url": "chapter12.html#arithmetic-series-accumulating-a-constant-difference", "text": "A series adds terms of a sequence. Write \\(S_n=a_1+a_2+\\cdots+a_n\\) . For an arithmetic\nsequence, pairing the first and last terms gives \\(a_1+a_n\\) ; the second and next-to-last also\nadd to \\(a_1+a_n\\) . Counting \\(n\\) terms and dividing by two yields \\[\\boxed{S_n=\\frac n2(a_1+a_n)=\\frac\nn2\\bigl(2a_1+(n-1)d\\bigr)}.\\] This formula works for odd and even \\(n\\) ; the pairing argument can be\nformalized by writing the sum forwards and backwards and adding the two\nequations. Example 4. A community program enrolls 15 new\nparticipants in its first week and increases weekly enrollment by 4. In\n12 weeks the weekly enrollment is \\(15+11(4)=59\\) , so total enrollment is \\(S_{12}=12(15+59)/2=444\\) participants,\nassuming no person enrolls twice. Guided practice 4. Calculate \\(5+9+13+\\cdots+65\\) by first finding the\nnumber of terms. Verify your result by pairing terms."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Geometric sequences:\nconstant ratios", "url": "chapter12.html#geometric-sequences-constant-ratios", "text": "A geometric sequence satisfies \\(a_{n+1}/a_n=r\\) when \\(a_n\\ne0\\) . Equivalently \\(a_{n+1}=ra_n\\) , which also defines the\nall-zero case. Repeated multiplication gives \\[\\boxed{a_n=a_1r^{n-1}}.\\] When \\(r>1\\) and \\(a_1>0\\) , the sequence grows; when \\(0<r<1\\) , it decays; when \\(r<0\\) , signs alternate. The case \\(r=1\\) is constant. For a negative ratio, do\nnot infer growth or decay from the sign alone: examine \\(|r|\\) . Example 5. A simulated culture begins with 500 cells\nand doubles each generation. Generation \\(n\\) has \\(a_n=500\\cdot2^{n-1}\\) . At generation 6, the\nmodel predicts 16,000 cells. Real cultures cannot double indefinitely\nbecause nutrients and space are finite. Guided practice 5. A concentration is multiplied by \\(0.75\\) each hour, starting at \\(80\\) units. Write an explicit formula and\ncompute the value after four full hours. Carefully state whether the\nstarting time is indexed by \\(n=0\\) or \\(n=1\\) . Visual\ninvestigation 3: ratios versus differences. Compare \\(a_n=4+3(n-1)\\) with \\(b_n=4(1.5)^{n-1}\\) for \\(n=1,\\ldots,8\\) . Calculate consecutive\ndifferences and ratios. Which measure is constant for each model? When\ndo the geometric values overtake the arithmetic values?"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Finite geometric\nseries and their derivation", "url": "chapter12.html#finite-geometric-series-and-their-derivation", "text": "For \\(S_n=a_1+a_1r+\\cdots+a_1r^{n-1}\\) , multiply\nby \\(r\\) and subtract: \\[S_n-rS_n=a_1-a_1r^n.\\] Therefore, for \\(r\\ne1\\) , \\[\\boxed{S_n=a_1\\frac{1-r^n}{1-r}}.\\] For \\(r=1\\) , all terms equal \\(a_1\\) and \\(S_n=na_1\\) . The finite-sum formula is valid\nfor negative ratios and ratios larger than one; the infinite-sum formula\ndiscussed later needs additional conditions. Example 6. A scholarship fund distributes \\(1000\\) in year 1 and 90% of the previous\nyear’s distribution in each of the next four years. The five-year total\nis \\(1000(1-0.9^5)/(1-0.9)=\\$4095.10\\) . Guided practice 6. Find \\(3+6+12+24+48+96\\) using both direct\naddition and the geometric formula. What changes if the ratio equals\none?"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Sigma notation and\nindex transformations", "url": "chapter12.html#sigma-notation-and-index-transformations", "text": "Sigma notation \\(\\sum_{k=m}^{n}f(k)\\) means evaluate \\(f(k)\\) at each integer from \\(m\\) through \\(n\\) and add the results. The letter \\(k\\) is a dummy index; replacing it\nconsistently with \\(j\\) does not change\nthe sum. Useful properties are \\[\\sum_{k=1}^n(cu_k+dv_k)=c\\sum_{k=1}^n\nu_k+d\\sum_{k=1}^n v_k,\\quad \\sum_{k=1}^n1=n.\\] In particular \\(\\sum_{k=1}^n k=n(n+1)/2\\) . Changing\nthe starting index requires changing the summand: \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{4}2^k=\\sum_{j=1}^{5}2^{j-1}\\) . Example 7. Evaluate \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{5}(2k+1)=2(1+2+3+4+5)+5=35\\) .\nChecking by listing the terms \\(3+5+7+9+11\\) gives the same result. Guided practice 7. Rewrite \\(7+10+13+16+19\\) using sigma notation in two\ndifferent ways, one beginning at index 0 and one at index 1."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Mathematical\ninduction: proving a pattern", "url": "chapter12.html#mathematical-induction-proving-a-pattern", "text": "Observing a pattern in the first ten terms is not a proof that it\nholds for every positive integer. Mathematical induction has two parts.\nFirst verify the base case. Next assume a statement \\(P(n)\\) is true and use that assumption to\nprove \\(P(n+1)\\) . The conclusion then\nholds for every integer from the starting index onward. Example 8. Prove \\(1+2+\\cdots+n=n(n+1)/2\\) . Base case \\(n=1\\) : both sides equal 1. Assume \\(\\sum_{k=1}^n k=n(n+1)/2\\) . Then \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{n+1}k=n(n+1)/2+(n+1)=(n+1)(n+2)/2\\) ,\nwhich is the desired formula at \\(n+1\\) . Guided practice 8. Prove \\(\\sum_{k=1}^n(2k-1)=n^2\\) by induction.\nIdentify exactly where the induction hypothesis is used."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Infinite geometric\nseries and convergence", "url": "chapter12.html#infinite-geometric-series-and-convergence", "text": "An infinite series is defined by the limit of its partial sums, not\nby performing infinitely many additions at once. If \\(|r|<1\\) , then \\(r^n\\to0\\) , so \\[\\boxed{\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}ar^k=\\frac{a}{1-r}\\qquad(|r|<1)}.\\] If \\(|r|\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\ne0\\) , the terms do not tend to zero and\nthe geometric series does not converge. The special all-zero series\nconverges trivially. Convergence is about partial sums approaching a\nfinite value, not merely individual terms becoming small. Example 9. A patient receives an idealized sequence\nof doses contributing \\(8,4,2,1,\\ldots\\) units to a cumulative\ntotal. The theoretical sum is \\(8/(1-1/2)=16\\) units. This is a sum of\ncontributions, not automatically a model for the amount of medication\npresent in the body at one time. Guided practice 9. Determine whether \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}5(-0.4)^k\\) converges\nand compute its sum. Explain why alternating signs do not prevent\nconvergence in this example. Visual investigation 4:\npartial sums. Draw \\(S_n\\) for \\(a=10\\) and \\(r=0.6\\) , with \\(n=1,\\ldots,12\\) . Add the horizontal line \\(S=25\\) . Why does the graph approach\nbut never exceed 25? What changes for \\(r=-0.6\\) ?"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Recursive models with\nconstant input", "url": "chapter12.html#recursive-models-with-constant-input", "text": "Many processes combine retention and repeated input: \\(a_{n+1}=ra_n+b\\) . For \\(r\\ne1\\) , an equilibrium \\(L\\) satisfies \\(L=rL+b\\) , hence \\(L=b/(1-r)\\) . Subtracting the equilibrium\ngives \\(a_{n+1}-L=r(a_n-L)\\) and\ntherefore \\[\\boxed{a_n=L+(a_1-L)r^{n-1}}.\\] If \\(|r|<1\\) , the sequence approaches \\(L\\) . If \\(r=1\\) , the recurrence is arithmetic: \\(a_n=a_1+(n-1)b\\) . If \\(|r|>1\\) , deviations from equilibrium\ngenerally grow rather than shrink. Example 10. A simulated medication model retains 70%\nof the previous amount each day and adds 12 mg at the next daily\nmeasurement: \\(a_{n+1}=0.7a_n+12\\) ,\nwith \\(a_1=12\\) mg. The equilibrium is \\(L=12/0.3=40\\) mg, so \\(a_n=40-28(0.7)^{n-1}\\) . This is a\nsimplified discrete model, not a clinical dosing recommendation. Guided practice 10. Solve \\(a_{n+1}=0.8a_n+5\\) , \\(a_1=5\\) . Find its equilibrium and its sixth\nterm."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Compounding,\nannuities, and financial interpretation", "url": "chapter12.html#compounding-annuities-and-financial-interpretation", "text": "With interest rate \\(i\\) per\ncompounding period and initial balance \\(P\\) , a single deposit grows to \\(P(1+i)^n\\) after \\(n\\) periods. If a fixed deposit \\(D\\) is made at the end of each\nperiod, its future value after \\(n\\) deposits is \\[F_n=D\\bigl[1+(1+i)+\\cdots+(1+i)^{n-1}\\bigr]=D\\frac{(1+i)^n-1}{i},\\quad\ni\\ne0.\\] For \\(i=0\\) , \\(F_n=nD\\) . Timing matters:\nbeginning-of-period deposits earn one additional period of interest.\nFees, taxes, and varying rates are not included in this idealized\nformula. Example 11. A student deposits \\(50\\) at the end of each month for 12 months\nat a hypothetical monthly rate of \\(0.005\\) . The future value is \\(50((1.005)^{12}-1)/0.005\\approx\\$616.78\\) .\nTotal deposits are \\(\\$600\\) ; the\ndifference represents modeled interest. Guided practice 11. Derive the future value for\ndeposits of \\(25\\) at the beginning of\neach month for six months, with monthly interest rate \\(0.01\\) ."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Difference tables and\nfinite differences", "url": "chapter12.html#difference-tables-and-finite-differences", "text": "For a sequence \\(a_n\\) , its first\ndifferences are \\(\\Delta\na_n=a_{n+1}-a_n\\) ; second differences are \\(\\Delta^2a_n=\\Delta a_{n+1}-\\Delta a_n\\) . A\nlinear formula has constant first differences, and a quadratic formula \\(a_n=An^2+Bn+C\\) has constant second\ndifferences equal to \\(2A\\) when\nindices increase by one. These patterns are useful for recognizing\ncandidate formulas, but finite data alone cannot guarantee that a model\ncontinues indefinitely. Example 12. For \\(a_n=n^2+2n\\) , the first five terms are \\(3,8,15,24,35\\) . First differences are \\(5,7,9,11\\) ; second differences are \\(2,2,2\\) . This is consistent with a\nquadratic whose leading coefficient is \\(1\\) . Guided practice 12. The observed values are \\(4,9,16,25,36\\) . Construct a difference\ntable and propose an explicit formula. Give one reason the next value is\nnot logically forced by only five observations."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Patterns in real\ndata: modeling and limitations", "url": "chapter12.html#patterns-in-real-data-modeling-and-limitations", "text": "Observed sequences contain noise, missed measurements, changing\nconditions, and possible structural breaks. Arithmetic and geometric\npatterns are models, not laws. Compare predictions to data, examine\nresiduals \\(e_n=y_n-\\hat y_n\\) , and\nstate the measurement interval. A ratio of 1.1 per week differs from a\nratio of 1.1 per month. Predictions far beyond the observed indices are\nextrapolations and should be interpreted cautiously. Example 13. Weekly test demand is \\(100,108,117,126\\) units. Ratios are\napproximately \\(1.080,1.083,1.077\\) ; an\n8% geometric growth model is plausible as a short-term approximation,\nnot an exact identity. Predict week 5 as \\(100(1.08)^4\\approx136\\) and compare with\nlater observations when available. Guided practice 13. A clinic records \\(40,44,48,53,57\\) visits over five weeks.\nCompare arithmetic and geometric descriptions. Which model has the\nclearer interpretation for this short series? What additional evidence\nwould you seek?"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "From discrete change to\ncalculus", "url": "chapter12.html#from-discrete-change-to-calculus", "text": "The average change between successive terms is \\(\\Delta a_n=a_{n+1}-a_n\\) . For \\(a_n=f(nh)\\) with time step \\(h\\) , the rate per unit time is \\([f((n+1)h)-f(nh)]/h\\) . When \\(h\\) becomes very small, calculus studies\nthe limit of this quotient. Likewise, a sum of rectangular contributions \\(\\sum f(t_k)\\Delta t\\) anticipates a\ndefinite integral. Sequences and finite sums therefore connect the\ndiscrete and continuous viewpoints. Example 14. For \\(f(t)=t^2\\) , the forward difference quotient\nis \\([(t+h)^2-t^2]/h=2t+h\\) . As \\(h\\to0\\) , this approaches \\(2t\\) , the derivative of \\(t^2\\) . At a fixed time step \\(h=1\\) , the difference quotient is \\(2t+1\\) rather than \\(2t\\) . Guided practice 14. Compute the forward difference\nquotient for \\(f(t)=3t^2-2t\\) and\nidentify its limiting expression as \\(h\\to0\\) . Visual\ninvestigation 5: discrete accumulation. For \\(f(t)=t\\) on \\([0,4]\\) , calculate the left-endpoint sums\nusing 4 and 8 equal rectangles. Compare each estimate with the triangle\narea \\(8\\) . Explain why finer\nsubdivisions improve the approximation."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Deeper\ninvestigation: geometric patterns and the doubling-time question", "url": "chapter12.html#deeper-investigation-geometric-patterns-and-the-doubling-time-question", "text": "A common scientific question asks how many time steps are required\nfor an initially positive quantity to reach a target. If \\(a_n=a_1r^{n-1}\\) with \\(r>1\\) , then \\(a_n\\ge T\\) is equivalent to \\(r^{n-1}\\ge T/a_1\\) . Because \\(\\ln r>0\\) , taking natural logarithms\npreserves the inequality: \\[n\\ge\n1+\\frac{\\ln(T/a_1)}{\\ln r}.\\] The smallest feasible integer index\nis the ceiling of the right-hand side, subject to \\(n\\ge1\\) . If \\(0<r<1\\) , the logarithm of the ratio\nis negative and inequality directions must be handled with care. A\ndoubling time measured in steps is \\(\\ln2/\\ln\nr\\) for \\(r>1\\) ; it need not\nbe an integer, so observed doubling at discrete measurements may occur\nonly after rounding to a later index. Example 15. A simulated population starts at 250 and\ngrows by 12% each generation. To exceed 500, solve \\(250(1.12)^{n-1}>500\\) . This requires \\(n-1>\\ln2/\\ln1.12\\approx6.12\\) , so\nthe first qualifying term is \\(n=8\\) .\nAt \\(n=7\\) the threshold has not yet\nbeen crossed. Guided practice 15. A culture starts with 600 units\nand grows by 7% per generation. Determine the first term that exceeds\n1000. Explain why a fractional index is not a valid observation\nnumber. Visual investigation\n6: threshold crossing. Plot \\(600(1.07)^{n-1}\\) for \\(n=1,\\ldots,15\\) , together with a horizontal\nline at 1000. Mark the first discrete term above the threshold. Compare\nthis with the continuous exponential extension and interpret the\ndifference."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Deeper\ninvestigation: what happens when the ratio is negative?", "url": "chapter12.html#deeper-investigation-what-happens-when-the-ratio-is-negative", "text": "Negative ratios are mathematically important even when a direct\nphysical interpretation is less obvious. Consider \\(a_n=10(-0.5)^{n-1}\\) . The signs alternate,\nwhile the absolute values shrink: \\(10,-5,2.5,-1.25,\\ldots\\) . Its partial sums\noscillate around the infinite-series value \\(10/(1+0.5)=20/3\\) . Because \\(|-0.5|<1\\) , the oscillations shrink and\nthe series converges. By contrast, for \\(r=-1\\) , the terms alternate between \\(a\\) and \\(-a\\) without approaching zero, so the\npartial sums oscillate without settling. Example 16. For \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}12(-1/3)^k\\) , the\ninfinite sum is \\(12/(1+1/3)=9\\) . The\nfirst four partial sums are \\(12,8,28/3,80/9\\) , alternately above and\nbelow 9. The finite geometric formula verifies each value. Guided practice 16. Compute the first six partial\nsums for \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}8(-1/2)^k\\) . Identify\nwhich partial sums lie above the limit and which lie below. Visual\ninvestigation 7: alternating partial sums. Draw the partial sums as dots and the limiting sum as a horizontal\nline. Explain why the error changes sign at each new term and why its\nmagnitude decreases geometrically."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Deeper\ninvestigation: comparing two competing models", "url": "chapter12.html#deeper-investigation-comparing-two-competing-models", "text": "Suppose a program’s weekly demand can be represented either by an\narithmetic model \\(A_n=100+8(n-1)\\) or\nby a geometric model \\(G_n=100(1.06)^{n-1}\\) . Both begin at 100,\nbut their long-run behavior differs. The arithmetic model increases by\nexactly eight units each week; the geometric model increases by 6% of\nits current level. At week 2, the arithmetic prediction is 108 and the\ngeometric prediction is 106. At week 10, the arithmetic prediction is\n172, while the geometric prediction is approximately 169. 1 At\nlater indices, compounding eventually produces larger increments in the\ngeometric model. Example 17. To decide which model is more suitable,\ncollect additional observations and calculate residuals \\(e_n=y_n-\\widehat y_n\\) for both. Compare\nresidual patterns and not only the final prediction. If the program has\na strict capacity limit, neither model is credible indefinitely. Guided practice 17. Evaluate both models at weeks 5,\n15, and 25. Find the first week when the geometric model exceeds the\narithmetic model. Explain how a capacity constraint would change your\nrecommendation. Visual investigation\n8: a model crossover. Plot both models on the same coordinate system. Annotate their shared\nstarting value, their different short-term changes, and their eventual\ncrossover. Discuss how a graph can reveal a pattern but cannot establish\nthe correctness of an empirical model."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Additional\nguided-practice clinic: selecting the right formula", "url": "chapter12.html#additional-guided-practice-clinic-selecting-the-right-formula", "text": "Problem A: a sum or a term? A health department\nreceives 30 shipments in month 1 and two additional shipments each\nmonth. Find (i) shipments received in month 12 and (ii) total shipments\nover 12 months. Solution strategy: The first question asks for \\(a_{12}=30+11(2)=52\\) ; the second asks\nfor \\(S_{12}=12(30+52)/2=492\\) . The\ndifferent units of interpretation help distinguish the two answers. Problem B: an explicit formula or a recurrence? A\nsystem starts at 4 and triples the previous output, then subtracts 2.\nThe recurrence is \\(a_{n+1}=3a_n-2\\) , \\(a_1=4\\) . The equilibrium is 1, so \\(a_n=1+3(3^{n-1})\\) . Check that \\(a_2=10\\) and \\(a_3=28\\) . Follow-up: Explain why\nsimply writing \\(4(3^{n-1})-2\\) is\nincorrect. Problem C: is the infinite sum allowed? Compare \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}4(0.9)^k\\) and \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}4(1.1)^k\\) . The\nfirst converges to 40; the second diverges because the terms do not\napproach zero. Follow-up: Compute both finite ten-term sums and\nexplain why finite sums always exist even when an infinite series\ndiverges. Problem D: what does the index mean? A medication\namount is measured immediately after a dose at time 0 and then once per\nday. If the first amount is 20 mg and 65% remains each day, \\(M_n=20(0.65)^n\\) for \\(n=0,1,2,\\ldots\\) . Re-indexing the same data\nat \\(n=1\\) gives \\(a_n=20(0.65)^{n-1}\\) . Follow-up: Explain why these formulas describe the same measurements but assign\ndifferent index labels. Problem E: interpreting a limiting total. A program\nspends \\(500\\) in its first phase and\n80% as much in each subsequent phase. The modeled infinite total is \\(500/(1-0.8)=\\$2500\\) . Follow-up: Determine how many phases are needed to reach at\nleast 95% of this total, and discuss whether infinitely many phases are\nrealistic."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: arithmetic and geometric patterns", "url": "chapter12.html#computational-laboratory-a-arithmetic-and-geometric-patterns", "text": "Prediction. An arithmetic and a geometric sequence\nboth begin at 4. Which will be larger at term 10 if the arithmetic\ndifference is 3 and the geometric ratio is 1.5? Predict before running\neither program."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter12.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code n <- 1 : 12 arithmetic <- 4 + 3 * (n -1 ) geometric <- 4 * 1.5 ^ (n -1 ) print ( data.frame (n, arithmetic, geometric)) cat ( \"First overtake:\" , n[ which (geometric > arithmetic)[ 1 ]], \" \\n \" ) plot (n, arithmetic, pch= 19 , col= \"blue\" , ylim= range ( c (arithmetic, geometric)), xlab= \"Term index\" , ylab= \"Term value\" , main= \"Arithmetic and geometric sequences\" ) points (n, geometric, pch= 17 , col= \"red\" ) legend ( \"topleft\" , c ( \"Arithmetic\" , \"Geometric\" ), col= c ( \"blue\" , \"red\" ), pch= c ( 19 , 17 ))"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter12.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt n = np.arange( 1 , 13 ) arithmetic = 4 + 3 * (n - 1 ) geometric = 4 * 1.5 ** (n - 1 ) for k, x, y in zip (n, arithmetic, geometric): print ( f\"n= { k :2d} arithmetic= { x :7.2f} geometric= { y :9.2f} \" ) print ( \"First overtake:\" , n[np.where(geometric > arithmetic)[ 0 ][ 0 ]]) plt.plot(n, arithmetic, \"o\" , label = \"Arithmetic\" ) plt.plot(n, geometric, \"^\" , label = \"Geometric\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Term index\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"Term value\" ) ; plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"labA_python.png\" , dpi = 180 ) ; plt.close() Interpretation. Check term 1, term 2, and term 10 by\nhand. Use the graph to distinguish isolated terms from continuous\nmodels. Find the first index at which the geometric sequence exceeds the\narithmetic sequence."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: partial sums and convergence", "url": "chapter12.html#computational-laboratory-b-partial-sums-and-convergence", "text": "Prediction. For \\(a=10\\) and \\(r=0.6\\) , the first partial sum is 10 and\nthe infinite sum is 25. Predict whether the tenth partial sum is closer\nto 20 or 25."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter12.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code n <- 1 : 15 a <- 10 ; r <- 0.6 terms <- a * r ^ (n -1 ) partial <- cumsum (terms) formula <- a * ( 1 - r ^ n) / ( 1 - r) print ( data.frame (n, partial, formula, error= partial - formula)) plot (n, partial, type= \"b\" , pch= 19 , xlab= \"Number of terms\" , ylab= \"Partial sum\" , main= \"Convergent geometric series\" ) abline ( h= a / ( 1 - r), lty= 2 , col= \"red\" )"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter12.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt n = np.arange( 1 , 16 ) a, r = 10.0 , 0.6 terms = a * r ** (n - 1 ) partial = np.cumsum(terms) formula = a * ( 1 - r ** n) / ( 1 - r) print ( \"Largest discrepancy:\" , np. max (np. abs (partial - formula))) for k, s in zip (n, partial): print ( f\"S_ { k } = { s :.6f} \" ) plt.plot(n, partial, \"o-\" ) ; plt.axhline(a / ( 1 - r), linestyle = \"--\" , label = \"Infinite sum\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Number of terms\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"Partial sum\" ) ; plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"labB_python.png\" , dpi = 180 ) ; plt.close() Interpretation. Compare direct cumulative sums with\nthe formula. Explain why \\(|r|<1\\) is needed for the infinite sum. Change \\(r\\) to \\(-0.6\\) and describe oscillation around the\nlimit."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: repeated input and model sensitivity", "url": "chapter12.html#computational-laboratory-c-repeated-input-and-model-sensitivity", "text": "Prediction. Consider \\(a_{n+1}=ra_n+12\\) , \\(a_1=12\\) . Compare the equilibrium when \\(r=0.7\\) and when \\(r=0.85\\) . Which system approaches a larger\nlong-run amount, and which approaches its equilibrium more slowly?"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter12.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code n <- 1 : 25 b <- 12 plot ( NA , xlim= c ( 1 , 25 ), ylim= c ( 0 , 85 ), xlab= \"Measurement index\" , ylab= \"Modeled amount (mg)\" , main= \"Retention and repeated input\" ) for (r in c ( 0.7 , 0.85 )) { x <- numeric ( length (n)); x[ 1 ] <- 12 for (k in 2 : length (n)) x[k] <- r * x[k -1 ] + b equilibrium <- b / ( 1 - r) exact <- equilibrium + (x[ 1 ] - equilibrium) * r ^ (n -1 ) print ( data.frame ( n= n[ 1 : 6 ], modeled= x[ 1 : 6 ], explicit= exact[ 1 : 6 ])) lines (n,x, type= \"b\" , pch= ifelse (r == 0.7 , 19 , 17 ), col= ifelse (r == 0.7 , \"blue\" , \"red\" )) abline ( h= equilibrium, lty= 2 , col= ifelse (r == 0.7 , \"blue\" , \"red\" )) } legend ( \"topleft\" , c ( \"r=0.70\" , \"r=0.85\" ), col= c ( \"blue\" , \"red\" ), pch= c ( 19 , 17 ))"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter12.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt n = np.arange( 1 , 26 ) b = 12.0 for r in ( 0.7 , 0.85 ): x = np.zeros( len (n)) ; x[ 0 ] = 12 for k in range ( 1 , len (n)): x[k] = r * x[k - 1 ] + b equilibrium = b / ( 1 - r) explicit = equilibrium + (x[ 0 ] - equilibrium) * r ** (n - 1 ) assert np.allclose(x, explicit) print ( f\"r= { r } , equilibrium= { equilibrium :.2f} , first six= { np . round (x[: 6 ], 2 ) } \" ) plt.plot(n, x, \"o-\" , label = f\"r= { r } \" ) plt.axhline(equilibrium, linestyle = \"--\" , alpha = 0.5 ) plt.xlabel( \"Measurement index\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"Modeled amount (mg)\" ) ; plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"labC_python.png\" , dpi = 180 ) ; plt.close() Interpretation. Verify \\(a_2\\) manually. Compare the numerical\nrecurrence with the closed form. Explain why the model is an\nillustration of repeated input and retention, not a medical treatment\nrecommendation."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Extended guided workshops", "url": "chapter12.html#extended-guided-workshops", "text": ""}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Workshop 1: staffing\nand cumulative workload", "url": "chapter12.html#workshop-1-staffing-and-cumulative-workload", "text": "A health program expects 24 appointments in week 1, with six\nadditional appointments each subsequent week for 10 weeks. (a) Explain\nwhy the weekly appointments form an arithmetic sequence. (b) Find week\n10 demand. (c) Find the cumulative appointments over 10 weeks. (d) If a\nstaff member can handle 48 appointments weekly, identify the first week\nwhen demand exceeds that capacity. Worked solution. \\(a_n=24+6(n-1)\\) , so \\(a_{10}=78\\) . Total \\(S_{10}=10(24+78)/2=510\\) . Solve \\(24+6(n-1)>48\\) , giving \\(n>5\\) , so the first overloaded week is\nweek 6. Extension. Discuss why the model may need\nrevision when staff availability changes."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Workshop 2: culture\ngrowth and resource limits", "url": "chapter12.html#workshop-2-culture-growth-and-resource-limits", "text": "A simulated cell population begins at 300 and increases by 20% each\ngeneration. (a) Write \\(a_n\\) with \\(a_1=300\\) . (b) Find generation 5. (c)\nCalculate the sum of the first five generation counts, and explain why\nthat sum does not equal the population at generation 5. Worked solution. \\(a_n=300(1.2)^{n-1}\\) ; \\(a_5=300(1.2)^4=622.08\\) . The sum is \\(300((1.2)^5-1)/0.2=2232.48\\) cell-count\nobservations. Adding repeated snapshots does not measure distinct cells\nor a single-time population. Extension. Identify the\nbiological limits of sustained exponential growth."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Workshop 3: repeated-dose\nretention model", "url": "chapter12.html#workshop-3-repeated-dose-retention-model", "text": "A simplified measurement model has \\(a_1=10\\) mg and \\(a_{n+1}=0.6a_n+10\\) . (a) Find the first\nfour terms. (b) Find the equilibrium. (c) Derive the explicit formula.\n(d) Determine when the amount first exceeds 24 mg. Worked\nsolution. The terms are \\(10,16,19.6,21.76\\) . The equilibrium is \\(10/(1-0.6)=25\\) mg. The explicit\nformula is \\(a_n=25-15(0.6)^{n-1}\\) .\nSolve \\(25-15(0.6)^{n-1}>24\\) ,\nequivalent to \\((0.6)^{n-1}<1/15\\) .\nTaking logarithms gives \\(n-1>\\ln(1/15)/\\ln(0.6)\\approx5.30\\) ;\nhence the first integer is \\(n=7\\) . Extension. Check the threshold numerically and\ninterpret why the equilibrium is never reached exactly from below."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Workshop 4:\nfinite differences and a candidate model", "url": "chapter12.html#workshop-4-finite-differences-and-a-candidate-model", "text": "A researcher records \\(7,12,19,28,39\\) at equally spaced visits.\n(a) Find first and second differences. (b) Propose a quadratic model\nindexed from \\(n=1\\) . (c) Predict the\nsixth value. (d) Explain why this is an extrapolation rather than a\nproven future outcome. Worked solution. First\ndifferences \\(5,7,9,11\\) ; second\ndifferences \\(2,2,2\\) . Set \\(a_n=n^2+Bn+C\\) . Using \\(a_1=7\\) and \\(a_2=12\\) gives \\(B=2\\) , \\(C=4\\) , so \\(a_n=n^2+2n+4\\) and \\(a_6=52\\) . Many alternative functions can\nmatch the same five data points."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Workshop 5: geometric sums\nand savings", "url": "chapter12.html#workshop-5-geometric-sums-and-savings", "text": "A community organization sets aside \\(200\\) at the end of each quarter for eight\nquarters. Assume a hypothetical quarterly interest rate of 1%. (a)\nExpress the final balance as a geometric sum. (b) Calculate the modeled\nfuture value. (c) Compare with total contributions. (d) Explain the\neffect of deposits at the beginning rather than end of each quarter. Worked solution. \\(F=200\\sum_{k=0}^{7}(1.01)^k=200((1.01)^8-1)/0.01\\approx\\$1657.14\\) .\nContributions total \\(\\$1600\\) .\nBeginning-of-quarter deposits multiply the future value by \\(1.01\\) . The model assumes a fixed interest\nrate and ignores fees and taxes."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Common errors and\ndiagnostic questions", "url": "chapter12.html#common-errors-and-diagnostic-questions", "text": "Confusing the index \\(n\\) with\nthe term \\(a_n\\) : explain the\ndifference using \\(a_n=2n+1\\) . Writing \\(a_n=a_1+nd\\) for an\narithmetic sequence indexed at \\(n=1\\) :\nidentify and correct the off-by-one error. Using \\(S_n=a_1(1-r^n)/(1-r)\\) when \\(r=1\\) : explain the special\ncase. Applying the infinite geometric sum when \\(r=1.2\\) : explain why it fails. Treating cumulative observations as a single-time measurement:\ngive a scientific example. Assuming a visually convincing pattern proves a model:\ndistinguish conjecture, proof, and empirical fit. Forgetting deposit timing in an annuity: compare beginning and\nend-of-period contributions. Confusing the equilibrium \\(L\\) of a recurrence with its first term: derive \\(L=b/(1-r)\\) ."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Chapter review and\nself-assessment", "url": "chapter12.html#chapter-review-and-self-assessment", "text": "Instructions. Show mathematical reasoning and state\nthe units and indexing conventions for applied questions. List the first five terms of \\(a_n=4n-3\\) . Find \\(a_{12}\\) if \\(a_n=2n^2-1\\) . Give an explicit rule for \\(6,10,14,18,\\ldots\\) . Find the 25th term of \\(11,8,5,2,\\ldots\\) . Find \\(a_1\\) and \\(d\\) when \\(a_3=15\\) and \\(a_8=35\\) . Determine whether \\(2,6,18,54\\) is arithmetic, geometric, or neither. Find the eighth term of a geometric sequence with \\(a_1=5\\) and \\(r=3\\) . Find \\(a_6\\) when \\(a_1=100\\) and \\(r=0.8\\) . Write a recurrence for \\(a_n=7+5(n-1)\\) . Generate five terms from \\(a_1=2\\) , \\(a_{n+1}=3a_n-1\\) . Evaluate \\(\\sum_{k=1}^8(2k-1)\\) . Evaluate \\(\\sum_{k=0}^5\n3(2)^k\\) . Rewrite \\(4+7+10+13+16\\) in\nsigma notation. Find the sum of the first 20 terms of \\(5,9,13,\\ldots\\) . Find the sum \\(12+9+6+\\cdots-15\\) . Derive the arithmetic series formula by pairing terms. Derive the finite geometric sum formula by subtraction. Evaluate \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}6(1/3)^k\\) . Determine whether \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}2(-1.1)^k\\) converges. Find the sum of \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}5(-0.2)^k\\) . Explain the difference between a sequence and its partial\nsums. Prove \\(1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1)=n^2\\) by induction. Solve \\(a_{n+1}=0.5a_n+8\\) with \\(a_1=8\\) . Find the equilibrium of \\(a_{n+1}=0.9a_n+3\\) . Find the future value of six end-of-month deposits of \\(100\\) at 0.5% monthly interest. Construct first and second differences for \\(2,7,14,23,34\\) . Propose a quadratic rule for the preceding data. Explain why five observations cannot prove an infinite\npattern. Find the forward difference quotient for \\(f(t)=t^2+3t\\) . Explain how a Riemann sum differs from a finite sum of observed\ncounts."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter12.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "Explain how \\(\\Delta\na_n=a_{n+1}-a_n\\) parallels a difference quotient. For \\(f(t)=t^2\\) , derive \\([f(t+h)-f(t)]/h\\) and explain what changes\nas \\(h\\to0\\) . Draw a picture showing\nfour left-endpoint rectangles approximating the area under \\(f(t)=t\\) on \\([0,4]\\) . Why does increasing the number of\nrectangles improve the estimate? Finally, explain how a convergent\nsequence of partial sums can define an infinite series."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Looking ahead", "url": "chapter12.html#looking-ahead", "text": "Chapter 13 studies conic sections and parametric equations. Sequences\nprovide a natural way to sample points on a curve, while finite\ndifferences and sums will reappear in the study of change and\naccumulation in calculus."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Extended\napplication: Repeated sampling accumulation", "url": "chapter12.html#extended-application-repeated-sampling-accumulation", "text": "A simulated collection process yields 100 units on its first round\nand 80 percent as many units on each subsequent round. The quantities in\nthis investigation are illustrative rather than measured\nobservations."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter12.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[S_n=500(1-0.8^n).\\] Before calculating,\nidentify the input and output, their units, and the interval on which\nthe model is being studied. Distinguish the mathematical domain from the\npractical domain. The numerical study uses inputs from \\(1\\) to \\(15\\) . Example 18: Analytical investigation. Derive the\nfinite sum and its limiting value. Find the smallest integer \\(n\\) with \\(S_n\\ge450\\) . Worked solution. Geometric sum gives the formula and\nlimit 500. Since \\(0.8^n\\le0.1\\) , \\(n\\ge\\ln(0.1)/\\ln(0.8)\\) , so 11 rounds."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter12.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Completed rounds Cumulative units 1 100 5 336.2 8 416.1 11 457.1 15 482.4 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter12.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Repeated sampling accumulation # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- 1 : 15 y <- 500 * ( 1 -0.8 ^ x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"p\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Completed rounds\" , ylab = \"Cumulative units\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter12.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Repeated sampling accumulation import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.arange( 1 , 16 ) y = 500 * ( 1 - 0.8 ** x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"o\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Completed rounds\" , ylabel = \"Cumulative units\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter12.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Repeated sampling accumulation", "url": "chapter12.html#chapter-project-investigating-repeated-sampling-accumulation", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter12.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100 Numerical approximations should be verified with a\ncalculator or the provided scripts. ↩︎"}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "url": "chapter13.html#conic-sections-and-parametric-equations", "text": ""}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Opening context:\ngeometry in science and motion", "url": "chapter13.html#opening-context-geometry-in-science-and-motion", "text": "Circular calibration targets, elliptical cross sections, parabolic\ntrajectories, and moving sensors all connect algebra with geometry.\nConic sections give concise equations for important curves; parametric\nequations add a description of how a point moves along a curve. This\nchapter builds on coordinate geometry (Chapter 3), functions (Chapter\n4), quadratic equations (Chapter 2), and trigonometry (Chapters\n8–11)."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter13.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "By the end of this chapter, students will be able to (1) derive and\ngraph standard conic equations; (2) complete the square to find\ngeometric features; (3) distinguish nondegenerate conics from special or\ndegenerate cases; (4) locate vertices, foci, directrices, and\nasymptotes; (5) construct and interpret parametric curves with parameter\nrestrictions; (6) connect parameterization with motion; (7) use R and\nPython to visualize conics; and (8) assess assumptions and units in\napplied models."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Working with R and\nPython", "url": "chapter13.html#working-with-r-and-python", "text": "Each computational laboratory begins with a prediction, then presents\na full-width R code listing followed by a full-width Python code listing. Run them separately, compare\nresults with exact mathematics, and interpret plots in context."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Coordinate geometry\nand the conic family", "url": "chapter13.html#coordinate-geometry-and-the-conic-family", "text": "A conic section is a curve obtained by intersecting a plane with a\ndouble cone. Algebraically, the familiar axis-aligned conics arise from\nsecond-degree equations. The general quadratic equation \\(Ax^2+Bxy+Cy^2+Dx+Ey+F=0\\) can represent a\nconic, a degenerate conic, or no real points. This chapter focuses first\non axis-aligned forms, then introduces the role of the \\(xy\\) term. A graph is a set of ordered\npairs; an equation alone must be interpreted together with its real\nsolutions. Example. The equation \\(x^2+y^2=9\\) describes all points at\ndistance 3 from the origin, whereas \\(x^2+y^2=-9\\) has no real solutions. Guided practice 1. Explain why \\(x^2+y^2=0\\) represents a single point, not\na circle of positive radius."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Circles: distance\nand completing the square", "url": "chapter13.html#circles-distance-and-completing-the-square", "text": "From the distance formula, points \\((x,y)\\) a distance \\(r>0\\) from \\((h,k)\\) satisfy \\(\\sqrt{(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2}=r\\) . Squaring gives \\(\\boxed{(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2}\\) . The\ncenter is \\((h,k)\\) and radius \\(r\\) . Completing the square converts\nexpanded equations to standard form. Worked example. Rewrite \\(x^2+y^2-6x+4y-12=0\\) . Group terms: \\((x^2-6x)+(y^2+4y)=12\\) . Add 9 and 4 to both\nsides to obtain \\((x-3)^2+(y+2)^2=25\\) .\nCenter \\((3,-2)\\) , radius 5. Check that \\((8,-2)\\) lies on the circle. Guided practice 2. Convert \\(x^2+y^2+8x-2y+8=0\\) to standard form;\nidentify center, radius, and intercepts. Visual investigation 1. Sketch the relevant curve by hand before computing coordinates. Mark\nits center or vertex, the direction of its axes, and two diagnostic\npoints. Explain what the graph communicates that a list of numerical\nvalues does not. Compare a geometric prediction with a\ncomputer-generated graph. Reflection. Identify one feature that changes under\ntranslation and one that remains unchanged."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Parabolas as loci and\nquadratic graphs", "url": "chapter13.html#parabolas-as-loci-and-quadratic-graphs", "text": "A parabola consists of points equidistant from a fixed point (focus)\nand a fixed line (directrix). For vertical axis, \\(\\boxed{(x-h)^2=4p(y-k)}\\) has vertex \\((h,k)\\) , focus \\((h,k+p)\\) , directrix \\(y=k-p\\) . The parameter \\(p\\) is signed: positive opens upward,\nnegative downward. Equivalently \\(y=k+(x-h)^2/(4p)\\) . Horizontal-axis form is \\((y-k)^2=4p(x-h)\\) . Derivation. For vertex at the origin, focus \\((0,p)\\) and directrix \\(y=-p\\) , equate \\(\\sqrt{x^2+(y-p)^2}=|y+p|\\) . Squaring and\ncanceling \\(y^2+p^2\\) gives \\(x^2=4py\\) . Worked example. For \\((x-2)^2=-8(y+1)\\) , \\(4p=-8\\) , so \\(p=-2\\) . Vertex \\((2,-1)\\) , focus \\((2,-3)\\) , directrix \\(y=1\\) . Guided practice 3. Find the focus and directrix of \\(y=(x+1)^2/12+4\\) . Explain the meaning\nof the sign of \\(p\\) . Visual investigation 2. Sketch the relevant curve by hand before computing coordinates. Mark\nits center or vertex, the direction of its axes, and two diagnostic\npoints. Explain what the graph communicates that a list of numerical\nvalues does not. Compare a geometric prediction with a\ncomputer-generated graph. Reflection. Identify one feature that changes under\ntranslation and one that remains unchanged."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Ellipses: constant sum of\ndistances", "url": "chapter13.html#ellipses-constant-sum-of-distances", "text": "An ellipse is the set of points whose sum of distances to two foci is\nconstant and greater than the distance between the foci. For a\nhorizontal major axis, \\(\\boxed{\\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2}+\\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1}\\) with \\(a>b>0\\) . Vertices \\((h\\pm a,k)\\) , co-vertices \\((h,k\\pm b)\\) , and foci \\((h\\pm c,k)\\) where \\(c^2=a^2-b^2\\) . For a vertical major axis,\ninterchange the denominators. The eccentricity \\(e=c/a\\) satisfies \\(0<e<1\\) for a noncircular ellipse; a\ncircle is the limiting case \\(e=0\\) . Worked example. \\(\\frac{(x-1)^2}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^2}{9}=1\\) has center \\((1,-2)\\) , \\(a=5\\) , \\(b=3\\) , \\(c=4\\) , vertices \\((-4,-2)\\) and \\((6,-2)\\) , foci \\((-3,-2)\\) and \\((5,-2)\\) . Guided practice 4. Analyze \\(\\frac{(x+2)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^2}{16}=1\\) and sketch its major and minor axes. Visual investigation 3. Sketch the relevant curve by hand before computing coordinates. Mark\nits center or vertex, the direction of its axes, and two diagnostic\npoints. Explain what the graph communicates that a list of numerical\nvalues does not. Compare a geometric prediction with a\ncomputer-generated graph. Reflection. Identify one feature that changes under\ntranslation and one that remains unchanged."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Hyperbolas:\nconstant difference of distances", "url": "chapter13.html#hyperbolas-constant-difference-of-distances", "text": "A hyperbola consists of points whose absolute difference of distances\nto two foci is a positive constant smaller than the focal separation. A\nhorizontal hyperbola satisfies \\(\\boxed{\\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2}-\\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1}\\) .\nVertices \\((h\\pm a,k)\\) , foci \\((h\\pm c,k)\\) , \\(c^2=a^2+b^2\\) , and asymptotes \\(y-k=\\pm(b/a)(x-h)\\) . For a vertical\nhyperbola the positive term is the \\(y\\) term and asymptote slopes are \\(\\pm a/b\\) . Eccentricity \\(e=c/a>1\\) . Worked example. \\(\\frac{(x+1)^2}{9}-\\frac{(y-2)^2}{16}=1\\) has center \\((-1,2)\\) , vertices \\((-4,2),(2,2)\\) , and asymptotes \\(y-2=\\pm\\frac43(x+1)\\) . Its foci are \\((-6,2)\\) and \\((4,2)\\) . Guided practice 5. Find the center, vertices, foci,\nand asymptotes of \\(\\frac{(y+3)^2}{4}-\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}=1\\) . Visual investigation 4. Sketch the relevant curve by hand before computing coordinates. Mark\nits center or vertex, the direction of its axes, and two diagnostic\npoints. Explain what the graph communicates that a list of numerical\nvalues does not. Compare a geometric prediction with a\ncomputer-generated graph. Reflection. Identify one feature that changes under\ntranslation and one that remains unchanged."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Recognizing\nconics and avoiding classification errors", "url": "chapter13.html#recognizing-conics-and-avoiding-classification-errors", "text": "When there is no \\(xy\\) term,\ncompare the squared coefficients: equal positive coefficients suggest a\ncircle; same-sign unequal nonzero coefficients suggest an ellipse;\nopposite signs suggest a hyperbola; exactly one squared variable\nsuggests a parabola. This classification requires care: constants may\nproduce a point, intersecting lines, a single line, or no real graph.\nFor \\(Ax^2+Bxy+Cy^2+\\cdots=0\\) , the\ndiscriminant \\(B^2-4AC\\) distinguishes\nthe basic nondegenerate types: negative for ellipse type, zero for\nparabola type, and positive for hyperbola type. It does not alone\nguarantee a nondegenerate real curve. Worked example. \\(9x^2+4y^2=36\\) is an ellipse; \\(9x^2-4y^2=36\\) is a hyperbola; \\(x^2=12y\\) is a parabola. Guided practice 6. Classify \\(x^2-y^2=0\\) , \\(x^2+y^2+4=0\\) , and \\(x^2+4y^2=0\\) and explain why these are\nspecial cases."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Completing the square in\ntwo variables", "url": "chapter13.html#completing-the-square-in-two-variables", "text": "To convert an expanded conic, group \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms, factor out unequal squared\ncoefficients, complete squares inside parentheses, and divide to\nnormalize. Keep every added constant balanced on both sides. Worked example. \\(4x^2+9y^2-16x+18y-11=0\\) becomes \\(4(x^2-4x)+9(y^2+2y)=11\\) , hence \\(4(x-2)^2+9(y+1)^2=36\\) . Thus \\(\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4}=1\\) .\nCenter \\((2,-1)\\) , horizontal major\naxis, \\(a=3\\) , \\(b=2\\) , \\(c=\\sqrt5\\) . Guided practice 7. Put \\(9x^2-4y^2-18x-16y-43=0\\) in standard form,\nchecking each constant."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Parametric equations and\norientation", "url": "chapter13.html#parametric-equations-and-orientation", "text": "A parametric curve is described by \\(x=f(t)\\) and \\(y=g(t)\\) over a specified parameter\ninterval. The same geometric curve can have different parametrizations\nand different directions of travel. Eliminating \\(t\\) often gives a Cartesian equation but\ncan lose restrictions or orientation. For a circle centered at \\((h,k)\\) , \\(x=h+r\\cos t\\) , \\(y=k+r\\sin t\\) for \\(0\\le t<2\\pi\\) traces it once\ncounterclockwise. Worked example. \\(x=2t+1\\) , \\(y=t^2\\) gives \\(t=(x-1)/2\\) , so \\(y=(x-1)^2/4\\) . If \\(-1\\le t\\le2\\) , then \\(-1\\le x\\le5\\) ; the graph is only that part\nof the parabola. Guided practice 8. Eliminate \\(t\\) from \\(x=3\\cos t\\) , \\(y=2\\sin t\\) , \\(0\\le t\\le\\pi\\) ; describe which half of the\nellipse is traced. Visual investigation 5. Sketch the relevant curve by hand before computing coordinates. Mark\nits center or vertex, the direction of its axes, and two diagnostic\npoints. Explain what the graph communicates that a list of numerical\nvalues does not. Compare a geometric prediction with a\ncomputer-generated graph. Reflection. Identify one feature that changes under\ntranslation and one that remains unchanged."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Parametric lines, motion,\nand velocity", "url": "chapter13.html#parametric-lines-motion-and-velocity", "text": "A moving point can be modeled by \\(x(t)=x_0+v_xt\\) , \\(y(t)=y_0+v_yt\\) . The vector \\((v_x,v_y)\\) gives constant velocity in\ncoordinate units per unit time. A nonlinear path may have varying\ndirection and speed. In calculus, velocity is \\(\\bigl(x'(t),y'(t)\\bigr)\\) , and when \\(x'(t)\\ne0\\) , the tangent slope is \\(dy/dx=y'(t)/x'(t)\\) . These\nare previews rather than prerequisites. Worked example. A simulated tracking point has \\(x(t)=1+3t\\) , \\(y(t)=5-2t\\) . At \\(t=4\\) , its position is \\((13,-3)\\) and speed is \\(\\sqrt{3^2+(-2)^2}=\\sqrt{13}\\) distance\nunits per time unit. Guided practice 9. For \\(x(t)=2t\\) , \\(y(t)=t^2-1\\) , tabulate positions at \\(t=-2,-1,0,1,2\\) and indicate direction."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Parametric ellipses and\ncircular motion", "url": "chapter13.html#parametric-ellipses-and-circular-motion", "text": "The equations \\(x=h+a\\cos t\\) , \\(y=k+b\\sin t\\) trace an ellipse, with \\(a,b>0\\) . Substitution into standard form\nconfirms the identity \\(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1\\) . Replacing \\(t\\) by \\(\\omega\nt+\\phi\\) changes angular frequency and starting phase. When \\(a=b\\) , the path is circular. If \\(a\\ne b\\) , the coordinate parameter \\(t\\) is not the polar angle from the center\nin general. Worked example. \\(x(t)=4+5\\cos(2t)\\) , \\(y(t)=-1+2\\sin(2t)\\) traces an ellipse\ncentered at \\((4,-1)\\) with semiaxes 5\nand 2, completing a cycle in \\(\\pi\\) time units. Guided practice 10. Find the period and initial\nposition of \\(x=3\\cos(\\pi t)\\) , \\(y=3\\sin(\\pi t)\\) . Visual investigation 6. Sketch the relevant curve by hand before computing coordinates. Mark\nits center or vertex, the direction of its axes, and two diagnostic\npoints. Explain what the graph communicates that a list of numerical\nvalues does not. Compare a geometric prediction with a\ncomputer-generated graph. Reflection. Identify one feature that changes under\ntranslation and one that remains unchanged."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Projectile-style\npaths as mathematical models", "url": "chapter13.html#projectile-style-paths-as-mathematical-models", "text": "A simple idealized two-dimensional model with constant horizontal\nvelocity and vertical acceleration is \\(x(t)=x_0+v_xt\\) , \\(y(t)=y_0+v_yt-\\tfrac12gt^2\\) , with \\(g>0\\) . Eliminating \\(t\\) when \\(v_x\\ne0\\) produces a downward-opening\nparabola. The model neglects air resistance and should not be applied to\nclinical or engineering decisions without validation. Worked example. For \\(x(t)=4t\\) , \\(y(t)=2+6t-5t^2\\) , substitute \\(t=x/4\\) to obtain \\(y=2+\\frac32x-\\frac5{16}x^2\\) . Restrict to\nphysically relevant \\(t\\ge0\\) and\nbefore reaching the chosen ground level. Guided practice 11. Find the maximum height and the\ntime at which it occurs by completing the square in \\(y(t)\\) ."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Intersection\nproblems and algebraic verification", "url": "chapter13.html#intersection-problems-and-algebraic-verification", "text": "Intersections of conics can be found by substitution or elimination.\nAlgebraic solutions must be checked in the original equations,\nespecially after squaring. A tangent intersection may yield a repeated\nalgebraic root. Numerical graphs can suggest solutions but cannot\ncertify exact values. Worked example. Intersect the circle \\(x^2+y^2=25\\) with the line \\(y=3\\) . Then \\(x^2=16\\) , giving \\((-4,3)\\) and \\((4,3)\\) . Each satisfies both original\nequations. Guided practice 12. Find the intersection of \\(y=x^2\\) and \\(y=2x+3\\) and interpret the solutions\ngeometrically."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Applied modeling:\nelliptical cross sections", "url": "chapter13.html#applied-modeling-elliptical-cross-sections", "text": "Suppose an idealized imaging cross section is approximated by \\(x^2/16+y^2/9=1\\) with lengths in\ncentimeters. The full width is 8 cm and full height is 6 cm. Its\nenclosed area is \\(\\pi\nab=12\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2\\) . This is a geometric approximation to a\ncross section, not a diagnostic measurement. Units must be squared when\nreporting area. Worked example. If a uniform scaling factor \\(s=1.1\\) is applied to both axes, the area\nbecomes \\(s^2(12\\pi)=14.52\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2\\) . A 10%\nincrease in linear dimensions gives a 21% increase in area. Guided practice 13. Compute the area if the\nhorizontal semiaxis increases by 10% but the vertical semiaxis is\nunchanged."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Model\nselection, residuals, and measurement uncertainty", "url": "chapter13.html#model-selection-residuals-and-measurement-uncertainty", "text": "Choosing a conic from measured coordinates is a modeling decision.\nScatterplots, units, symmetry, and scientific context matter. A fitted\ncurve may be descriptive without being mechanistic. Residuals are\nobserved minus predicted values; for parametric curves, vertical\nresiduals may be inappropriate when multiple \\(y\\) values correspond to one \\(x\\) . Measurement error can alter fitted\ncenters and axes. Worked example. If an observed ellipse has estimated\nsemiaxes \\(a=4.0\\pm0.1\\) and \\(b=3.0\\pm0.1\\) cm, compare the areas \\(\\pi ab\\) at \\((a,b)=(3.9,2.9)\\) and \\((4.1,3.1)\\) to illustrate uncertainty\nbounds. Guided practice 14. Explain why a circle fitted to\nelongated observations may systematically underfit near the major-axis\nends."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Connections to\ncalculus and future mathematics", "url": "chapter13.html#connections-to-calculus-and-future-mathematics", "text": "Conics recur as level curves in multivariable calculus, while\nparametric curves allow position, velocity, tangent vectors, and arc\nlength to be described using a single parameter. For \\(x=a\\cos t\\) and \\(y=b\\sin t\\) , derivatives give \\(x'=-a\\sin t\\) and \\(y'=b\\cos t\\) , so the tangent slope is \\(-(b/a)\\cot t\\) where \\(\\sin t\\ne0\\) . At \\(t=0\\) , the tangent is vertical,\nillustrating why the quotient \\(y'/x'\\) requires care. Guided practice 15. Use nearby parameter values to\nestimate the tangent direction at \\(t=\\pi/2\\) for the ellipse \\(x=4\\cos t\\) , \\(y=2\\sin t\\) ."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Visual gallery: comparing\nthe conics", "url": "chapter13.html#visual-gallery-comparing-the-conics", "text": "image These curves are drawn together for comparison, not as one fitted\nmodel. A hyperbola has disconnected branches and is explored in\nLaboratory A."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: graphing and classifying conics", "url": "chapter13.html#computational-laboratory-a-graphing-and-classifying-conics", "text": "Prediction. Which plotted curves are bounded? Which\nhave asymptotes? Explain before executing the code."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter13.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code t <- seq ( 0 , 2 * pi, length.out= 400 ) par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 2 )) plot ( 3 * cos (t), 2 * sin (t), type= \"l\" , asp= 1 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"y\" , main= \"Ellipse\" ) u <- seq ( - 1.4 , 1.4 , length.out= 300 ) plot ( c ( 2 * cosh (u), - 2 * cosh (u)), c ( 3 * sinh (u), 3 * sinh (u)), type= \"n\" , asp= 1 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"y\" , main= \"Hyperbola\" ) lines ( 2 * cosh (u), 3 * sinh (u), col= \"blue\" ); lines ( - 2 * cosh (u), 3 * sinh (u), col= \"red\" ) abline ( a= 0 , b= 1.5 , lty= 2 ); abline ( a= 0 , b= - 1.5 , lty= 2 ) par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 1 ))"}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter13.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt t = np.linspace( 0 , 2 * np.pi, 400 ) ; u = np.linspace( - 1.4 , 1.4 , 300 ) fig,ax = plt.subplots( 1 , 2 ,figsize = ( 10 , 4 )) ax[ 0 ].plot( 3 * np.cos(t), 2 * np.sin(t)) ; ax[ 0 ].set_title( \"Ellipse\" ) ax[ 1 ].plot( 2 * np.cosh(u), 3 * np.sinh(u)) ; ax[ 1 ].plot( - 2 * np.cosh(u), 3 * np.sinh(u)) x = np.linspace( - 5 , 5 , 200 ) ; ax[ 1 ].plot(x, 1.5 * x, \"--\" ) ; ax[ 1 ].plot(x, - 1.5 * x, \"--\" ) ax[ 1 ].set_title( \"Hyperbola\" ) for a in ax:a.set_aspect( \"equal\" ,adjustable = \"box\" ) ; a.grid( True ) ; a.set_xlabel( \"x\" ) ; a.set_ylabel( \"y\" ) fig.tight_layout() ; fig.savefig( \"labA_python.png\" ,dpi = 180 ) ; plt.close(fig) Interpretation. Verify ellipse intercepts and\nhyperbola asymptote slopes by hand. Explain why equal axis scales are\nessential."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: parametric curves and orientation", "url": "chapter13.html#computational-laboratory-b-parametric-curves-and-orientation", "text": "Prediction. Predict the starting point and direction\nfor \\(x=3\\cos t\\) , \\(y=2\\sin t\\) when \\(0\\le t\\le2\\pi\\) ."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter13.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code t <- seq ( 0 , 2 * pi, length.out= 400 ) x <- 3 * cos (t); y <- 2 * sin (t) plot (x,y, type= \"l\" , asp= 1 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"y\" , main= \"Parameterized ellipse\" ) points (x[ 1 ],y[ 1 ], pch= 19 , col= \"red\" ) idx <- c ( 1 , 101 , 201 , 301 ) arrows (x[idx],y[idx],x[idx + 4 ],y[idx + 4 ], length= 0.12 , col= \"blue\" ) cat ( \"Start:\" ,x[ 1 ],y[ 1 ], \" \\n \" )"}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter13.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt t = np.linspace( 0 , 2 * np.pi, 400 ) ; x = 3 * np.cos(t) ; y = 2 * np.sin(t) fig,ax = plt.subplots() ; ax.plot(x,y) ; ax.scatter([x[ 0 ]],[y[ 0 ]],label = \"start\" ) for k in [ 0 , 100 , 200 , 300 ]:ax.annotate( \"\" ,xy = (x[k + 5 ],y[k + 5 ]),xytext = (x[k],y[k]),arrowprops = { \"arrowstyle\" : \"->\" }) ax.set_aspect( \"equal\" ) ; ax.grid( True ) ; ax.legend() ; ax.set_xlabel( \"x\" ) ; ax.set_ylabel( \"y\" ) fig.savefig( \"labB_python.png\" ,dpi = 180 ) ; plt.close(fig) Interpretation. Compare the full curve with \\(0\\le t\\le\\pi\\) . Describe the effect of\nreversing the parameter."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: fitting an ellipse and examining residuals", "url": "chapter13.html#computational-laboratory-c-fitting-an-ellipse-and-examining-residuals", "text": "Prediction. Noisy observations near an ellipse\nshould not lie exactly on the model. What should a residual plot\nreveal?"}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter13.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code set.seed ( 13 ) t <- seq ( 0 , 2 * pi, length.out= 80 ) a <- 4 ; b <- 2 r <- 1 + rnorm ( length (t), 0 , 0.04 ) x <- a * r * cos (t); y <- b * r * sin (t) radial_residual <- sqrt ((x / a) ^ 2 + (y / b) ^ 2 ) - 1 par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 2 )) plot (x,y, asp= 1 , pch= 19 , cex= 0.5 , main= \"Simulated measurements\" ) lines (a * cos ( seq ( 0 , 2 * pi, length.out= 300 )),b * sin ( seq ( 0 , 2 * pi, length.out= 300 )), col= \"blue\" ) plot (t,radial_residual, pch= 19 , cex= 0.5 , main= \"Normalized radial residuals\" ) abline ( h= 0 , lty= 2 ); par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 1 )) print ( summary (radial_residual))"}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter13.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt rng = np.random.default_rng( 13 ) ; t = np.linspace( 0 , 2 * np.pi, 80 ) ; a,b = 4 , 2 r = 1 + rng.normal( 0 , 0.04 , len (t)) ; x = a * r * np.cos(t) ; y = b * r * np.sin(t) residual = np.sqrt((x / a) ** 2 + (y / b) ** 2 ) - 1 fig,ax = plt.subplots( 1 , 2 ,figsize = ( 10 , 4 )) ; ax[ 0 ].scatter(x,y,s = 12 ) u = np.linspace( 0 , 2 * np.pi, 300 ) ; ax[ 0 ].plot(a * np.cos(u),b * np.sin(u)) ; ax[ 0 ].set_aspect( \"equal\" ) ax[ 1 ].scatter(t,residual,s = 12 ) ; ax[ 1 ].axhline( 0 ,linestyle = \"--\" ) ax[ 0 ].set_title( \"Simulated observations\" ) ; ax[ 1 ].set_title( \"Normalized radial residuals\" ) fig.tight_layout() ; fig.savefig( \"labC_python.png\" ,dpi = 180 ) ; plt.close(fig) print ( \"Mean residual:\" ,np.mean(residual), \"SD:\" ,np.std(residual)) Interpretation. The data are simulated. Compare\nradial residuals and explain why they are not the same as vertical\nresiduals."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Extended guided\nworkshops with worked solutions", "url": "chapter13.html#extended-guided-workshops-with-worked-solutions", "text": ""}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Workshop 1: geometric\nimaging approximation", "url": "chapter13.html#workshop-1-geometric-imaging-approximation", "text": "A simulated cross section is modeled by \\((x-1)^2/25+(y+2)^2/9=1\\) , with coordinates\nin centimeters. Find the center, vertices, foci, and enclosed area. Solution. Center \\((1,-2)\\) ; \\(a=5\\) , \\(b=3\\) , \\(c=\\sqrt{25-9}=4\\) . Vertices \\((-4,-2),(6,-2)\\) , foci \\((-3,-2),(5,-2)\\) , area \\(15\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2\\) . The model is\ngeometric only; clinical interpretation requires validated\nmeasurements."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Workshop 2: parabolic\nreflector geometry", "url": "chapter13.html#workshop-2-parabolic-reflector-geometry", "text": "A simplified reflector cross section has vertex at the origin and\nfocus at \\((0,2)\\) centimeters. Derive\nits equation and determine the width at height \\(y=8\\) . Solution. Here \\(p=2\\) , so \\(x^2=8y\\) . At \\(y=8\\) , \\(x=\\pm8\\) , hence the width is 16 cm. Verify\nthat each endpoint is 10 cm from the focus and 10 cm from the directrix \\(y=-2\\) ."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Workshop 3: a hyperbolic\nlocation model", "url": "chapter13.html#workshop-3-a-hyperbolic-location-model", "text": "The curve \\(x^2/9-y^2/16=1\\) describes an idealized difference-of-distances locus. Identify vertices,\nfoci, and asymptotes. Solution. \\(a=3\\) , \\(b=4\\) , \\(c=5\\) . Vertices \\((\\pm3,0)\\) , foci \\((\\pm5,0)\\) , asymptotes \\(y=\\pm4x/3\\) . The difference of distances to\nthe foci is \\(2a=6\\) on either\nbranch."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Workshop 4: moving sensor on\nan ellipse", "url": "chapter13.html#workshop-4-moving-sensor-on-an-ellipse", "text": "A simulated point moves as \\(x(t)=4\\cos(\\pi\nt)\\) , \\(y(t)=2\\sin(\\pi t)\\) ,\nwhere \\(t\\) is in seconds. Determine\nits Cartesian path, period, initial position, and location at \\(t=1/2\\) . Solution. \\(x^2/16+y^2/4=1\\) . Period \\(2\\) seconds; initial position \\((4,0)\\) ; at \\(t=1/2\\) , position \\((0,2)\\) . The parameterization is\ncounterclockwise."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Workshop 5: intersection\nand model choice", "url": "chapter13.html#workshop-5-intersection-and-model-choice", "text": "A circle \\(x^2+y^2=25\\) intersects a\nparabola \\(y=x^2-5\\) . Find all\nintersection points. Solution. Substitute \\(y=x^2-5\\) : \\(x^2+(x^2-5)^2=25\\) . Let \\(u=x^2\\) to obtain \\(u^2-9u=0\\) , so \\(u=0\\) or \\(9\\) . Thus \\((0,-5)\\) and \\((\\pm3,4)\\) . Verify all three in both\nequations."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Problem-solving\nclinic: a systematic method", "url": "chapter13.html#problem-solving-clinic-a-systematic-method", "text": "Identify whether the equation describes a locus, a function, or a\nparameterized path. Normalize units and complete squares carefully. Locate defining features before graphing. Check algebraic solutions in the original equation. State parameter or domain restrictions. Use software to test and visualize, not to replace\nproof. Interpret the model only within its scientific\nassumptions."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Guided\npractice studio: building a conic from geometric information", "url": "chapter13.html#guided-practice-studio-building-a-conic-from-geometric-information", "text": ""}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Activity A:\ntranslating a circle", "url": "chapter13.html#activity-a-translating-a-circle", "text": "Start with the unit circle \\(x^2+y^2=1\\) . Translate every point three\nunits right and two units down. Write the new equation and explain why\nthe new coordinates must appear as \\(x-3\\) and \\(y+2\\) . Next scale the radius by four,\nkeeping the center fixed. Give four points on the resulting circle and\ncheck each algebraically. Solution and reasoning. A translated point has\ncoordinates \\((x+3,y-2)\\) when \\((x,y)\\) is on the original circle, so the\nnew locus is \\((X-3)^2+(Y+2)^2=1\\) .\nIncreasing the radius gives \\((X-3)^2+(Y+2)^2=16\\) . The four axis points\nare \\((7,-2)\\) , \\((-1,-2)\\) , \\((3,2)\\) , and \\((3,-6)\\) . Substituting any of them gives\n16. The center changes under translation but not under scaling of the\nradius."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Activity B: choosing\nan ellipse from dimensions", "url": "chapter13.html#activity-b-choosing-an-ellipse-from-dimensions", "text": "A diagram shows an ellipse centered at \\((2,-1)\\) with full horizontal axis length\n12 and full vertical axis length 8. Construct its equation, vertices,\nfoci, and area. Explain why using 12 and 8 directly in the denominators\nwould be incorrect. Solution and reasoning. The semiaxes are \\(a=6\\) and \\(b=4\\) , so \\((x-2)^2/36+(y+1)^2/16=1\\) . The vertices are \\((-4,-1)\\) and \\((8,-1)\\) ; \\(c=\\sqrt{36-16}=2\\sqrt5\\) , giving foci \\((2\\pm2\\sqrt5,-1)\\) . Area is \\(24\\pi\\) square units. The denominators are\nsquares of semiaxis lengths, not squares of full widths."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Activity\nC: identifying a parabola from focus and directrix", "url": "chapter13.html#activity-c-identifying-a-parabola-from-focus-and-directrix", "text": "A point is equidistant from focus \\((3,4)\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) . Find the parabola’s vertex,\nparameter \\(p\\) , and equation. Check\nthe point \\((7,6)\\) and decide whether\nit lies on the curve. Solution and reasoning. The vertex lies halfway\nbetween focus and directrix: \\((3,2)\\) .\nThus \\(p=2\\) and \\((x-3)^2=8(y-2)\\) . At \\((7,6)\\) the left side is 16 while the right\nside is 32, so it does not lie on the parabola. The focus-directrix\ndefinition is a useful independent check."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Activity D: comparing\ntwo parameterizations", "url": "chapter13.html#activity-d-comparing-two-parameterizations", "text": "Compare \\(x=2\\cos t\\) , \\(y=2\\sin t\\) and \\(x=2\\cos(2t)\\) , \\(y=2\\sin(2t)\\) on \\(0\\le t\\le2\\pi\\) . Do they trace the same\ngeometric set? Do they travel in the same way? Solution and reasoning. Both trace \\(x^2+y^2=4\\) . The first completes one\ncounterclockwise revolution and the second completes two. Eliminating\nthe parameter gives the same Cartesian equation but loses information\nabout how often the curve is traversed."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Visual\nreasoning workshop: sketch before plotting", "url": "chapter13.html#visual-reasoning-workshop-sketch-before-plotting", "text": "Investigation 7: the role of asymptotes. Draw \\(x^2/4-y^2/9=1\\) by first sketching the\nrectangle with corners \\((\\pm2,\\pm3)\\) .\nIts diagonals extend along \\(y=\\pm3x/2\\) . Mark the vertices \\((\\pm2,0)\\) and sketch the two branches\napproaching the diagonals. Check a point with \\(x=4\\) using the equation. Explain why the\nhyperbola does not cross its asymptotes. Investigation 8: parameter intervals. Plot the five\npoints generated by \\(x=3\\cos t\\) , \\(y=2\\sin t\\) at \\(t=0,\\pi/2,\\pi,3\\pi/2,2\\pi\\) . Connect them\nsmoothly, then repeat for \\(0\\le\nt\\le\\pi\\) . Label arrows and distinguish a full ellipse from its\nupper half. Investigation 9: model uncertainty. Sketch ellipses\nwith semiaxes \\((4,3)\\) , \\((4.1,3.1)\\) , and \\((3.9,2.9)\\) on common axes. Compute their\nareas and explain why uncertainty in both dimensions can have a larger\nimpact on area than uncertainty in either dimension alone. Investigation 10: tangents from secants. For \\(x=4\\cos t\\) , \\(y=2\\sin t\\) , use \\(t=\\pi/2\\pm0.1\\) to form a secant line near\nthe top of the ellipse. Repeat with \\(0.01\\) and explain why the tangent is\nhorizontal at the top. Distinguish this from the vertical tangent at \\((4,0)\\) ."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Independent practice\nwith worked checks", "url": "chapter13.html#independent-practice-with-worked-checks", "text": "A circle has diameter endpoints \\((-3,1)\\) and \\((5,7)\\) . Find its center, radius, and\nequation. Check: midpoint \\((1,4)\\) , radius \\(5\\) , so \\((x-1)^2+(y-4)^2=25\\) . A parabola has vertex \\((-2,3)\\) and directrix \\(x=1\\) . Find its focus\nand equation. Check: \\(p=-3\\) , focus \\((-5,3)\\) , and \\((y-3)^2=-12(x+2)\\) . An ellipse has foci \\((\\pm3,0)\\) and vertices \\((\\pm5,0)\\) . Find its\nequation. Check: \\(a=5\\) , \\(c=3\\) , \\(b^2=16\\) , giving \\(x^2/25+y^2/16=1\\) . A hyperbola has vertices \\((0,\\pm2)\\) and foci \\((0,\\pm\\sqrt{13})\\) . Find its equation and\nasymptotes. Check: \\(a^2=4\\) , \\(b^2=9\\) , so \\(y^2/4-x^2/9=1\\) and \\(y=\\pm(2/3)x\\) . A particle follows \\(x=2+4\\cos\nt\\) , \\(y=1+3\\sin t\\) . Determine\nits location at \\(t=\\pi\\) and its\ngeometric path. Check: \\((-2,1)\\) and \\((x-2)^2/16+(y-1)^2/9=1\\) ."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Chapter review exercises", "url": "chapter13.html#chapter-review-exercises", "text": "Find the center and radius of \\((x-2)^2+(y+3)^2=49\\) . Complete the square in \\(x^2+y^2+4x-6y-12=0\\) . Explain why \\(x^2+y^2=-1\\) has\nno real points. Find the focus and directrix of \\(x^2=12y\\) . Find the focus of \\((y-2)^2=-16(x+1)\\) . Derive \\(x^2=4py\\) from the\nfocus-directrix definition. Identify the vertices and foci of \\(x^2/36+y^2/20=1\\) . Find the eccentricity of \\(x^2/25+y^2/9=1\\) . Graph \\((x-1)^2/4+(y+2)^2/16=1\\) . Explain the distinction between semiaxis and full-axis\nlength. Find the asymptotes of \\(x^2/4-y^2/9=1\\) . Find the foci of \\(y^2/16-x^2/9=1\\) . Explain why a hyperbola has two branches. Classify \\(4x^2+9y^2=36\\) . Classify \\(x^2-y^2=0\\) and\ndescribe the degeneracy. Explain the meaning and limitation of \\(B^2-4AC\\) . Complete squares in \\(9x^2+4y^2-18x+16y-11=0\\) . Convert \\(x=2+3\\cos t\\) , \\(y=-1+5\\sin t\\) to Cartesian form. Eliminate \\(t\\) from \\(x=1+t\\) , \\(y=2t^2\\) , \\(0\\le\nt\\le2\\) . Find initial point and period of \\(x=4\\cos(2t)\\) , \\(y=4\\sin(2t)\\) . Describe the orientation of \\(x=\\cos(-t)\\) , \\(y=\\sin(-t)\\) . Find the position at \\(t=3\\) for \\(x=1+2t\\) , \\(y=5-t\\) . Find the speed for \\(x=3t\\) , \\(y=4t\\) . Eliminate \\(t\\) from \\(x=5t\\) , \\(y=3t-t^2\\) . Find intersections of \\(x^2+y^2=10\\) and \\(y=1\\) . Find intersections of \\(y=x^2\\) and \\(y=x+2\\) . Calculate the area of an ellipse with semiaxes 7 and 3\ncm. Explain why unequal plotting scales distort conics. Distinguish radial residuals from vertical residuals. Explain how parametric equations prepare students for\nderivatives."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter13.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "For \\(x(t)=4\\cos t\\) and \\(y(t)=2\\sin t\\) , calculate positions at \\(t=\\pi/2-h\\) , \\(\\pi/2\\) , and \\(\\pi/2+h\\) for small positive \\(h\\) . Estimate the tangent direction using\nsecant vectors. Then compare with \\(x'(t)=-4\\sin t\\) and \\(y'(t)=2\\cos t\\) . Explain why \\(dy/dx\\) is undefined when \\(x'(t)=0\\) , and distinguish a vertical\ntangent from a nonexistent curve."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Looking ahead", "url": "chapter13.html#looking-ahead", "text": "Chapter 14 develops vectors and polar coordinates. Parametric curves\nprovide a bridge from geometric loci to vector-valued functions and\nmotion in calculus."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Extended\napplication: Elliptical cross section", "url": "chapter13.html#extended-application-elliptical-cross-section", "text": "An idealized cross section has horizontal semiaxis 5 cm and vertical\nsemiaxis 3 cm. Study its upper boundary. The quantities in this\ninvestigation are illustrative rather than measured observations."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter13.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[y=3\\sqrt{1-x^2/25}.\\] Before calculating,\nidentify the input and output, their units, and the interval on which\nthe model is being studied. Distinguish the mathematical domain from the\npractical domain. The numerical study uses inputs from \\(-5\\) to \\(5\\) . Example 1: Analytical investigation. Derive the\nupper boundary from the standard ellipse equation. Find the height at \\(x=3\\) and the area of the full\nellipse. Worked solution. \\(x^2/25+y^2/9=1\\) gives the stated branch;\nheight 2.4 cm and area \\(15\\pi\\) square\ncm."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter13.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Horizontal position (cm) Vertical position (cm) -5 0 -2.5 2.598 0 3 2.5 2.598 5 0 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter13.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Elliptical cross section # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( - 5 , 5 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 3 * sqrt ( pmax ( 0 , 1 - x ^ 2 / 25 )) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Horizontal position (cm)\" , ylab = \"Vertical position (cm)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter13.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Elliptical cross section import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( - 5 , 5 , 301 ) y = 3 * np.sqrt(np.maximum( 0 , 1 - x ** 2 / 25 )) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Horizontal position (cm)\" , ylabel = \"Vertical position (cm)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter13.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Elliptical cross section", "url": "chapter13.html#chapter-project-investigating-elliptical-cross-section", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter13.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "url": "chapter14.html#vectors-polar-coordinates-and-geometric-modeling", "text": ""}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Opening\ncontext: directions matter", "url": "chapter14.html#opening-context-directions-matter", "text": "A displacement of five meters is not a complete description of motion\nunless its direction is also specified. Likewise, the location of a\nsensor relative to a rotating device is often more naturally described\nby a distance and angle than by two rectangular coordinates. Vectors and\npolar coordinates provide complementary languages for representing\ngeometric quantities. This chapter builds on coordinate geometry\n(Chapter 3), functions (Chapters 4–5), trigonometry (Chapters 8–11), and\nparametric curves (Chapter 13). Its central goal is to connect exact\nmathematical reasoning with graphical and numerical exploration."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter14.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "By the end of the chapter, students should be able to (1) represent\nand interpret vectors in component and magnitude–direction forms; (2)\ncompute vector sums, differences, scalar multiples, magnitudes, and unit\nvectors; (3) use dot products to calculate angles and projections; (4)\nsolve elementary force and displacement models; (5) convert between\nCartesian and polar coordinates while attending to quadrants and\nnonuniqueness; (6) sketch basic polar curves and identify their\nsymmetries; (7) use polar coordinates to describe motion; and (8)\nimplement and interpret corresponding R and Python investigations."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "How to use the\ncomputational laboratories", "url": "chapter14.html#how-to-use-the-computational-laboratories", "text": "Each laboratory begins with a mathematical prediction, followed by R code and then Python code , in\nseparate full-width listings. Students should complete the analytical\nwork first, execute one language at a time, and compare results. All\nangles passed to trigonometric functions in the code are measured in\nradians."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Directed segments and\nvector notation", "url": "chapter14.html#directed-segments-and-vector-notation", "text": "A scalar has magnitude but no direction; a vector has both magnitude and direction. Write \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle a,b\\rangle\\) for the\ndisplacement \\(a\\) units horizontally\nand \\(b\\) units vertically. The vector\nfrom \\(P(x_1,y_1)\\) to \\(Q(x_2,y_2)\\) is \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle\nx_2-x_1,y_2-y_1\\rangle\\) . Two arrows at different locations\nrepresent the same free vector when their components agree. This is\ndistinct from identifying a vector with a particular point. Worked example. From \\(P(-2,3)\\) to \\(Q(4,-1)\\) , the displacement is \\(\\langle6,-4\\rangle\\) . Starting instead at \\((1,2)\\) , the same displacement ends\nat \\((7,-2)\\) . Guided practice. Find \\(\\overrightarrow{AB}\\) for \\(A(3,-5)\\) and \\(B(-1,2)\\) ; then draw a translated copy\nstarting at the origin."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Vector\naddition, subtraction, and scalar multiplication", "url": "chapter14.html#vector-addition-subtraction-and-scalar-multiplication", "text": "For \\(\\mathbf u=\\langle\nu_1,u_2\\rangle\\) and \\(\\mathbf\nv=\\langle v_1,v_2\\rangle\\) , define \\(\\mathbf u+\\mathbf v=\\langle\nu_1+v_1,u_2+v_2\\rangle\\) and \\(c\\mathbf\nu=\\langle cu_1,cu_2\\rangle\\) . Subtraction is addition of the\nopposite vector. The head-to-tail and parallelogram constructions\nexplain why componentwise addition describes consecutive displacements.\nThe vector \\(\\mathbf0=\\langle0,0\\rangle\\) has zero\nmagnitude and no defined direction. Worked example. If \\(\\mathbf u=\\langle3,-2\\rangle\\) and \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle-1,5\\rangle\\) , then \\(2\\mathbf u-\\mathbf\nv=\\langle7,-9\\rangle\\) . Guided practice. Calculate \\(3\\mathbf u+2\\mathbf v\\) and sketch the\ncorresponding resultant. Explain why multiplying by a negative scalar\nreverses direction. Visual investigation 1. Visual investigation: tip-to-tail addition. Draw \\(\\mathbf u=\\langle3,1\\rangle\\) and \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle-1,2\\rangle\\) on\nsquared paper. First place both tails at the origin; then translate \\(\\mathbf v\\) to start at the tip of \\(\\mathbf u\\) . Draw the resultant from\nthe origin. Repeat the construction in reverse order. Explain\ngeometrically why \\(\\mathbf u+\\mathbf\nv=\\mathbf v+\\mathbf u\\) . Reflection. Record a numerical prediction, a\ngeometric explanation, and a method for checking your conclusion."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Magnitude, distance, and\nunit vectors", "url": "chapter14.html#magnitude-distance-and-unit-vectors", "text": "The magnitude of \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle\na,b\\rangle\\) follows from the Pythagorean theorem: \\(\\|\\mathbf v\\|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\\) . For \\(\\mathbf v\\ne\\mathbf0\\) , the vector \\(\\widehat{\\mathbf v}=\\mathbf v/\\|\\mathbf\nv\\|\\) has length one and the same direction. The distance formula\nis therefore the magnitude of a displacement vector. Distinguish a unit\nvector (dimensionless direction) from a physical displacement carrying\nunits. Worked example. For \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle-6,8\\rangle\\) , \\(\\|\\mathbf v\\|=10\\) and \\(\\widehat{\\mathbf\nv}=\\langle-3/5,4/5\\rangle\\) . Verify \\(9/25+16/25=1\\) . Guided practice. Normalize \\(\\langle5,12\\rangle\\) and explain why \\(\\mathbf0\\) cannot be normalized."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Direction angles\nand trigonometric components", "url": "chapter14.html#direction-angles-and-trigonometric-components", "text": "If a nonzero vector has magnitude \\(r\\) and direction angle \\(\\theta\\) measured counterclockwise from the\npositive \\(x\\) axis, then \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle\nr\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta\\rangle\\) . Conversely \\(r=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\\) and \\(\\theta\\) must be chosen in the correct\nquadrant. The expression \\(\\arctan(b/a)\\) alone can lose quadrant\ninformation; numerical software often provides an atan2 function. Worked example. A 20-newton force acting at \\(150^\\circ\\) has components \\(\\langle20\\cos150^\\circ,20\\sin150^\\circ\\rangle=\\langle-10\\sqrt3,10\\rangle\\) newtons. Guided practice. Express a 12-meter displacement at \\(240^\\circ\\) in exact component\nform. Visual investigation 2. Visual investigation: rotating a fixed-length\nvector. Sketch arrows of length 4 at angles \\(0,\\pi/6,\\pi/2,\\pi,3\\pi/2\\) . Complete a\ntable of their horizontal and vertical components. Explain why the\nendpoints form a circle and identify the maximum and minimum possible \\(x\\) components. Reflection. Record a numerical prediction, a\ngeometric explanation, and a method for checking your conclusion."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "The dot product\nand the angle between vectors", "url": "chapter14.html#the-dot-product-and-the-angle-between-vectors", "text": "The dot product is \\(\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf\nv=u_1v_1+u_2v_2\\) . Geometry gives the equivalent identity \\(\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v=\\|\\mathbf u\\|\\|\\mathbf\nv\\|\\cos\\theta\\) for nonzero vectors, where \\(0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi\\) . One derivation expands \\(\\|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf v\\|^2\\) by\ncomponents and compares with the law of cosines. Thus \\(\\cos\\theta=(\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v)/(\\|\\mathbf\nu\\|\\|\\mathbf v\\|)\\) ; the denominator must be nonzero. Worked example. \\(\\langle1,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle2,-1\\rangle=0\\) .\nSince both are nonzero, the vectors are perpendicular. Guided practice. Compute the angle between \\(\\langle1,0\\rangle\\) and \\(\\langle1,1\\rangle\\) exactly."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Orthogonality,\nprojection, and component along a direction", "url": "chapter14.html#orthogonality-projection-and-component-along-a-direction", "text": "Two nonzero vectors are perpendicular exactly when their dot product\nis zero. The scalar component of \\(\\mathbf\nu\\) along \\(\\mathbf v\\) is \\((\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v)/\\|\\mathbf v\\|\\) .\nThe vector projection is \\(\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf v}\\mathbf u=((\\mathbf\nu\\cdot\\mathbf v)/\\|\\mathbf v\\|^2)\\mathbf v\\) , provided \\(\\mathbf v\\ne\\mathbf0\\) . The remainder \\(\\mathbf u-\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf v}\\mathbf\nu\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\mathbf\nv\\) . Worked example. Project \\(\\mathbf u=\\langle3,4\\rangle\\) onto \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle1,0\\rangle\\) . The result\nis \\(\\langle3,0\\rangle\\) , leaving \\(\\langle0,4\\rangle\\) perpendicular to the\nhorizontal axis. Guided practice. Project \\(\\langle4,2\\rangle\\) onto \\(\\langle1,1\\rangle\\) and verify the\northogonality of the residual. Visual investigation 3. Visual investigation: projection geometry. Draw \\(\\mathbf u=\\langle4,3\\rangle\\) and \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle2,1\\rangle\\) from\nthe origin. Drop a perpendicular from the tip of \\(\\mathbf u\\) to the line spanned by \\(\\mathbf v\\) . Compute the projected vector\nand residual and compare the diagram with the dot-product formulas. Reflection. Record a numerical prediction, a\ngeometric explanation, and a method for checking your conclusion."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Applied\nvectors: resultant forces and navigation", "url": "chapter14.html#applied-vectors-resultant-forces-and-navigation", "text": "A simplified planar force balance uses vector addition. If a force of\nmagnitude \\(F_1\\) acts at angle \\(\\theta_1\\) and another of magnitude \\(F_2\\) acts at \\(\\theta_2\\) , their resultant is \\(\\langle\nF_1\\cos\\theta_1+F_2\\cos\\theta_2,F_1\\sin\\theta_1+F_2\\sin\\theta_2\\rangle\\) .\nAn equilibrium force is the negative of this resultant. A force model\nmust identify its reference axes and units. Navigation uses the same\nmathematics, although bearings are commonly measured clockwise from\nnorth rather than counterclockwise from east. Worked example. Forces \\(\\langle8,0\\rangle\\) N and \\(\\langle0,6\\rangle\\) N produce a resultant\nof magnitude \\(10\\) N, at angle \\(\\arctan(6/8)\\) above the positive\nhorizontal axis. Guided practice. Two displacements are 3 km east and\n4 km north. Find the resultant and a return displacement."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Polar\ncoordinates and nonunique representations", "url": "chapter14.html#polar-coordinates-and-nonunique-representations", "text": "A polar coordinate pair \\((r,\\theta)\\) represents a point a signed\nradial distance \\(r\\) along the\ndirection \\(\\theta\\) . For \\(r\\ge0\\) , this is the usual\ndistance-and-angle description. Polar coordinates are not unique: \\((r,\\theta)\\) and \\((r,\\theta+2\\pi k)\\) describe the same\npoint; \\((r,\\theta)\\) and \\((-r,\\theta+\\pi)\\) also describe the same\npoint. At the origin the angle is arbitrary. These subtleties matter\nwhen solving equations and interpreting plots. Worked example. \\((3,\\pi/6)\\) and \\((-3,7\\pi/6)\\) represent the same point.\nBoth convert to \\((3\\sqrt3/2,3/2)\\) . Guided practice. Give three distinct polar\ndescriptions of the Cartesian point \\((-2,0)\\) . Visual investigation 4. Visual investigation: many names for one point. Plot \\((2,\\pi/3)\\) , \\((2,7\\pi/3)\\) , and \\((-2,4\\pi/3)\\) . Show that they coincide.\nThen mark \\((0,\\theta)\\) for three\ndistinct angles and explain the special role of the origin. Reflection. Record a numerical prediction, a\ngeometric explanation, and a method for checking your conclusion."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Converting\nbetween Cartesian and polar coordinates", "url": "chapter14.html#converting-between-cartesian-and-polar-coordinates", "text": "The conversion formulas are \\(x=r\\cos\\theta\\) , \\(y=r\\sin\\theta\\) , and \\(r^2=x^2+y^2\\) . For the standard choice \\(r\\ge0\\) , set \\(r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\) and choose \\(\\theta\\) from the signs of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) . When \\(x\\ne0\\) , \\(\\tan\\theta=y/x\\) , but this ratio does not\nuniquely identify the quadrant. Worked example. Cartesian \\((-3,3)\\) gives \\(r=3\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\theta=3\\pi/4\\) in \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Conversely \\((4,5\\pi/6)\\) gives \\((-2\\sqrt3,2)\\) . Guided practice. Convert \\((0,-5)\\) to polar form with \\(0\\le\\theta<2\\pi\\) ; convert \\((6,-\\pi/3)\\) to Cartesian form."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Polar equations and\nelementary graphs", "url": "chapter14.html#polar-equations-and-elementary-graphs", "text": "In polar coordinates, \\(r=c\\) with \\(c>0\\) describes a circle centered\nat the origin; \\(\\theta=\\alpha\\) describes a line through the origin when negative radii are allowed.\nEquations \\(r=2a\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(r=2a\\sin\\theta\\) convert to \\((x-a)^2+y^2=a^2\\) and \\(x^2+(y-a)^2=a^2\\) . Graphs of \\(r=f(\\theta)\\) should be built from an angle\ntable, sign analysis, and symmetry, rather than treating \\(r\\) as a Cartesian vertical coordinate. Worked example. For \\(r=4\\cos\\theta\\) , multiply by \\(r\\) : \\(r^2=4r\\cos\\theta\\) , hence \\(x^2+y^2=4x\\) and \\((x-2)^2+y^2=4\\) . Guided practice. Convert \\(r=6\\sin\\theta\\) to Cartesian form and\nidentify its center and radius. Visual investigation 5. Visual investigation: Cartesian and polar\ndescriptions. Draw the circle \\((x-2)^2+y^2=4\\) and superimpose rays from\nthe origin at angles \\(0,\\pi/6,\\pi/3,\\pi/2\\) . Use the polar\nequation \\(r=4\\cos\\theta\\) to locate\nintersections and explain what happens when \\(\\cos\\theta<0\\) . Reflection. Record a numerical prediction, a\ngeometric explanation, and a method for checking your conclusion."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Rose curves, limacons, and\nspirals", "url": "chapter14.html#rose-curves-limacons-and-spirals", "text": "The family \\(r=a\\cos(n\\theta)\\) or \\(r=a\\sin(n\\theta)\\) produces rose\ncurves: for integer \\(n>0\\) , odd \\(n\\) yields \\(n\\) petals and even \\(n\\) yields \\(2n\\) petals over a full tracing interval.\nLimacons \\(r=a+b\\cos\\theta\\) exhibit\ndifferent shapes depending on \\(|a/b|\\) , including inner loops when \\(0<|a|<|b|\\) . The Archimedean spiral \\(r=a+b\\theta\\) (for a specified angle\ninterval) increases radial distance linearly with angle. Negative values\nof \\(r\\) are plotted in the opposite\ndirection. Worked example. \\(r=2\\cos(3\\theta)\\) has three petals of\nmaximum radial length 2. At \\(\\theta=0\\) , \\(r=2\\) ; at \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\) , \\(r=0\\) . Guided practice. Predict the number of petals for \\(r=3\\sin(4\\theta)\\) and locate three\nangles where \\(r=0\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Polar symmetry and\ngraphing cautions", "url": "chapter14.html#polar-symmetry-and-graphing-cautions", "text": "A polar equation may be symmetric about the polar axis, the vertical\naxis, or the pole. Common algebraic checks include replacing \\(\\theta\\) by \\(-\\theta\\) , \\(\\theta\\) by \\(\\pi-\\theta\\) , and \\(r\\) by \\(-r\\) (or \\(\\theta\\) by \\(\\theta+\\pi\\) ), respectively. A failed test\ndoes not by itself prove a graph lacks symmetry because polar\nrepresentations are nonunique. Numerical plots also depend on sampling\ndensity: a coarse angle grid can miss narrow loops or crossings. Worked example. Since \\(\\cos(-\\theta)=\\cos\\theta\\) , the curve \\(r=2+\\cos\\theta\\) is symmetric about the\npolar axis. Guided practice. Explain why a graph of \\(r=\\sin(2\\theta)\\) requires careful\ntreatment of negative radii. Visual investigation 6. Visual investigation: symmetry and sampling. Sketch \\(r=2\\cos(3\\theta)\\) using a table with\nincrements \\(\\pi/12\\) . Then repeat\nusing only increments \\(\\pi/3\\) . Which\nfeatures disappear from the coarse plot? Explain why graphing technology\ncannot replace mathematical analysis. Reflection. Record a numerical prediction, a\ngeometric explanation, and a method for checking your conclusion."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Polar motion\nand connections to parametric curves", "url": "chapter14.html#polar-motion-and-connections-to-parametric-curves", "text": "A polar path \\(r=r(t)\\) , \\(\\theta=\\theta(t)\\) becomes the Cartesian\nparametric path \\(x(t)=r(t)\\cos\\theta(t)\\) and \\(y(t)=r(t)\\sin\\theta(t)\\) . For constant\nradius and increasing angle, the point travels on a circle; when radius\nchanges, it may trace a spiral. The parameter \\(t\\) can represent time, but it need not.\nThe geometric path alone does not specify speed or timing. Worked example. \\(r(t)=2\\) , \\(\\theta(t)=\\pi t\\) traces a circle of radius\n2, counterclockwise, completing one revolution every 2 time units. Guided practice. For \\(r(t)=t\\) , \\(\\theta(t)=2\\pi t\\) on \\(0\\le t\\le2\\) , find the Cartesian position\nat \\(t=0,1/2,1,2\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Vector modeling and\nmeasurement uncertainty", "url": "chapter14.html#vector-modeling-and-measurement-uncertainty", "text": "A mathematical model should identify which quantities are measured,\nthe coordinate system, the units, and the assumptions. For example,\ndisplacement components estimated with error may change the resultant\nmagnitude and direction. If \\(\\mathbf\nv=\\langle a,b\\rangle\\) , a small component perturbation \\(\\langle\\delta a,\\delta b\\rangle\\) changes\nthe magnitude by at most \\(\\sqrt{(\\delta\na)^2+(\\delta b)^2}\\) , by the reverse triangle inequality. This\nprovides a useful error bound without calculus. Worked example. If a displacement estimate is \\(\\langle3,4\\rangle\\) m and the total vector\nerror is at most 0.2 m, its true magnitude lies between 4.8 and 5.2\nm. Guided practice. Explain why angle uncertainty can\nbe large for vectors whose magnitudes are near zero. Visual investigation 7. Visual investigation: error and direction. Draw\nvectors \\(\\langle3,4\\rangle\\) , \\(\\langle3.2,4\\rangle\\) , and \\(\\langle3,4.2\\rangle\\) . Estimate how their\nlengths and angles differ. Repeat with a very short vector \\(\\langle0.05,0.05\\rangle\\) and comparable\ncomponent errors; discuss the difference in stability. Reflection. Record a numerical prediction, a\ngeometric explanation, and a method for checking your conclusion."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Calculus\nreadiness: tangent directions and changing vectors", "url": "chapter14.html#calculus-readiness-tangent-directions-and-changing-vectors", "text": "In calculus, a vector-valued position \\(\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle x(t),y(t)\\rangle\\) has\ninstantaneous velocity \\(\\mathbf\nr'(t)=\\langle x'(t),y'(t)\\rangle\\) . Before\ndifferentiating, estimate velocity by the secant vector \\([\\mathbf r(t+h)-\\mathbf r(t)]/h\\) . For a\ncircular path \\(\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle R\\cos\nt,R\\sin t\\rangle\\) , the secant approaches a tangent direction as \\(h\\to0\\) . The radius vector and\ntangent vector are perpendicular, foreshadowing dot-product arguments in\ncalculus. Worked example. At \\(t=0\\) , compare \\(\\mathbf r(0)=\\langle R,0\\rangle\\) with \\(\\mathbf r(h)=\\langle R\\cos h,R\\sin\nh\\rangle\\) for small positive \\(h\\) ; the displacement points approximately\nupward. Guided practice. Use \\(h=0.1\\) and \\(h=0.01\\) to estimate the tangent direction\nfor \\(R=3\\) at \\(t=0\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Visual gallery: vectors\nand polar curves", "url": "chapter14.html#visual-gallery-vectors-and-polar-curves", "text": "image image The second graph is the three-petal rose \\(r=2\\cos(3\\theta)\\) . Its Cartesian\nparameterization is \\(x=r\\cos\\theta\\) , \\(y=r\\sin\\theta\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: vectors, magnitudes, and projections", "url": "chapter14.html#computational-laboratory-a-vectors-magnitudes-and-projections", "text": "Prediction. For \\(\\mathbf\nu=\\langle4,3\\rangle\\) and \\(\\mathbf\nv=\\langle2,1\\rangle\\) , estimate the angle between the vectors and\nthe length of the projection of \\(\\mathbf\nu\\) onto \\(\\mathbf v\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter14.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory A: vectors and projections u <- c ( 4 , 3 ) v <- c ( 2 , 1 ) dot <- sum (u * v) angle <- acos (dot / ( sqrt ( sum (u ^ 2 )) * sqrt ( sum (v ^ 2 )))) projection <- dot / sum (v ^ 2 ) * v residual <- u - projection print ( list ( angle_degrees= angle * 180 / pi, projection= projection, residual= residual, orthogonality= sum (residual * v))) plot ( c ( 0 ,u[ 1 ],v[ 1 ],projection[ 1 ]), c ( 0 ,u[ 2 ],v[ 2 ],projection[ 2 ]), type= \"n\" , xlim= c ( 0 , 5 ), ylim= c ( 0 , 4 ), asp= 1 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"y\" , main= \"Vectors and projection\" ) arrows ( 0 , 0 ,u[ 1 ],u[ 2 ], col= \"blue\" , lwd= 2 ); arrows ( 0 , 0 ,v[ 1 ],v[ 2 ], col= \"red\" , lwd= 2 ); arrows ( 0 , 0 ,projection[ 1 ],projection[ 2 ], col= \"darkgreen\" , lwd= 2 )"}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter14.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory A: vectors and projections import math import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt u = np.array([ 4. , 3. ]) ; v = np.array([ 2. , 1. ]) dot = float (np.dot(u, v)) angle = math.degrees(math.acos(dot / (np.linalg.norm(u) * np.linalg.norm(v)))) projection = dot / np.dot(v,v) * v residual = u - projection print ( 'Angle (degrees):' , angle, 'Projection:' , projection, 'Residual:' , residual) print ( 'Residual dot v:' , np.dot(residual,v)) fig, ax = plt.subplots() for vec, color, name in [(u, 'blue' , 'u' ),(v, 'red' , 'v' ),(projection, 'green' , 'projection' )]: ax.arrow( 0 , 0 , * vec,head_width = .13 ,length_includes_head = True ,color = color,label = name) ax. set (xlim = ( 0 , 5 ),ylim = ( 0 , 4 ),xlabel = 'x' ,ylabel = 'y' ,title = 'Vectors and projection' ) ax.set_aspect( 'equal' ) ; ax.grid( True ) ; ax.legend() ; fig.savefig( 'figures/labA_python.png' ,dpi = 180 ,bbox_inches = 'tight' ) Interpretation. Compare exact and numerical values;\nverify the residual is perpendicular to \\(\\mathbf v\\) . What happens if the reference\nvector is zero?"}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: polar curves and coordinate conversion", "url": "chapter14.html#computational-laboratory-b-polar-curves-and-coordinate-conversion", "text": "Prediction. Sketch \\(r=2\\cos(3\\theta)\\) and \\(r=1+2\\cos\\theta\\) before running either\nscript. Predict the number of petals and whether an inner loop\noccurs."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter14.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory B: polar curves theta <- seq ( 0 , 2 * pi, length.out= 1500 ) r1 <- 2 * cos ( 3 * theta); r2 <- 1 + 2 * cos (theta) x1 <- r1 * cos (theta); y1 <- r1 * sin (theta) x2 <- r2 * cos (theta); y2 <- r2 * sin (theta) par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 2 )) plot (x1,y1, type= \"l\" , asp= 1 , col= \"blue\" , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"y\" , main= \"Three-petal rose\" ) plot (x2,y2, type= \"l\" , asp= 1 , col= \"red\" , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"y\" , main= \"Limacon with inner loop\" ) par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 1 ))"}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter14.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory B: polar curves import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt theta = np.linspace( 0 , 2 * np.pi, 1500 ) fig, axes = plt.subplots( 1 , 2 ,figsize = ( 10 , 4 )) for ax, r, title in [(axes[ 0 ], 2 * np.cos( 3 * theta), 'Three-petal rose' ),(axes[ 1 ], 1 + 2 * np.cos(theta), 'Limacon with inner loop' )]: ax.plot(r * np.cos(theta),r * np.sin(theta)) ax.set_aspect( 'equal' ) ; ax.set_title(title) ; ax.set_xlabel( 'x' ) ; ax.set_ylabel( 'y' ) ; ax.grid( True ) fig.tight_layout() ; fig.savefig( 'figures/labB_python.png' ,dpi = 180 ,bbox_inches = 'tight' ) Interpretation. Explain how negative radial values\nare converted to Cartesian coordinates. Compare coarse and fine angular\nsampling."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: displacement modeling and uncertainty", "url": "chapter14.html#computational-laboratory-c-displacement-modeling-and-uncertainty", "text": "Prediction. A nominal displacement is \\(\\langle3,4\\rangle\\) meters. If each\ncomponent can vary by \\(\\pm0.2\\) m,\nestimate the range of possible resultant lengths."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter14.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory C: component uncertainty, reproducible simulation set.seed ( 14 ) n <- 2000 x <- runif (n, 2.8 , 3.2 ); y <- runif (n, 3.8 , 4.2 ) magnitude <- sqrt (x ^ 2 + y ^ 2 ) cat ( \"Nominal magnitude:\" , 5 , \" \\n \" ) cat ( \"Simulated min/max:\" , range (magnitude), \" \\n \" ) cat ( \"Componentwise theoretical bounds:\" , sqrt ( 2.8 ^ 2 +3.8 ^ 2 ), sqrt ( 3.2 ^ 2 +4.2 ^ 2 ), \" \\n \" ) plot (x,y, asp= 1 , pch= 16 , cex= . 25 , col= \"gray50\" , xlab= \"Horizontal displacement (m)\" , ylab= \"Vertical displacement (m)\" , main= \"Component uncertainty\" ) points ( 3 , 4 , col= \"red\" , pch= 19 , cex= 1.3 )"}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter14.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code # Laboratory C: component uncertainty, reproducible simulation import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt rng = np.random.default_rng( 14 ) n = 2000 x = rng.uniform( 2.8 , 3.2 ,n) ; y = rng.uniform( 3.8 , 4.2 ,n) magnitude = np.hypot(x,y) print ( 'Nominal magnitude:' , 5 ) print ( 'Simulated min/max:' ,magnitude. min (),magnitude. max ()) print ( 'Componentwise theoretical bounds:' ,np.hypot( 2.8 , 3.8 ),np.hypot( 3.2 , 4.2 )) fig, ax = plt.subplots() ax.scatter(x,y,s = 4 ,alpha = .35 ) ; ax.scatter([ 3 ],[ 4 ],s = 55 ) ax. set (xlabel = 'Horizontal displacement (m)' ,ylabel = 'Vertical displacement (m)' ,title = 'Component uncertainty' ) ax.set_aspect( 'equal' ) ; ax.grid( True ) ; fig.savefig( 'figures/labC_python.png' ,dpi = 180 ,bbox_inches = 'tight' ) Interpretation. Distinguish the componentwise\nuncertainty model from a bound on total vector error. State whether the\nsimulation proves a universal bound."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Extended\napplied workshops with worked solutions", "url": "chapter14.html#extended-applied-workshops-with-worked-solutions", "text": ""}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Workshop 1: Patient\ntransport displacement", "url": "chapter14.html#workshop-1-patient-transport-displacement", "text": "A hospital transporter moves a cart 40 m east and then 30 m north.\nFind the net displacement, its magnitude, and its direction from\neast. Solution. \\(\\mathbf\nd=\\langle40,30\\rangle\\) m; \\(\\|\\mathbf\nd\\|=50\\) m; \\(\\theta=\\tan^{-1}(3/4)\\approx36.87^\\circ\\) north of east. The distance traveled is 70 m, which differs from the\ndisplacement magnitude."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Workshop 2:\nResultant force", "url": "chapter14.html#workshop-2-resultant-force", "text": "Two idealized planar forces are \\(12\\) N east and \\(5\\) N south. Find the resultant and the\nbalancing force. Solution. \\(\\mathbf\nF=\\langle12,-5\\rangle\\) N; magnitude \\(13\\) N and direction approximately \\(22.62^\\circ\\) south of east. The balancing\nforce is \\(\\langle-12,5\\rangle\\) N."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Workshop 3: Sensor\nalignment and projection", "url": "chapter14.html#workshop-3-sensor-alignment-and-projection", "text": "A sensor reads \\(\\mathbf\ns=\\langle6,2\\rangle\\) units, while its reference direction is \\(\\mathbf d=\\langle3,4\\rangle\\) . Find\nthe component of the reading along the reference and the projected\nvector. Solution. \\(\\mathbf\ns\\cdot\\mathbf d=26\\) , \\(\\|\\mathbf\nd\\|=5\\) , so the scalar component is \\(26/5=5.2\\) . The projection is \\((26/25)\\langle3,4\\rangle=\\langle78/25,104/25\\rangle\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Workshop 4: Circular scanning\ngeometry", "url": "chapter14.html#workshop-4-circular-scanning-geometry", "text": "An idealized rotating instrument traces points at radius 8 cm and\nangle \\(\\theta\\) from a fixed\nhorizontal axis. Find the Cartesian coordinates at \\(\\theta=\\pi/3\\) , and the angle of the point \\((-4,4\\sqrt3)\\) cm. Solution. At \\(\\pi/3\\) , \\((x,y)=(4,4\\sqrt3)\\) cm. The point \\((-4,4\\sqrt3)\\) has radius 8 cm and\nprincipal angle \\(2\\pi/3\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Workshop 5: Polar curve\ninterpretation", "url": "chapter14.html#workshop-5-polar-curve-interpretation", "text": "A simplified geometric path has polar equation \\(r=4\\cos\\theta\\) . Identify its Cartesian\nlocus and explain why the graph is not a circle centered at the\norigin. Solution. Multiply by \\(r\\) to obtain \\(x^2+y^2=4x\\) , or \\((x-2)^2+y^2=4\\) . The circle has center \\((2,0)\\) and radius 2; it passes\nthrough the pole."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Problem-solving\nclinic: selecting the right representation", "url": "chapter14.html#problem-solving-clinic-selecting-the-right-representation", "text": "Sketch the situation and state the coordinate axes, positive\ndirections, and units. Decide whether components, magnitude–direction form, or polar\ncoordinates are most informative. Convert angles to radians before using trigonometric software\nfunctions. Use exact expressions when possible; round only after\ninterpreting the result. Check whether a computed angle is in the correct\nquadrant. Distinguish a geometric path from a time-dependent motion\nmodel. Check domain restrictions and special cases such as zero vectors\nand the pole. Compare analytic answers with numerical and graphical\nevidence."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Guided practice studio", "url": "chapter14.html#guided-practice-studio", "text": ""}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Activity\nA: reconstructing a vector", "url": "chapter14.html#activity-a-reconstructing-a-vector", "text": "A vector has magnitude 10 and direction \\(\\theta\\) with \\(\\cos\\theta=-3/5\\) and \\(\\sin\\theta=4/5\\) . Find its components,\nidentify its quadrant, and find a unit vector in the opposite\ndirection. Solution. \\(\\mathbf\nv=\\langle-6,8\\rangle\\) , quadrant II, opposite unit vector \\(\\langle3/5,-4/5\\rangle\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Activity\nB: orthogonal decomposition", "url": "chapter14.html#activity-b-orthogonal-decomposition", "text": "Decompose \\(\\mathbf\nu=\\langle5,1\\rangle\\) into a component parallel to \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle1,1\\rangle\\) and a\nperpendicular remainder. Solution. The projection is \\((6/2)\\langle1,1\\rangle=\\langle3,3\\rangle\\) ;\nremainder \\(\\langle2,-2\\rangle\\) has\ndot product zero with \\(\\mathbf\nv\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Activity C: two polar\nrepresentations", "url": "chapter14.html#activity-c-two-polar-representations", "text": "Find Cartesian coordinates for \\((r,\\theta)=(-4,\\pi/6)\\) and give an\nequivalent polar representation with nonnegative radius. Solution. \\((x,y)=(-2\\sqrt3,-2)\\) , equivalent to \\((4,7\\pi/6)\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Activity D: identifying a\npolar circle", "url": "chapter14.html#activity-d-identifying-a-polar-circle", "text": "Show that \\(r=6\\sin\\theta\\) is a\ncircle and determine its center, radius, and intercepts. Solution. \\(r^2=6r\\sin\\theta\\) implies \\(x^2+y^2=6y\\) , so \\(x^2+(y-3)^2=9\\) . The circle meets the \\(y\\) axis at \\((0,0)\\) and \\((0,6)\\) and touches the \\(x\\) axis at the origin."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Independent practice\nwith worked checks", "url": "chapter14.html#independent-practice-with-worked-checks", "text": "Compute \\(\\langle2,-3\\rangle+\\langle-5,7\\rangle\\) . Check: \\(\\langle-3,4\\rangle\\) . Find the magnitude of \\(\\langle8,15\\rangle\\) . Check: \\(17\\) . Normalize \\(\\langle-3,4\\rangle\\) . Check: \\(\\langle-3/5,4/5\\rangle\\) . Evaluate \\(\\langle2,5\\rangle\\cdot\\langle3,-1\\rangle\\) . Check: \\(1\\) . Convert \\((2,\\pi/2)\\) from polar\nto Cartesian. Check: \\((0,2)\\) . Convert \\((-1,-\\sqrt3)\\) to\npolar form with \\(r>0\\) and \\(0\\le\\theta<2\\pi\\) . Check: \\((2,4\\pi/3)\\) . Convert \\(r=2\\cos\\theta\\) to\nCartesian form. Check: \\((x-1)^2+y^2=1\\) . Determine whether \\(\\langle1,3\\rangle\\) and \\(\\langle3,-1\\rangle\\) are perpendicular. Check: yes, dot product zero."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Deeper\ninvestigation: deriving the dot-product formula", "url": "chapter14.html#deeper-investigation-deriving-the-dot-product-formula", "text": "The formula \\(\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf\nv=\\|\\mathbf u\\|\\|\\mathbf v\\|\\cos\\theta\\) is not an arbitrary\ncomputational rule. To understand why it works, start with the triangle\nformed by \\(\\mathbf u\\) , \\(\\mathbf v\\) , and \\(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf v\\) . The law of cosines\ngives \\[\\|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf v\\|^2=\\|\\mathbf\nu\\|^2+\\|\\mathbf v\\|^2-2\\|\\mathbf u\\|\\|\\mathbf v\\|\\cos\\theta.\\] On\nthe other hand, expand the left side component by component: \\[\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf\nv\\|^2&=(u_1-v_1)^2+(u_2-v_2)^2\\\\&=u_1^2+u_2^2+v_1^2+v_2^2-2(u_1v_1+u_2v_2).\n\\end{aligned}\\] Comparing the two expressions and canceling\ncommon terms yields the geometric formula. This derivation also explains\nwhy the dot product is positive for acute angles, zero for right angles,\nand negative for obtuse angles. Guided derivation. Take \\(\\mathbf u=\\langle2,1\\rangle\\) and \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle-1,2\\rangle\\) . Compute all\nthree squared side lengths, use the law of cosines, and show that \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\) . Concept check. Can two vectors have a negative dot\nproduct even when all four component magnitudes are positive? Explain\nthe distinction between component magnitudes and signed components."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Deeper\ninvestigation: building a polar graph by hand", "url": "chapter14.html#deeper-investigation-building-a-polar-graph-by-hand", "text": "Consider the polar curve \\(r=1+2\\cos\\theta\\) . To graph it accurately,\nstart with a table rather than a memorized picture. At \\(\\theta=0\\) , \\(r=3\\) ; at \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\) , \\(r=1\\) ; at \\(\\theta=\\pi\\) , \\(r=-1\\) ; at \\(\\theta=3\\pi/2\\) , \\(r=1\\) . A negative radius at \\(\\theta=\\pi\\) means plotting one unit in the\nopposite direction, namely at \\((1,0)\\) . The curve passes through the pole\nwhen \\(1+2\\cos\\theta=0\\) , or \\(\\theta=2\\pi/3,4\\pi/3\\) . Between these\nangles, the radius is negative, and the curve traces an inner loop. Guided practice, step 1. Evaluate \\(r\\) at \\(\\theta=\\pi/3,2\\pi/3,5\\pi/6,\\pi,7\\pi/6,4\\pi/3,5\\pi/3\\) .\nRecord each point as \\((r,\\theta)\\) and\nconvert it to Cartesian coordinates. Guided practice, step 2. Sketch the points and\nconnect them in increasing order of \\(\\theta\\) . Use symmetry about the polar axis\nto check your sketch. Label the crossings at the pole and the inner\nloop. Guided practice, step 3. Compare this curve with \\(r=2+\\cos\\theta\\) . Explain why the\nsecond curve does not have an inner loop. Your explanation should use\nthe minimum possible radius, not just the appearance of a graph."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Additional\npractice: representations and reasoning", "url": "chapter14.html#additional-practice-representations-and-reasoning", "text": ""}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Exercise set A: vector\ninterpretation", "url": "chapter14.html#exercise-set-a-vector-interpretation", "text": "Two students describe the same motion: one says “8 meters\nnortheast,” while another gives the vector \\(\\langle4\\sqrt2,4\\sqrt2\\rangle\\) meters.\nExplain why these descriptions agree. A vector has components \\(\\langle-8,-6\\rangle\\) . Compute its\nmagnitude, identify its quadrant, and give a direction angle in\ndegrees. A wind vector \\(\\langle3,-4\\rangle\\) m/s acts on an object\nmoving at \\(\\langle7,2\\rangle\\) m/s\nrelative to still air. Under a simple additive-velocity model, find the\nresulting velocity and speed. Explain why the scalar projection of \\(\\mathbf u\\) onto \\(\\mathbf v\\) can be negative even though the\nmagnitude of the vector projection is nonnegative."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Exercise set\nB: polar reasoning", "url": "chapter14.html#exercise-set-b-polar-reasoning", "text": "Compare \\((2,\\pi/4)\\) , \\((-2,5\\pi/4)\\) , and \\((2,9\\pi/4)\\) . Are they the same point?\nVerify using Cartesian coordinates. Convert \\(x^2+y^2=8y\\) to a\npolar equation and identify the curve’s center and radius. A sensor rotates through one full revolution at fixed radius \\(r=5\\) cm. Write its position as a\nfunction of angle, then as a function of time if the angular speed is \\(\\pi/3\\) radians per second. Explain why \\(r=\\theta\\) and \\(r=\\theta+2\\pi\\) are not the same\npolar graph even though angles differing by \\(2\\pi\\) point in the same\ndirection."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Reflection and\nsynthesis", "url": "chapter14.html#reflection-and-synthesis", "text": "Write a paragraph explaining when you would prefer (a) rectangular\nvector components, (b) magnitude–direction form, and (c) polar\ncoordinates. Give one scientific or geometric example for each choice.\nInclude a statement about units and the limitations of a mathematical\nmodel."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Chapter review exercises", "url": "chapter14.html#chapter-review-exercises", "text": "For each problem, show the mathematical steps, label units where\napplicable, and check whether a graphical interpretation agrees with the\nalgebra. Find \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\) for \\(P(-4,2)\\) and \\(Q(3,-5)\\) . Compute \\(2\\langle1,-4\\rangle-3\\langle-2,5\\rangle\\) . Find the length of \\(\\langle-7,24\\rangle\\) . Find a unit vector parallel to \\(\\langle8,-6\\rangle\\) . Find the endpoint of a vector \\(\\langle-5,9\\rangle\\) starting at \\((4,-2)\\) . Express a vector of magnitude 16 and direction \\(\\pi/6\\) in components. Find the direction angle of \\(\\langle-1,1\\rangle\\) in \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Find the angle between \\(\\langle1,0\\rangle\\) and \\(\\langle-1,1\\rangle\\) . Find \\(\\langle2,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-3,4\\rangle\\) . Determine whether \\(\\langle3,2\\rangle\\) and \\(\\langle-4,6\\rangle\\) are\northogonal. Project \\(\\langle4,0\\rangle\\) onto \\(\\langle1,1\\rangle\\) . Decompose \\(\\langle1,5\\rangle\\) into parts parallel and perpendicular to \\(\\langle0,1\\rangle\\) . Add forces of 5 N east and 12 N north; find the\nmagnitude. Find the balancing force for \\(\\langle-3,7\\rangle\\) N. Convert polar \\((5,\\pi/3)\\) to\nCartesian coordinates. Convert polar \\((-2,\\pi/2)\\) to\nCartesian coordinates. Convert Cartesian \\((3,-3)\\) to\npolar form with \\(r\\ge0\\) . Give two polar descriptions of \\((-4,0)\\) . Explain why the polar angle at the origin is not unique. Convert \\(r=8\\cos\\theta\\) to a\nCartesian equation. Convert \\(r=4\\sin\\theta\\) to a\nCartesian equation. Sketch \\(r=3\\) and identify its\nCartesian equation. Predict the number of petals of \\(r=\\cos(5\\theta)\\) . Predict the number of petals of \\(r=2\\sin(4\\theta)\\) . Find three zeros of \\(r=2\\cos(3\\theta)\\) on \\([0,2\\pi)\\) . Describe the symmetry of \\(r=2+\\cos\\theta\\) . Convert \\(r(t)=2\\) , \\(\\theta(t)=t\\) to Cartesian parametric\nequations. Find the location of \\(r(t)=t\\) , \\(\\theta(t)=\\pi t\\) at \\(t=1\\) . Explain the difference between distance traveled and net\ndisplacement. Explain why \\(\\tan^{-1}(y/x)\\) may give an incorrect quadrant. Estimate the secant velocity of \\(\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle\\) at \\(t=0\\) using \\(h=0.1\\) . Explain why \\(\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf0}\\mathbf u\\) is undefined. Find the range of possible magnitudes when \\(\\|\\mathbf v\\|=5\\) and the total vector\nerror is at most 0.3. Write a short modeling critique of a constant-speed circular\nmotion assumption. Compare a polar graph sampled at 12 angles and at 360 angles;\ndescribe possible numerical artifacts."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter14.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "For \\(\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2\\cos t,2\\sin\nt\\rangle\\) , compute \\([\\mathbf\nr(h)-\\mathbf r(0)]/h\\) for \\(h=0.1\\) and \\(h=0.01\\) ; identify the limiting\ndirection. Explain how the dot product can test whether a proposed velocity\nvector is tangent to a circle. A polar path is \\(r=1+\\theta\\) for \\(0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi\\) . Write\nCartesian parametric equations and discuss how the radial distance\nchanges. State one question about a vector-valued motion model that cannot\nbe answered from the geometric path alone."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Looking ahead", "url": "chapter14.html#looking-ahead", "text": "Chapter 15 introduces rates of change, difference quotients, and\nlimits. The vector and polar representations developed here will\nreappear in differential and multivariable calculus, where positions\nbecome functions of time and tangent directions become derivatives."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Extended application:\nDirectional motion", "url": "chapter14.html#extended-application-directional-motion", "text": "A mobile robot travels 10 meters at a heading measured\ncounterclockwise from the positive horizontal axis. The quantities in\nthis investigation are illustrative rather than measured\nobservations."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter14.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[Y(\\theta)=10\\sin\\theta.\\] Before\ncalculating, identify the input and output, their units, and the\ninterval on which the model is being studied. Distinguish the\nmathematical domain from the practical domain. The numerical study uses\ninputs from \\(0\\) to \\(6.28\\) . Example 1: Analytical investigation. Find both\ndisplacement components at \\(\\pi/6\\) and verify the magnitude. Find headings with vertical displacement 5\nm. Worked solution. Components \\((5\\sqrt3,5)\\) ; magnitude 10. Headings \\(\\pi/6\\) and \\(5\\pi/6\\) modulo \\(2\\pi\\) ."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter14.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Heading (rad) Vertical displacement (m) 0 0 1.57 10 3.14 -2.068e-12 4.71 -10 6.28 4.136e-12 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter14.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Directional motion # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 6.28318530718 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 10 * sin (x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Heading (rad)\" , ylab = \"Vertical displacement (m)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter14.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Directional motion import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 6.28318530718 , 301 ) y = 10 * np.sin(x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Heading (rad)\" , ylabel = \"Vertical displacement (m)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter14.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Directional motion", "url": "chapter14.html#chapter-project-investigating-directional-motion", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter14.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "url": "chapter15.html#rates-of-change-difference-quotients-and-limits", "text": ""}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Opening\ncontext: from patterns to instantaneous change", "url": "chapter15.html#opening-context-from-patterns-to-instantaneous-change", "text": "Earlier chapters developed functions, graphical models, and algebraic\nreasoning. This chapter asks a new question: how quickly does a quantity\nchange at a particular instant ? Average change is easy to\ncalculate from two measurements; instantaneous change requires a\nlimiting argument. This bridge from precalculus to calculus is\nespecially useful for analyzing motion, medication concentrations, and\nlaboratory sensor readings. All applied data in this chapter are\nillustrative unless otherwise stated."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter15.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "Students will compute and interpret average rates; construct and\nsimplify difference quotients; estimate tangent slopes numerically and\ngraphically; evaluate elementary limits by substitution, factoring, and\nrationalization; distinguish one-sided and two-sided limits; classify\ndiscontinuities; and explain how measurement noise affects numerical\nestimates."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "How to use the\ncomputational laboratories", "url": "chapter15.html#how-to-use-the-computational-laboratories", "text": "Predict analytically before executing code. Each laboratory presents R first , then Python , in separate\nfull-width listings. R and Python use different indexing conventions,\nbut both use radians for trigonometric calculations. Record mathematical\nconclusions in complete sentences with units where appropriate."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Why rates of change matter", "url": "chapter15.html#why-rates-of-change-matter", "text": "A quantity can change even when its current value is known precisely.\nA concentration of \\(8\\) mg/L does not\ntell us whether the concentration is rising or falling. Rates compare a\nchange in an output with a change in an input. For a function \\(f\\) , the average rate of change on \\([a,b]\\) is \\([f(b)-f(a)]/(b-a)\\) , provided \\(a\\ne b\\) . Units are output units per input\nunit. A negative rate describes a decrease, not an invalid\nmeasurement. Worked example. A temperature increases from \\(18^\\circ\\) C at 8:00 to \\(24^\\circ\\) C at 11:00. Its average rate is \\((24-18)/(11-8)=2^\\circ\\) C per hour.\nThis does not imply the temperature rose at exactly that rate every\nminute. Guided practice. A laboratory concentration drops\nfrom \\(15\\) to \\(9\\) mg/L over 4 hours. Find the average\nrate and interpret its sign."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Secant lines and average\nrates", "url": "chapter15.html#secant-lines-and-average-rates", "text": "On the graph \\(y=f(x)\\) , the average\nrate of change over \\([a,b]\\) equals\nthe slope of the secant line through \\((a,f(a))\\) and \\((b,f(b))\\) . The secant equation is \\(y-f(a)=m_{a,b}(x-a)\\) . A secant slope\ndepends on two input values; it is not generally the slope at one point.\nMoving \\(b\\) toward \\(a\\) creates a sequence of secant lines that\nmay approach a tangent. Worked example. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , the secant from \\(x=1\\) to \\(x=3\\) has slope \\((9-1)/(3-1)=4\\) and equation \\(y-1=4(x-1)\\) . Guided practice. For \\(f(x)=x^2-2x\\) , find the secant slope on \\([0,3]\\) and write the secant\nequation. Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "The difference quotient", "url": "chapter15.html#the-difference-quotient", "text": "The expression \\([f(a+h)-f(a)]/h\\) is the difference quotient at \\(a\\) with increment \\(h\\ne0\\) . It measures\nan average rate over an interval of width \\(h\\) . The algebraic form matters:\nsimplifying first often reveals what happens as \\(h\\) becomes small. The condition \\(h\\ne0\\) must be maintained during\ncancellation. Worked example. For \\(f(x)=x^2+3x\\) , \\(f(a+h)-f(a)=2ah+h^2+3h\\) , so the difference\nquotient is \\(2a+h+3\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) . Guided practice. Compute and simplify the difference\nquotient for \\(f(x)=2x^2-5x+1\\) . State\nthe restriction on \\(h\\) . Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Difference\nquotients for rational functions", "url": "chapter15.html#difference-quotients-for-rational-functions", "text": "Rational functions require attention to domain restrictions before\nand after simplification. When \\(f(x)=1/x\\) , \\([f(a+h)-f(a)]/h=-1/[a(a+h)]\\) as long as \\(h\\ne0\\) , \\(a\\ne0\\) , and \\(a+h\\ne0\\) . A canceled factor does not\nrestore a previously excluded input. This distinction prepares students\nto understand removable discontinuities. Worked example. At \\(a=2\\) , the difference quotient for \\(f(x)=1/x\\) becomes \\(-1/[2(2+h)]\\) , which approaches \\(-1/4\\) as \\(h\\) approaches zero. Guided practice. For \\(f(x)=3/x\\) , simplify the difference\nquotient at \\(a=1\\) and list every\nexcluded value of \\(h\\) ."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Numerical estimates\nof instantaneous change", "url": "chapter15.html#numerical-estimates-of-instantaneous-change", "text": "A table of difference quotients for smaller positive and negative \\(h\\) can suggest a limiting rate.\nCompare both sides: numerical agreement is evidence, not a proof.\nFloating-point arithmetic can lose accuracy when \\(h\\) is extremely small because subtraction\nof nearly equal values introduces cancellation error. Use moderate\ndecreasing increments and algebra whenever possible. Worked example. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) at \\(a=2\\) , the difference quotient equals \\(4+h\\) . Values at \\(h=0.1,0.01,-0.01,-0.1\\) are \\(4.1,4.01,3.99,3.9\\) , suggesting a rate of \\(4\\) . Guided practice. Construct a table for \\(f(x)=x^2+1\\) at \\(a=3\\) with \\(h=\\pm0.1,\\pm0.01\\) . Predict the limiting\nslope. Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Tangent lines as limiting\nsecants", "url": "chapter15.html#tangent-lines-as-limiting-secants", "text": "A tangent line represents the local linear direction of a smooth\ncurve. If secant slopes approach a finite value \\(m\\) as \\(h\\to0\\) , the tangent line at \\(x=a\\) is \\(y-f(a)=m(x-a)\\) . A tangent need not touch a\ngraph at only one point. Some graphs have corners, cusps, vertical\ntangents, or discontinuities, where a finite tangent slope may fail to\nexist. Worked example. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) at \\(a=2\\) , the limiting slope is \\(4\\) . The tangent is \\(y-4=4(x-2)\\) , or \\(y=4x-4\\) . Guided practice. Find the tangent line to \\(f(x)=x^2+3x\\) at \\(x=1\\) by simplifying the difference\nquotient. Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "The intuitive idea of a\nlimit", "url": "chapter15.html#the-intuitive-idea-of-a-limit", "text": "The notation \\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)=L\\) says that values of \\(f(x)\\) can be\nmade arbitrarily close to \\(L\\) by\nchoosing \\(x\\) sufficiently close to \\(a\\) but different from \\(a\\) . The value \\(f(a)\\) may differ from \\(L\\) or may not be defined. A limit is about\nbehavior near a point, not necessarily at the point. A table and graph\ncan motivate a conjecture; algebra can establish it in familiar\ncases. Worked example. For \\(f(x)=(x^2-4)/(x-2)\\) with \\(x\\ne2\\) , factoring gives \\(f(x)=x+2\\) . Thus the limit as \\(x\\to2\\) is \\(4\\) even though the original expression is\nundefined at \\(2\\) . Guided practice. Evaluate \\(\\lim_{x\\to3}(x^2-9)/(x-3)\\) and explain why\ndirect substitution into the original fraction fails. Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "One-sided limits", "url": "chapter15.html#one-sided-limits", "text": "A left-hand limit \\(\\lim_{x\\to\na^-}f(x)\\) approaches \\(a\\) through smaller inputs; a right-hand limit \\(\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f(x)\\) approaches through\nlarger inputs. A finite two-sided limit exists exactly when both\none-sided limits exist and are equal. This is essential for piecewise\nmodels with changes in rules at a threshold. Worked example. Let \\(f(x)=x+1\\) for \\(x<2\\) and \\(f(x)=5-x\\) for \\(x\\ge2\\) . Both one-sided limits at \\(2\\) equal \\(3\\) , so the two-sided limit is \\(3\\) . Guided practice. For \\(g(x)=x^2\\) if \\(x<1\\) and \\(g(x)=x+2\\) if \\(x\\ge1\\) , find both one-sided limits at \\(1\\) . Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Limit laws and direct\nsubstitution", "url": "chapter15.html#limit-laws-and-direct-substitution", "text": "For functions with finite limits, sums, differences, products, and\nquotients (with nonzero denominator limit) preserve limits. Polynomials\nare continuous everywhere, so their limits can be found by direct\nsubstitution. Rational functions are continuous wherever their\ndenominators are nonzero. These facts justify many quick evaluations,\nbut they do not justify dividing by zero. Worked example. \\(\\lim_{x\\to2}(3x^2-x+1)=3(4)-2+1=11\\) . For \\(\\lim_{x\\to2}(x+1)/(x-4)\\) , the\ndenominator is nonzero, giving \\(-3/2\\) . Guided practice. Evaluate \\(\\lim_{x\\to-1}(2x^3+5x)\\) and \\(\\lim_{x\\to1}(x^2+2)/(x+3)\\) ."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Indeterminate forms and\nfactoring", "url": "chapter15.html#indeterminate-forms-and-factoring", "text": "A quotient that yields \\(0/0\\) under\nsubstitution is indeterminate: it does not tell us the limit. Factoring,\nrationalizing, or rewriting may reveal a simpler expression for nearby\ninputs. Never treat \\(0/0\\) as a\nnumerical answer. Each simplification should preserve equality on a\npunctured neighborhood of the target point. Worked example. \\(\\lim_{x\\to3}(x^2-9)/(x-3)=\\lim_{x\\to3}(x+3)=6\\) after factoring and canceling for \\(x\\ne3\\) . Guided practice. Evaluate \\(\\lim_{x\\to-2}(x^2+5x+6)/(x+2)\\) by\nfactoring. State why the cancellation is valid for the limit. Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Rationalization and radical\nlimits", "url": "chapter15.html#rationalization-and-radical-limits", "text": "When radicals produce a \\(0/0\\) expression, multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate. The\nidentity \\((A-B)(A+B)=A^2-B^2\\) often\nremoves the troublesome radical difference. The resulting expression is\nequivalent only where the original is defined, which is sufficient for\nevaluating a nearby limit. Worked example. For \\(x\\ne0\\) , \\((\\sqrt{4+x}-2)/x=1/(\\sqrt{4+x}+2)\\) .\nTherefore the limit as \\(x\\to0\\) is \\(1/4\\) . Guided practice. Evaluate \\(\\lim_{h\\to0}(\\sqrt{9+h}-3)/h\\) using a\nconjugate. Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Infinite behavior and\nasymptotes", "url": "chapter15.html#infinite-behavior-and-asymptotes", "text": "An infinite limit describes unbounded growth or decrease near an\ninput; it is not a finite limit. For example, \\(1/(x-2)\\) tends to \\(-\\infty\\) from the left and \\(+\\infty\\) from the right. A vertical\nasymptote may occur at \\(x=2\\) .\nHorizontal asymptotes concern behavior as \\(x\\to\\pm\\infty\\) . These are different\nquestions and should not be conflated. Worked example. For \\(f(x)=2+1/x\\) , the function approaches \\(2\\) as \\(x\\to\\infty\\) , so \\(y=2\\) is a horizontal asymptote; \\(x=0\\) is a vertical asymptote. Guided practice. Describe the one-sided behavior of \\(1/(x+3)\\) near \\(x=-3\\) and its behavior as \\(x\\to\\infty\\) . Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Continuity at a point", "url": "chapter15.html#continuity-at-a-point", "text": "A function is continuous at \\(a\\) if\n(1) \\(f(a)\\) is defined, (2) \\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)\\) exists, and (3) the\nlimit equals \\(f(a)\\) . Discontinuities\ninclude removable holes, jumps, and infinite behavior. A physical model\nmay be continuous over a time range even if a piecewise formula changes\nthere; matching one-sided values is the key. Worked example. Define \\(f(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1)\\) for \\(x\\ne1\\) and \\(f(1)=2\\) . Then \\(f\\) is continuous at \\(1\\) , because the nearby expression is \\(x+1\\) and its limit is \\(2\\) . Guided practice. Choose \\(c\\) so \\(f(x)=2x+1\\) for \\(x<2\\) and \\(f(x)=x+c\\) for \\(x\\ge2\\) is continuous at \\(2\\) . Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Absolute value and corners", "url": "chapter15.html#absolute-value-and-corners", "text": "The absolute-value function \\(f(x)=|x|\\) is continuous at zero but has\ndifferent left and right secant slopes there. For \\(h>0\\) , \\(|h|/h=1\\) ; for \\(h<0\\) , it equals \\(-1\\) . Thus a single finite limiting slope\ndoes not exist. Continuity alone is not sufficient for\ndifferentiability. Worked example. For \\(f(x)=|x-3|\\) , the graph has a corner at \\(x=3\\) . The left slope is \\(-1\\) and the right slope is \\(1\\) . Guided practice. Use the difference quotient to\ndecide whether \\(f(x)=|x+2|\\) has a\ntangent slope at \\(x=-2\\) . Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Piecewise models and\nthreshold decisions", "url": "chapter15.html#piecewise-models-and-threshold-decisions", "text": "Piecewise functions can represent different regimes, such as a\nbaseline response below a threshold and a new response above it. Check\none-sided limits, the assigned function value, and units. A\ndiscontinuity may be intentional (an instantaneous policy change) or a\nmodeling defect (an implausible jump in a physiological quantity).\nMathematical conclusions depend on the assumptions of the model. Worked example. A device reports \\(S(t)=2t+1\\) for \\(t<4\\) and \\(S(t)=t+5\\) for \\(t\\ge4\\) . Both rules approach \\(9\\) at \\(t=4\\) , so the model is continuous\nthere. Guided practice. Replace the second rule by \\(S(t)=t+c\\) and find \\(c\\) that preserves continuity at \\(t=4\\) ."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Average versus\ninstantaneous velocity", "url": "chapter15.html#average-versus-instantaneous-velocity", "text": "For a position model \\(s(t)\\) ,\naverage velocity over \\([t,t+h]\\) is \\([s(t+h)-s(t)]/h\\) . Its limiting\nvalue, when it exists, is instantaneous velocity. Velocity is signed;\nspeed is its magnitude in one-dimensional motion. Units must be carried\nthrough the calculation. A position model need not be physically valid\nfor all times. Worked example. For \\(s(t)=5t^2\\) meters, the average velocity\nfrom \\(t=2\\) to \\(t=2+h\\) is \\(20+5h\\) m/s. As \\(h\\to0\\) , instantaneous velocity is \\(20\\) m/s. Guided practice. For \\(s(t)=3t^2+2t\\) meters, find average\nvelocity on \\([1,1+h]\\) and estimate\ninstantaneous velocity at \\(t=1\\) . Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Measurement\nnoise and numerical differentiation", "url": "chapter15.html#measurement-noise-and-numerical-differentiation", "text": "Real measurements are noisy. When a difference quotient divides a\nsmall difference by a small time interval, noise can be amplified. For\nobservations \\(y_i=f(t_i)+\\epsilon_i\\) ,\na forward-difference estimate \\([y_{i+1}-y_i]/\\Delta t\\) contains noise\ndifference \\((\\epsilon_{i+1}-\\epsilon_i)/\\Delta t\\) .\nTherefore smaller spacing does not always yield a better empirical\nestimate. Compare trends across several scales and report\nuncertainty. Worked example. If two readings each have errors as\nlarge as \\(0.02\\) units and the time\ngap is \\(0.01\\) s, the difference\nquotient can have error as large as \\(0.04/0.01=4\\) units/s. Guided practice. If readings have errors at most \\(0.01\\) mg/L and the time gap is \\(0.1\\) h, bound the possible error in the\nforward-difference estimate. Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Limits and model\ninterpretation", "url": "chapter15.html#limits-and-model-interpretation", "text": "The mathematical limit of a model need not correspond to an\nattainable physical measurement. For instance, a concentration model may\napproach zero without ever equaling zero. Distinguish an algebraic limit\nfrom an empirical claim. Always state the model’s domain, assumptions,\nunits, and whether the question concerns a local limit or a long-term\nprediction. Worked example. For \\(C(t)=12e^{-0.3t}\\) mg/L with \\(t\\ge0\\) , \\(C(t)\\) approaches \\(0\\) as \\(t\\to\\infty\\) , but the formula is strictly\npositive for every finite \\(t\\) . Guided practice. For \\(P(t)=100/(1+4e^{-0.2t})\\) , predict the\nlong-run level and explain its meaning in context. Visual investigation. Sketch the relevant function on graph paper or using technology. Mark\nthe inputs and output values from the worked example. Predict the\nbehavior before calculating it; compare estimates from the left and\nright where meaningful. Reflection: Which features are\nestablished by algebra, and which are only suggested by the graph?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "A preview of the derivative", "url": "chapter15.html#a-preview-of-the-derivative", "text": "The derivative at \\(a\\) is defined,\nwhen the finite limit exists, by \\(f'(a)=\\lim_{h\\to0}[f(a+h)-f(a)]/h\\) . In\nthis chapter the emphasis is on constructing and understanding that\nlimit, not memorizing differentiation rules. Later calculus chapters\ndevelop systematic methods for evaluating derivatives and using them to\noptimize models and analyze change. Worked example. For \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , algebra gives \\([f(a+h)-f(a)]/h=2a+h\\) . The limiting value\nis \\(2a\\) , which is the slope of the\ntangent at \\(x=a\\) . Guided practice. Use the limit definition to find\nthe derivative of \\(f(x)=4x+7\\) at an\narbitrary input \\(a\\) ."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Visual\ngallery: secants, tangents, and removable discontinuities", "url": "chapter15.html#visual-gallery-secants-tangents-and-removable-discontinuities", "text": "image image"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory A: secant slopes and tangent estimates", "url": "chapter15.html#computational-laboratory-a-secant-slopes-and-tangent-estimates", "text": "Prediction. For \\(f(x)=x^2+3x\\) at \\(a=2\\) , simplify the difference quotient\nbefore running the code. What value should the slopes approach?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter15.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory A: secant slopes and tangent estimates f <- function (x) x ^ 2 + 3 * x a <- 2 h <- c ( 1 , . 5 , . 1 , . 01 , - . 01 , - . 1 , - . 5 ) result <- data.frame ( h= h, secant= ( f (a + h) - f (a)) / h, exact= 2 * a + h + 3 ) print (result) x <- seq ( - 1 , 4 , length.out= 300 ) plot (x, f (x), type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"f(x)\" , main= \"Secant to tangent\" ) lines (x, f (a) + 7 * (x - a), col= \"red\" , lwd= 2 ) points (a, f (a), pch= 19 ) legend ( \"topleft\" , c ( \"f(x)\" , \"tangent\" ), col= c ( \"black\" , \"red\" ), lty= 1 , bty= \"n\" )"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter15.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt from pathlib import Path Path( \"figures\" ).mkdir(exist_ok = True ) a = 2.0 f = lambda x: x * x + 3 * x h = np.array([ 1 , .5 , .1 , .01 , - .01 , - .1 , - .5 ]) print ( \"h, secant slope, exact simplified slope\" ) for step in h: print ( f\" { step :8.3f} { (f(a + step) - f(a)) / step :12.6f} { 2 * a + step + 3 :12.6f} \" ) x = np.linspace( - 1 , 4 , 300 ) plt.plot(x, f(x), label = \"f(x)=x^2+3x\" ) plt.plot(x, f(a) + 7 * (x - a), label = \"tangent estimate at a=2\" ) plt.scatter([a],[f(a)]) plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"f(x)\" ) ; plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"figures/labA_python.png\" , dpi = 180 ) plt.close() Interpretation. Explain why very tiny \\(h\\) can produce unreliable floating-point\nresults. Compare the output with the exact formula."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory B: one-sided limits and continuity", "url": "chapter15.html#computational-laboratory-b-one-sided-limits-and-continuity", "text": "Prediction. For the piecewise function \\(g(x)=x+1\\) when \\(x<2\\) and \\(g(x)=5-x\\) when \\(x\\ge2\\) , find both one-sided limits at \\(2\\) ."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter15.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory B: one-sided limits and continuity g <- function (x) ifelse (x < 2 ,x + 1 , 5 - x) for (h in c (. 1 ,. 01 ,. 001 )) { cat ( \"h:\" ,h, \"left:\" , g ( 2 - h), \"right:\" , g ( 2 + h), \" \\n \" ) } x <- seq ( 0 , 4 , length.out= 401 ) plot (x, g (x), type= \"l\" , lwd= 2 , xlab= \"x\" , ylab= \"g(x)\" , main= \"Piecewise continuity\" ) points ( 2 , 3 , pch= 19 )"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter15.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt from pathlib import Path Path( \"figures\" ).mkdir(exist_ok = True ) def g(x): return np.where(x < 2 , x + 1 , 5 - x) for h in [ .1 , .01 , .001 ]: print ( f\"h= { h :g} , left= { g( 2 - h) :.6f} , right= { g( 2 + h) :.6f} \" ) x = np.linspace( 0 , 4 , 401 ) plt.plot(x,g(x),label = \"continuous piecewise model\" ) plt.scatter([ 2 ],[ 3 ]) ; plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"g(x)\" ) plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() ; plt.savefig( \"figures/labB_python.png\" ,dpi = 180 ) plt.close() Interpretation. Modify the second branch to \\(6-x\\) and describe what changes at the\njoin."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Computational\nlaboratory C: noisy measurement rates", "url": "chapter15.html#computational-laboratory-c-noisy-measurement-rates", "text": "Prediction. Consider a simulated concentration \\(C(t)=10e^{-0.25t}\\) mg/L. Will a shorter\nmeasurement interval always improve a rate estimate when readings are\nnoisy?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter15.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory C: noisy concentration measurements set.seed ( 15 ) Cfun <- function (t) 10 * exp ( - . 25 * t) t <- seq ( 0 , 8 , by= . 1 ) y <- Cfun (t) + rnorm ( length (t), mean= 0 , sd= . 04 ) dt <- diff (t) rate <- diff (y) / dt truth <- - . 25 * Cfun (t[ - length (t)]) print ( head ( data.frame ( t= t[ - length (t)], observed= rate, model= truth), 12 )) plot (t[ - length (t)],rate, type= \"l\" , xlab= \"time (hours)\" , ylab= \"rate (mg/L/hour)\" , main= \"Noisy rates\" ) lines (t[ - length (t)],truth, col= \"red\" , lwd= 2 ) legend ( \"bottomright\" , c ( \"noisy forward differences\" , \"model rate\" ), col= c ( \"black\" , \"red\" ), lty= 1 , bty= \"n\" )"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter15.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt from pathlib import Path Path( \"figures\" ).mkdir(exist_ok = True ) rng = np.random.default_rng( 15 ) def C(t): return 10 * np.exp( - .25 * t) t = np.linspace( 0 , 8 , 81 ) noise = rng.normal( 0 , .04 , len (t)) y = C(t) + noise dt = t[ 1 ] - t[ 0 ] rate = np.diff(y) / dt truth = - .25 * C(t[: - 1 ]) print ( \"time, observed forward rate, model instantaneous rate\" ) for i in range ( 0 , len (rate), 10 ): print ( round (t[i], 2 ), round (rate[i], 4 ), round (truth[i], 4 )) plt.plot(t[: - 1 ],rate,label = \"noisy forward differences\" ) plt.plot(t[: - 1 ],truth,label = \"model instantaneous rate\" ) plt.xlabel( \"time (hours)\" ) ; plt.ylabel( \"rate (mg/L/hour)\" ) plt.legend() ; plt.tight_layout() ; plt.savefig( \"figures/labC_python.png\" ,dpi = 180 ) plt.close() Interpretation. Compare the noiseless theoretical\nslope with forward differences computed from noisy measurements. Discuss\nunits and sensitivity."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Extended\napplied workshops with worked solutions", "url": "chapter15.html#extended-applied-workshops-with-worked-solutions", "text": ""}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Workshop\n1: Medication concentration", "url": "chapter15.html#workshop-1-medication-concentration", "text": "Problem. A simulated concentration follows \\(C(t)=20/(1+t)\\) mg/L, with \\(t\\) in hours. (a) Find the average change\nfrom \\(t=1\\) to \\(t=3\\) . (b) Simplify \\([C(2+h)-C(2)]/h\\) . (c) Find its limit as \\(h\\to0\\) . (d) Interpret the sign and\nunits. Worked solution. (a) \\((5-10)/2=-2.5\\) mg/L/h. (b) \\(-20/[(3+h)3]\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) and \\(h\\ne-3\\) . (c) \\(-20/9\\) mg/L/h. (d) The model predicts\ndecreasing concentration near hour 2."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Workshop 2:\nSensor calibration", "url": "chapter15.html#workshop-2-sensor-calibration", "text": "Problem. A sensor output is modeled by \\(S(x)=(x^2-16)/(x-4)\\) for \\(x\\ne4\\) . (a) Simplify for permitted inputs.\n(b) Find the limit as \\(x\\to4\\) . (c)\nWhat value would fill the hole continuously? (d) Explain why this does\nnot prove a real sensor operates at \\(x=4\\) . Worked solution. For \\(x\\ne4\\) , \\(S(x)=x+4\\) . The limit is 8. Defining \\(S(4)=8\\) makes the model continuous, but\nthe physical operating range must be validated independently."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Workshop 3: Piecewise\nmonitoring threshold", "url": "chapter15.html#workshop-3-piecewise-monitoring-threshold", "text": "Problem. A monitoring score uses \\(M(t)=2t+3\\) for \\(t<5\\) and \\(M(t)=t+c\\) for \\(t\\ge5\\) . (a) Find \\(c\\) for continuity. (b) Find the average\nrate over \\([4,6]\\) with that value.\n(c) Explain whether a continuous model must have a single tangent slope\nat \\(5\\) . Worked solution. Left limit \\(13\\) , right value \\(5+c\\) , so \\(c=8\\) . Then \\(M(4)=11\\) and \\(M(6)=14\\) , giving \\(1.5\\) score units per time unit. The left\nand right slopes are 2 and 1, so the join is a corner."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Workshop 4:\nQuadratic motion", "url": "chapter15.html#workshop-4-quadratic-motion", "text": "Problem. A simplified position is \\(s(t)=4t^2-3t\\) meters. (a) Compute average\nvelocity on \\([2,2+h]\\) . (b) Find the\nlimiting velocity. (c) Write the tangent line to the position graph at \\(t=2\\) . (d) Explain why this does not\nimply constant velocity. Worked solution. \\([s(2+h)-s(2)]/h=13+4h\\) , so the limit is \\(13\\) m/s. Since \\(s(2)=10\\) , the tangent is \\(s=10+13(t-2)\\) . The quadratic model has\nchanging slope."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Workshop\n5: Measurement resolution", "url": "chapter15.html#workshop-5-measurement-resolution", "text": "Problem. Two noisy readings each differ from the\ntrue reading by at most \\(0.015\\) units. (a) Bound the worst-case error in a forward-difference rate with \\(\\Delta t=0.1\\) s. (b) Repeat with \\(\\Delta t=0.01\\) s. (c) Explain the\ntradeoff between local approximation and noise amplification. Worked solution. Worst-case difference error is \\(0.03\\) units, yielding rate errors at\nmost \\(0.3\\) units/s and \\(3\\) units/s respectively. Smaller spacing\nimproves local approximation for smooth functions but magnifies\nmeasurement error."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Guided\nproblem-solving studio: four methods for estimating a rate", "url": "chapter15.html#guided-problem-solving-studio-four-methods-for-estimating-a-rate", "text": "Consider \\(f(x)=x^2-2x+4\\) at \\(a=3\\) . We will obtain the same local slope\nusing four complementary representations."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Method 1:\na table of nearby values", "url": "chapter15.html#method-1-a-table-of-nearby-values", "text": "Evaluate \\(f(3)=7\\) . For \\(h=0.5,0.1,0.01,-0.01,-0.1,-0.5\\) , compute \\([f(3+h)-7]/h\\) . The results are \\(4.5,4.1,4.01,3.99,3.9,3.5\\) . Notice that\nthe forward and backward estimates bracket \\(4\\) ."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Method 2: exact\nalgebra", "url": "chapter15.html#method-2-exact-algebra", "text": "Expand \\(f(3+h)=(3+h)^2-2(3+h)+4=7+4h+h^2\\) .\nConsequently, for \\(h\\ne0\\) , \\[\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}=\\frac{4h+h^2}{h}=4+h.\\] The expression approaches \\(4\\) as \\(h\\to0\\) . This algebraic result\nexplains the numerical table rather than merely agreeing with it."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Method 3: a\ngraphical secant", "url": "chapter15.html#method-3-a-graphical-secant", "text": "Plot \\((3,7)\\) and \\((3+h,f(3+h))\\) for \\(h=1\\) and \\(h=-1\\) . The two secants have slopes \\(5\\) and \\(3\\) . As the second point moves toward the\nfirst, the secant lines approach the line \\(y-7=4(x-3)\\) . Sketch this limiting line and\ncheck that it passes through \\((3,7)\\) ."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Method 4:\ninterpretation", "url": "chapter15.html#method-4-interpretation", "text": "If \\(x\\) measures hours and \\(f(x)\\) measures a simulated response in\narbitrary units, the instantaneous rate is \\(4\\) response units per hour at hour 3. This\nlocal rate is not the same as a long-run average. Guided\nextension: Repeat all four methods for \\(g(x)=2x^2+x\\) at \\(a=1\\) . Compare which representation is most\nuseful for explanation, estimation, and exact verification."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation: a limit can exist without a function value", "url": "chapter15.html#visual-investigation-a-limit-can-exist-without-a-function-value", "text": "Consider the rule \\[q(x)=\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1},\\qquad x\\ne1.\\] Construct a table at \\(x=0.9,0.99,0.999,1.001,1.01,1.1\\) . Simplify the expression for \\(x\\ne1\\) . Explain why \\(q(1)\\) is not defined even though the\nsimplified expression \\(x+1\\) has a\nvalue at \\(1\\) . Sketch the line \\(y=x+1\\) with\nan open circle at \\((1,2)\\) . Define a new function \\(Q\\) by\nassigning \\(Q(1)=2\\) and keeping \\(Q(x)=q(x)\\) elsewhere. Is \\(Q\\) continuous at \\(1\\) ? Instead assign \\(Q(1)=5\\) . What\nhappens to the limit? What happens to continuity? Takeaway. A limit describes nearby behavior and is\nunchanged by altering the value at the target input alone. Continuity\nrequires that nearby behavior and the assigned value agree."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Visual\ninvestigation: continuity versus smoothness", "url": "chapter15.html#visual-investigation-continuity-versus-smoothness", "text": "Three graphs may all pass through the same point but behave\ndifferently nearby: a smooth parabola, a V-shaped absolute-value graph,\nand a step function. Examine \\(f(x)=x^2\\) , \\(g(x)=|x|\\) , and \\(h(x)=0\\) for \\(x<0\\) and \\(h(x)=1\\) for \\(x\\ge0\\) . Find the left and right limits at \\(x=0\\) for all three functions. Determine which functions are continuous at \\(0\\) . Calculate the left and right difference-quotient limits for \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) at \\(0\\) . Explain why a corner can be continuous without having a single\ntangent slope. Give an applied scenario where a jump is reasonable and another\nwhere a jump would suggest a poor model. Worked synthesis. For \\(f\\) , both one-sided difference-quotient\nlimits are \\(0\\) . For \\(g\\) , they are \\(-1\\) and \\(1\\) . For \\(h\\) , the left and right function limits are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) , so there is no two-sided limit at\nzero. These distinctions prepare students for the formal definition of\ndifferentiability."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Practice\nwith feedback: identify the mathematical mistake", "url": "chapter15.html#practice-with-feedback-identify-the-mathematical-mistake", "text": "A student writes \\(\\lim_{x\\to2}(x^2-4)/(x-2)=0/0=0\\) . Correction: \\(0/0\\) is\nindeterminate; factor to obtain a limit of \\(4\\) . A student cancels \\(h\\) in a\ndifference quotient and concludes the original quotient is defined at \\(h=0\\) . Correction: the simplified formula agrees only for nonzero \\(h\\) ; a limit can still exist. A student claims a graph approaching \\(3\\) from the left and \\(5\\) from the right has limit \\(4\\) . Correction: the\none-sided limits differ, so no two-sided limit exists. A student says a continuous function must have a tangent line at\nevery point. Correction: \\(|x|\\) is continuous at zero but has a\ncorner. A student interprets a rate of \\(-2\\) mg/L/h as a negative concentration. Correction: a negative rate means the concentration is\ndecreasing; it says nothing by itself about the concentration’s\nsign. A student computes a noisy difference quotient using a very small\ntime gap and assumes the result is automatically more accurate. Correction: small denominators amplify measurement\nnoise; resolution and uncertainty matter."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Chapter\nreview: conceptual, algebraic, and applied exercises", "url": "chapter15.html#chapter-review-conceptual-algebraic-and-applied-exercises", "text": "Instructions. Show reasoning, identify excluded\ninputs, and report units in applications. For numerical estimates,\ncompare both sides of the target input. Find the average rate of \\(f(x)=3x+7\\) on \\([1,5]\\) . Find the average rate of \\(f(x)=x^2\\) on \\([-2,1]\\) . Write the secant line to \\(y=x^2+1\\) through inputs \\(1\\) and \\(4\\) . Simplify the difference quotient for \\(f(x)=x^2-4x\\) . Simplify the difference quotient for \\(f(x)=5x^2+2\\) . Simplify the difference quotient for \\(f(x)=1/x\\) at \\(a=3\\) . Find the limiting slope of \\(f(x)=x^2\\) at \\(a=-1\\) . Find the tangent line to \\(f(x)=x^2+2x\\) at \\(a=1\\) . Evaluate \\(\\lim_{x\\to3}(2x^2-x)\\) . Evaluate \\(\\lim_{x\\to-1}(x^2+3)/(x-2)\\) . Evaluate \\(\\lim_{x\\to2}(x^2-4)/(x-2)\\) . Evaluate \\(\\lim_{x\\to-3}(x^2+5x+6)/(x+3)\\) . Evaluate \\(\\lim_{h\\to0}(\\sqrt{16+h}-4)/h\\) . Evaluate \\(\\lim_{x\\to1}(\\sqrt{x+3}-2)/(x-1)\\) . Explain why \\(0/0\\) is not a\nlimit value. Find left and right limits of \\(1/(x-1)\\) at \\(x=1\\) . Find the horizontal asymptote of \\(f(x)=4+2/x\\) . Determine whether \\(f(x)=|x|\\) has a finite tangent slope at \\(0\\) . Choose \\(c\\) for continuity of \\(f(x)=x+2\\) for \\(x<3\\) , \\(f(x)=2x+c\\) for \\(x\\ge3\\) . Give an example of a removable discontinuity. Give an example of a jump discontinuity. Explain why a graph cannot prove a limit exists. Explain the distinction between a limit and a function\nvalue. Find the average velocity of \\(s(t)=2t^2\\) on \\([1,4]\\) . Estimate the instantaneous velocity of \\(s(t)=t^2+5t\\) at \\(t=2\\) . For \\(C(t)=8e^{-0.2t}\\) , find\nthe long-run limit and interpret. Bound the rate error if two measurements have errors at most \\(0.01\\) and \\(\\Delta t=0.02\\) . Compare forward and backward secant slopes for \\(f(x)=x^2\\) at \\(a=2\\) . Explain why continuity does not guarantee\ndifferentiability. Describe the units of \\([f(t+h)-f(t)]/h\\) if \\(f\\) measures mg/L and \\(t\\) measures hours. Construct a piecewise function continuous at \\(x=1\\) with different left and right\nslopes. Compute \\(\\lim_{h\\to0}[(a+h)^2-a^2]/h\\) for arbitrary \\(a\\) . Explain why \\(h=0\\) is excluded\nfrom a difference quotient. For \\(f(x)=1/(x+2)\\) , list its\ndomain and vertical asymptote. Design a table to estimate \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}(\\sin x)/x\\) in\nradians."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter15.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "Explain in words what \\(\\lim_{h\\to0}[f(a+h)-f(a)]/h\\) measures. Compute this limit for \\(f(x)=x^2+4x\\) at \\(a=2\\) . Distinguish a secant line, a tangent line, and a vertical\nasymptote. Explain how one-sided limits diagnose a jump\ndiscontinuity. Describe why numerical evidence must be checked against algebraic\nstructure."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Extended\napplication: Estimating instantaneous velocity", "url": "chapter15.html#extended-application-estimating-instantaneous-velocity", "text": "A cart follows the idealized position law \\(s(t)=2t^2+3t\\) meters. Estimate its\nvelocity at time 2 using a nonzero time increment. The quantities in\nthis investigation are illustrative rather than measured\nobservations."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter15.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[Q(h)=11+2h.\\] Before calculating, identify\nthe input and output, their units, and the interval on which the model\nis being studied. Distinguish the mathematical domain from the practical\ndomain. The numerical study uses inputs from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\) . Example 1: Analytical investigation. Derive \\(Q(h)\\) from the difference quotient for \\(h\\ne0\\) . Find the limit and explain\nthe meaning of the missing input. Worked solution. Expansion gives \\([s(2+h)-s(2)]/h=11+2h\\) . Limit is 11 m/s;\nthe original quotient is undefined at zero."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter15.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Increment (s) Secant velocity (m/s) -1 9 -0.5 10 0 11 0.5 12 1 13 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter15.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Estimating instantaneous velocity # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( - 1 , 1 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 11 + 2 * x # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Increment (s)\" , ylab = \"Secant velocity (m/s)\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter15.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Estimating instantaneous velocity import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( - 1 , 1 , 301 ) y = 11 + 2 * x # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Increment (s)\" , ylabel = \"Secant velocity (m/s)\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter15.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Estimating instantaneous velocity", "url": "chapter15.html#chapter-project-investigating-estimating-instantaneous-velocity", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter15.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits", "section": "Chapter summary", "url": "chapter15.html#chapter-summary", "text": "The transition to calculus begins with a precise question about\nchange. Secant slopes quantify average change; difference quotients\nprepare a limiting process; tangent slopes capture local change when the\nlimit exists. Limits describe behavior near an input, and continuity\ncompares that behavior with the assigned function value. Mathematical\nmodeling adds domain, units, uncertainty, and interpretation to these\nideas."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "url": "chapter16.html#integrated-mathematical-modeling-with-r-and-python", "text": ""}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Opening context:\nmathematics as a connected toolkit", "url": "chapter16.html#opening-context-mathematics-as-a-connected-toolkit", "text": "In the preceding chapters, algebra, functions, geometry,\ntrigonometry, sequences, and limits were developed as distinct topics.\nReal applications seldom announce which chapter they belong to. Instead,\na mathematical modeler identifies a question, selects variables and\nunits, proposes a relationship, estimates parameters, tests assumptions,\nand communicates a decision. This capstone chapter combines the major\ntools of precalculus through reproducible investigations in R and\nPython. All numerical datasets in this chapter are synthetic\nteaching examples , not clinical evidence."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Learning outcomes", "url": "chapter16.html#learning-outcomes", "text": "By the end of this chapter, students should be able to: formulate a\nmeasurable question; distinguish observations from assumptions; select\namong linear, quadratic, exponential, periodic, and piecewise models;\nestimate and interpret parameters; examine residuals and prediction\nerrors; assess model domain and limitations; compare models with\nmeaningful criteria; conduct sensitivity analyses; interpret average\nrates of change and finite differences; reproduce an analysis in R and\nPython; and write a defensible modeling conclusion."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "How to use this\nchapter", "url": "chapter16.html#how-to-use-this-chapter", "text": "Work the mathematics by hand before running software. Every\nlaboratory gives R first , followed by Python in a separate full-width listing. Run the\nstandalone files in the code/ch16/ directory to reproduce\nthe plots in figures/ch16/ . The projects intentionally\nrequire interpretation as well as calculation."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "The mathematical modeling\ncycle", "url": "chapter16.html#the-mathematical-modeling-cycle", "text": "A useful modeling workflow has seven stages: (1) articulate the\nquestion; (2) define inputs, outputs, and units; (3) examine the data or\ngeometric situation; (4) propose a model and identify assumptions; (5)\ndetermine parameters; (6) validate and revise; and (7) communicate\nconclusions and limitations. Modeling is iterative: an implausible\nprediction or patterned residual often sends us back to the assumptions. Worked example. A simulated laboratory instrument\nreports signal \\(S\\) at concentration \\(c\\) . A first model is \\(S(c)=a+bc\\) . Here \\(a\\) is the baseline signal, \\(b\\) is the change in signal per\nconcentration unit, and \\(c\\) must be\nrestricted to the calibrated range. A numerical value of \\(b\\) without its units is incomplete. Guided practice. Identify input, output, parameters,\nunits, and one limitation for a model \\(H(t)=70+5\\sin(2\\pi t/24)\\) , where \\(t\\) is measured in hours and \\(H\\) in beats/minute. Visual investigation. Draw a flow diagram connecting the seven stages. Mark two feedback\narrows: one from residual analysis to model selection and another from\ninterpretation to the original question. Explain why a fitted curve is\nnot automatically a valid scientific explanation."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Units, domains, and\ndimensional consistency", "url": "chapter16.html#units-domains-and-dimensional-consistency", "text": "Units constrain valid equations. A sum requires compatible units; an\nexponential argument must be dimensionless. In \\(C(t)=C_0e^{-kt}\\) , \\(k\\) has units of inverse time. In \\(d(t)=v_0t+\\tfrac12at^2\\) , both terms have\ndistance units. A model domain is the set of inputs for which the\nmathematics is defined and the scientific interpretation is\nmeaningful. Worked example. A concentration is modeled by \\(C(t)=18e^{-0.2t}\\) mg/L with \\(t\\) in hours. Explain why the coefficient \\(0.2\\) must have units h \\(^{-1}\\) . Calculate \\(C(5)=18e^{-1}\\approx6.62\\) mg/L. Guided practice. A model \\(P(t)=400(1.08)^t\\) uses\nyears. What units does \\(t\\) have, and\nwhy is a prediction for \\(t=-100\\) unlikely to be meaningful?"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Tables, graphs,\nand initial pattern recognition", "url": "chapter16.html#tables-graphs-and-initial-pattern-recognition", "text": "A scatterplot is a diagnostic, not a proof. Approximately equal first\ndifferences suggest a linear pattern; nearly constant ratios suggest\ngeometric or exponential behavior; regularly repeating peaks suggest\nperiodic behavior. Real measurements rarely follow any pattern exactly.\nPlotting observations before fitting helps identify outliers,\nthresholds, and plausible ranges. Worked example. For \\(t=0,1,2,3\\) , observations \\(y=4,7,10,13\\) have successive\ndifferences \\(3,3,3\\) . The linear rule \\(y=4+3t\\) reproduces every value. Guided practice. Classify the patterns \\(5,10,20,40\\) and \\(2,6,12,20\\) using ratios and\nfirst differences. Does either pattern uniquely determine a real-world\nmechanism? Visual investigation. Sketch or graph the model from the worked example. Label axes and\nunits, indicate its meaningful domain, and predict the effect of\nchanging one parameter before calculating new values. Write two\nobservations supported by the graph and one claim the graph alone cannot\nestablish."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Linear calibration\nand inverse prediction", "url": "chapter16.html#linear-calibration-and-inverse-prediction", "text": "For a line \\(S=a+bc\\) , two exact\ncalibration points determine \\(b=(S_2-S_1)/(c_2-c_1)\\) and \\(a=S_1-bc_1\\) . If \\(b\\ne0\\) , inverse prediction gives \\(c=(S-a)/b\\) . With noisy data, least squares\nminimizes \\(\\sum_i[S_i-(a+bc_i)]^2\\) .\nPredictions outside the observed concentration interval are\nextrapolations and require extra caution. Worked example. Signals 2.1 and 8.1 correspond to concentrations 0 and 3. Thus \\(b=2\\) signal units/(mg/L) and \\(a=2.1\\) . A signal of 6.1 corresponds to \\((6.1-2.1)/2=2\\) mg/L. Guided practice. Using \\(S=1.5+0.8c\\) , estimate \\(c\\) for \\(S=5.1\\) ; state the units and whether the\ninverse is defined when \\(b=0\\) ."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Quadratic models\nand constrained optimization", "url": "chapter16.html#quadratic-models-and-constrained-optimization", "text": "Quadratic models \\(q(x)=ax^2+bx+c\\) describe curvature and may possess an interior optimum. Completing the\nsquare gives \\(q(x)=a(x+b/(2a))^2+c-b^2/(4a)\\) , so the\nvertex occurs at \\(x=-b/(2a)\\) for \\(a\\ne0\\) . In applications, verify that\nthis input lies in the feasible domain; otherwise a boundary may give\nthe relevant optimum. Worked example. For \\(q(x)=-2x^2+12x+5\\) on \\(0\\le x\\le5\\) , the vertex occurs at \\(x=3\\) and \\(q(3)=23\\) . Both endpoints give smaller\noutputs ( \\(5\\) and \\(15\\) ). Guided practice. For \\(q(x)=-x^2+8x\\) on \\(0\\le x\\le3\\) , find the vertex and the\nmaximum on the actual domain. Visual investigation. Sketch or graph the model from the worked example. Label axes and\nunits, indicate its meaningful domain, and predict the effect of\nchanging one parameter before calculating new values. Write two\nobservations supported by the graph and one claim the graph alone cannot\nestablish."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Exponential\nchange, half-life, and doubling time", "url": "chapter16.html#exponential-change-half-life-and-doubling-time", "text": "For constant proportional change, \\(Y(t)=Y_0e^{kt}\\) . If \\(k<0\\) , the half-life is \\(\\ln2/|k|\\) ; if \\(k>0\\) , doubling time is \\(\\ln2/k\\) . A model based on \\(Y_0r^t\\) can be rewritten with \\(k=\\ln r\\) . Distinguish additive change (a\nfixed increment per time step) from multiplicative change (a fixed\nproportion per time step). Worked example. For \\(C(t)=20e^{-0.35t}\\) , the\nhalf-life is \\(\\ln2/0.35\\approx1.98\\) hours. This is a mathematical decay model, not a recommendation for\nmedication timing. Guided practice. A simulated quantity doubles every 6 days. Find \\(k\\) in \\(Y(t)=Y_0e^{kt}\\) and calculate \\(Y(12)/Y_0\\) ."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Periodic models and\nphase interpretation", "url": "chapter16.html#periodic-models-and-phase-interpretation", "text": "A periodic model \\(y(t)=M+A\\sin(\\omega(t-\\tau))\\) has midline \\(M\\) , amplitude \\(|A|\\) , angular frequency \\(|\\omega|\\) , period \\(T=2\\pi/|\\omega|\\) , and horizontal shift \\(\\tau\\) when \\(A>0\\) and \\(\\omega>0\\) . Interpret all parameters in\ncontext. A period inferred from short or irregular data may be\nunstable. Worked example. For \\(y(t)=70+8\\sin(2\\pi(t-3)/24)\\) ,\nthe midline is 70, amplitude is 8, and period is 24 hours. At \\(t=3\\) , the model crosses its midline\nupward. Guided practice. Write a cosine model with midline 12, amplitude 4, period 8 hours,\nand a maximum at \\(t=0\\) . Visual investigation. Sketch or graph the model from the worked example. Label axes and\nunits, indicate its meaningful domain, and predict the effect of\nchanging one parameter before calculating new values. Write two\nobservations supported by the graph and one claim the graph alone cannot\nestablish."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Piecewise models and\nthresholds", "url": "chapter16.html#piecewise-models-and-thresholds", "text": "Piecewise functions represent systems whose rules change at\nthresholds. A well-constructed model specifies each interval, endpoint\nconvention, and whether the outputs match at transitions.\nDiscontinuities may represent actual jumps or artifacts of a simplified\ndescription. A continuous piecewise function need not have matching\nslopes at a threshold. Worked example. Let \\(f(t)=2t+1\\) for \\(0\\le t<3\\) and \\(f(t)=t+4\\) for \\(t\\ge3\\) . Both rules approach 7 at \\(t=3\\) , so the model is continuous\nthere. Guided practice. Find \\(a\\) so \\(g(t)=3t+2\\) for \\(t<2\\) and \\(g(t)=at+4\\) for \\(t\\ge2\\) is continuous at \\(2\\) ."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Geometric and vector models", "url": "chapter16.html#geometric-and-vector-models", "text": "A displacement vector \\(\\mathbf v=\\langle\nv_x,v_y\\rangle\\) has magnitude \\(\\|\\mathbf v\\|=\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}\\) . Its\ndirection may be represented by an angle using \\(\\operatorname{atan2}(v_y,v_x)\\) , which\naccounts for the quadrant. The dot product \\(\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v\\) describes\nalignment; the projection of \\(\\mathbf\nu\\) onto nonzero \\(\\mathbf v\\) is \\((\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v/\\|\\mathbf\nv\\|^2)\\mathbf v\\) . Worked example. A displacement of \\(\\langle3,4\\rangle\\) km has length 5 km. The\nunit direction vector is \\(\\langle3/5,4/5\\rangle\\) . Guided practice. A sensor moves 6 m east and 8 m north. Find its net displacement\nmagnitude and direction north of east. Visual investigation. Sketch or graph the model from the worked example. Label axes and\nunits, indicate its meaningful domain, and predict the effect of\nchanging one parameter before calculating new values. Write two\nobservations supported by the graph and one claim the graph alone cannot\nestablish."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Parametric trajectories\nand elimination", "url": "chapter16.html#parametric-trajectories-and-elimination", "text": "Parametric equations \\(x=x(t)\\) and \\(y=y(t)\\) describe a curve and its\ntraversal. Eliminating \\(t\\) can reveal\nthe Cartesian curve but may lose direction or parameter restrictions.\nFor \\(x(t)=r\\cos t\\) , \\(y(t)=r\\sin t\\) , the path is a circle; the\nparameter determines its direction and speed of traversal. Worked example. For \\(x(t)=2t\\) and \\(y(t)=t^2\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\) , eliminating \\(t\\) gives \\(y=x^2/4\\) with \\(x\\ge0\\) . The restriction matters. Guided practice. Eliminate \\(t\\) from \\(x(t)=3\\cos t\\) , \\(y(t)=2\\sin t\\) and describe the resulting\ncurve."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Sequences,\naccumulation, and discrete change", "url": "chapter16.html#sequences-accumulation-and-discrete-change", "text": "A discrete process can be represented by a recurrence \\(x_{n+1}=rx_n+b\\) . The increment \\(x_{n+1}-x_n\\) is a finite difference.\nCumulative totals are sums, and a geometric accumulation has a closed\nform when \\(r\\ne1\\) . These ideas\nforeshadow integration and differential equations, but the discrete\nmodel is not itself a continuous-time differential equation. Worked example. For \\(x_{n+1}=0.8x_n+5\\) , an\nequilibrium satisfies \\(x=0.8x+5\\) ,\ngiving \\(x=25\\) . Starting from \\(x_0=0\\) , the first three terms are \\(0,5,9\\) . Guided practice. For \\(x_{n+1}=0.9x_n+2\\) , calculate\nthe equilibrium and the first four values when \\(x_0=10\\) . Visual investigation. Sketch or graph the model from the worked example. Label axes and\nunits, indicate its meaningful domain, and predict the effect of\nchanging one parameter before calculating new values. Write two\nobservations supported by the graph and one claim the graph alone cannot\nestablish."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Residuals and measures of\nfit", "url": "chapter16.html#residuals-and-measures-of-fit", "text": "A residual is \\(e_i=y_i-\\widehat\ny_i\\) . Plot residuals against time or fitted values to detect\ncurvature, systematic drift, changing variability, or outliers. Mean\nabsolute error is \\(\\mathrm{MAE}=n^{-1}\\sum_i|e_i|\\) and root\nmean squared error is \\(\\mathrm{RMSE}=\\sqrt{n^{-1}\\sum_i e_i^2}\\) .\nRMSE weights large errors more heavily. Neither metric alone establishes\ncausal validity. Worked example. For observations \\(4,6,8\\) and\npredictions \\(5,5,8\\) , residuals are \\(-1,1,0\\) , MAE is \\(2/3\\) , and RMSE is \\(\\sqrt{2/3}\\approx0.816\\) . Guided practice. Compute MAE and RMSE for residuals \\(2,-2,1,-1\\) . Which is larger, and why?"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Training, validation, and\noverfitting", "url": "chapter16.html#training-validation-and-overfitting", "text": "A model can match observed data closely yet predict new observations\npoorly. Use separate fitting and validation data where possible. Compare\nerrors on held-out observations, not just on the points used to estimate\nparameters. More parameters increase flexibility but may capture random\nnoise rather than a stable pattern. For small synthetic datasets,\ndescribe the split and its limitations explicitly. Worked example. A degree-4 polynomial passes through five calibration observations\nexactly. Its training residuals are zero, but this does not imply\naccurate prediction between or beyond those points. Guided practice. Explain why a model with lower training RMSE might still be less\nsuitable than a simpler model for future predictions. Visual investigation. Sketch or graph the model from the worked example. Label axes and\nunits, indicate its meaningful domain, and predict the effect of\nchanging one parameter before calculating new values. Write two\nobservations supported by the graph and one claim the graph alone cannot\nestablish."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Sensitivity and uncertainty", "url": "chapter16.html#sensitivity-and-uncertainty", "text": "Sensitivity measures how a prediction changes when inputs or\nparameters vary. For \\(Y=ab\\) , small\nrelative perturbations approximately add: \\(\\Delta Y/Y\\approx\\Delta a/a+\\Delta b/b\\) when changes are small. A scenario analysis recalculates predictions\nunder plausible parameter values; it is not automatically a statistical\nconfidence interval. State whether uncertainty concerns measurement,\nparameters, or model structure. Worked example. For \\(C(t)=20e^{-kt}\\) at \\(t=4\\) , predictions are approximately \\(9.0\\) when \\(k=0.20\\) and \\(7.4\\) when \\(k=0.25\\) . The predicted concentration is\nsensitive to the decay constant. Guided practice. Calculate \\(C(5)\\) for \\(C(t)=10e^{-kt}\\) at \\(k=0.1,0.15,0.2\\) . Explain the trend."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Average rates and the\ncalculus bridge", "url": "chapter16.html#average-rates-and-the-calculus-bridge", "text": "The average rate of change on \\([a,b]\\) is \\([f(b)-f(a)]/(b-a)\\) . The difference\nquotient \\([f(a+h)-f(a)]/h\\) approaches\nthe instantaneous rate when its limit exists. Numerical secants connect\ndirectly to derivative ideas, while finite sums connect to accumulation.\nA smooth fitted curve does not make numerical derivatives immune to\nmeasurement noise. Worked example. For \\(f(t)=t^2+2t\\) , \\([f(a+h)-f(a)]/h=2a+h+2\\) . As \\(h\\to0\\) , the limiting rate is \\(2a+2\\) . At \\(a=3\\) , it equals 8 output units per input\nunit. Guided practice. Find the difference quotient for \\(f(t)=3t^2-t\\) and use it to estimate the\ninstantaneous rate at \\(t=2\\) . Visual investigation. Sketch or graph the model from the worked example. Label axes and\nunits, indicate its meaningful domain, and predict the effect of\nchanging one parameter before calculating new values. Write two\nobservations supported by the graph and one claim the graph alone cannot\nestablish."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Communicating a mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter16.html#communicating-a-mathematical-model", "text": "A responsible modeling report contains the question, data provenance,\nvariables and units, model equation, parameter interpretation, fit\ndiagnostics, range of applicability, limitations, and a conclusion\nanswering the original question. Distinguish measured data, modeled\npredictions, and hypothetical scenarios. Avoid causal language when the\nanalysis supports only association or pattern description. Worked example. A report should not merely say “the model fits well.” Instead: “On\nthe six simulated calibration observations, the fitted linear model has\nRMSE 0.12 signal units; predictions should be limited to the observed\nconcentration range until independently validated. Guided practice. Write a three-sentence conclusion for a periodic model that predicts\na 24-hour cycle but has increasing residuals toward the end of the\nobservation window."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "A reproducible analysis\nworkflow", "url": "chapter16.html#a-reproducible-analysis-workflow", "text": "Each laboratory follows the same protocol: state a question, inspect\nobservations, derive or select a model, execute code, interpret outputs,\nexamine limitations, and record an answer in mathematical language. The\ncode files provided with this chapter are self-contained and use only\nbase R or common Python scientific packages (NumPy and Matplotlib)."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Computational\nLaboratory A: Calibration and residual diagnostics", "url": "chapter16.html#computational-laboratory-a-calibration-and-residual-diagnostics", "text": "Question. Can a linear model describe simulated\nsignal measurements across a calibration range? Use concentrations \\(c=(0,1,2,3,4,5)\\) mg/L and signals \\(S=(1.2,3.0,5.1,6.9,9.2,10.8)\\) arbitrary\nunits. Estimate the least-squares slope and intercept; plot observations\nand fitted line; calculate residuals and RMSE. Interpret the intercept\nand slope, and explain why predictions beyond 5 mg/L require\ncaution."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter16.html#r-code", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory A: linear calibration and residuals concentration <- 0 : 5 signal <- c ( 1.2 , 3.0 , 5.1 , 6.9 , 9.2 , 10.8 ) fit <- lm (signal ~ concentration) prediction <- predict (fit) residual <- signal - prediction rmse <- sqrt ( mean (residual ^ 2 )) print ( coef (fit)); print (rmse) par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 2 )) plot (concentration, signal, xlab= \"Concentration (mg/L)\" , ylab= \"Signal (units)\" ) abline (fit, col= \"blue\" ) plot (concentration, residual, xlab= \"Concentration (mg/L)\" , ylab= \"Residual\" ) abline ( h= 0 , lty= 2 ) par ( mfrow= c ( 1 , 1 ))"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter16.html#python-code", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt c = np.arange( 6 , dtype = float ) y = np.array([ 1.2 , 3.0 , 5.1 , 6.9 , 9.2 , 10.8 ]) b, a = np.polyfit(c,y, 1 ) pred = a + b * c res = y - pred rmse = np.sqrt(np.mean(res ** 2 )) print ( f\"Intercept= { a :.4f} ; slope= { b :.4f} ; RMSE= { rmse :.4f} \" ) fig, axes = plt.subplots( 1 , 2 ,figsize = ( 9 , 3.5 )) axes[ 0 ].scatter(c,y,label = \"Observations\" ) axes[ 0 ].plot(c,pred,label = \"Linear fit\" ) axes[ 0 ]. set (xlabel = \"Concentration (mg/L)\" ,ylabel = \"Signal (units)\" ) axes[ 0 ].legend() axes[ 1 ].scatter(c,res) axes[ 1 ].axhline( 0 ,linestyle = \"--\" ,color = \"gray\" ) axes[ 1 ]. set (xlabel = \"Concentration (mg/L)\" ,ylabel = \"Residual (units)\" ) fig.tight_layout() fig.savefig( \"figures/labA_python.png\" ,dpi = 170 ) plt.close(fig) Simulated calibration observations and\nfitted line, with residual diagnostics (Python output). Interpretation prompts. (1) What is the unit of the\nfitted slope? (2) Is the intercept scientifically meaningful? (3) Are\nresiduals distributed without a clear pattern? (4) How would one\nunusually large residual change RMSE?"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Computational\nLaboratory B: Exponential versus linear models", "url": "chapter16.html#computational-laboratory-b-exponential-versus-linear-models", "text": "Question. Which simple model better describes a\nsimulated decreasing measurement? At times \\(t=(0,1,2,3,4,5)\\) hours, let \\(y=(19.8,15.0,11.7,8.7,6.8,5.2)\\) units. Fit\na linear model \\(y=a+bt\\) and an\nexponential model \\(y=Ae^{kt}\\) using a\nleast-squares line on \\(\\log y\\) .\nCompare predictions and RMSE on the original measurement scale.\nExplain why this is a descriptive comparison rather than a clinical\nrecommendation."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter16.html#r-code-1", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory B: compare linear and exponential models time <- 0 : 5 y <- c ( 19.8 , 15.0 , 11.7 , 8.7 , 6.8 , 5.2 ) linear_fit <- lm (y ~ time) exp_fit <- lm ( log (y) ~ time) linear_pred <- predict (linear_fit) exp_pred <- exp ( predict (exp_fit)) rmse <- function (actual, predicted) sqrt ( mean ((actual - predicted) ^ 2 )) print ( c ( linear= rmse (y,linear_pred), exponential= rmse (y,exp_pred))) plot (time,y, xlab= \"Time (hours)\" , ylab= \"Measurement (units)\" ) lines (time,linear_pred, col= \"blue\" ) lines (time,exp_pred, col= \"red\" ) legend ( \"topright\" , legend= c ( \"Linear\" , \"Exponential\" ), col= c ( \"blue\" , \"red\" ), lty= 1 )"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter16.html#python-code-1", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt t = np.arange( 6 ,dtype = float ) y = np.array([ 19.8 , 15.0 , 11.7 , 8.7 , 6.8 , 5.2 ]) b, a = np.polyfit(t,y, 1 ) k, logA = np.polyfit(t,np.log(y), 1 ) linear = a + b * t expo = np.exp(logA + k * t) rmse = lambda z: np.sqrt(np.mean((y - z) ** 2 )) print ( f\"Linear RMSE= { rmse(linear) :.4f} \" ) print ( f\"Exponential A= { np . exp(logA) :.4f} , k= { k :.4f} , RMSE= { rmse(expo) :.4f} \" ) plt.figure(figsize = ( 7 , 4 )) plt.scatter(t,y,label = \"Observations\" ) plt.plot(t,linear,label = \"Linear fit\" ) plt.plot(t,expo,label = \"Exponential fit\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Time (hours)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Measurement (units)\" ) plt.legend() plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"figures/labB_python.png\" ,dpi = 170 ) plt.close() Simulated decreasing measurements with\nlinear and exponential fits. Interpretation prompts. (1) Why must all \\(y\\) values be positive to fit the\nlog-transformed model? (2) Why compare RMSE on the original scale? (3)\nWhat changes if the final observation is removed? (4) How might\nmeasurement error alter the preferred model?"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Computational\nLaboratory C: Periodic signals and sensitivity", "url": "chapter16.html#computational-laboratory-c-periodic-signals-and-sensitivity", "text": "Question. A simulated sensor follows \\(y(t)=70+8\\sin(2\\pi(t-3)/24)\\) with small\ndeterministic measurement perturbations. Plot the observations and the\nproposed curve, calculate residuals, and compare predicted values when\nthe period is 22, 24, or 26 hours. Discuss why an assumed period should\nbe validated using observations over multiple cycles."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "R code", "url": "chapter16.html#r-code-2", "text": "R Copy code # Laboratory C: periodic model sensitivity time <- seq ( 0 , 48 , by= 3 ) y <- 70 + 8 * sin ( 2 * pi * (time -3 ) / 24 ) + 0.7 * cos ( 0.8 * time) nominal <- 70 + 8 * sin ( 2 * pi * (time -3 ) / 24 ) print ( sqrt ( mean ((y - nominal) ^ 2 ))) plot (time,y, xlab= \"Time (hours)\" , ylab= \"Signal (units)\" , ylim= c ( 60 , 80 )) dense <- seq ( 0 , 48 , length.out= 400 ) for (period in c ( 22 , 24 , 26 )) { lines (dense, 70 + 8 * sin ( 2 * pi * (dense -3 ) / period), lty= which ( c ( 22 , 24 , 26 ) == period)) } legend ( \"topright\" , legend= c ( \"22 h\" , \"24 h\" , \"26 h\" ), lty= 1 : 3 )"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Python code", "url": "chapter16.html#python-code-2", "text": "Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt t = np.arange( 0 , 49 , 3 ,dtype = float ) y = 70 + 8 * np.sin( 2 * np.pi * (t - 3 ) / 24 ) + 0.7 * np.cos( 0.8 * t) plt.figure(figsize = ( 7 , 4 )) plt.scatter(t,y,label = \"Synthetic observations\" ) for period in [ 22 , 24 , 26 ]: dense = np.linspace( 0 , 48 , 400 ) model = 70 + 8 * np.sin( 2 * np.pi * (dense - 3 ) / period) plt.plot(dense,model,label = f\"Period { period } h\" ) res = y - ( 70 + 8 * np.sin( 2 * np.pi * (t - 3 ) / 24 )) print ( f\"Nominal-period RMSE= { np . sqrt(np.mean(res ** 2 )) :.4f} \" ) plt.xlabel( \"Time (hours)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Signal (units)\" ) plt.legend() plt.tight_layout() plt.savefig( \"figures/labC_python.png\" ,dpi = 170 ) plt.close() Simulated periodic sensor data and\nsensitivity to the assumed period. Interpretation prompts. (1) State the amplitude,\nmidline, and nominal period. (2) Identify where the curves differ most.\n(3) Explain the difference between deterministic perturbations and\nrandom sampling uncertainty."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Integrated applied workshops", "url": "chapter16.html#integrated-applied-workshops", "text": ""}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Project\n1: Laboratory calibration and inverse estimation", "url": "chapter16.html#project-1-laboratory-calibration-and-inverse-estimation", "text": "A simulated instrument records concentration (mg/L) and signal\n(units): \\((0,1.0),(1,2.9),(2,5.2),(3,6.8),(4,9.1),(5,11.0)\\) .\n(a) Plot the points. (b) Derive a two-point calibration equation using\nthe first and last observations. (c) Fit a least-squares line using\nsoftware. (d) Estimate concentration when the signal is 7.5. (e)\nCalculate residuals and RMSE for both models. (f) Explain why the\ninverse estimate should be restricted to the calibrated range. (g) Refit\nafter changing the final signal from 11.0 to 12.0 and compare the\ninverse estimate. Guided reasoning. First compute the two-point slope \\((11-1)/5=2\\) and intercept 1; then\ncalculate the two-point inverse \\((7.5-1)/2=3.25\\) mg/L. Next use software\nfor the least-squares fit and explain any difference. Extension. Explain why a calibration equation does\nnot establish that the instrument is accurate without independent\nreference measurements."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Project 2: Repeated\nmeasurements and decay", "url": "chapter16.html#project-2-repeated-measurements-and-decay", "text": "A hypothetical concentration is modeled as \\(C(t)=24e^{-0.28t}\\) mg/L. (a) Calculate \\(C(0),C(2),C(4),C(6)\\) . (b) Find the\nhalf-life algebraically. (c) Find when the model first reaches 5 mg/L.\n(d) Compute average rates on \\([0,2]\\) and \\([4,6]\\) . (e) Evaluate predictions\nfor \\(k=0.25,0.28,0.31\\) at \\(t=6\\) . (f) Explain why the model cannot by\nitself determine safe dosing or clinical decisions. Guided reasoning. Solving \\(24e^{-0.28t}=5\\) gives \\(t=\\ln(24/5)/0.28\\approx5.60\\) hours. The\nhalf-life is \\(\\ln2/0.28\\approx2.48\\) hours. Extension. Contrast a continuous exponential model\nwith a recurrence \\(C_{n+1}=rC_n\\) measured once per hour."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Project\n3: A periodic environmental or physiological signal", "url": "chapter16.html#project-3-a-periodic-environmental-or-physiological-signal", "text": "Consider \\(H(t)=68+7\\cos(2\\pi(t-2)/24)\\) , where \\(t\\) is hours. (a) Identify midline,\namplitude, period, and phase shift. (b) Determine the maximum and\nminimum predicted values and when they occur in one day. (c) Solve \\(H(t)=68\\) on \\(0\\le t<24\\) . (d) Plot the function and\nlabel extrema. (e) Compare it with \\(68+7\\cos(2\\pi(t-2)/22)\\) . (f) Discuss why a\nsingle-day observation may be insufficient to establish a 24-hour\nrhythm. Guided reasoning. The maximum is 75 at \\(t=2\\) ; the minimum is 61 at \\(t=14\\) . Midline crossings occur when the\ncosine is zero. Extension. Propose a residual diagnostic that could\nreveal an incorrect period."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Project 4:\nGeometry, navigation, and measurement", "url": "chapter16.html#project-4-geometry-navigation-and-measurement", "text": "A mobile sensor moves from \\((0,0)\\) to \\((6,8)\\) meters and then to \\((10,5)\\) meters. (a) Find both displacement\nvectors. (b) Find the total path length and net displacement magnitude.\n(c) Determine the direction of the net displacement. (d) Give a\nparametric equation for each straight segment. (e) Explain why path\nlength and displacement differ. (f) If each recorded coordinate can vary\nby \\(\\pm0.2\\) m, examine at least four\nendpoint scenarios. Guided reasoning. The first segment has length 10 m\nand the second has length 5 m; total path length is 15 m. Net\ndisplacement is \\(\\langle10,5\\rangle\\) m, with magnitude \\(\\sqrt{125}\\approx11.18\\) m. Extension. Sketch a path with the same endpoints but\na different total distance."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Project\n5: Discrete accumulation and calculus readiness", "url": "chapter16.html#project-5-discrete-accumulation-and-calculus-readiness", "text": "A simplified inventory follows \\(I_{n+1}=0.85I_n+12\\) with \\(I_0=20\\) . (a) Compute \\(I_1\\) through \\(I_6\\) . (b) Find the equilibrium. (c)\nExplain whether the values move toward or away from equilibrium. (d)\nDerive \\(I_n=80+(20-80)(0.85)^n\\) . (e)\nCalculate the accumulated total \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{5}I_n\\) . (f) Compare the finite\ndifferences \\(I_{n+1}-I_n\\) with the\nderivative of the continuous interpolation \\(I(t)=80-60(0.85)^t\\) . Guided reasoning. At equilibrium, \\(I=0.85I+12\\) , so \\(I=80\\) . Subtract 80 from the recurrence to\nreveal a geometric pattern. Extension. Explain why a discrete difference is not\nidentical to an instantaneous derivative."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Problem-solving clinic:\nchoosing a model", "url": "chapter16.html#problem-solving-clinic-choosing-a-model", "text": "Scenario A. Data show nearly equal increases over\nequal time intervals. Begin with a linear model, then inspect residuals\nfor curvature. Scenario B. Positive data have nearly constant\nratios. Begin with an exponential model; compare errors on the original\nscale. Scenario C. Measurements repeat with approximately\nregular peaks. Explore a periodic model and check whether the estimated\nperiod remains stable across cycles. Scenario D. The process changes rules after a\nthreshold. Use a piecewise model and explicitly check continuity and\nunits at the transition. Scenario E. Position is recorded as horizontal and\nvertical coordinates. Use vectors or parametric equations; distinguish\ntrajectory from displacement. Guided studio. For each scenario, sketch a plausible\ngraph, write a candidate equation, identify at least two parameters, and\nname one diagnostic that might reveal model failure."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Chapter review exercises", "url": "chapter16.html#chapter-review-exercises", "text": "Foundations and representation. Identify the input, output, and units for \\(S(c)=1.2+2.1c\\) . Explain why \\(e^{-kt}\\) requires\na dimensionless exponent. Classify \\(3,7,11,15,\\ldots\\) as\nan arithmetic or geometric sequence. Classify \\(2,6,18,54,\\ldots\\) and write an explicit rule. Find the slope and intercept through \\((1,4)\\) and \\((5,12)\\) . Invert \\(y=3+0.5x\\) and state\nany parameter restriction. Find the vertex of \\(q(x)=-x^2+6x+2\\) . Find the maximum of that quadratic on \\(0\\le x\\le2\\) . Calculate the half-life for \\(C(t)=10e^{-0.4t}\\) . Find the doubling time for \\(P(t)=100e^{0.12t}\\) . Identify amplitude and period of \\(4+3\\sin(\\pi t/6)\\) . Construct a cosine function with amplitude 5 and period\n10. Find \\(a\\) to make a two-piece\nfunction \\(2x+1\\) for \\(x<3\\) and \\(ax-2\\) for \\(x\\ge3\\) continuous. Compute \\(\\|\\langle-5,12\\rangle\\|\\) . Compute \\(\\langle2,3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle4,-1\\rangle\\) . Eliminate \\(t\\) from \\(x=2t\\) , \\(y=3t+1\\) . Find the equilibrium of \\(x_{n+1}=0.75x_n+10\\) . Calculate the first four values when \\(x_0=0\\) for the preceding\nrecurrence. Compute MAE for residuals \\(-2,0,1,3\\) . Compute RMSE for the same residuals. Explain why low training error does not guarantee predictive\naccuracy. Describe two limitations of extrapolating a calibration\ncurve. Calculate the average rate of change of \\(f(t)=t^2\\) on \\([1,4]\\) . Simplify the difference quotient for \\(f(t)=2t^2+t\\) . Find the limiting rate at \\(t=3\\) from the preceding quotient. Explain why a periodic model based on one cycle may be\nunreliable. Compare the interpretations of a slope and an exponential decay\nconstant. Distinguish a residual from a measurement error. Explain why scenario ranges are not necessarily confidence\nintervals. Explain the difference between total path length and net\ndisplacement. For \\(y=20e^{-0.2t}\\) , solve for\nthe time at which \\(y=8\\) . Find \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{4}2(1.5)^n\\) . Give one example of a physical domain restriction not required by\nalgebra. Propose a diagnostic for an incorrectly specified quadratic\nmodel. Write a short model conclusion that reports a parameter, units,\nand a limitation. Explain why a more complicated polynomial can fit training data\nyet predict poorly. Calculate the magnitude of \\(\\langle6,8\\rangle\\) and its unit\ndirection. Find the mean of the outputs \\(4,7,9,12\\) and describe its units. For \\(f(t)=|t-2|\\) , explain why\nthe instantaneous rate fails at \\(t=2\\) . Outline a seven-step modeling workflow for a new\ndataset."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Calculus-readiness\ncheckpoint", "url": "chapter16.html#calculus-readiness-checkpoint", "text": "For \\(f(x)=x^2+4x\\) , simplify \\([f(a+h)-f(a)]/h\\) for \\(h\\ne0\\) , then determine its limit as \\(h\\to0\\) . For \\(C(t)=12e^{-0.3t}\\) ,\ncompare the average rate over \\([0,2]\\) with the limiting instantaneous rate at \\(t=0\\) . Explain the difference in\nwords. Explain how a sum of small rectangular contributions can\napproximate total accumulation. Why does using narrower intervals often\nimprove an approximation for a smooth function? A parametric trajectory is \\(x(t)=t\\) , \\(y(t)=t^2\\) . Estimate the tangent slope at \\(t=2\\) using secants. Compare your\nresult with the Cartesian curve. Identify which ideas in this chapter prepare you for derivatives,\ndefinite integrals, and multivariable geometry."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Extended\napplication: Comparing two growth models", "url": "chapter16.html#extended-application-comparing-two-growth-models", "text": "Two candidate models predict a synthetic culture count: a linear\nmodel \\(100+20t\\) and an exponential\nmodel \\(100(1.2)^t\\) . The quantities in\nthis investigation are illustrative rather than measured\nobservations."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Formulating the mathematical\nmodel", "url": "chapter16.html#formulating-the-mathematical-model", "text": "The proposed relationship is \\[D(t)=100(1.2)^t-(100+20t).\\] Before\ncalculating, identify the input and output, their units, and the\ninterval on which the model is being studied. Distinguish the\nmathematical domain from the practical domain. The numerical study uses\ninputs from \\(0\\) to \\(10\\) . Example 1: Analytical investigation. Compare\npredictions at times 0, 1 and 5. Explain why agreement at two times does\nnot establish equivalence or validate either model. Worked solution. Differences at 0 and 1 are zero; at\n5 the exponential predicts 248.832 versus 200, a difference of 48.832.\nIndependent observations are needed."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Connecting\nnumerical and graphical representations", "url": "chapter16.html#connecting-numerical-and-graphical-representations", "text": "Selected predictions from the illustrative model. Values are\nrounded for display. Time (h) Prediction difference 0 0 2.5 7.744 5 48.83 7.5 142.5 10 319.2 Model predictions over the stated\ninvestigation interval. The graph represents a mathematical model, not\nobserved data. Interpretation. Compare the tabulated values with\nthe graph. Identify the intervals over which the output increases or\ndecreases, and distinguish a sampled extremum from an exact analytical\nextremum. Explain whether interpolation and extrapolation have the same\njustification in this setting."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "R implementation", "url": "chapter16.html#r-implementation", "text": "The following standalone script defines the model, generates\npredictions, and draws a labeled graph. Read the formula before running\nthe code. The sampled range is a numerical diagnostic, not a proof of\nthe exact range. R Copy code # Applied investigation: Comparing two growth models # 1. Define the input domain and model x <- seq ( 0 , 10 , length.out = 301 ) y <- 100 * 1.2 ^ x - ( 100 + 20 * x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx <- unique ( round ( seq ( 1 , length (x), length.out = 5 ))) print ( data.frame ( input = x[idx], output = y[idx])) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes plot (x, y, type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , col = \"#176B87\" , xlab = \"Time (h)\" , ylab = \"Prediction difference\" ) grid () # 4. Check the sampled output range print ( range (y))"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Python implementation", "url": "chapter16.html#python-implementation", "text": "Repeat the same investigation using NumPy arrays and Matplotlib.\nCompare the printed predictions with the R output, allowing for\nrounding. Python Copy code # Applied investigation: Comparing two growth models import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # 1. Define the input domain and model x = np.linspace( 0 , 10 , 301 ) y = 100 * 1.2 ** x - ( 100 + 20 * x) # 2. Inspect numerical predictions idx = np.unique(np.linspace( 0 , len (x) - 1 , 5 ). round ().astype( int )) print (np.column_stack((x[idx], y[idx]))) # 3. Visualize the model with labeled axes fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize = ( 7 , 3.6 )) ax.plot(x, y, \"-\" , color = \"#176B87\" , linewidth = 2 ) ax. set (xlabel = \"Time (h)\" , ylabel = \"Prediction difference\" ) ax.grid(alpha = 0.22 ) ax.spines[[ \"top\" , \"right\" ]].set_visible( False ) fig.tight_layout() plt.show() # 4. Check the sampled output range print (np. min (y), np. max (y))"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Guided practice: checking\nthe model", "url": "chapter16.html#guided-practice-checking-the-model", "text": "Calculate the outputs at the two endpoints by hand. Compare them\nwith the numerical table. Select an interior input not listed in the table. Predict the\noutput before evaluating it in either language. Explain the meaning of the difference between the two endpoint\noutputs, including units. If appropriate, calculate the average rate of\nchange. Identify a physical assumption of the model and describe a\ncircumstance in which it would fail."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Chapter\nproject: investigating Comparing two growth models", "url": "chapter16.html#chapter-project-investigating-comparing-two-growth-models", "text": "Prepare a short technical report for a reader who has not studied the\nmodel. Use the preceding case as a starting point, but develop your own\ninvestigation rather than merely reproducing the worked example. State a quantitative question that can be answered using the\nmodel. Define all variables, units, and restrictions. Modify one meaningful numerical parameter in the formula. Explain\nwhat the change represents in the original context. Derive the revised\nformula and predict the direction of change before computing. Compare the original and revised models at five common admissible\ninputs. Present a labeled table and explain the largest difference in\nthe table. Produce a graph containing both models on the same axes. Use a\nlegend and distinguish the curves with line styles or markers. Explain\nthe shape and any intersections. Solve your quantitative question algebraically where possible.\nVerify the result using R and Python; report any differences due to\nnumerical precision. Discuss at least two limitations. Describe what measurements\nwould be needed to assess the model in practice. Do not present\nsimulated predictions as empirical evidence."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Project\ndeliverables and assessment", "url": "chapter16.html#project-deliverables-and-assessment", "text": "Submit a two- to three-page report, the two runnable scripts, and the\nlabeled figure. The report should stand on its own without requiring the\nreader to inspect the code. Criterion Points Mathematical formulation and domain 20 Analytical reasoning and verification 25 Reproducible R and Python\ncomputations 20 Graph and table quality 15 Interpretation and limitations 20 Total 100"}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python", "section": "Chapter summary", "url": "chapter16.html#chapter-summary", "text": "A defensible model connects mathematics with a clearly stated\nquestion. Algebra establishes relationships, graphs expose structure,\ncomputation estimates parameters, residuals challenge assumptions, and\nsensitivity analysis tests stability. The transition to calculus arises\nnaturally when average changes become instantaneous rates and discrete\nsums approximate continuous accumulation. The most important habit is\nnot choosing the most elaborate formula: it is explaining what the model\nmeans, where it works, and where it may fail."}, {"title": "Preface", "section": "Preface", "url": "index.html#preface", "text": "This volume prepares students for calculus by developing algebraic\nfluency, function sense, trigonometric reasoning, geometric\nrepresentation, and mathematical modeling. Each chapter combines\nmathematical explanations with worked examples, visual investigations,\nguided practice, and computational laboratories. The R and Python\nexamples appear sequentially, not side by side. Students should first\nreason about the mathematics, then use code to explore, check, and\ninterpret results. The scientific and health-related settings are\ninstructional examples, not clinical recommendations."}, {"title": "Preface", "section": "How to Use This\nBook", "url": "index.html#how-to-use-this-book", "text": "Begin each chapter with its learning outcomes. Work examples by hand\nand attempt guided practice before reviewing solutions. For\ncomputational activities, execute the complete R listing and then the\nPython listing. Separate, runnable scripts are provided in the companion\nproject under code/chXX/ . Figures are collected under figures/chXX/ . The selected-answer files are retained\nseparately for instructor preparation. Next →"}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "Getting Started with R and Python", "url": "programming.html#app:programming", "text": "Purpose. This appendix is a practical,\nbeginner-friendly reference for the computational activities throughout\nthis book. You do not need previous programming experience. The goal is\nto understand the mathematics first, then use a small amount of code to\ncalculate, visualize, check, and explain your results. The two sections\ncover the same essential skills so that you can work in either language\nor compare both."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "Getting Started with R", "url": "programming.html#app:r", "text": ""}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "A.1 What is\nR, and what do I need?", "url": "programming.html#a.1-what-is-r-and-what-do-i-need", "text": "R is a free programming language designed for numerical analysis,\nstatistics, and graphics. R is the language; RStudio is an optional development environment that\nmakes it easier to edit and run R scripts. Install R from https://cran.r-project.org/ , then optionally install\nRStudio Desktop from https://posit.co/download/rstudio-desktop/ . Follow your\noperating system’s installation instructions. In RStudio, you can type\nshort commands in the Console , but save reusable code in a\nscript ( File > New File > R Script ). Save a script\nwith the extension .R . Select a line or lines and use the\nRun command, or use Source to run the entire script. For a\nfirst exercise, type: R Copy code 2 + 3 sqrt ( 49 ) pi You should obtain 5, 7, and an approximation to \\(\\pi\\) . If an error appears, read the\nmessage carefully: R is case-sensitive, so Sqrt(49) is not\nthe same as sqrt(49) ."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "A.2\nArithmetic, variables, and comments", "url": "programming.html#a.2-arithmetic-variables-and-comments", "text": "R uses + , - , * , / , and ^ for addition, subtraction,\nmultiplication, division, and powers. Parentheses control the order of\noperations. Assign a value with <- ; a # starts a comment that R ignores. R Copy code # A circle with radius 4 centimeters radius <- 4 area <- pi * radius ^ 2 circumference <- 2 * pi * radius print (area) print (circumference) Interpretation. The calculated area is measured in\nsquare centimeters, whereas circumference is measured in centimeters.\nCode returns numbers, but you must supply and check the units. Try it: change the radius to 6. Predict the factor by\nwhich the area changes before running the code. Answer: \\((6/4)^2=2.25\\) ."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "A.3\nVectors, sequences, and tables", "url": "programming.html#a.3-vectors-sequences-and-tables", "text": "A vector stores several values of the same basic type. Use c() to combine values, seq() to create a\nsequence, and data.frame() to assemble a table. R’s vector\nindices begin at 1. R Copy code t <- c ( 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ) concentration <- 80 * ( 0.75 ) ^ t measurements <- data.frame ( time_hours = t, concentration = concentration) print (measurements) print (concentration[ 1 ]) # first observation The concentration is modeled by \\(C(t)=80(0.75)^t\\) . This is a hypothetical example. The multiplier 0.75 means that 75% of the\nprevious amount remains after each hour. Try it: replace t with seq(0, 8, by = 0.5) and observe\nhow the table changes."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "A.4\nFunctions and function evaluation", "url": "programming.html#a.4-functions-and-function-evaluation", "text": "A function takes an input and returns an output. Mathematical\nnotation \\(f(x)=x^2-4x+3\\) corresponds\nto the following R definition: R Copy code f <- function (x) { x ^ 2 - 4 * x + 3 } print ( f ( 2 )) x <- seq ( - 1 , 5 , by = 0.5 ) print ( data.frame ( x = x, f_x = f (x))) R automatically applies arithmetic operations to each element of a\nnumeric vector. Note that multiplication must be written explicitly as 4*x . Check by hand: \\(f(2)=4-8+3=-1\\) ."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "A.5 Plotting a\nfunction", "url": "programming.html#a.5-plotting-a-function", "text": "The following example uses base R , so no extra package is\nrequired. Always include axis labels, appropriate units, and a\ndescriptive title. R Copy code f <- function (x) x ^ 2 - 4 * x + 3 x <- seq ( - 1 , 5 , length.out = 301 ) plot (x, f (x), type = \"l\" , lwd = 2 , xlab = \"x\" , ylab = \"f(x)\" , main = \"Quadratic function\" ) abline ( h = 0 , v = 0 , col = \"gray70\" ) points ( c ( 1 , 3 ), c ( 0 , 0 ), pch = 19 ) The graph crosses the horizontal axis at \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=3\\) , consistent with \\(f(x)=(x-1)(x-3)\\) . A plotted curve is\nevidence to explore; algebra verifies the exact zeros. Figure\nquality checklist: readable axes, correct units, sufficient\nsampling resolution, visible important features, and a caption that\nexplains the mathematical takeaway."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "A.6\nTrigonometry and radians", "url": "programming.html#a.6-trigonometry-and-radians", "text": "R’s sin() , cos() , and tan() functions expect angles in radians . Convert degrees using \\(\\theta_{\\mathrm{rad}}=\\theta_{\\mathrm{deg}}\\pi/180\\) . R Copy code angle_deg <- 30 angle_rad <- angle_deg * pi / 180 print ( sin (angle_rad)) # 0.5 angles <- seq ( 0 , 2 * pi, length.out = 401 ) plot (angles, sin (angles), type = \"l\" , xlab = \"Angle (radians)\" , ylab = \"sin(angle)\" , main = \"One complete sine cycle\" ) A common mistake is to type sin(30) and assume that R\ninterprets 30 degrees. It does not."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "A.7\nSolving equations numerically", "url": "programming.html#a.7-solving-equations-numerically", "text": "Use algebra whenever possible, then use numerical methods to check or\napproximate solutions. The uniroot() function finds a zero\nwithin an interval whose endpoints have opposite signs (for a continuous\nfunction). R Copy code g <- function (x) x ^ 3 - x - 2 print ( g ( 1 )) print ( g ( 2 )) root <- uniroot (g, interval = c ( 1 , 2 )) $ root print (root) print ( g (root)) The numerical root is approximately 1.52138. The final residual \\(g(\\text{root})\\) should be close to zero. A\nsmall residual is useful but is not, by itself, a proof of\nuniqueness."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "A.8 Data, basic model\nfitting, and residuals", "url": "programming.html#a.8-data-basic-model-fitting-and-residuals", "text": "A residual is observed minus predicted . In this example we\nfit a straight line to illustrative calibration measurements. R Copy code x <- c ( 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ) y <- c ( 1.1 , 2.9 , 5.2 , 7.0 , 9.1 ) fit <- lm (y ~ x) predicted <- predict (fit) residual <- y - predicted print ( data.frame (x, y, predicted, residual)) plot (x, y, pch = 19 , xlab = \"Input (units)\" , ylab = \"Response (units)\" , main = \"Calibration\" ) abline (fit, lwd = 2 ) The fitted line summarizes the relationship; it does not guarantee\nthat the model is appropriate outside the observed input range. Ask\nwhether residuals show a pattern, whether units make sense, and whether\nextrapolation is justified."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "A.9 Common\nerrors and good habits", "url": "programming.html#a.9-common-errors-and-good-habits", "text": "Missing multiplication: write 2*x ,\nnot 2x . Case sensitivity: Radius and radius are different names. Mismatched vector lengths: check the lengths of\nquantities combined in a table. Domain restrictions: sqrt(-1) and log(0) do not yield ordinary real-valued results. Reproducibility: keep scripts, record units and\nassumptions, and add comments explaining the mathematical goal. R practice task. Create a table and graph of \\(h(t)=120(0.8)^t\\) for \\(t=0,1,\\ldots,10\\) . Find the first integer \\(t\\) for which \\(h(t)<30\\) . Check the result\nalgebraically using logarithms. Check: \\(t=7\\) ."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "Getting Started with Python", "url": "programming.html#app:python", "text": ""}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.1\nWhat is Python, and what do I need?", "url": "programming.html#b.1-what-is-python-and-what-do-i-need", "text": "Python is a general-purpose programming language widely used in\nscience, engineering, and data analysis. Download Python from https://www.python.org/downloads/ . You may work in a\nPython editor or notebook (for example, IDLE, VS Code, or Jupyter). For\nthis textbook, a basic editor that can run .py files is\nsufficient. Some later laboratories use the numpy and matplotlib libraries; install them from a terminal with python -m pip install numpy matplotlib . On systems where\nthe Python command is python3 , use python3 -m pip install numpy matplotlib instead.\nInstallation steps can vary by operating system. Python Copy code print ( 2 + 3 ) print ( 49 ** 0.5 ) You should see 5 and 7.0. Python uses ** for powers, not ^ . A # begins a comment. Save reusable scripts\nwith the extension .py ."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.2 Arithmetic,\nvariables, and the math library", "url": "programming.html#b.2-arithmetic-variables-and-the-math-library", "text": "Python Copy code import math # A circle with radius 4 centimeters radius = 4 area = math.pi * radius ** 2 circumference = 2 * math.pi * radius print (area) print (circumference) import math makes functions and constants such as math.sqrt , math.log , and math.pi available. Python uses = for assignment; use == to test equality. Try it: change the\nradius to 6 and compare the areas, then explain why the ratio is\n2.25."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.3 Lists, loops,\nand tables", "url": "programming.html#b.3-lists-loops-and-tables", "text": "A list holds multiple values. Python indices begin at 0. A for loop can repeat a calculation; the indented lines form\nthe loop body. Python Copy code times = [ 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ] concentrations = [ 80 * ( 0.75 ** t) for t in times] for t, c in zip (times, concentrations): print ( f\"time= { t } h, concentration= { c :.2f} \" ) print (concentrations[ 0 ]) # first observation This computes the same hypothetical model \\(C(t)=80(0.75)^t\\) used in the R section. A\nlist comprehension such as [expression for t in times] creates a new list from repeated calculations. Try it: use range(9) to produce times from 0 through 8."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.4 Functions\nand evaluation", "url": "programming.html#b.4-functions-and-evaluation", "text": "Python functions are defined using def ; indentation is\nrequired. The return statement supplies the function’s\noutput. Python Copy code def f(x): return x ** 2 - 4 * x + 3 print (f( 2 )) for x in [ - 1 , 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 ]: print (x, f(x)) Check by hand that \\(f(2)=-1\\) and\nfactor \\(f(x)\\) to find its zeros. In\ncontrast with R, applying a function written for a single number to an\nordinary Python list requires a loop or comprehension."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.5 Arrays and\nplotting with NumPy and Matplotlib", "url": "programming.html#b.5-arrays-and-plotting-with-numpy-and-matplotlib", "text": "NumPy arrays support elementwise mathematical calculations.\nMatplotlib produces graphs. The following complete script plots the same\nquadratic as the R example. Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt def f(x): return x ** 2 - 4 * x + 3 x = np.linspace( - 1 , 5 , 301 ) plt.plot(x, f(x), linewidth = 2 , label = \"f(x)\" ) plt.scatter([ 1 , 3 ], [ 0 , 0 ], label = \"Zeros\" ) plt.axhline( 0 , color = \"gray\" , linewidth = 0.7 ) plt.xlabel( \"x\" ) plt.ylabel( \"f(x)\" ) plt.title( \"Quadratic function\" ) plt.legend() plt.tight_layout() plt.show() np.linspace(-1,5,301) produces 301 equally spaced inputs\nfrom \\(-1\\) to 5. The show() call displays the figure in a standard Python\nscript. To save a publication-quality image, call plt.savefig(\"quadratic.png\", dpi=300) before plt.show() ."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.6\nTrigonometry and radians", "url": "programming.html#b.6-trigonometry-and-radians", "text": "Python’s math.sin() and NumPy’s np.sin() both expect radians. Python Copy code import math import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt angle_deg = 30 angle_rad = math.radians(angle_deg) print (math.sin(angle_rad)) # approximately 0.5 angles = np.linspace( 0 , 2 * np.pi, 401 ) plt.plot(angles, np.sin(angles)) plt.xlabel( \"Angle (radians)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"sin(angle)\" ) plt.title( \"One complete sine cycle\" ) plt.tight_layout() plt.show() The conversion math.radians(30) is equivalent to \\(30\\pi/180\\) . Never assume a trigonometric\nfunction uses degrees."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.7 Numerical\nsolutions without extra packages", "url": "programming.html#b.7-numerical-solutions-without-extra-packages", "text": "Bisection is a reliable way to approximate a root of a continuous\nfunction when a sign change is known. It repeatedly halves an interval\ncontaining the root. Python Copy code def g(x): return x ** 3 - x - 2 a, b = 1.0 , 2.0 assert g(a) * g(b) < 0 for step in range ( 40 ): mid = (a + b) / 2 if g(a) * g(mid) <= 0 : b = mid else : a = mid root = (a + b) / 2 print (root) print (g(root)) The root is approximately 1.52138. The loop is an implementation of\nthe mathematical interval-halving procedure; it does not replace\nunderstanding why the sign-change condition matters."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.8\nData, linear models, and residuals", "url": "programming.html#b.8-data-linear-models-and-residuals", "text": "The following example uses NumPy to fit a straight line to the same\nillustrative calibration data used in R. Python Copy code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt x = np.array([ 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ], dtype = float ) y = np.array([ 1.1 , 2.9 , 5.2 , 7.0 , 9.1 ]) slope, intercept = np.polyfit(x, y, 1 ) predicted = slope * x + intercept residuals = y - predicted print ( \"slope:\" , slope, \"intercept:\" , intercept) print ( \"residuals:\" , residuals) plt.scatter(x, y, label = \"Observed\" ) plt.plot(x, predicted, label = \"Fitted line\" ) plt.xlabel( \"Input (units)\" ) plt.ylabel( \"Response (units)\" ) plt.legend() plt.tight_layout() plt.show() Compare the slope and intercept with those obtained from lm(y   x) in R. Small differences caused by rounding are\nexpected, but both programs should fit the same least-squares line."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.9 Common\nerrors and good habits", "url": "programming.html#b.9-common-errors-and-good-habits", "text": "Powers: use x**2 , not x2̂ . Indentation: align statements inside for loops and def blocks\nconsistently. Missing imports: np and plt are available only after the corresponding import statements. Lists versus arrays: [1,2,3] * 2 repeats a list; np.array([1,2,3]) * 2 doubles each\nentry. Domains: verify that square roots and logarithms\nare evaluated on appropriate real-valued inputs. Reproducibility: include comments, document\nunits, use meaningful variable names, and save both scripts and\nfigures. Python practice task. Create a table and graph of \\(h(t)=120(0.8)^t\\) for \\(t=0,1,\\ldots,10\\) . Find the first integer \\(t\\) for which \\(h(t)<30\\) and verify the result\nalgebraically. Check: \\(t=7\\) ."}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.10 Moving\nbetween R and Python", "url": "programming.html#b.10-moving-between-r-and-python", "text": "Task R Python Assign a variable x <- 3 x = 3 Power x2̂ x**2 Square root sqrt(x) math.sqrt(x) Natural logarithm log(x) math.log(x) Create values c(1,2,3) [1,2,3] Sequence seq(0,1,by=.1) np.linspace(0,1,11) First item x[1] x[0] Display output print(x) print(x)"}, {"title": "Getting Started with R and Python", "section": "B.11 A repeatable\nworkflow for every laboratory", "url": "programming.html#b.11-a-repeatable-workflow-for-every-laboratory", "text": "State the mathematical question. Identify the\nunknown quantity, assumptions, domain, and units. Predict before computing. Estimate a value,\ngraph shape, or direction of change by hand. Run the complete code. Use the chapter’s code/chXX/ script or type the listing carefully. Inspect output critically. Check axes, units,\nnumerical precision, restrictions, and possible errors. Verify independently. Substitute an answer into\nthe original expression or compare with an algebraic result. Interpret in words. State what the result means\nin context and where the model may fail. Final reminder. Code is a tool for mathematical\nexploration. A convincing mathematical explanation identifies the\nrelevant properties, shows the reasoning, and interprets the result; a\nscreenshot or numerical answer alone is not enough. ← Previous Next →"}, {"title": "Computational\nResource Guide", "section": "Computational\nResource Guide", "url": "resources.html#computational-resource-guide", "text": "Each chapter has a numbered folder of R and Python scripts in the\ncompanion source package. Run the scripts from their own folders or set\nthe working directory appropriately. The code is intended to support\nnumerical experimentation, graphical verification, and interpretation,\nnot to replace algebraic derivation or mathematical proof. Figures\nreproduced by scripts should be checked for labeling, units, domains,\nand computational assumptions."}, {"title": "Computational\nResource Guide", "section": "Editorial\nand Verification Notes", "url": "resources.html#editorial-and-verification-notes", "text": "This expanded edition preserves the original sixteen chapters and\nadds a model-based application, paired computational scripts, and a\nreport project to each chapter. New examples use illustrative rather\nthan empirical data. Mathematical peer review, comprehensive R execution\ntesting, independent exercise-solution verification, and permission\nchecks should be completed before publication. Page counts depend on\nfinal print trim, typography, and placement of figures and code. ← Previous"}, {"title": "Equations,\nInequalities, and Applications | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Linear inequalities", "url": "chapter02.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Linear inequalities Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Identify where the first curve is above the second. What changes when the slope is negative?"}, {"title": "Rates of\nChange, Difference Quotients, and Limits | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Secant approaching a tangent", "url": "chapter15.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Secant approaching a tangent Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Reduce h and compare the secant slope with 2a."}, {"title": "Exponential and\nLogarithmic Functions | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Exponential growth and decay", "url": "chapter07.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Exponential growth and decay Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Compare doubling and halving times for opposite rates."}, {"title": "Polynomial and Rational\nFunctions | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Polynomial roots", "url": "chapter06.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Polynomial roots Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Make two roots coincide. How does the graph behave at a repeated root?"}, {"title": "Coordinate\nGeometry, Lines, and Systems of Equations | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Intersection of two lines", "url": "chapter03.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Intersection of two lines Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Find the intersection algebraically. When are the lines parallel or identical?"}, {"title": "Real\nNumbers, Algebraic Expressions, and Mathematical Reasoning | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Absolute value and distance", "url": "chapter01.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Absolute value and distance Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Where is the minimum? Solve |x − a| = 2."}, {"title": "Trigonometric Identities\nand Equations | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Trigonometric equations", "url": "chapter10.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Trigonometric equations Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Count solutions on one full period. When are there none?"}, {"title": "Vectors, Polar\nCoordinates, and Geometric Modeling | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Vector components", "url": "chapter14.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Vector components Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Calculate the magnitude and direction; explain the zero vector."}, {"title": "Applications\nof Trigonometry and Periodic Models | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Periodic temperature model", "url": "chapter11.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Periodic temperature model Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Predict the daily maximum, minimum and their times."}, {"title": "Functions,\nGraphs, and Multiple Representations | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Function representations", "url": "chapter04.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Function representations Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Predict the range and evaluate the function at x = 2."}, {"title": "Trigonometric\nFunctions and Their Graphs | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Sinusoidal graphs", "url": "chapter09.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Sinusoidal graphs Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Determine the period and midline from the parameters."}, {"title": "Angles, Radian\nMeasure, and the Unit Circle | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Unit circle coordinates", "url": "chapter08.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Unit circle coordinates Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Predict the signs of cosine and sine in each quadrant."}, {"title": "Conic Sections and\nParametric Equations | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Ellipse geometry", "url": "chapter13.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Ellipse geometry Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: When is the ellipse a circle? Identify its intercepts."}, {"title": "Sequences, Series,\nand Mathematical Patterns | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Geometric sequences", "url": "chapter12.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Geometric sequences Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Compare convergence for ratios inside and outside the interval (−1,1)."}, {"title": "Integrated\nMathematical Modeling with R and Python | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Comparing growth models", "url": "chapter16.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Comparing growth models Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Compare predictions at early and late times. Discuss which model is appropriate for a real dataset."}, {"title": "Transformations,\nCompositions, and Inverse Functions | Applied Precalculus", "section": "Interactive investigation: Function transformations", "url": "chapter05.html#interactive-investigation", "text": "Interactive investigation: Function transformations Enable JavaScript to explore the model. Explore and explain: Predict the vertex before moving each slider. What happens at zero stretch?"}];